regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_minimax.txt:1:VERDICT LINE FIRST: **NO SURVIVING (d) MUTANT — swept: the four author-named candidates plus all predicate/operand swaps I could derive from the inlined code. Disclosure gaps remain (literal pins, SH-coverage in rldimi pattern rows) but none enable a survivor.**
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_minimax.txt:11:| # | Mutant | Caught by | Why it cannot survive |
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_minimax.txt:45:- `do/while` → `while`: skips body when `mb == (64-sh)&63`, i.e. when `mb + sh ≡ 0 mod 64`. Pattern rows listed (`sh40 mb40`, `sh63 mb63`, `sh48 mb32`) have `mb+sh ∈ {80, 126, 80} mod 64 = {16, 62, 16}`, none ≡ 0; but the 495-cell census reaches `mb+sh ≡ 0` cells. I cannot enumerate 495 cells from the packet — see disclosure gap below — but the cell `sh=32, mb=32` is overwhelmingly likely in any dense census. If the census is missing all `mb+sh ≡ 0 (mod 64)` cells, this mutant would survive the rldimi leg. **Flag, not refuted.**
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_minimax.txt:52:- **rldimi pattern rows span SH ∈ {0, 8, 16, 32, 40, 48, 63}.** Missing: 1–7, 9–15, 17–31, 33–39, 41–47, 49–62. A rotate defect periodic in SH with period dividing one of the covered values AND only visible at uncovered SH would escape. I could not construct one among the natural operand/predicate swaps above — every one I tried either fires at SH=8 (the most-covered intermediate) or at SH=63 (covers all parities). But this is "I couldn't build one", not "no such mutant exists"; an exotic defect (e.g., a conditional rotate guard keyed on a specific SH bit-pattern) could in principle slip through.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_kimi.txt:4:## 1. The one question — DEPENDS-ON enumeration and survivor search
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_kimi.txt:17:- **End test without `& 63` (`m != 64 - sh`, brief's candidate #3): DEAD — by hang, not by value.** For sh≥1, 64-sh ∈ 1..63, identical. At sh=0, marker = 64, unreachable since m ∈ 0..63 mod 64: infinite loop → session timeout → gate red (READ precedent: D_NOCIRC timed out at cell 182 once and "fails the gate either way"). So this one is loud, not a survivor.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_kimi.txt:20:- **`while (n-- > 1)` and the whole rotate class (`n-- >= 0`, `n = sh ± k`, `n = sh & 31`, direction reversal, `(s >> 63)` operand swaps): CANNOT BE SETTLED FROM THE PACKET.** REASONED: `n-- > 1` rotates sh−1 times for sh≥1, 0 times for sh=0 (no hang, n unused afterward, mask completely untouched). The census sees none of it (rotation-invariant source, READ). The only channels are the 9 pattern rows (sh values 8, 32, 16, 0, 40, 63, 48). If any one of them Book-I-computes from an aperiodic 64-bit source, this mutant is dead on ≥6 rows — e.g. `nowrap sh8 mb0` observes 56 of 64 bits, and ROTL(s,7) ≡ ROTL(s,8) on 56 consecutive bits forces s periodic with period 1. If the pattern rows perversely reuse an all-ones (or short-period) source, **every rotate mutant on rldimi survives** — off-by-one, direction, wrong width, no-rotate. **Single fact that settles it: the source literal and expected-value expression of any nonzero-sh pattern row (e.g. `wrap sh48 mb32`).** Second-best fact: one more leg in the frozen table, `D_ROT_M1` (`n-- > 1`), predicted 27 CONTROL=OK with ≤6 row fails if the rows are live, 27/27 green if they are not. The packet's own kill table contains no rotate-pure leg (READ: D_NOKEEP_SH couples correct rotation with a corrupted end marker; D_ORIG is compound), so the brief's sentence "the pattern rows do" is asserted, not evidenced, in this packet — exactly the shape of premise this panel has been burned ratifying.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_glm.txt:3:**1. The survivor [READ + REASONED].** READ: the rldimi probe's 27 rows are 13 controls, 5 nowrap, 4 wrap, 3 preservation, 2 census rows — no aliasing row; the rlwimi probe has three (`alias nowrap`, `alias wrap`, `alias both`), so the asymmetry is in the packet, not my guess. READ: the census is "from an all-ones source" into seeded destinations, mirroring the rlwimi census's "from RS_VAL" phrasing — a distinct source register. REASONED: the mutant re-reads `gpr[rs]` after `gpr[ra] &= ~tmp`; for RS ≠ RA that register is never written by rldimi, so the re-read equals the snapshot — provably identical, 27/27 plus 28/28 on the rlwimi probe (mutants are cross-probe green by the table's own isolation property [READ]). For RS == RA the masked bits were just cleared, so `tmp & gpr[rs]` = 0: masked positions get zero-filled instead of merged. That is real, idiomatic code (`rldimi r3,r3,sh,mb` in-place field insert), so this is a genuine defect, not an equivalent mutant. rlwimi cannot host the analogous mutant: it commits `reg(arg[1]) = ra` once at the end, so a mid-loop re-read of `reg(arg[0])` returns the original RS even under aliasing — and its alias rows would kill it anyway. Scope note: "clause (d)" is not defined in the packet; I take it as Q1's predicate/operand-dependence scope, which the author's own legs already extend past the diff (D_NO_MERGE mutates the merge line). If clause (d) is strictly diff-lines, demote this to "probe gap, out of charter." Settling fact: whether *any* rldimi row or census cell drives RS == RA.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_glm.txt:5:**2. A conditional second survivor class [READ + REASONED].** READ: the rldimi census uses an all-ones source, so it is blind by construction to every mutant that alters only the rotated value (rotate amount, rotate direction, carry tap position — any rotation of all-ones is all-ones). It is *not* blind to lost-carry mutants (`b & 0` turns all-ones into `~0 << sh`, visible) or mask-shape mutants. READ: the author asserts "the pattern rows do [test the rotate amount]" — but the pattern rows' RS literals are **not inlined** (unlike the rlwimi probe, where RS_VAL/MUT_RS/SENT are all stated). REASONED: if the pattern sources are non-uniform, `n-- > 1` (rotates sh−1), `n = mb`, rotate-right, and `>>63 → >>62` are killed by `nowrap sh8 mb0`/`wrap sh40 mb40`; if the pattern rows share the census's all-ones convention, all of them are green — note no measured leg isolates the rotate amount (D_NOKEEP_SH's visible defect is the mask via sh = −1, not the rotation [REASONED]). Settling fact: the RS literal and SH of one pattern row, e.g. `wrap sh40 mb40`, plus the fields of `nowrap single` and `wrap full`.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\ollama_glm.txt:8:- `mb > me` → `mb >= me`: **killed** by census cells (SH,0,0) and (SH,31,31) for SH ∈ {0,1,8,16,31} — ten cells into SENT; the mutant writes rotate(0xCAFEF00D,SH) where 0x5A5A5A5A must survive. BASE green proves the author's RTL model preserves the high word at MB==ME, so the model must flag this. Corroboration that the census compares the full 64-bit GPR: R_REV_COND=13 = 14 named rows + census wrong; R_ONLY_CAST=19 = 8 named wrap rows + census wrong; D_NULL=12 = 14 named + census wrong + cr0 record — all exact fits that require census wrong to be a live high-word channel. Strongest: R_POST_LOOP=19 = R_ONLY_CAST=19, explained because the loop exits with mb==me, making the moved guard `(me > me)` dead — the two legs are the same defect and measured identically.
