regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:81:  **Candidate: `sh ?` → always rotate** `(tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))` with sh=0: `tmp >> 32` UB on uint32_t. In practice (x86) shift count masked → tmp >> 0 = tmp... wait, for 32-bit shifts on x86, count masked to 5 bits → 32 & 31 = 0 → tmp. So (tmp << 0) | (tmp >> 0) = tmp. Behaviorally identical on x86-64! Hmm — is that a surviving mutant? The guard `sh ?` exists precisely to avoid UB `tmp >> (32-0) = tmp >> 32`. On MSVC x86-64, `>>` for uint32_t with count 32: the compiler may emit `shr eax, cl` with cl=32 → masked to 0 → returns tmp. So behaviorally tmp. So mutant `tmp = (tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh));` unconditionally is behaviorally equivalent on common hardware (though UB in C). Is that a "(d) mutant"? Clause (d) seems to be "predicates dominating the edited lines and operand swaps". `sh ?` is a predicate dominating... but is the rotate line one of "the edited lines"? The fix edits were: (1) `uint32_t tmp` (was MODE_uint_t), (2) the `if (mb > me)` line added. The rotate line `tmp = sh ? (...) : tmp` — was that pre-existing? Looking at the original gxemul code... In original gxemul 0.6.x, rlwimi is:
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:103:  Also on gcc, `tmp >> 32` where tmp is uint32_t: compile-time-constant? sh is runtime, so emitted shift instruction; on x86 SHR with CL masked to 31 → count 0 → unchanged. So identity. Equivalent mutant. Mutation testing convention: equivalent mutants are noted, not counted as survivors (they're unkillable). But strictly it "stays green". I should mention it as a noted equivalent-on-x86 mutant — honest disclosure. Actually wait — is it really equivalent? `(tmp << 0) | (tmp >> 32)`: if the compiler does the math at O2 with UB exploitation... gcc could assume sh != 0? No — sh is a runtime value 0..31, and the compiler can't assume the shift count. UB exploitation requires the compiler to prove the count is out of range, which it can't. So emitted code: mov ecx, 32; sub ecx, sh... it computes 32-sh at runtime = 32 when sh=0; shr with count 32 masked → 0 → tmp. Fine. Equivalent in practice on x86-64. I'll note it.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:119:  Oh wait — R_POST_LOOP measured 19, i.e., killed. But semantically, moving that line after the loop... the loop only modifies bits 32..63 (C numbering, low word)? mask = 1 << (31-mb), mb in 0..31 → bits 0..31 of the uint64. Yes loop only touches low word. And the high-word line sets high word, preserving low. Order doesn't matter! Both commute! So R_POST_LOOP should be BEHAVIORALLY EQUIVALENT — a survivor — yet measured 19/28 (killed)?!
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:140:  So why 19? Something's off. Possibilities: (a) the measured table is fictional/wrong — but my job is to check premises, and this is a premise I can check by reading... actually I can't re-run (rule says don't run gates, but building a single mutant in my own temp dir is allowed... though that takes a long time; also I can't easily replicate their harness quickly. Hmm, actually maybe I can — but let me think more first). (b) The mutation was applied differently than described — e.g., moved after the loop but after `reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra;`? That would also be equivalent (ra local, written back unchanged... no wait — if moved after writeback, the high-word change to local `ra` never gets written back → high word never updated → equals R_ONLY_CAST ≈ 19!). 
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:198:  Candidate **rotate loop `n-- > 1`**: rotates sh-1 times instead of sh (for sh≥1; for sh=0: n-- > 1 with n=0 → 0 > 1 false → no rotate, same as fix ✓; for sh=1: n=1 → 1 > 1 false → 0 rotations instead of 1 ✗). So mutant = rotate by sh-1 for sh≥1. Detector: census source is ALL ONES — rotate of all-ones is all-ones regardless! Census is BLIND to rotate amount. Pattern rows: `nowrap sh8 mb0`, `nowrap sh32 mb0`, `nowrap sh16 mb16`, `wrap sh40 mb40`, `wrap sh63 mb63`, `wrap sh48 mb32`... what's the source for pattern rows? For rldimi the brief doesn't explicitly state the pattern-row source value. rlwimi used RS_VAL with halves differ; rldimi rows presumably use some non-trivial pattern. If pattern rows use a value where rotl by sh-1 ≠ rotl by sh (any non-degenerate value), then rows with sh≥1 kill it: `nowrap sh8 mb0` (rotate 7 vs 8). Unless the pattern rows only check the masked-merged result against expected computed with correct rotate → yes they'd fail under mutant. So killed by pattern rows — PROVIDED pattern-row source is not all-ones. The brief says census source is all-ones; pattern rows presumably use a different pattern (else they'd be redundant... not necessarily). Hmm. Need to read the probe to confirm. If pattern rows also used all-ones, `n-- > 1` would survive! That's candidate question 2 territory: "the census source for rldimi is all-ones: which rotate defects is that blind to, and do the pattern rows actually cover them?" I need to read ppc_rldimi_probe.py.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:250:  Hold on: is `0xffffffff` → `0xfffffffe` in scope for clause (d)? Clause (d) mutants are: predicate mutations dominating edited lines, and operand swaps from body-read fields. A constant tweak `0xffffffff` → `0xfffffffe` is neither a predicate flip nor an operand swap. It's a boundary/constant mutation — arguably clause (e) or out of scope. The question says "Enumerate DEPENDS-ON for each diff: the predicates DOMINATING the edited lines up to the function boundary... and operand swaps drawn from fields the body reads". So constants are out of scope. OK — but I could still mention it if it survives... let me double check its survival: under `ra & 0xfffffffe`: after line, low bit 0 cleared. Loop wrap case writes all bits in mb..31,0..me — includes PPC31 (C0) always in wrap → C0 = tmp bit 0. So final identical to fix. For non-wrap, line doesn't run. So yes, equivalent mutant. But out of (d) scope. Mention as a curiosity? Maybe briefly. Actually — it's strictly behaviorally equivalent, i.e., not even a real bug. Equivalent mutants aren't survivors in the actionable sense. I'll note it only if space permits. Hmm, actually careful: it IS a semantic change in general (if the loop didn't cover that bit) but in context it's dead. Equivalent-mutant. Skip or one line.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:266:  So R_POST_LOOP is behaviorally equivalent and CANNOT be killed by any behavioral detector. Measured 19 ⇒ the leg as described didn't do what it says, or the measurement is bogus. Strong premise flag. BUT — maybe I'm misreading "the loop". If "move after the loop" meant after the ENTIRE remaining body (including writeback), then dead store → high word never written → behavior = R_ONLY_CAST = 19/28 exactly. The identical 19s support this reading. So most likely explanation: the mutation placed the statement after `reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra;`, making it dead code, and 19 matches R_ONLY_CAST. The leg name then mis-describes the edit ("after the loop" vs "after the writeback"). Either way the table's claim "move AFTER the loop → 19 kills" is not evidence that a true post-loop (pre-writeback) placement is caught — because that mutant is equivalent and green. But since it's equivalent, it doesn't matter for survival. It matters as a detector-honesty note: the panel should not count R_POST_LOOP as a meaningful kill. Hmm, wait — actually, let me reconsider: is it possible the harness moved it right after the loop, before writeback, and the probe killed it via... `cr0 record form`? rc rows: update_cr0(cpu, ra) — same ra either way. Disasm rows: no. No row can see a difference. So no. Flag it.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\kimi.txt:268:  Actually, hmm, one more possibility: the mutation harness might have moved the line after the loop but ALSO the writeback reads `ra`... no. Or the mutation introduced a compile error (unused?) — no. Or the probe rows that "killed" it were the wrap rows and the mutation put it after the loop INSIDE the loop braces mis-placed → compile error → build failed → all rows fail? 19 ≠ 0... no, build fail would give 0 or CONTROL=FAIL everywhere. The clean 19=R_ONLY_CAST strongly suggests dead-store placement. I'll flag it as: described placement is behaviorally equivalent; measured kill implies actual placement differed (likely post-writeback, dead store, equivalent to R_ONLY_CAST). Not a survivor — but a premise inaccuracy in the table.
