regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2389:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\pass2_20260910\grok.txt:1:I'll verify the unsigned-compare defect, the signed-range fix, and the 13-row kill table against the tree rather than ratifying the brief. Starting with the product code, the probe, and the premises the round depends on.The checkpoint still says three accept rows are owed; I'll treat that as stale and check the probe, Alpha invalidate path, and `vaddr_mask` default against the tree.The two-sided window may make the signed cast a no-op; I'll enumerate that, then check PAL siblings, ASM metadata, and whether the comment overclaims architecture.**VERDICT: RATIFY the executable line and the 13-row table.** No remaining clause-(d) hole in the rowed selector window. Do not spend a row on “delete the signed casts.” The axis still unrowed is **cast width** (and, separately, an `a1` conjunct at a VA no row uses). L4 on two sentences in the product comment, not on the fix.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2395:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\pass3_panel_20260906_102412\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-06T15:30:15.135372679Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me work through this carefully. I'm a \"packet-fed seat\" \u2014 I must reason only from the inlined text, no file access, no measurements. My verdict line first, claims labeled READ or REASONED, never MEASURED. The brief asks:\n\n1. THE ONE QUESTION: Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the shipped composite (fix v2 + probe v3)? The enumeration: the dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (its constant's source, its bound both ways, its slot set 0..3); the body-read fields `interrupt_status` (per device line), `interrupt_enable[cpu]`, `software_interrupt_status[cpu]`, `irqActive[cpu]`, `cpu_irq[cpu]`; joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device line, none} \u00d7 {irqActive}).\n\n2. Second question: of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings/annotations \u2014 lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438), WHICH IS WRONG?\n\nWait \u2014 count the nine dispositions: (1) the capture move, (2-4) three new sessions (B2, C2, E), (5-6) two disclosed equivalents/debts (M_NULLAB equivalent, M_NOREASSERT debt), (7-9) three filings/annotations (lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438). That's nine. The question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 implying at least one is wrong. I need to audit them.\n\nLet me now audit the packet internally.\n\nFirst, the settled premises:\n\nPremise 1: the walk. Per slot: status = d->interrupt_status (machine-wide, six device lines are 1 << (line+25), lines 1..6 \u2192 bits 26..31); enable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu]; if software_interrupt_status[cpu] then status |= (1 << 26); if (status & enable) \u2192 assert if !irqActive, set latch; else \u2192 deassert if irqActive, clear latch.\n\nWait \u2014 interesting: software interrupt sets bit 26, which is line 1's device bit. So SOFT_INT sets the same bit as device line 1. Bit 26 is shared between device line 1 and software interrupt. Hmm, that's from the packet: lines 1..6 \u2192 bits 26..31, and `if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)`. So the software interrupt ORs in bit 26, same bit as device line 1. That's a potential aliasing in the model but presumably upstream behavior. Actually wait \u2014 is that right? Let me re-read: \"the six device lines are `1 << (line + 25)`, lines 1..6 -> bits 26..31\". And \"if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)\". So yes, SOFT_INT uses bit 26 which is the same bit as device line 1. Hmm, that means a software interrupt and device line 1 share a bit. This is stated as settled premise. OK. Actually this might be a real quirk of luna88k hardware (soft interrupts are bit 26?). Whatever \u2014 it's READ from the packet as a premise.\n\nPremise 2: INTERRUPT_ASSERT(istruct) is (istruct).interrupt_assert(&(istruct)) \u2014 no NULL check. DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4; later in DEVINIT ncpus > 4 prints and exit(1)s. emul.c maps ncpus 0 \u2192 1; -n is 1..256.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait: \"-n is 1..256\" and \"ncpus > 4 exits at init\". So -n 5..256 all exit at DEVINIT. So reachable configs are ncpus 1..4. MAX_CPUS is presumably 4 (slot set 0..3 mentioned in the enumeration: \"its slot set 0..3\"). Actually the enumeration says \"its slot set 0..3\" \u2014 so MAX_CPUS = 4? The diff shows `struct interrupt cpu_irq[MAX_CPUS]`. If MAX_CPUS were larger than 4, the walk would go beyond 4 and hit never-connected slots even with ncpus=4. The premise says DEVINIT connects slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4 and exits if ncpus > 4. So on the largest reachable machine (4 cpus), slots 0..3 are all connected. So the bound d->ncpus \u2208 {1,2,3,4} and slot set 0..3. OK.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a subtlety: if -n can be 1..256 and DEVINIT exits when ncpus > 4, then d->ncpus = machine->ncpus could be up to 256 if the capture were ABOVE the exit. The capture is placed BELOW the exit, so d->ncpus \u2264 4 always. The comment in fix v2 says exactly this: \"Captured BELOW the `ncpus > 4' exit so the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction, not by that exit.\" Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 actually, is d->ncpus \u2264 4 guaranteed by capture below the exit? The exit happens at DEVINIT; the capture is after it in DEVINIT. machine->ncpus \u2264 4 past the exit. Yes. But also: is MAX_CPUS == 4? The packet never states MAX_CPUS's value explicitly. Hmm. It says \"its slot set 0..3\" in the question, implying the connected slots are 0..3 max, i.e., MAX_CPUS \u2265 4 and reachable connects are 0..3. If MAX_CPUS were 8, the bound d->ncpus \u2264 4 still keeps the walk within 0..3, all connected on a 4-cpu machine. So the fix is safe regardless of MAX_CPUS value as long as d->ncpus \u2264 4 and connects cover 0..ncpus-1. Fine. But there's a premise: \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4\" \u2014 so connects cover exactly 0..ncpus-1 for ncpus \u2208 {1,2,3,4}. So bound == connect predicate. Good \u2014 \"The walk bound and the connect predicate are the same number on purpose.\"\n\nNow audit the diff itself.\n\nThe diff adds `int ncpus;` to struct luna88k_data, changes the walk bound to `cpu < d->ncpus`, and adds `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` below the `ncpus > 4` exit.\n\nOne thing to check: the diff hunk at line 1038 shows:\n\n```\n \t\texit(1);\n \t}\n \n+\t/* comment */\n+\td->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;\n```\n\nThe context above shows `exit(1); }` \u2014 presumably the `ncpus > 4` check. But wait \u2014 is there any other `exit(1)` before this point in DEVINIT? The context line just above is `exit(1); }`. The comment says the capture is below the ncpus > 4 exit. The packet asserts this in multiple places (premise 2, the minimax finding, the fix comment). I can't verify the exact position beyond the assertion, but the diff context at @@ -1038,6 +1043,19 @@ shows only a fragment. The hunk's context is `exit(1); }` then the added comment, then `add_cmmu_for_cpu(devinit, 0, ...)`. Hmm \u2014 the `ncpus > 4` exit at 1038: is it plausible that the check is `if (machine->ncpus > 4) { fprintf(...); exit(1); }` and then the capture? The context shows three lines before the addition: `exit(1);`, `}`, and blank. That's consistent. But note \u2014 is there possibly an earlier `exit(1)` (e.g., \"no bootrom found\") that could be the context instead? The packet says the capture is below the ncpus>4 exit and the minimax finding drove the move. I'll take it as READ (from packet) that the capture site is below the exit; the diff context is consistent with it but doesn't uniquely prove WHICH exit \u2014 actually, hmm. The diff context shows just `exit(1); }` \u2014 the packet premise says \"later in DEVINIT `ncpus > 4` prints and `exit(1)`s\". The diff's context doesn't show the condition. So strictly, from the diff alone, I can't confirm the capture is below THAT exit; but the packet asserts it repeatedly and the table shows M_HARD1 etc. I'll note it: the packet asserts the capture site; the diff context alone is consistent but not conclusive. Actually \u2014 wait, does it matter? If the capture were above some earlier exit(1) \u2014 there could be an earlier exit for missing ROM etc. If capture is before the ncpus>4 exit, then on -n 256, d->ncpus = 256 and the walk would go to cpu=255 \u2192 crash on unconnected slots. But that's exactly the minimax finding (capture above the exit makes the exit load-bearing) \u2014 addressed by moving it below. The premise says it moved below. OK.\n\nAnother check on the fix: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 is the field `machine->ncpus` the right one? Premise 2: \"emul.c maps ncpus 0 -> 1; -n is 1..256\". And DEVINIT connects based on ncpus thresholds \u2014 presumably reading devinit->machine->ncpus. The fix reads the same source. The enumeration says \"its constant's source\" is part of the audit. If DEVINIT's connect predicate uses a different source than the walk bound... e.g., if DEVINIT used `devinit->machine->ncpus` and the fix captured from somewhere else, mismatch. The fix uses `devinit->machine->ncpus`. The premise says \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under `ncpus >= 2/3/4`\" \u2014 presumably the same machine->ncpus. Not fully verifiable from packet whether DEVINIT's predicate reads machine->ncpus, but it's strongly implied (\"The walk bound and the connect predicate are the same number on purpose\"). READ-ish.\n\nNow \u2014 the probe diff. Session B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 \u2190 0x40000000 (bit 30 = SIO line 5), then stores SIOCMD0 0x01 then 0x02 (WR1 select then TX_IE). Then park, breakpoint VBR+8, continue.\n\nCheck bit math: line 5 \u2192 bit (5+25) = bit 30. 1 << 30 = 0x40000000. \u2713. And bit 30 is within bits 26..31. \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one wrinkle: SOFT_INT also ORs bit 26 into status. In B2, only bit 30 is enabled and SIO line 5 raised. Fine.\n\nSession C2: -n 2, both slots pending: MASK0+MASK1 \u2190 0x04000000 (bit 26), SOFT0+SOFT1 \u2190 1. Bit 26 = 1 << 26 = 0x04000000. \u2713. Both slots pending with software interrupt \u2192 both cpus should enter VBR+8.\n\nSession E: -n 4, cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set, MASK3 (MASK0+12) \u2190 0x04000000, SOFT3 (SOFT0+12) \u2190 1. cpu 0 must observe cpu 3 at VBR+8 and cpus 0..2 not.\n\nCheck addressing: INT_ST_MASK0 base, MASK0+4 = MASK1, +8 = MASK2, +12 = MASK3. Consistent with premise 3 (`cpunr = (addr - INT_ST_MASK0)/4`). \u2713.\n\nNow the table. Scores:\n\n- M_MASK30: 26/27 FAIL, sio5 deliver STOP.\n- M_SWONLY: 26/27 FAIL, sio5 deliver STOP.\n- M_IRQACTIVE0: 26/27 FAIL, both deliver STOP.\n- M_AND1: 25/27 FAIL, cpu3 deliver STOP + others idle BADREGS. That's two red rows \u2192 25/27. \u2713 arithmetic: 27 - 2 = 25. \u2713.\n- M_MIN2: 26/27 FAIL, cpu3 deliver STOP.\n- M_REVERT: 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM, soft1, siofirst HALT.\n- M_OFFBY1 (<=): 20/22.\n- M_NOASSIGN: 18/23 FAIL \u2014 red rows: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 that's 5 red rows. 23 - 5 = 18. \u2713.\n- M_HARD1: 21/24 FAIL \u2014 red: both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 3 red. 24 - 3 = 21. \u2713.\n- M_SKIP1: 23/25 FAIL \u2014 red: both deliver, cpu1 deliver \u2014 2 red. 25 - 2 = 23. \u2713.\n- M_NULLA: 21/22 \u2014 soft1 HALT. 1 red. \u2713.\n- M_NULLAB: 27/27 PASS.\n- M_LATCH: 20/22 \u2014 soft1, siofirst HALT \u2014 2 red. \u2713.\n- M_GUARDASSERT: 21/22 \u2014 soft1 HALT \u2014 1 red. \u2713.\n- M_CALLGUARD: 21/23 \u2014 sio5, siofirst HALT \u2014 2 red. \u2713.\n- M_NOREASSERT: 27/27 PASS.\n- HEAD: 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM with final instrument; soft1, siofirst HALT signal 11; every new session (B2, C2, E) passes on HEAD.\n\nDenominators: the note says \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". Base is 27/27.\n\nLet me now cross-check the denominators per mutant for consistency. The probe rows (v3): Let me enumerate rows from the diff and context.\n\nFrom the probe docstring and code, rows visible: session A2 (rows?), session B (rows: slot0 deliver? siofirst? soft1? \u2014 from HEAD result: soft1, siofirst HALT in session B), session B2 (sio5 deliver), session C (cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow? \u2014 v2 docstring: \"cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\" \u2014 hmm that's three labels but the row is \"cpu1 deliver\"? Actually the docstring line lists \"two cpus / cpu1 setup / mask1 land / survive / cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\" \u2014 these look like row names for session C: maybe rows \"two cpus\", \"cpu1 setup\", \"mask1 land\", \"survive\", \"cpu1 deliver\", \"cpu1 shadow\", \"cpu0 parked\"? That would be 7 rows for session C. Hmm wait, session C in v2 was described: \"session C (-n 2, FREE-RUNNING) cpu 1: VBR..., cpu 0 steps MASK1 <- 0x04000000 and SOFT_INT1 <- 1, parks; breakpoint VBR+8; continue\" with rows \"two cpus / cpu1 setup / mask1 land / survive / cpu1 deliver / cpu1 shadow / cpu0 parked\". So session C has up to 7 rows? But some might be description words not rows. The v2 description column format seems to be a list of row names separated by \" / \". So session C emits rows: two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, survive, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked. That's 7 rows.\n\nSession D (control): \"nodeliver ctl\" \u2014 1 row.\n\nSession A2 and B: unknown row lists, but HEAD's result is 20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM with soft1 and siofirst HALT. Session B appears to include rows soft1, siofirst (and maybe others). Base probe v2 was 20 rows (\"measured 20/20\" appears twice for v2: \"measured 20/20\" for the both-sided NULL guard equivalence, and \"TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20\" hmm \u2014 actually the v2\u2192v3 diff of session C's description changes \"measured 20/20\" to \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". Wait, re-read:\n\nSession C description in v2: \"M_HARD1 (`d->ncpus = 1' hardcoded -- the clause-(d) null repair: a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20): cpu 1 enters its vector...\" \u2014 hmm, this is confusing. In v2, M_HARD1 escaped totally (20/20 with d->ncpus=1 hardcoded?). Wait, if d->ncpus=1 hardcoded, on session C (-n 2), the walk only does slot 0, so cpu 1's IPI would never be delivered \u2192 cpu1 deliver should be STOP (red). So \"TOTAL ESCAPE\" with 20/20 seems wrong for v2 \u2014 and indeed v3's diff changes it: \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". So the v2 docstring claimed M_HARD1 was a total escape measured 20/20, and the v3 diff CORRECTS that to 19/20 with cpu1 deliver red. Wait, but the diff direction: `-` lines are v2, `+` lines are v3. The `-` line: \"a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row, measured 20/20): cpu 1 enters\" and `+` line: \"a TOTAL ESCAPE of every 1-cpu row; measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red): cpu 1 enters\". So v3 fixes a stale/incorrect claim in the v2 docstring. OK \u2014 that's a correction of a prediction. Note \"a predicted sweep is a measurement owed\" \u2014 hmm.\n\nAlso in v3 CONTRACT changes: \"measured 20/20 + 8/8\" \u2192 \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\". So the equivalence claim for M_NULLAB is now 22/22 (the v2-era claim was 20/20 + 8/8 from a different table). And M_NULLAB in table 2 is 27/27 PASS. Hmm wait \u2014 the CONTRACT says \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 killtable (table 1?) vs killtable2 (table 2)? Table 2 header says \"regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/killtable2/\". The CONTRACT v3 says 22/22 in killtable/ \u2014 maybe killtable was the pass-2 table (22 rows after pass-2 additions?) and killtable2 is the new one. Slight inconsistency but not necessarily a defect: v2's probe had 20 rows; pass-2 added sio5 rows? Hmm, actually the pass-2 findings led to fix v2 + probe v3, and killtable/ (22 rows) was presumably the pass-2-era table where M_NULLAB scored 22/22. Fine.\n\nNow, row count: base 27/27 for v3. Sessions: A2 (?), B (?), B2 (1 row: sio5 deliver), C (7 rows), C2 (1 row: both deliver), D (1 row: nodeliver ctl), E (3 rows: four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle). 1+7+1+3 = 12 plus sessions A2 and B. For total 27, A2+B must contribute 15 rows. Plausible if B has many rows (soft1, siofirst, slot0 deliver, mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks, etc.). Hmm, wait \u2014 M_NOASSIGN red rows: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver (5 red), 18/23. Denominator 23 \u2014 smaller than 27. Why? Because if the bound is never assigned (d->ncpus stays 0 from memset), the walk does NOTHING \u2014 no crash, no delivery anywhere. Then which sessions die? Hmm, actually with d->ncpus = 0, no delivery at all \u2192 slot0 deliver STOP, siofirst STOP?, soft1 STOP?... But the table says red rows are slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver \u2014 five rows. Denominator 23 means 4 rows didn't run. Which session died mid-way? With bound 0, the walk never touches cpu_irq at all \u2014 nothing crashes. Hmm, so why would the denominator shrink to 23? Unless... hmm.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Denominators per mutant:\n- M_MASK30, M_SWONLY, M_IRQACTIVE0, M_MIN2: 26/27 \u2192 one row missing from 27? No wait \u2014 26/27 with ONE red row (sio5 deliver STOP) means 26 OK out of 27 total, i.e., 1 red, full 27 ran. \u2713 (27 - 1 red = 26 OK).\n- M_AND1: 25/27, two red \u2192 27 ran. \u2713.\n- M_REVERT, M_OFFBY1, M_LATCH: 20/22 \u2192 22 ran, 2 red (soft1, siofirst HALT). So 5 rows didn't run (27 \u2192 22): the host died mid-session, killing subsequent rows of that session. Which session? soft1 and siofirst are presumably early rows of session B. If session B dies at soft1... wait but both soft1 AND siofirst are red, so the host died at/after siofirst. Rows after siofirst in session B (and maybe session B is last?) \u2014 hmm. Actually if session B comes after session A2 (in main: A2, B, B2, C, C2, D, E), a death in B would kill B's remaining rows AND subsequent sessions? No \u2014 wait, does the probe start a NEW emulator process per session (`boot(n)`)? Yes, each session boots its own emulator. So a HALT in session B kills session B's remaining rows only; sessions B2, C, C2, D, E boot fresh hosts. Hmm, but then M_REVERT at 20/22 means only 22 rows total ran \u2014 so 5 rows lost, all within one session? If session B has, say, 12 rows and dies at row 2 (soft1 is row 1? siofirst row 2?), then 10 rows of B are lost \u2192 total 27 - 10 = 17, not 22. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me recount. Maybe session order and rows: Let me think about the \"20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM\" for HEAD. HEAD (unfixed) crashes on absent-slot walks: session B (1 cpu) with mask1/soft1 \u2192 soft1 HALT. siofirst HALT \u2014 \"siofirst\" might be \"SIO first\" \u2014 a row where the SIO line is raised BEFORE the soft int, so the first walk that touches the absent slot is via the device caller \u2192 HALT. So in session B, rows soft1 and siofirst both HALT (two separate crashes \u2014 each row restarts? or the session continues with a new boot per row?). If each row has its own boot, a HALT kills just that row (HALT result) and subsequent rows still run. Then why does the denominator shrink to 22 for M_REVERT/HEAD?\n\nThe note: \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". So a session = one host boot, multiple rows within it. If the host dies at row k, rows k+1.. of that session can't run. For HEAD: 20/22 \u2192 22 ran, 5 lost. soft1 and siofirst both HALT \u2014 so the host died twice? That means after a HALT, the probe reboots? Hmm, or soft1 and siofirst are in DIFFERENT sessions. Maybe \"siofirst\" is a row in session B2's predecessor... Actually maybe there are sessions: A2 (1 cpu, several rows incl. soft1?), B (siofirst?), etc.\n\nHonestly, the exact session layout isn't fully inlined \u2014 v2's full row list isn't in the packet; only the diff fragments. The denominators 22, 23, 24, 25 for different mutants are explained by mid-session deaths at different points. Let me sanity check a few:\n\nM_NOASSIGN (bound never assigned \u2192 d->ncpus = 0, walk never delivers): red rows listed: slot0 deliver, sio5 deliver, both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver. 5 red, denominator 23 \u2192 18 OK. 27 - 23 = 4 rows lost. Which? With bound 0, no crash ever (walk does nothing). But wait \u2014 would ANY row HALT? No. So why do 4 rows not run? Hmm. Unless some session's row sequencing depends on delivery: e.g., session C parks cpu 1 at `br .` and expects... if delivery never happens, the probe `cont_until_breakpoint` times out (STOP), session continues. Hmm, so why 23 not 27?\n\nHold on \u2014 maybe with the bound never assigned, d->ncpus is 0 \u2014 but the `boot()` itself: DEVINIT... the fix's capture line is `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 M_NOASSIGN removes that line, so d->ncpus = 0 (memset). Walk: `for (cpu = 0; cpu < 0; cpu++)` \u2014 zero iterations. Nothing delivers. All delivery rows STOP. But also mask readback rows should still be OK (stores still work \u2014 the walk isn't involved in stores... wait, is it? The store arms CALL reassert_interrupts (four callers). With the walk doing nothing, stores still write storage; readbacks OK). So red rows = every delivery-expecting row: slot0 deliver (session A2 or B), sio5 deliver (B2), both deliver (C2), cpu1 deliver (C), cpu3 deliver (E), maybe \"mask1 land\" (session C row \u2014 does it expect vector entry? \"mask1 land\" might be a readback row \u2192 OK). 5 red listed. And 4 rows lost (27\u219223). Why 4 lost? Possibly session C's rows after cpu1 deliver: session C has 7 rows (two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, survive, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked). Hmm, if cpu1 deliver STOPs (timeout, 15 s each), the probe continues... unless the session code `return`s early on STOP. Actually look at session C2's code: on STOP it just rows and returns. In session_C2, if len(entered) < 2 after attempts, row \"both deliver\" STOP and s.close(). No other rows in C2. Fine.\n\nFor M_NOASSIGN, maybe session E: \"four cpus\" row OK (machine listing), then MASK3 store OK, SOFT3 store OK, park, continue \u2192 timeout \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle: pcs \u2014 cpu 3 is parked at PARK, not VBR+8; cpus 0..2 also not at VBR+8 \u2192 idle = True \u2192 \"others idle\" OK? Wait, that row says OK if no other cpu entered the vector. With no delivery, no cpu entered \u2192 OK. Hmm, but that row would pass vacuously \u2014 it's a control that only means something when cpu3 deliver is OK. Fine, that's a design quirk: \"others idle\" can pass vacuously on a total no-deliver mutant. Not a defect per se since cpu3 deliver is red anyway.\n\nSo why denominator 23? 4 rows lost. Maybe: in session C, the code parks cpu1, cpu0 steps stores, then `cont_until_breakpoint` \u2014 if no delivery, the FIRST breakpoint hit never comes; maybe session C does TWO continues (v2 description: \"breakpoint VBR+8; `continue'\"). Each timeout is 15 s. Timeouts don't kill rows though.\n\nAlternatively \u2014 maybe with bound 0, some row expects a HALT and instead gets... no.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I'm misreading which rows exist. Maybe some rows are in a session that comes AFTER a session that hangs/dies. The \"remaining rows of that session cannot run\" \u2014 for M_NOASSIGN, maybe the host doesn't die but the probe's cont_until_breakpoint returns with s.dead False, and then subsequent rows still run. 4 lost rows is unexplained by the mutant's behavior... unless the probe has rows that DEPEND on a previous row's OK (e.g., session C: cpu1 shadow expects... no).\n\nAlternatively the denominator differences might be because some mutants crash the host mid-session: M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1/M_LATCH die at soft1 (HALT) \u2014 wait but both soft1 AND siofirst are red. If the host died at soft1, siofirst couldn't run \u2192 but siofirst shows HALT, so it DID run. So soft1 and siofirst must each have their own boot (or the session reboots per row). If each row reboots, denominators wouldn't shrink... but they do (22 vs 27). Unless: the 22 vs 27 loss is from session E or C2 etc. not running because... hmm, no \u2014 wait. For HEAD/M_REVERT, the table says red rows: soft1, siofirst HALT. Denominator 22. 27 - 22 = 5 rows lost. Maybe rows lost are in the SAME session after the second HALT? If soft1 and siofirst are rows 1 and 2 of a 7-row session, losing 5 rows after \u2192 22. That fits! Session with 7 rows where soft1 is row 1, siofirst row 2, and 5 rows after die because the host exited at siofirst... but then how did siofirst itself run if the host died at soft1? Unless each row boots fresh and the \"remaining rows\" note refers to rows after a NOSTART...\n\nHmm wait, actually re-read the note: \"The RESULT denominators shrink when the host exits mid-session (the remaining rows of that session cannot run)\". For M_REVERT: soft1 HALT and siofirst HALT. If soft1 is in session A2 (or B) and siofirst is in session B (or B2-precursor), and the death at soft1 killed the remaining rows of ITS session, and the death at siofirst killed the remaining rows of ITS session...\n\nLet me try: session A2 rows: ... soft1 ... ; session B rows: siofirst, ... Let me not over-infer. The packet doesn't inline the full row list of v2 sessions; I can't fully verify denominator arithmetic. But I CAN check the arithmetic that IS inlined:\n\n- 27 total base. M_MASK30: 1 red, 26/27. \u2713.\n- M_AND1: 2 red, 25/27. \u2713.\n- M_NOASSIGN: 5 red listed, 18/23 \u2192 23 - 18 = 5 red. \u2713 consistent (5 red, 23 ran).\n- M_HARD1: 3 red listed, 21/24 \u2192 24 - 21 = 3. \u2713.\n- M_SKIP1: 2 red, 23/25 \u2192 25-23=2. \u2713.\n- M_NULLA: 1 red (soft1), 21/22 \u2192 22-21=1. \u2713.\n- M_LATCH: 2 red, 20/22 \u2192 22-20=2. \u2713.\n- M_GUARDASSERT: 1 red, 21/22. \u2713.\n- M_CALLGUARD: 2 red (sio5, siofirst), 21/23 \u2192 23-21=2. \u2713.\n- M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1: 2 red, 20/22 \u2192 \u2713.\n- HEAD: 20/22, soft1+siofirst red. \u2713.\n\nAll internally consistent. Good.\n\nNow the KEY question: does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration?\n\nThe (d) enumeration: dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (constant's source, bound both ways \u2014 i.e., < vs <=, and the bound variable, slot set 0..3); body-read fields: interrupt_status (per device line), interrupt_enable[cpu], software_interrupt_status[cpu], irqActive[cpu], cpu_irq[cpu]; joint cells {slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device, none} \u00d7 {irqActive}.\n\nA \"clean sweep\" mutant = a mutant that passes all 27 rows (escapes the probe) while being non-equivalent (a real defect). The contract discloses: M_NULLAB (both-sided NULL guard) is equivalent on every reachable configuration \u2014 so it's NOT a defect (warrant-based choice). M_NOREASSERT (SOFT arm reassert removed) passes 27/27 \u2014 disclosed debt: the clock's reassert carries delivery. Is that in the (d) enumeration? The SOFT arm's call is a caller, not the walk body... the enumeration is about the walk's dominating predicate and body reads. M_NOREASSERT is about a caller. Hmm \u2014 the question says \"in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)\" \u2014 the enumeration given: dominating predicate, body-read fields, joint cells.\n\nLet me hunt for a clean-sweep mutant in that enumeration.\n\nWalk body per slot:\n```\nstatus = d->interrupt_status;  // machine-wide\nenable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu];\nif (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26);\nif (status & enable) {\n    if (!d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);\n    d->irqActive[cpu] = 1;\n} else {\n    if (d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);\n    d->irqActive[cpu] = 0;\n}\n```\n\nCandidate mutants:\n\n1. `enable = d->interrupt_enable[0]` (collapse per-cpu enable onto slot 0). Rows: session B2 uses MASK0 (slot 0's enable) with SIO line 5 \u2192 status bit 30 & enable bit 30 \u2192 delivers to cpu 0. Collapsing enable[cpu] \u2192 enable[0]: on 1-cpu machine identical. Session C (-n 2): MASK1 \u2190 bit 26, SOFT_INT1 \u2192 slot 1: enable would read interrupt_enable[0] instead of [1]. MASK0 was NOT written in session C? v2 session C description: \"cpu 0 steps MASK1 <- 0x04000000 and SOFT_INT1 <- 1\". MASK0 (slot 0's enable) \u2014 what's its reset value? If interrupt_enable[] starts 0 (memset) and session C only writes MASK1, then enable[0] = 0 \u2192 status & 0 = 0 \u2192 NO delivery \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP \u2192 caught (red). Unless something else writes MASK0. Hmm, wait \u2014 but does the walk see slot 0? Yes. With enable[cpu] \u2192 enable[0]: slot 0: status (bit 26 from SOFT0? no \u2014 SOFT_INT0 not written in session C) \u2014 software_interrupt_status[0] = 0, status = interrupt_status (machine-wide device lines; none pending in session C? the clock might raise... hmm). Slot 0: enable[0] = 0 \u2192 no delivery to cpu 0 (correct anyway \u2014 cpu 0 parked). Slot 1: enable collapses to enable[0] = 0 \u2192 no delivery to cpu 1 \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP. RED. Caught. Good \u2014 so `interrupt_enable[cpu]` \u2192 `[0]` is caught by session C (cpu1 deliver), PROVIDED MASK0 isn't set in session C and reset value is 0. Hmm \u2014 wait, actually is MASK0's reset value 0? The struct arrays: interrupt_enable[MAX_CPUS] \u2014 presumably memset 0 at DEVINIT. If interrupt_enable[0] resets to 0, then yes caught. But hold on \u2014 what if the mutant `enable = d->interrupt_enable[cpu & 1]`? Session E (4 cpus): MASK3 written, SOFT3 written. cpu3's enable would read enable[1] = 0 \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 caught. And cpu 1's slot would read enable[1]... wait with cpu&1: slot 1 \u2192 enable[1] = 0 (MASK1 not written in E) \u2192 fine. cpu 3 \u2192 enable[1] = 0 \u2192 no delivery \u2192 STOP. Caught. OK.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there a mutant on interrupt_enable that sweeps? What about reading `interrupt_enable[cpu]` but the probe never tests a slot whose enable is DISABLED... The probe tests: mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks (declined store rows?) \u2014 \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm.\n\n2. `software_interrupt_status[cpu]` \u2192 `software_interrupt_status[0]`: collapse. Session C: SOFT_INT1 written; slot 1's software_interrupt_status[1] = 1, but mutant reads [0] = 0 \u2192 status for slot 1 = interrupt_status only (bit 26 not set) & enable[1] (bit 26 set via MASK1) \u2192 interrupt_status machine-wide: is bit 26 set by anything? Device line 1 pending? In session C, no device lines pending (parked cpus, no SIO armed). The clock? \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\" \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert with some line. If the clock raises a device line whose bit is in interrupt_status and MASK1 only enables bit 26 \u2192 status & enable = 0 \u2192 no delivery \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP. Caught. But hmm \u2014 wait: does SOFT_INT store itself set software_interrupt_status[1]? Yes (premise: SOFT_INTn store arm is a caller of the walk). So the mutant reads software_interrupt_status[0] = 0 for slot 1 \u2192 red. Caught by C. What about session C2 (both): SOFT0 and SOFT1 both written \u2192 [0] = 1 \u2192 both slots see bit 26 \u2192 both deliver \u2192 OK. And session E: SOFT3 written, reads [0] = 0 \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 caught anyway. OK.\n\n3. `irqActive[cpu]` \u2192 `irqActive[0]`: that's M_IRQACTIVE0 \u2014 caught by C2 (both deliver STOP). 26/27. \u2713 per table.\n\n4. `cpu_irq[cpu]` \u2192 `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]`: M_AND1 \u2014 caught by E (cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS). \u2713. What about `cpu_irq[0]` (collapse)? Session C: cpu1 deliver \u2014 slot 1 asserts cpu_irq[0] \u2192 cpu 0 enters vector, cpu 1 doesn't \u2192 cpu1 deliver STOP (red), and cpu0 parked row \u2192 BADREGS? cpu 0 was parked at `br .` \u2014 it would take the interrupt and enter VBR+8 \u2192 \"cpu0 parked\" row would fail too (it's a row: \"cpu0 parked\"). So caught. \u2713.\n\n5. Dominating predicate: `cpu < d->ncpus` \u2192 `cpu <= d->ncpus` (M_OFFBY1 \u2014 caught), `cpu < MAX_CPUS` (M_REVERT \u2014 caught), `cpu < 4` hardcoded \u2014 hmm! `cpu < 4` hardcoded: on 1-cpu machine (session B, A2, B2), walk goes 0..3 \u2192 slots 1..3 unconnected \u2192 NULL assert \u2192 HALT at soft1 (same as M_REVERT). Caught. `cpu < 2` (M_MIN2 \u2014 caught by E). `cpu < 1` (M_HARD1 \u2014 caught). Bound never assigned (M_NOASSIGN \u2014 caught). So predicate mutants are covered by the table.\n\n6. `interrupt_status` per device line: M_MASK30 masks bit 30 \u2014 caught by B2. What about masking OTHER bits, e.g., bit 26 (`status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)`)? Hmm \u2014 bit 26 is the SOFT bit. If a mutant zeroed bit 26 of interrupt_status: session C (SOFT1, MASK1 bit 26): status = interrupt_status (bit 26 clear, no device line 1) & ~(1<<26) \u2192 still 0... wait but then `if (software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26)` re-adds bit 26 AFTER. Order: status = interrupt_status (mutated: & ~(1<<26)); then SOFT adds bit 26 back. So masking bit 26 in the device read is INVISIBLE to sw-pending slots (bit re-added), and affects only device-line-1 delivery. Is there a row where device line 1 delivers? B2 uses line 5 (bit 30). siofirst/sio5 rows from session B (v2 era) \u2014 \"sio5\" red in M_CALLGUARD \u2014 so session B has a sio5 row (1-cpu, device line 5). Hmm, what device lines do the v2 rows exercise? soft1 = software interrupt slot 1; siofirst = SIO first?; sio5 = SIO line 5 (session B?). If no row delivers device line 1 (bit 26 via the device path), then a mutant `status &= ~(1 << 26)` on the device read would sweep... but wait \u2014 would it be non-equivalent? Device line 1 raised by a real device (which device uses line 1?) with slot's MASK enabling bit 26 \u2192 delivery lost. That IS a real behavior change not covered IF no row covers device-line-1 delivery. Hmm. But the enumeration says \"interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 the question explicitly asks per device line! So: does probe v3 have a row for EACH device line (1..6)? B2 covers line 5. sio5/siofirst (session B rows) \u2014 which lines? Not specified in the packet. The packet doesn't inline session B's device lines. So I CANNOT settle from the packet whether device line 1 (bit 26) delivery is covered. Hmm \u2014 but wait: bit 26 is shared with SOFT. A mutant masking bit 26 in the device status read: for sw-pending slots, the SOFT arm re-adds bit 26 \u2192 delivery works. For device line 1: lost. Which rows raise device line 1? Unknown from packet. If none, this mutant sweeps (all 27 OK). Is it equivalent? No \u2014 device line 1 is a real line (lines 1..6 exist). So THIS could be the clean-sweep mutant... but the packet doesn't give me session B's line coverage, so I can't settle it. Hmm, but hold on \u2014 actually, let me reconsider: is the mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)` even sensible in the enumeration? \"body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 yes, the enumeration asks: for each device line, is the body's read of interrupt_status covered? The probe covers line 5 (B2: bit 30) \u2014 and per the docstring \"MASK0 <- 0x40000000 (bit 30 = SIO line 5)\". M_MASK30 kills via sio5 deliver. But what about lines 1,2,3,4,6 (bits 26,27,28,29,31)? A mutant masking ANY of those bits out of the status read would kill delivery of that line. If no row exercises that line's delivery, the mutant sweeps. From the packet, only line 5 (bit 30) is explicitly covered by a device-line delivery row, plus whatever siofirst/sio5 do in session B (v2 rows \u2014 possibly line 5 as well, since they're named \"sio5\"). \"siofirst\" \u2014 SIO first? Maybe \"SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the SIO assert/deassert callers. Hmm.\n\nActually wait \u2014 the contract says: \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is CAUGHT \u2014 via session B2's sio5 deliver row (bit 30 only). The contract's claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is demonstrated ONLY for bit 30 (M_MASK30). For bits 26,27,28,29,31 \u2014 a masking mutant on those bits is caught only if a row delivers that line. The probe raises SIO line 5 (WR1 TX_IE \u2192 sio_tx_pending \u2192 line 5). The clock \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert with some line (the clock/timer route is disclosed as NOT steppable/host-timer-driven). So device lines other than 5 might never be raised in any row. A mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & 0xc0000000` (keep only bits 30,31)? No wait, that would break line 5? No \u2014 bit 30 kept. Lines 1..4 (bits 26..29) dropped. All rows: sw-pending rows re-add bit 26 via the SOFT arm \u2192 still deliver. Device rows: only line 5 (bit 30) \u2192 still deliver. So ALL 27 rows pass \u2192 CLEAN SWEEP. And it's non-equivalent: a real guest with, say, line 2 pending and MASK enabling bit 27 loses delivery.\n\nHmm wait, but is that in the (d) enumeration? \"the body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 yes, explicitly. So the enumeration includes per-device-line coverage of interrupt_status. And the answer: the probe covers line 5 only (plus the clock's line, whatever it is \u2014 the clock route is disclosed as not caught/not steppable, and the clock's line is raised in the background \u2014 hmm, actually wait: the clock DOES call luna88k_interrupt_assert periodically? \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\" \u2014 in M_NOREASSERT, the SOFT arm's reassert call is removed, but delivery still happens because the clock's assert \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 calls the walk (4th caller? \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the third/fourth callers are luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert). So the clock periodically calls the walk via interrupt_assert. But the clock ASSERTS some line \u2014 if the clock's line bit is in interrupt_status machine-wide and no MASK enables it, status & enable = 0 \u2192 no delivery. The clock's assert call happens but delivers nothing unless a mask enables its bit.\n\nSo: which device lines have delivery rows? From the packet: line 5 (bit 30) \u2014 B2's sio5 deliver, and session B's sio5/siofirst rows (v2-era, likely also line 5 given the names). NO evidence of rows for lines 1,2,3,4,6. So a mutant that masks bits 26,27,28,29,31 (i.e., keeps only bit 30 from interrupt_status) sweeps all 27 rows. Actually even simpler: `status = (d->interrupt_status & (1 << 30)); if (d->software_interrupt_status[cpu]) status |= (1 << 26);` \u2014 a \"keep only line 5\" read. All rows pass. Non-equivalent (lines 1-4,6 lost).\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but hold on. Is this really in the shipped composite's DEPENDS-ON enumeration? The question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)?\" The enumeration includes \"the body-read fields interrupt_status (per device line)\". So yes \u2014 per device line is exactly this cell. The fix's correctness depends on interrupt_status being read intact for ALL six lines; the probe only witnesses line 5 (bit 30) and the sw bit (26, re-added). So lines 1 (device path), 2, 3, 4, 6 are unwitnessed. A mutant killing any of those sweeps.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is that a defect in the FIX or in the PROBE? The clean-sweep question is about the composite: a mutant that the probe can't catch. The probe's contract claims \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught \u2014 but it's only caught for line 5. For the OTHER five lines, masking them out of the walk is NOT caught. So the CONTRACT's claim is overbroad: it says \"a device line masked out of the walk\" as if any line; only line 5 is witnessed. Hmm, but is that a \"floor breach\"? The repair-chain rule: \"the chain closes on the first VALID pass that finds zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\". A clean-sweep mutant in the (d) enumeration = presumably a floor breach (the probe fails to kill a non-equivalent mutant in the depended-on surface). If I find one, the answer is ONE MORE REPAIR (add a row) \u2014 or is it? The question says: \"If you find one, give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so)\".\n\nSo my primary answer: the per-device-line cell for lines other than 5 is unwitnessed. Candidate exact edit: `status = d->interrupt_status & (1u << 30);` (keep only the SIO line-5 bit from the machine-wide status; the SOFT arm still ORs bit 26). Predicted: 27/27 PASS (sweep). The row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu row arming MASK0 with another line's bit and raising that device line \u2014 but can the probe even raise lines 1-4/6? The probe drives the SIO (line 5) via WR1 TX_IE. Raising other lines requires other devices (the clock's line \u2014 host-timer-driven, not steppable, disclosed). Hmm \u2014 so maybe the probe CANNOT raise lines 1..4,6 deterministically. In that case the honest disposition is: the cell is unwitnessable by this probe (like the clock route \u2014 disclosed debt), and the contract should DISCLOSE it rather than claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught. Either way: the contract's \"Catches: ... a device line masked out of the walk\" is true only for line 5; the enumeration's per-device-line cells for lines 1,2,3,4,6 are unswept and (from the packet) untestable by this probe \u2192 the contract overclaims. That's a real defect in the packet AS STATED: the CONTRACT claims a kill the table doesn't support for 5 of 6 lines.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but let me double-check: is bit 26 (line 1) covered via the DEVICE path anywhere? The sw rows re-add bit 26 AFTER the status read, so a mutant masking bit 26 in the device read is invisible to sw rows. Unless a row has a device line 1 pending AND MASK enabling bit 26 \u2014 then the device bit 26 would deliver... but the probe raises only SIO line 5 (and the clock's line in the background). So no. And actually, subtle: in session C (sw row), status read = interrupt_status (masked bit 26 \u2192 0) then SOFT re-adds bit 26 \u2192 delivery. So sw rows can't witness the device-bit-26 read. Right.\n\nHmm, but also: what about `interrupt_status` being machine-wide vs per-slot? It IS machine-wide (premise 1). A mutant making it per-slot doesn't exist (no per-slot array). OK.\n\nNow \u2014 before I commit to that as THE clean-sweep, let me check the OTHER enumerated cells more carefully, because the question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" about the nine dispositions \u2014 implying the intended answer to Q2 is one specific disposition. And Q1's answer per the packet's framing (\"SHORT BY DESIGN\", \"closes if zero floor breaches\") might be \"none \u2014 close\". But my audit should be honest. Let me look harder for internal contradictions.\n\nThe nine dispositions:\n1. The capture move (below the ncpus>4 exit) \u2014 minimax's finding. Is it right? Yes: capture above the exit makes d->ncpus potentially >4 (up to 256) while only 0..3 connected \u2192 walk would hit NULLs. Wait, no: if capture were ABOVE the exit... the exit still runs (ncpus>4 \u2192 exit(1)), so the process dies before any guest code. Hmm \u2014 minimax's finding: \"with the capture ABOVE the DEVINIT ncpus > 4 exit, that exit had become load-bearing for the walk's bound.\" I.e., the fix's safety then DEPENDS on the exit(1) \u2014 if the exit were removed/broken, d->ncpus could be 256 \u2192 walk crashes. By moving the capture below the exit, d->ncpus \u2264 4 by construction of the statement order (the assignment happens only on paths where ncpus \u2264 4). Hmm \u2014 actually even below the exit, the bound's safety depends on DEVINIT connecting exactly 0..ncpus-1 \u2014 the connect predicate. It's \"the same number on purpose\". Fine \u2014 the capture move is sound. But is there a wrinkle: `emul.c` maps ncpus 0 \u2192 1, so d->ncpus \u2265 1 \u2014 walk runs at least slot 0. If ncpus could be 0 (machine->ncpus = 0), d->ncpus = 0 \u2192 walk does nothing \u2192 slot 0 (connected!) never gets interrupts. But emul.c maps 0 \u2192 1, so unreachable. OK, READ.\n\n2-4. Three new sessions: B2 (sio5 deliver \u2014 deepseek/grok), C2 (both deliver \u2014 kimi), E (four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle \u2014 glm/grok). Are these sound? B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO WR1 TX_IE raises line 5 \u2192 device line delivers to CONNECTED slot 0 \u2192 cpu 0 enters VBR+8. Sound, addresses M_MASK30/M_SWONLY. C2: both slots pending \u2192 per-slot latch witnessed. Sound (M_IRQACTIVE0 killed). E: 4 cpus, slot 3 delivery + aliasing check (M_AND1, M_MIN2). Sound. Each new session also passes on HEAD (accept-side control) \u2014 stated: \"every new session -- B2, C2, E -- passes on HEAD\". Good \u2014 the new rows don't false-positive on unfixed HEAD? Wait, that's odd: HEAD is UNFIXED (walk goes to MAX_CPUS, crashes on absent slots). Session E on HEAD: -n 4, MASK3 + SOFT3 \u2192 slot 3 IS connected on 4 cpus \u2192 walk 0..3 all connected \u2192 no crash \u2192 cpu3 delivers. \u2713 passes. Session B2 on HEAD: 1 cpu, MASK0 + SIO line 5 \u2192 slot 0 connected; walk continues to slots 1..3: their enable = MASK1..3 = 0 (never written), sw = 0 \u2192 status & 0 = 0 \u2192 deassert branch: irqActive[0]? = 0 \u2192 no call \u2192 no crash. \u2713 passes (the deassert branch only calls if latch set). Session C2 on HEAD: -n 2, both slots pending \u2192 walk 0..3; slots 2,3: enable 0 \u2192 else-branch, latch 0 \u2192 no call. \u2713 passes. Consistent with \"every new session passes on HEAD\". Good \u2014 internally consistent.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 is that right for B2 on HEAD? status for slot 1: status = interrupt_status machine-wide = bit 30 set (line 5 pending!) \u2014 machine-wide, so slots 1..3 see bit 30 too; enable[1] = MASK1 = 0 \u2192 0 & x = 0 \u2192 else branch \u2192 latch 0 \u2192 no call. \u2713. Right.\n\n5-6. Two disclosed equivalents/debts: M_NULLAB (both-sided NULL guard, EQUIVALENT, warrant-based choice) and M_NOREASSERT (SOFT arm reassert removed, disclosed debt: clock carries delivery). Is the M_NULLAB equivalence claim sound? \"EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/\". Both-sided NULL guard: `if (d->cpu_irq[cpu].interrupt_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(...) else nothing` \u2014 hmm, wait: a both-sided NULL guard means the walk still walks ALL MAX_CPUS slots but skips NULL ones. On reachable configs (ncpus 1..4), unconnected slots have NULL \u2192 skipped; connected slots behave normally. Is that equivalent to the bound? On reachable configs: yes \u2014 slots \u2265 ncpus have NULL \u2192 skipped; slots < ncpus behave identically. One difference: the walk still READS the arrays for unconnected slots (no side effects though \u2014 the guard skips both assert and deassert; the latch write `d->irqActive[cpu] = 0/1` still happens! Wait \u2014 M_NULLAB: \"a both-sided NULL guard instead of the bound\". Where's the guard placed? If the guard wraps only the INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT calls, the latch writes still happen for unconnected slots \u2014 but those slots' latches are dead storage, invisible. So behaviorally equivalent on reachable configs. \u2713 sound. Edge: MAX_CPUS > 4 and ncpus \u2264 4: unconnected slots 4..MAX-1 have NULL \u2192 skipped. Equivalent. And a config with ncpus > 4 is unreachable (DEVINIT exits). So EQUIVALENT claim is sound, READ-measured 22/22 (and 27/27 in table 2). OK.\n\nM_NOREASSERT: \"a deleted reassert in the SOFT arm alone (the clock's own reassert carries the delivery; disclosed debt)\". Is this sound? The SOFT_INT store arm calls reassert_interrupts after the store. If removed: the store sets software_interrupt_status[cpu] but no immediate walk; the clock periodically calls luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 walk \u2192 delivery eventually. So functionally, delivery still happens (delayed). The probe rows pass (they wait 15 s). Is this a real defect? Latency change \u2014 the sw interrupt wouldn't be delivered until the next clock tick. On real hardware, an IPI should be immediate-ish. The disclosed debt: the probe can't catch it because the clock carries delivery anyway. Hmm \u2014 but WAIT: is the clock guaranteed to call luna88k_interrupt_assert in these sessions? The clock's assert \u2014 \"also the clock\" (premise 1: luna88k_interrupt_assert and deassert are the device lines; also the clock). The clock asserts periodically (timer). In session B (1 cpu, soft1 \u2014 wait, soft1 is slot 1's SOFT on a 1-cpu machine \u2192 NULL assert crash \u2014 that's the HEAD symptom). Hmm, for M_NOREASSERT the walk is BOUNDED (fix v2), so slot 1 isn't walked; the SOFT arm's reassert removed \u2192 delivery of slot 0's soft int relies on the clock \u2192 passes. OK.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 is M_NOREASSERT within the (d) enumeration? It's a CALLER edit (the SOFT arm), not the walk body. The enumeration for Q1: dominating predicate + body reads + joint cells. M_NOREASSERT is outside that enumeration (it was disclosed). Fine.\n\n7. lunadefaultstop LOW \u2014 \"the switch's default: graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms \u2014 pass-1 F13 said 'file separately' and nobody did\". Filed as a row this round. Sound? It's a filing \u2014 LOW severity. Plausible.\n\n8. lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW documented-only \u2014 \"the absent slots' registers stay storage with readback -- the design hold from pass 1\". Design decision: unconnected slots' MASK/SOFT registers remain guest-writable storage. Filed/documented. Plausible board-model hold.\n\n9. es438: \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round\". Hmm \u2014 es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair of ROWS (from an earlier round?). The probe v3 has mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows (\"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\"). So es438's owed rows are discharged by the detector's existing rows \u2192 annotate/close. Plausible.\n\nNow, WHICH IS WRONG? The question demands one of the nine. Let me hunt for an internal contradiction in one.\n\nHmm \u2014 think about the M_CALLGUARD note: \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 correction of pass-2's grok finding. In table 2, M_CALLGUARD: \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2192 21/23, red rows sio5, siofirst HALT. Wait \u2014 M_CALLGUARD bounds the reassert CALLS (in the store arms?) instead of the walk. Red rows: sio5, siofirst HALT. Hmm \u2014 if the CALLS are guarded/bounded but the walk is NOT bounded (walk still MAX_CPUS), then... the calls happen only for cpu < n? Hmm, \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2014 presumably: don't bound the walk; instead bound where reassert_interrupts is CALLED?? That doesn't prevent the walk from touching unconnected slots when called... Confusing, but the table shows it's NOT a fix (device-line callers: the SIO's assert/deassert calls the walk with no bound \u2192 crash). Red rows sio5, siofirst HALT \u2014 the SIO route crashes. OK consistent.\n\nNow the \"records this round carries\" \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding said \"three of the main loop's 'unchanged' dispositions named rows that are not ledger rows (lunadefaultstop, es438cells, lunaabsentmaskdecl -- `es438` is the row)\". So the ledger row names: lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438. And the records: lunadefaultstop LOW, lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW, es438 annotate/close. OK.\n\nNow \u2014 THE CAPTURE MOVE again. Let me scrutinize: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` placed below the exit. The exit: `if (ncpus > 4) { ...; exit(1); }` \u2014 hmm, wait, actually what variable does DEVINIT's connect predicate read? \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under `ncpus >= 2/3/4`\". The connects happen BEFORE the exit? The diff hunk at 1038/1043: the capture is inserted right after the exit(1) block, and BEFORE add_cmmu_for_cpu calls. Where are the INTERRUPT_CONNECTs relative to the capture? If the connects are ABOVE (earlier in DEVINIT), then the capture below them \u2014 fine, order doesn't matter for the capture (it just reads machine->ncpus). The connect predicate presumably reads machine->ncpus directly (not d->ncpus). So the bound and predicate share the source (machine->ncpus) \u2014 \"the same number on purpose\". \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 one more check on the fix: is `d->ncpus` assigned BEFORE any reassert_interrupts call can happen? The walk's callers: store arms (guest stores \u2014 after boot), interrupt_assert/deassert (device events \u2014 after boot). During DEVINIT, add_cmmu_for_cpu... no walk calls between struct alloc and the capture? If a device (clock) asserts before the capture... DEVINIT order: connect slot 0, ..., exit check, capture, add_cmmu... If the clock asserts during DEVINIT before the capture, the walk would run with d->ncpus = 0 (memset) \u2192 walk does nothing \u2192 no crash, just a missed assert (would be re-asserted later by the clock). Not a floor breach. OK.\n\nNow, the SECOND question's framing: \"of the nine pass-3 dispositions ..., WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the packet expects me to find one wrong disposition. Let me think harder.\n\nWait \u2014 count the nine: (1) the capture move; (2,3,4) three new sessions (B2, C2, E); (5,6) two disclosed equivalents/debts (M_NULLAB, M_NOREASSERT); (7,8,9) three filings/annotations (lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438). Total 9. \u2713.\n\nWhich is wrong? Let me examine each for a contradiction with the inlined facts.\n\n(5) M_NULLAB disclosed as EQUIVALENT. Hmm \u2014 is it REALLY equivalent on every reachable configuration? Both-sided NULL guard instead of the bound: walk all MAX_CPUS, guard both assert and deassert with NULL checks. On reachable configs (ncpus 1..4): unconnected slots skipped, latch writes still occur on dead storage. Behavior identical. BUT \u2014 wait: what about INTERRUPT_DEASSERT on a CONNECTED slot whose irqActive latch... no, connected slots have non-NULL pointers. Equivalent. \u2713 sound.\n\nHmm, unless MAX_CPUS < 4?? If MAX_CPUS were, say, 4, then... doesn't matter.\n\n(6) M_NOREASSERT disclosed debt. The claim: \"the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\". Wait \u2014 the CLOCK doesn't \"reassert\" \u2014 the clock calls luna88k_interrupt_assert (an assert, not a reassert call per se, but luna88k_interrupt_assert CALLS the walk \u2014 premise 1: four callers including luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert). So deleting the SOFT arm's reassert call: delivery still happens on the next clock assert \u2192 walk \u2192 sees software_interrupt_status[cpu] \u2192 delivers. So on reachable configs delivery is delayed but happens. Is M_NOREASSERT a REAL defect (non-equivalent)? Delivery latency for IPIs becomes clock-tick-bound. The probe discloses it as debt (can't catch \u2014 the clock carries delivery within the 1