ready claimed mask1/2/3 readback rows. And es438 \"is the row\" per grok's finding. So es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair \u2014 discharged by the detector's rows. Hmm \u2014 but grok's pass-2 finding said the three named \"rows\" (lunadefaultstop, es438cells, lunaabsentmaskdecl) \"are not ledger rows\" \u2014 es438 IS the row. So es438 was a ledger row with an owed mask2/mask3 pair. If the detector has mask1/2/3 readback rows, the pair is discharged. Plausible \u2014 can't fully verify (the rows aren't inlined), but the contract's claim covers it. Hmm.\n\n(2-4) the three new sessions. Let me scrutinize session E's \"others idle\" row code:\n\n```python\nidle = all(pcs[c] != VBR + 8 for c in (0, 1, 2))\nrow(\"others idle\", \"OK\" if idle else \"BADREGS\", ...)\n```\n\npcs[c] = s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c). For M_AND1 (cpu_irq[cpu & 1]): slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8. pcs[1] == VBR+8 \u2192 idle False \u2192 BADREGS. Table says \"others idle BADREGS (cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt)\" \u2713 consistent.\n\nBut wait \u2014 session E: cpus 1..3 are parked at PARK (`br .`) with PSR 0x80000000. cpu 0 does the stores then parks. The breakpoint is hit when cpu 3 enters VBR+8 \u2192 cont_until_breakpoint returns. pcs read. If cpu 1 took the interrupt (M_AND1), the breakpoint hits with cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 row cpu3 deliver: pcs[3] != VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS... but table says cpu3 deliver STOP for M_AND1. Hmm!\n\nM_AND1 red rows: \"cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS\". With M_AND1, slot 3's assert goes to cpu_irq[3 & 1] = cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 enters the vector. The breakpoint at VBR+8 IS hit (by cpu 1) \u2192 `hit` True, s.dead False. pcs[3] = PARK (cpu 3 still parked) \u2192 cpu3 deliver row: value(...) pcs[3] == VBR+8? No \u2192 BADREGS. But the table says cpu3 deliver STOP! STOP is \"no vector entry before the wait ran out\" \u2014 but the breakpoint WAS hit (by cpu 1). Hmm \u2014 wait, look at the code:\n\n```python\nhit = s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)\n...\npcs = [s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c) for c in (0, 1, 2, 3)]\nif not hit:\n    row(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"STOP\", ...)\n    s.close(); return\nrow(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"OK\" if value(...) else \"BADREGS\")\n```\n\nIf hit (cpu 1 entered), cpu3 deliver \u2192 BADREGS (pcs[3] = PARK). Table says STOP. CONTRADICTION? Unless with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT enter... wait: M_AND1: `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT. Slot 3: status & enable (MASK3 bit 26, SOFT3) \u2192 assert cpu_irq[3&1]=cpu_irq[1] \u2192 cpu 1 (parked at `br .` with IND... PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 interrupts enabled?) \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 breakpoint hit. So cpu3 deliver should be BADREGS, not STOP.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but maybe with M_AND1 the LATCH: irqActive[3] set on slot 3; slot 1: enable[1] = MASK1 = 0 (not written in E) \u2192 else branch \u2192 deassert cpu_irq[1] if irqActive[1]... The walk order: cpu 0: enable[0]=MASK0=0 (not written? MASK3 written = MASK0+12; MASK0 itself not written) \u2192 slot 0: status & 0 = 0 \u2192 else, latch 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 1: enable[1] = 0 \u2192 else, latch[1] 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 2: enable 0 \u2192 nothing. Slot 3: enable = MASK3 bit 26, sw \u2192 status bit 26 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1]. So cpu 1 takes it. Breakpoint hit by cpu 1. cpu3 deliver row: hit=True \u2192 value(pcs[3] == VBR+8) \u2192 False \u2192 BADREGS. But table says cpu3 deliver STOP for M_AND1!\n\nUnless... the breakpoint mechanism: `cont_until_breakpoint` \u2014 when cpu 1 hits the breakpoint, the emulator stops ALL cpus? Then pcs[3] = PARK. hit=True. Row = BADREGS. Table says STOP. Contradiction!\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the table: \"M_AND1: `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT | 25/27 FAIL | cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS (cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt)\". Hmm. \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2014 so cpu 1 DID enter the vector. Then how is cpu3 deliver STOP (no vector entry within the wait)?? If cpu 1 entered VBR+8, the breakpoint was hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver would be BADREGS (pcs[3] != VBR+8), and others idle would be... pcs[0..2]: cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 idle False \u2192 BADREGS \u2713 (matches). But cpu3 deliver STOP contradicts \"cpu 1 took the interrupt\".\n\nUnless the STOP comes from a DIFFERENT mechanism: hmm, wait \u2014 maybe with M_AND1, cpu 1 is parked at PARK with a breakpoint... no. Let me re-trace: does cpu 1 actually take the interrupt? cpu 1 parked at `br .` (busy loop), PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 interrupt enable bit? For 88k, PSR bit... IND? The probe uses PSR 0x80000000 and says a cpu at VBR+8 with PSR 0x8000000b / EPSR 0x80000000 is the channel. PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 hmm, is the interrupt-enable bit set in 0x80000000? 88100 PSR: bit 31 is... PSR has IND (interrupt disable) at bit 19? Hmm \u2014 the packet says \"PSR 0x8000000b\" at the vector \u2014 so 0x80000000 | 0xb. Whatever \u2014 the probe's convention: parked with PSR 0x80000000 \u2192 interrupts enabled (else delivery rows would never work). \u2713 \u2014 session C works this way (cpu1 deliver OK on baseline).\n\nSo with M_AND1, cpu 1 should enter \u2192 hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver BADREGS. Table says STOP. INCONSISTENCY \u2014 unless... hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: M_AND1 at ASSERT and DEASSERT: slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1]. But ALSO: the walk for slot 1... no wait. Hmm, actually \u2014 hold on: which slot's pending bit... the SOFT_INT3 store sets software_interrupt_status[3]. The walk: slot 3 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[3&1] = cpu_irq[1] \u2192 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu 1's istruct) \u2192 cpu 1 interrupted. Also slot 1: enable[1] = 0 \u2192 else \u2192 irqActive[1]... the latch is per-slot: irqActive[1] (slot 1's latch) \u2014 0 \u2192 no deassert call. Note: the DEASSERT of cpu_irq[1] would only happen if slot 1's latch were set \u2014 it's per-slot, so slot 1's else-branch doesn't deassert cpu 1's line (which slot 3 just asserted through cpu_irq[1]). Hmm interesting \u2014 so with the alias, slot 1's else branch: `if (d->irqActive[1]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(cpu_irq[1&1]=cpu_irq[1])` \u2014 irqActive[1] is slot 1's latch = 0 \u2192 no call. OK so cpu 1 stays asserted \u2192 enters vector \u2192 breakpoint hit.\n\nSo table's \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" for M_AND1 looks WRONG \u2014 should be BADREGS. Unless the breakpoint only triggers for the FOCUSED cpu or... `s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)` \u2014 the probe's breakpoint at VBR+8; when ANY cpu reaches it, the emulator halts (gxemul singlestep/breakpoints are per-cpu? In gxemul, breakpoints might be per-cpu!). Hmm \u2014 gxemul debugger: `breakpoint add ADDR` \u2014 is it per-machine or per-cpu? In gxemul, I believe breakpoints are per-CPU (each cpu has its own breakpoints) or global? Actually in GXemul, the debugger commands like \"breakpoint\" are per-CPU (you focus a cpu and add breakpoints to IT). Hmm! If the breakpoint is attached to the FOCUSED cpu (cpu 0 \u2014 `s.send(\"focus 0\")` earlier), then cpu 1 entering VBR+8 does NOT hit the breakpoint \u2192 cont_until_breakpoint times out (15 s) \u2192 hit False \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP, pcs[3] = PARK... then others idle: pcs[0..2] \u2014 cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS \u2713. That MATCHES the table!\n\nBut wait \u2014 then on the BASELINE, session E: cpu 3 enters VBR+8 \u2014 would the breakpoint (on cpu 0?) hit? The probe does `s.send(\"focus 0\")` before stores, then `breakpoint add VBR+8`. If breakpoints are per-cpu (cpu 0's), cpu 3's entry wouldn't hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP on baseline \u2014 but the table says baseline is 27/27 PASS. Contradiction with per-cpu breakpoints. So breakpoints must be GLOBAL (any cpu hitting \u2192 emulator stops). Then M_AND1 \u2192 cpu 1 hits \u2192 BADREGS, contradicting STOP.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 unless with M_AND1 something prevents cpu 1 from entering. Let me re-check: is cpu 1's PSR set with interrupts enabled? Session E sets cpus 1..3: VBR, PSR 0x80000000, pc=PARK. Yes. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 maybe with M_AND1, the walk order matters: slot 3's ASSERT happens, but THEN... no, slot 3 is last. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 wait, maybe M_AND1's alias also affects the LATCH writes? No, M_AND1 is only cpu_irq[cpu & 1].\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe the alias `cpu & 1` \u2014 slot 3 \u2192 cpu_irq[1] \u2014 INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu 1) \u2192 cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 hits breakpoint \u2192 emulator stops all \u2192 pcs: cpu1 = VBR+8, cpu3 = PARK \u2192 cpu3 deliver = BADREGS (hit=True, value fails). So table should read \"cpu3 deliver BADREGS, others idle BADREGS\" \u2192 25/27 \u2713 score matches (2 red) but the RESULT WORD for cpu3 deliver is stated as STOP. Score 25/27 is consistent either way (2 red rows). So the defect is only in the red-row WORD (STOP vs BADREGS), not the score. Hmm \u2014 is that a \"defect\"? The table's reading column says \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2014 which implies cpu 1 entered \u2014 which implies the breakpoint was hit \u2014 which implies cpu3 deliver = BADREGS not STOP. So the table is internally inconsistent on the WORD for M_AND1's cpu3 deliver. Minor, but it IS an inconsistency in the packet AS STATED. Hmm \u2014 but is it one of the NINE dispositions? The nine dispositions are: capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings. The M_AND1 row is a killtable row, not one of the nine. Hmm.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe I should reconsider whether \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" could be right. What if, with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT take the interrupt because... the busy-loop `br .` at PARK \u2014 cpu 1 executes `br .` forever. INTERRUPT_ASSERT on cpu 1 \u2192 cpu 1 vectors to VBR+8. That requires cpu 1's PSR interrupt-enable. PSR 0x80000000 \u2014 hmm, for M88K: PSR bit 31 (0x80000000) is... PSR: bits 31..26 = mode bits (kernel mode etc.), IND is bit 19 (0x80000). Hmm! If IND (interrupt disable) is bit 19, then PSR 0x80000000 has IND CLEAR \u2192 interrupts ENABLED. And at the vector, PSR becomes 0x8000000b (0xb = bits 0,1,3 \u2014 serial mode bits etc.). OK so 0x80000000 = interrupts enabled. \u2713 (session C relies on this).\n\nSo cpu 1 WOULD take it. So \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" is wrong; should be BADREGS. BUT \u2014 hmm, wait: does the emulator's breakpoint stop only the cpu that hit it and continue others? `cont_until_breakpoint` returns hit=True when any cpu hits. Either way hit=True.\n\nHmm, hold on, wait. Let me re-read session E's code once more:\n\n```python\nhit = s.cont_until_breakpoint(15.0)\nif s.dead: ...\npcs = [s.reg(\"pc\", cpu=c) for c in (0, 1, 2, 3)]\nif not hit:\n    row(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"STOP\", ...)\n    s.close(); return\nrow(\"cpu3 deliver\", \"OK\" if value(s, \"E cpu3 pc == VBR+8 ...\", pcs[3], VBR + 8) else \"BADREGS\")\nidle = all(pcs[c] != VBR + 8 for c in (0, 1, 2))\nrow(\"others idle\", ...)\n```\n\nWith M_AND1 (cpu 1 enters): hit=True \u2192 cpu3 deliver = BADREGS; others idle = BADREGS. Table: \"cpu3 deliver STOP, others idle BADREGS\". So the WORD for cpu3 deliver in the table is inconsistent with the code + the reading's own parenthetical. Unless gxemul breakpoints are per-cpu AND session E adds the breakpoint while focused on cpu 0 \u2192 only cpu 0's entry hits \u2192 with M_AND1 cpu 1 enters, no hit \u2192 STOP \u2713, and \"others idle\" computed from pcs \u2014 cpu 1 at VBR+8 \u2192 BADREGS \u2713 \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\" \u2713. And on BASELINE: cpu 3 enters but breakpoint is cpu-0-scoped \u2192 no hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2192 baseline NOT 27/27. \u2717. Contradiction again \u2014 UNLESS cont_until_breakpoint has another detection... no.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 unless in gxemul, `breakpoint add` is machine-global (any cpu). Then baseline works and M_AND1 gives BADREGS/BADREGS. The table says STOP/BADREGS. Either way there's an inconsistency SOMEWHERE in session E's design vs the table:\n\n- Global breakpoints: baseline 27/27 \u2713, M_AND1 should be cpu3 deliver BADREGS (table says STOP \u2717).\n- Per-cpu breakpoints (on focused cpu 0): M_AND1 STOP \u2713 matches table, but baseline cpu3 deliver would STOP \u2717 (baseline is 27/27).\n\nWait \u2014 per-cpu: what if the probe focuses each parked cpu and adds the breakpoint to each? The inlined code only shows `s.send(\"breakpoint add ...\")` once after `focus 0`. Hmm \u2014 but sessions C/C2 (which work on baseline) also add the breakpoint after focus 0, and cpu 1's entry IS detected (session C: cpu 1 enters its vector, pc == 0x40008 observed). So breakpoints MUST be global (cpu 1's entry detected while focused on cpu 0). Therefore M_AND1's cpu 1 entry \u2192 hit \u2192 cpu3 deliver BADREGS. Table's STOP is WRONG.\n\nHmm hold on, wait. Session C: \"cpu 0 steps MASK1 ... parks; breakpoint VBR+8; `continue`\" \u2192 \"cpu 1 enters its vector (pc == 0x40008), IND set, cpu 0 still parked\". So yes \u2014 the breakpoint catches cpu 1's entry while focus is cpu 0 \u2192 breakpoints are global. CONFIRMED from packet.\n\nSo with M_AND1 in session E: cpu 1 enters VBR+8 \u2192 global breakpoint hit \u2192 hit=True \u2192 cpu3 deliver row = BADREGS (pcs[3]=PARK \u2260 VBR+8). Table says STOP. INCONSISTENT.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 is it possible that with M_AND1, cpu 1 does NOT enter because of the DEASSERT side? Walk order slot 0..3: slot 3 asserts cpu_irq[1]. No later slot deasserts it (slot 3 is last). Slot 1's else-branch: irqActive[1] \u2014 hmm wait, M_AND1's latch is still per-slot (irqActive[cpu], unmodified). Slot 1: status&enable=0 \u2192 else \u2192 if (irqActive[1]) \u2192 0 \u2192 no deassert. \u2713 cpu 1 stays asserted \u2192 enters. Yes.\n\nSo the M_AND1 table row's \"cpu3 deliver STOP\" is internally inconsistent with (a) session C proving global breakpoints, (b) the reading's own parenthetical \"cpu 1 took slot 3's interrupt\", (c) session E's code path (hit=True \u2192 BADREGS branch). The SCORE 25/27 is unaffected (2 red either way). So this is a reporting defect, not a scoring defect. Hmm \u2014 should this drive the verdict? The chain closes on \"zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\" \u2014 a floor breach is presumably a surviving defect in fix/probe. A mislabeled red-row word in the killtable is a records defect... The second question asks which of the NINE dispositions is wrong \u2014 the M_AND1 row isn't one of the nine.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me re-examine the nine again for the intended \"wrong\" one. Let me re-read the E session's description in the probe docstring: \"session E (`-n 4', FREE-RUNNING) cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set; MASK3 + SOFT_INT3 from cpu 0 \u2192 four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle \u2014 M_AND1 (`cpu_irq[cpu & 1]': slots 2/3 alias onto 0/1) and M_MIN2 (the bound clamped to two): the LAST slot delivers to cpu 3 and to nobody else\".\n\nOK so now \u2014 WAIT. I want to recheck M_MIN2: \"d->ncpus = ncpus > 2 ? 2 : ncpus\" \u2014 on 4 cpus \u2192 2 \u2192 walk slots 0,1 only \u2192 slot 3 never walked \u2192 cpu 3 never delivered \u2192 no vector entry \u2192 breakpoint NOT hit \u2192 hit=False \u2192 cpu3 deliver STOP \u2713 (table: cpu3 deliver STOP \u2713). pcs[3] = PARK \u2192 others idle: cpus 0..2 \u2014 no one entered \u2192 idle OK. So M_MIN2: 1 red (cpu3 deliver), 26/27 \u2713 consistent.\n\nAnd M_AND1: cpu 1 enters \u2192 hit \u2192 BADREGS/BADREGS. Table says STOP/BADREGS. The table's M_AND1 word is wrong. Score consistent.\n\nHmm OK. But which of the NINE is wrong? Let me think about the es438 disposition more. \"es438: its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding: \"three of the main loop's 'unchanged' dispositions named rows that are not ledger rows (`lunadefaultstop`, `es438cells`, `lunaabsentmaskdecl` -- `es438` is the row)\". So the ledger row is `es438` (not es438cells). And es438 owed a mask2/mask3 pair. The detector has mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows. So es438's debt is discharged \u2192 close. Hmm, plausible.\n\nBut WAIT \u2014 do the mask2/mask3 rows actually exist in probe v3? The v2\u2192v3 diff shows only the CONTRACT line \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\" as CONTEXT (unchanged between v2 and v3) \u2014 wait, actually, let me re-read the contract diff:\n\n```\n- bound never assigned (session B), the bound hardcoded to 1 (session C), a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/\n- mask3 readbacks), and the third/fourth MASK labels.  Does NOT catch: ...\n+ bound never assigned (session B), the bound hardcoded to 1 (session C) or clamped to 2 (session E), slots 2/3 aliased onto 0/1\n+ (session E), a device line masked out of the walk or a walk of software-pending slots only (session B2), the per-slot latch\n+ collapsed onto slot 0 (session C2), a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks), and the third/fourth MASK\n+ labels.  Does NOT catch: ...\n```\n\nSo v2 already had mask1/mask2/mask3 readback rows (claimed). v3 keeps them. And the base is 27 rows: v2 had 20 rows; v3 adds B2 (1), C2 (1), E (3) = 5 new \u2192 25?? But base is 27! Hmm! 20 + 5 = 25 \u2260 27. Wait \u2014 \"27 rows\" in the header: \"the CLOSING pass on the pass-2 repair (fix v2 + probe v3, 27 rows)\". And killtable2 BASE 27/27. And the v2-era measurements: \"measured 20/20\" (M_NULLAB in v2, corrected to \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\"). Hmm \u2014 so v2 was 20 rows; killtable (pass-2 table) was 22 rows?? \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 so by the time of killtable (pass 2's table?), the probe had 22 rows? Confusing. Let me recount v3's rows: 27. New sessions in v3: B2 (1 row: sio5 deliver), C2 (1 row: both deliver), E (3 rows: four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle) = 5 new rows. 27 - 5 = 22 \u2192 v2-final had 22 rows (matching \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\"). And the \"20/20\" was an EARLIER v2 state (the docstring's stale prediction \u2014 \"The header's stale predictions (20/20, 20/20 + 8/8) were corrected\"). OK consistent: v2 started at 20 rows, grew to 22 during pass 2 (killtable/), v3 = 27.\n\nNow session C's v2\u2192v3 diff line: \"measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\" \u2014 hmm, 19/20?? If v2-final was 22 rows, why 20? \"M_HARD1 (`d->ncpus = 1' hardcoded -- the clause-(d) null repair: ... measured 19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red)\". Hmm \u2014 this is in the SESSION C description \u2014 maybe the 20 refers to a session-C-era measurement when the probe had 20 rows. M_HARD1 with 22-row probe: red rows would be cpu1 deliver (C), both deliver (C2 didn't exist then)... hmm, in v2 (22 rows): M_HARD1 red: cpu1 deliver + any 2+-cpu row = cpu1 deliver alone? 22-row v2: sessions A2, B, C (7 rows), D \u2014 with -n 2 rows: cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow?, cpu0 parked? M_HARD1 in killtable2 (27 rows): red = both deliver, cpu1 deliver, cpu3 deliver (3 red, 21/24 \u2014 wait denominator 24 for M_HARD1: 27-24=3 rows lost; 24 ran, 3 red \u2192 21 OK \u2713).\n\nHmm \u2014 the \"19/20\" for M_HARD1 in v2: 20 rows ran, cpu1 deliver alone red \u2192 19 OK. So in the 20-row era (early v2? or v1?), M_HARD1 was ALREADY caught by cpu1 deliver (not a TOTAL ESCAPE). And the v2 docstring CLAIMED \"TOTAL ESCAPE ... measured 20/20\" \u2014 which was WRONG, corrected in v3 to \"19/20, cpu1 deliver alone red\". So the v2 docstring had a stale/wrong claim, corrected in v3. Fine \u2014 \"The header's stale predictions (20/20, 20/20 + 8/8) were corrected\". Hmm wait, but that says the HEADER's stale predictions were corrected \u2014 and this 19/20 correction is in the session-C description. OK, part of the same corrections. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 WHICH OF THE NINE IS WRONG? Let me think about the capture move once more. Actually \u2014 WAIT. Let me reconsider the capture-site claim: \"Captured BELOW the `ncpus > 4' exit so the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction\". Hmm \u2014 \"\u2264 MAX_CPUS\" \u2014 the exit is `ncpus > 4`, so below it ncpus \u2264 4. For the bound to be \u2264 MAX_CPUS, we need MAX_CPUS \u2265 4. If MAX_CPUS = 4, fine. The comment says \"the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction\" \u2014 with the exit at 4 and MAX_CPUS = 4, ncpus \u2264 4 = MAX_CPUS \u2713. OK.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 WAIT: is MAX_CPUS really 4? If MAX_CPUS were 4, then the v2-era M_REVERT (walk to MAX_CPUS) on a 4-cpu machine walks 0..3, all connected \u2192 fine. The premises say DEVINIT connects slots 1..3 under ncpus \u2265 2/3/4 and exits if ncpus > 4 \u2014 consistent with MAX_CPUS = 4. Also \"slot set 0..3\" in the enumeration. OK.\n\nNow, let me look for the INTENDED defect. The question says \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 with the count named (nine). Maybe the intended answer: the es438 disposition is wrong because... hmm. Or maybe the intended answer is about the CONTRACT's equivalence claim. Or maybe none is wrong and the honest answer is \"none \u2014 but here's what I can't settle\". The panel instructions reward honest \"cannot settle\" over confident guessing. But the question is phrased as if one IS wrong (\"WHICH IS WRONG?\"). It could be a test: the correct answer might be \"none of the nine is wrong as stated\" \u2014 or the packet contains exactly one internal contradiction among the nine.\n\nLet me re-scan the nine with fresh eyes:\n\n1. **The capture move** (capture below the ncpus>4 exit). Internally consistent. Sound. \u2713.\n\n2. **Session B2** (sio5 deliver): 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO WR1 TX_IE. Checks: M_MASK30 killed (sio5 deliver STOP \u2713 \u2014 wait: M_MASK30 masks bit 30 out of status \u2192 line 5 pending but status bit 30 cleared \u2192 no delivery \u2192 sio5 deliver STOP \u2713 26/27). M_SWONLY: skips slots without sw pending \u2192 slot 0 has NO sw pending (only device line 5) \u2192 continue \u2192 no delivery \u2192 STOP \u2713. Good. B2 passes on HEAD \u2713 (slot 0 connected; walk 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 else, latch 0 \u2192 no crash). \u2713 sound.\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 one check on B2: the SIO stores: `store(s, SIOCMD0, 0x01)` then `store(s, SIOCMD0, 0x02)` \u2014 \"WR1 select, then TX_IE\". Hmm \u2014 SIOCMD0 is the SIO command register address. Storing 0x01 then 0x02 \u2014 the comment says \"WR1 select, then TX_IE -- sio_tx_pending raises line 5\". Plausible (z8530-style: WR0 select WR1, then next store goes to WR1). The packet doesn't inline dev_sio behavior \u2014 premise not fully checkable, but it's the probe's own mechanics, measured rows confirm (M_MASK30 red means on baseline line 5 delivered). READ from table.\n\n3. **Session C2** (both deliver): -n 2, MASK0+MASK1 bit 26, SOFT0+SOFT1. Both cpus must enter. M_IRQACTIVE0 killed: with latch collapsed to [0]: walk slot 0: status&enable \u2192 assert cpu_irq[0] if !irqActive[0] (0) \u2192 assert, irqActive[0]=1. Slot 1: status&enable \u2192 if (!irqActive[0]) \u2192 1 \u2192 NO assert for cpu 1! \u2192 cpu 1 never enters \u2192 both deliver STOP \u2713 (table \u2713). Sound. Also catches the ORDER: \"the first arrival is parked off the vector, then continue again\" \u2014 the probe handles observing the second arrival \u2713 (premise 4).\n\n   Hmm wait \u2014 with M_IRQACTIVE0, cpu 0 DOES enter (first arrival parked off vector), cpu 1 doesn't \u2192 entered={0} \u2192 STOP \u2713.\n\n4. **Session E**: as analyzed \u2014 design sound; the M_AND1 table WORD issue is a killtable reporting defect, not a session defect. Hmm \u2014 but actually wait, let me recheck whether the M_AND1 row really is inconsistent, because if the breakpoint IS global, M_AND1 gives hit=True \u2192 the code rows cpu3 deliver as BADREGS... The table says STOP. One of them is wrong. UNLESS \u2014 hmm \u2014 WAIT: with M_AND1, does cpu 1 actually enter VBR+8? Let me re-examine: `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu & 1])` \u2014 M_AND1 replaces `cpu_irq[cpu]` with `cpu_irq[cpu & 1]` at ASSERT and DEASSERT. Slot 3 \u2192 cpu_irq[1] \u2014 cpu 1's interrupt struct \u2014 assert \u2192 cpu 1 vectors. BUT: hold on \u2014 was cpu 1's PSR set BEFORE the interrupt? Yes: cpus 1..3 parked with VBR/PSR set first. \u2713. So cpu 1 enters \u2192 global breakpoint hit \u2192 hit=True \u2192 \"cpu3 deliver\" = OK if pcs[3]==VBR+8 else BADREGS \u2192 pcs[3] = PARK \u2192 BADREGS. Table: STOP. INCONSISTENT \u2014 the table's M_AND1 row contradicts the inlined session-E code IF breakpoints are global (which session C's v2 description establishes). Hmm, unless \"STOP\" in the table's shorthand means something looser... The RESULT words are defined: OK / HALT / BADREGS / STOP / NOSTART. STOP = \"no vector entry before the wait ran out\". With cpu 1 entering, there WAS a vector entry within the wait. So STOP is wrong; BADREGS is right. This is a real internal inconsistency in Table 2's M_AND1 row \u2014 READ+REASONED. But again: not one of the NINE dispositions; it's a defect in the killtable's red-row words. It could flip a naive adjudication of M_AND1's reading but the score (25/27 FAIL) is right either way. I should report it as a secondary defect. Hmm, hmm.\n\n5. **M_NULLAB equivalence disclosure**: sound (argued above). \u2713.\n\n6. **M_NOREASSERT debt disclosure**: \"a deleted reassert in the SOFT arm alone (the clock's own reassert carries the delivery; disclosed debt)\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. Let me scrutinize this harder. The SOFT arm's reassert removed \u2192 delivery carried by \"the clock's own reassert\". But the clock route is ALSO disclosed as not steppable/host-timer-driven \u2014 \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven, not steppable -- same loop as sio5)\" is in the DOES-NOT-CATCH list. So the probe RELIES on the clock (to carry delivery in M_NOREASSERT) while also disclosing it can't control the clock. Is that a contradiction? Not really: the clock fires often enough that within 15 s it walks. Measured 27/27 PASS for M_NOREASSERT. Hmm \u2014 but here's a thought: is M_NOREASSERT actually non-equivalent in a way that MATTERS? Yes: without the SOFT arm's walk call, an IPI is delivered only on the next clock tick \u2014 a latency regression. Disclosed as debt \u2713 honest.\n\n   Hmm \u2014 but WAIT, actually: is the M_NOREASSERT disclosure one of the \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\"? Yes: \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\" = M_NULLAB (equivalent) + M_NOREASSERT (debt). OK.\n\n7. **lunadefaultstop LOW**: \"the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms\". Filed. Hmm \u2014 is filing it as LOW right? It's a pre-existing upstream gap (unimplemented register arms). LOW plausible. \u2713.\n\n8. **lunaabsentmaskdecl LOW documented-only**: the absent slots' MASK/SOFT registers stay guest-writable storage with readback. Hmm \u2014 is this the right call? Board-model question: on real hardware, MASK1..3 registers exist (the LUNA-88K has 4 CPUs max; the registers exist regardless). Keeping them storage-with-readback mirrors hardware. Design hold from pass 1. \u2713 plausible.\n\n9. **es438 annotate/close**: \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows\". Hmm \u2014 the detector's mask2/mask3 readback rows exist (contract). So es438's owed pair is discharged. But WAIT \u2014 grok's pass-2 finding says the main loop's \"unchanged\" dispositions \"named rows that are not ledger rows (`lunadefaultstop`, `es438cells`, `lunaabsentmaskdecl` -- `es438` is the row)\". So the LEDGER row is es438, and the disposition was to annotate/close es438 this round. Consistent.\n\nHmm. So which of the nine is WRONG? Let me re-read the second question: \"Second question, with the count named: of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move, three new sessions, two disclosed equivalents/debts, three filings/annotations -- lunadefaultstop, lunaabsentmaskdecl, es438), WHICH IS WRONG?\"\n\nMaybe the intended catch: the count is wrong?? \"of the nine pass-3 dispositions (the capture move [1], three new sessions [2,3,4], two disclosed equivalents/debts [5,6], three filings/annotations [7,8,9])\" \u2014 1+3+2+3 = 9 \u2713. The count is right.\n\nHmm \u2014 OR the intended catch is one of the dispositions is NOT a pass-3 disposition (it's a pass-2 disposition)? E.g., the capture move was made in pass 2's repair (fix v2)... no \u2014 fix v2 IS the pass-2 repair; pass 3 reviews it. The \"pass-3 dispositions\" = dispositions made in THIS packet. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 the intended catch: \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\" \u2014 are there really TWO? M_NULLAB (equivalent) and M_NOREASSERT (debt) \u2014 yes two. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 the intended catch: one of the three filings is wrong \u2014 e.g., es438's disposition. Let me think: es438 \"owed mask2/mask3 pair\" \u2014 but is that discharged by THIS detector's rows? The detector's mask rows are \"mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks\" \u2014 but WAIT: are the mask2/mask3 rows in probe v3, or were they in a DIFFERENT detector? The contract says the probe catches \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm \u2014 but hold on: \"a DECLINED store\"? The readback rows check that a store to an absent slot's MASK is NOT declined (stays storage with readback \u2014 lunaabsentmaskdecl). So mask1/2/3 readback rows exist in the probe. es438's owed pair (mask2/mask3) \u2286 {mask1, mask2, mask3} \u2192 discharged. \u2713.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at the \"records this round carries\" section again very carefully:\n\n\"File as rows: `lunadefaultstop` LOW (the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms -- pass-1 F13 said \"file separately\" and nobody did), `lunaabsentmaskdecl` LOW documented-only (the absent slots' registers stay storage with readback -- the design hold from pass 1).  `es438`: its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows -- annotate/close on this round.  The ledger witness field becomes `probe:regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py` at closure.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, \"the ledger witness field becomes `probe:regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`\" \u2014 but grok's pass-2 finding said \"the ledger's witness field is still `none`\" \u2014 and this round sets it. But \u2014 the probe file: the diff shows TWO files: `regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/luna_absentcpu_probe.v2.py` \u2192 `luna_absentcpu_probe.v3.py`, and the brief's context says the detector is `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`. So there's the canonical `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py` and versioned artifacts. The witness field points to the canonical. Presumably the canonical is updated to v3 (frozen md5). Can't verify from packet \u2014 minor.\n\nOK here's another angle: maybe the intended WRONG disposition is the **capture move** \u2014 because of a subtle flaw: the capture is below the exit, but is it below the CONNECTS? \"DEVINIT connects slot 0 always and slots 1..3 under ncpus >= 2/3/4; later in DEVINIT ncpus > 4 prints and exit(1)s.\" \u2014 connects FIRST, then exit, then capture. Fine. The bound is assigned after connects \u2014 no walk call in between (walk callers are guest-triggered + device events; DEVINIT doesn't call the walk... unless a device asserts during DEVINIT \u2014 clock starts later). OK fine.\n\nHmm, what about: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus;` \u2014 but premise 2 says \"emul.c maps ncpus 0 -> 1\". So machine->ncpus \u2265 1 always. \u2713.\n\nHmm, what about the SECOND part of the fix comment: \"It walked all MAX_CPUS and called the NULL interrupt_assert of an unpopulated slot: on the default one-cpu machine a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1 followed by a store to SOFT_INT1 (or any device line whose bit that mask enables) ended the emulator process with a segmentation fault -- measured, regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py.\"\n\nCheck: 0xfc000000 = bits 26..31 (0xfc000000 = 1111 1100 ... 0 \u2014 bits 26-31 set: 0x04000000 (26), 0x08000000 (27), 0x10000000 (28), 0x20000000 (29), 0x40000000 (30), 0x80000000 (31) \u2014 sum = 0xfc000000 \u2713). So MASK1 = all six bits \u2192 then SOFT_INT1 \u2192 slot 1: status = 0 (no device) | bit 26 (sw) = bit 26; enable = 0xfc000000 \u2192 nonzero \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1] \u2192 NULL \u2192 segfault \u2713. Consistent. \u2713.\n\nOK here's yet another angle on Q1 \u2014 maybe the intended clean-sweep: **the `else` branch's DEASSERT on a connected slot** \u2014 disclosed as not caught (\"no row observes a post-clear deassert free-running: handlers are `br .` with IND set and never rte\"). So a mutant breaking the DEASSERT path (e.g., `if (d->irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 `if (0)`, or removing the DEASSERT call) sweeps. Is that in the (d) enumeration? The enumeration: \"the body-read fields ... `irqActive[cpu]`, `cpu_irq[cpu]`; joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} x {pending: sw, device line, none} x {irqActive})\". The joint cell {connected slot} \u00d7 {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive: SET} \u2014 that's the DEASSERT path: a connected slot with a previously-asserted latch that becomes unpending \u2192 DEASSERT. No row observes it (disclosed). So a mutant deleting the DEASSERT call (or inverting the latch test) is a clean sweep BY THE PACKET'S OWN DISCLOSURE. But it's DISCLOSED \u2014 disclosed debt doesn't count as a \"floor breach surviving adjudication\"? Hmm \u2014 the question asks: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE?\" \u2014 the enumeration includes irqActive[cpu] and the joint cells {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive}. The deassert path IS in the enumeration. And the packet ITSELF discloses it's not caught (\"a connected slot's DEASSERT ... the `irqActive[cpu] = 0` clear itself (same reason)\"). So YES \u2014 a clean-sweep mutant exists in the enumeration, and the packet DISCLOSES it: e.g., M_NODEASSERT: delete `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]);` \u2014 sweeps 27/27, non-equivalent (a connected slot's line never deasserts \u2192 the cpu never sees the interrupt clear... well, IND set at handler, never rte, so the probe can't tell; but a real guest that rte's would re-take the interrupt forever \u2014 actually if the line stays asserted, rte \u2192 immediate re-entry \u2192 the guest thinks the device never cleared the interrupt \u2192 real defect). So the (d) enumeration contains at least the disclosed deassert cell \u2014 the honest answer: the enumeration includes cells the probe has ALREADY disclosed as uncatchable (deassert/clear), so \"zero clean sweeps in (d)\" is FALSE as literally asked \u2014 unless disclosed cells are excluded from \"the enumeration\". Hmm \u2014 the question: \"Does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE (fix v2 + probe v3)?\" \u2014 if the answer intended \"no, close\", then the disclosed cells must be OUT of (d). But the enumeration as WRITTEN includes \"joint cells where the body tests them ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: sw, device line, none} \u00d7 {irqActive})\" \u2014 {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive: set} is EXACTLY the disclosed-uncatchable deassert cell. So as WRITTEN, the answer is YES: the deassert cell admits clean sweeps, e.g.:\n\n- M_NODEASSERT: remove the `if (d->irqActive[cpu]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(...)` call \u2014 sweeps (no row observes a deassert).\n- M_LATCHCLEAR: `d->irqActive[cpu] = 0;` \u2192 `= 1;` hmm \u2014 wait: else-branch sets latch 0; if set to 1: next walk with pending: `if (!d->irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 1 \u2192 skip ASSERT \u2192 the slot never re-asserts after a clear... but the probe never rte's, so within a row, does the walk run twice with the latch in the wrong state? The clock calls the walk repeatedly! Session B2: line 5 pending, clock walks repeatedly: first walk asserts (latch 0\u21921). Later walks: status&enable nonzero \u2192 `if (!irqActive)` \u2192 latch=1 \u2192 skip \u2192 fine, no deassert needed. Delivery still OK \u2192 sweeps. And non-equivalent? With latch-clear broken (=1), a deassert scenario: slot pending \u2192 latch 1; then device clears (status&enable=0) \u2192 else \u2192 deassert called (latch was 1) \u2192 line deasserted, latch set to 1 (broken) \u2014 wait the mutant sets it to 1 instead of 0 \u2014 then next pending: `if (!irqActive[cpu])` \u2192 false \u2192 NO ASSERT \u2192 delivery LOST after a clear-then-repend cycle. Real defect. And the probe: never observes (handlers never rte, IND set). Sweep.\n\nHmm \u2014 but BOTH of these are exactly the disclosed \"Does NOT catch: a connected slot's DEASSERT ... the `irqActive[cpu] = 0` clear itself (same reason)\". So they're DISCLOSED. The question's enumeration, though, INCLUDES those cells. So there's a tension: the question asks \"does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration\" and the packet's own disclosure says YES for the deassert/clear cells. So either:\n(a) The intended answer: NO clean sweep \u2014 because the disclosed cells are excluded (the enumeration implicitly means UNDISCLOSED cells), or\n(b) The intended answer: YES \u2014 the deassert cell, but it's disclosed, so it's debt not a breach.\n\nThe question says: \"If you find one, give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so); if none, CLOSE with your own denominator.\"\n\n\"a predicted sweep is a measurement owed\" \u2014 i.e., if I PREDICT a mutant sweeps, the panel owes a measurement of it. So finding one \u2192 ONE MORE REPAIR? Hmm \u2014 the verdict options: CLOSE THE CHAIN / ONE MORE REPAIR: <which> / DO NOT SHIP.\n\nNow \u2014 my per-device-line finding (lines 1-4,6 unwitnessed) is NOT disclosed: the contract CLAIMS \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is CAUGHT (via B2) \u2014 but that's only line 5. The enumeration says \"interrupt_status (per device line)\" \u2014 six lines. The probe witnesses line 5 only. A mutant masking any OTHER line's bit out of the device status read sweeps 27/27 AND is non-equivalent. Is it disclosed? NO \u2014 the contract claims device-line masking is CAUGHT. So THIS is an UNDISCLOSED clean-sweep in the (d) enumeration. That's a floor breach: the probe's contract overclaims; the composite doesn't cover the per-device-line cells it enumerates.\n\nWait \u2014 but hold on. Can the mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 26)` (mask line 1's device bit) actually sweep? Let me re-verify against ALL 27 rows. Rows I know: soft1 (B), siofirst (B), sio5 (B), slot0 deliver (A2/B), mask1/2/3 readbacks, survive, two cpus, cpu1 setup, mask1 land, cpu1 deliver, cpu1 shadow, cpu0 parked (C), nodeliver ctl (D), sio5 deliver (B2), both deliver (C2), four cpus, cpu3 deliver, others idle (E). That's ~19 named; 27 total (some unnamed in packet). Which rows raise device lines other than 5? \"siofirst\" \u2014 SIO first? maybe \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 siofirst might be: SIO line raised BEFORE the soft store (so the FIRST walk through the device caller hits the absent slot) \u2014 on 1 cpu with MASK1 armed? Hmm: \"siofirst HALT\" on HEAD/M_REVERT \u2014 siofirst crashes the unfixed walk \u2192 it must make the walk reach an UNCONNECTED slot: 1 cpu, MASK1 armed (enable[1] \u2260 0) + a device line pending \u2192 slot 1: status&enable \u2260 0 \u2192 assert cpu_irq[1] \u2192 NULL \u2192 crash. WHICH device line? If siofirst uses SIO line 5 (bit 30) with MASK1 = 0x40000000 \u2014 then it's line 5 again. If it uses another line... unknown. The packet doesn't say. So: does ANY row deliver device line 1,2,3,4,6? From the packet: only line 5 is named (B2's bit 30, sio5). The clock's line is raised in the background in every free-running session \u2014 which line is the clock? If the clock's line is, say, line 2 (bit 27), and NO MASK ever enables bit 27, the clock's assert delivers nothing and can't witness a masked bit 27. Hmm \u2014 BUT WAIT: does any row enable a mask bit corresponding to the clock's line? The rows use 0x04000000 (bit 26) and 0x40000000 (bit 30). If the clock's line were line 1 (bit 26)... then in sw rows, bit 26 is enabled \u2014 and the clock's assert would deliver bit 26 even without the SOFT arm... hmm, that would make soft1 row pass even with the SOFT reassert removed \u2014 consistent with M_NOREASSERT passing! Interesting \u2014 but ALSO: if the clock's line is bit 26, then in session C (MASK1 bit 26, no SOFT store? no \u2014 C HAS the SOFT_INT1 store) \u2014 can't distinguish. Hmm \u2014 in session D (control: same as C WITHOUT the SOFT_INT1 store): \"NO vector entry within the wait\" \u2014 if the clock's line were bit 26, the clock's periodic asserts would deliver bit 26 to slot 1 (MASK1 enables it) \u2192 vector entry \u2192 nodeliver ctl would FAIL on baseline. Baseline is 27/27 \u2192 nodeliver ctl OK \u2192 the clock's line is NOT bit 26 (or the clock never asserts in the window \u2014 but M_NOREASSERT's pass says it does). So the clock's line \u2209 {26} and its bit is never enabled in any row \u2192 the clock's line is unwitnessed too.\n\nSo: device lines witnessed by delivery rows: line 5 only. Lines 1,2,3,4,6 (and the clock's line): no delivery rows. UNLESS siofirst/sio5 rows in session B use a different line \u2014 sio5 by name is line 5. \"siofirst\" \u2014 hmm, could be \"SIO first\" ordering (line 5 before soft1). Probably line 5.\n\nBUT \u2014 the counter-argument: is a per-line masking mutant REACHABLE as a mutation of the SHIPPED FIX? The enumeration is of the depended-on surface \u2014 the walk's body reads interrupt_status. A mutant `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27)` (drop line 2) \u2014 non-equivalent (line 2 delivery lost), sweeps the probe (no row raises line 2 with its bit enabled). This is squarely in \"(per device line)\". So: YES, a clean-sweep mutant exists \u2014 e.g., `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27);` \u2014 predicted 27/27 PASS; the row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu row arming MASK0 with bit 27 and raising device line 2 \u2014 IF the probe can raise line 2. CAN it? The probe drives the SIO (line 5). Other lines come from other devices (lan, scsi, kbd?) \u2014 the LUNA-88K's six device lines map to specific devices; the probe would need to trigger those devices. If the probe can't deterministically raise lines 1-4/6, the honest disposition is: the per-line cells for lines 1-4,6 are UNWITNESSABLE by this probe \u2192 the CONTRACT's \"Catches: a device line masked out of the walk\" is TRUE only for line 5 \u2192 the contract overclaims \u2192 the enumeration's per-line cells are disclosed-debt-able, but they are NOT disclosed. THAT's the defect: an undisclosed coverage gap in the contract's \"Catches\" list.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 actually, let me re-read the contract's claim once more: \"Catches: ... a device line masked out of the walk or a walk of software-pending slots only (session B2)\". The claim \"a device line masked out of the walk\" \u2014 with the parenthetical (session B2) \u2014 a careful reader could read it as \"a device line [i.e., THE device line exercised] masked out\" \u2014 but the natural reading is universal (\"a device line\" = any). M_MASK30's kill demonstrates line 5 only. So the contract's universal claim is unproven for 5 of 6 lines. This is a real, packet-internal finding: the CATCHES claim overreaches the measurement. Whether it's a \"floor breach\" \u2014 hmm. The floor: \"zero floor breaches surviving adjudication\". A contract overclaim on an enumerated cell = a defect in the shipped composite's disclosure. The remedy: either add rows (if lines are raisable) or move the per-line cells (except line 5) to the disclosed-debt list. That's a REPAIR to the probe's contract (and possibly rows) \u2192 ONE MORE REPAIR: the per-device-line cells of the (d) enumeration (lines 1-4,6 unwitnessed; contract overclaims \"a device line masked out\").\n\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 is that TOO aggressive? Let me reconsider: is the per-device-line cell really part of what the fix DEPENDS ON? The fix = the bound. The probe must catch mutations of the fix's repair surface AND the walk it bounds... The (d) enumeration given in the question explicitly includes \"the body-read fields `interrupt_status` (per device line)\". So the panel itself enumerates it. And asks: does a clean sweep exist in that enumeration? My answer: YES \u2014 for device lines other than 5 (and the sw bit via the DEVICE path \u2014 bit 26's device read is re-added by the SOFT arm so the device-bit-26 read is unwitnessed too \u2014 actually wait, bit 26: a mutant masking bit 26 from the DEVICE read: sw rows re-add it \u2192 pass; line-1-device rows: none \u2192 sweep. So bit 26's device-path cell is also unwitnessed. And bits 27,28,29,31: no rows at all. So FIVE of SIX per-line cells (line 1 via device path, lines 2,3,4,6 entirely) admit masking mutants that sweep).\n\nAnd is such a mutant non-equivalent? Yes \u2014 real devices raise those lines (the six device lines exist; luna88k_interrupt_assert is called with line 1..6 by real devices). A guest with line 2 pending and MASK enabling bit 27 loses the interrupt. Real defect. So: clean-sweep mutant EXISTS in (d). Predicted sweep \u2192 \"a measurement owed\" \u2014 I must SAY that: the predicted sweep needs a measurement (run M_MASK27 etc. through killtable2).\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but also the DEASSERT cells (disclosed). The question's enumeration includes {pending: none} \u00d7 {irqActive} cells \u2014 disclosed as not caught. If the panel counts disclosed cells as out-of-scope for \"clean sweep\" (since they're disclosed), then my per-line finding is the only UNDISCLOSED one. The deassert disclosure is itself one of the \"two disclosed equivalents/debts\"... hmm \u2014 no: the two disclosed equivalents/debts are M_NULLAB and M_NOREASSERT. The deassert/clear non-coverage is a DISCLOSED DOES-NOT-CATCH item, not one of the nine dispositions. OK.\n\nSo Q1 answer: YES \u2014 one clean-sweep family in (d): per-device-line masking of interrupt_status for lines other than 5 (bits 26-device-path, 27, 28, 29, 31). Exact edit: `status = d->interrupt_status & ~(1 << 27);` (or equivalently M_MASK30 generalized). Predicted 27/27 PASS \u2014 a measurement owed. The row that should have caught it: a 1-cpu free-running row arming MASK0 with that line's bit and raising that device line (B2's shape, per line) \u2014 IF the line is raisable; if not raisable by any steerable device, the cell must move to the disclosed-debt list and the CONTRACT's \"Catches: a device line masked out of the walk\" must be narrowed to \"line 5\". Since the contract as shipped CLAIMS the catch, the composite misstates its coverage \u2192 verdict: ONE MORE REPAIR (contract/rows for per-line cells), not CLOSE.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider whether the clock could witness other lines... The clock's line: raised periodically (host-timer). Its bit is in interrupt_status machine-wide in EVERY session. If a row enabled the clock's bit in a MASK, the clock's line would deliver \u2014 no row does (masks used: bit 26, bit 30, 0xfc000000 in the FIX COMMENT's example \u2014 not a row). Wait \u2014 the fix comment's 0xfc000000 example: \"a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1 followed by a store to SOFT_INT1\" \u2014 that's the BUG description, not a probe row. Probe rows use 0x04000000 and 0x40000000. So no row enables the clock's bit. OK.\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT, one more consideration: maybe the probe rows in session A2/B (not fully inlined) DO use other bits! The packet doesn't inline session A2/B's rows fully. soft1: 1-cpu, MASK1 store + SOFT1 store (crashes HEAD). Which MASK1 value? If MASK1 = 0xfc000000 (all bits), then the row enables bits 26-31 \u2014 and the crash happens on SOFT1 store \u2192 the walk sees status bit 26 (sw) & enable \u2192 crash. But device lines 2-4,6: no row RAISES them (a MASK bit alone doesn't raise a line). To witness a masked-out line, a row needs the line RAISED (device event) + its MASK bit enabled + a connected slot. Only the SIO (line 5) is raised deliberately; the clock (unknown line) raised incidentally with its bit never enabled. So regardless of MASK values in unseen rows, no line other than 5 (and the sw bit) is ever BOTH raised AND enabled. Hmm \u2014 unless the clock's line's bit IS enabled by some row's mask... if soft1's MASK1 = 0xfc000000 and the clock's line is, say, line 2 (bit 27): slot 1 is UNCONNECTED on 1 cpu \u2014 with the FIX, slot 1 isn't walked at all \u2192 clock's line never delivers to slot 1. Slot 0's enable (MASK0) \u2014 is MASK0 ever written in session B? If a row wrote MASK0 = 0xfc000000 on 1 cpu and the clock's line were, e.g., bit 27, then the clock would deliver bit 27 to slot 0 \u2192 a \"clock route\" delivery \u2014 the packet DISCLOSES \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven, not steppable)\" as not-caught \u2014 meaning the probe can't steer it, but if it delivers incidentally, rows might still observe... I can't settle which line the clock uses or session A2/B's mask values from the packet. HONESTY: I cannot settle whether lines other than 5 are incidentally witnessed via the clock. The single fact that would settle it: the clock's interrupt line number (and session A2/B's mask constants). Hmm \u2014 but even IF the clock's line is incidentally witnessed (say line 2), lines 1,3,4,6 remain unwitnessed. Six device lines, one steerable (5), at most one clock line \u2014 at least three lines remain unwitnessed no matter what. So the finding stands: at least 3 of 6 per-line cells are unwitnessed, and the contract claims \"a device line masked out of the walk\" is caught. The finding is robust to the unknowns. GOOD.\n\nLet me also double-check the {slot} dimension: slots witnessed: 0 (B2, A2?), 1 (C, C2, soft1-crash), 2 (mask2 readback \u2014 storage only; delivery to slot 2? NO row delivers to slot 2! Session E delivers to slot 3 only; mask2 readback is a store/readback row, not a delivery row). Hmm! {slot 2} \u00d7 {pending} delivery cell: -n 3 machines? NO session runs -n 3! Sessions: A2 (1?), B (1), B2 (1), C (2), C2 (2), D (2), E (4). Slot 2 delivery on a 3-cpu machine: UNWITNESSED. A mutant that skips slot 2 (e.g., `for (cpu = 0; cpu < d->ncpus; cpu++) { if (cpu == 2) continue; ... }`) \u2014 wait, that's M_SKIP-something. Table: M_SKIP1 (slot 1 skipped) \u2014 caught by C/C2 (cpu1 deliver, both deliver red, 23/25). A hypothetical M_SKIP2 (slot 2 skipped): which row red? Slot 2 delivery happens only on -n 3 \u2014 no session! So M_SKIP2 sweeps 27/27! Is M_SKIP2 in the enumeration? \"{slot} \u00d7 ...\" \u2014 the slot set 0..3 \u2014 YES: \"its slot set 0..3\". A mutant `if (cpu == 2) continue;` in the walk: on 1/2/4-cpu machines, slot 2 is either absent (1,2) or... on 4 cpus (session E), slot 2: MASK2 not written, enable[2]=0 \u2192 else-branch \u2192 nothing observable. So E doesn't witness slot 2 either! So M_SKIP2 sweeps 27/27 and is non-equivalent (a 3-cpu machine's cpu 2 gets no interrupts). CLEAN SWEEP \u2014 in the {slot} dimension! Hmm!! And is it disclosed? The contract's catches: \"the walk reaching an unconnected slot by any route... slots 2/3 aliased onto 0/1 (session E)...\" \u2014 nothing about skipping slot 2 on a 3-cpu machine. NOT disclosed. The probe never runs -n 3! The enumeration's {slot} cells: slot 0 (witnessed: B2 etc.), slot 1 (C/C2), slot 2 (only as absent-slot storage: mask2 readback \u2014 on 1-cpu machines! not as a CONNECTED slot), slot 3 (E). So {slot 2, connected, pending} \u2014 UNWITNESSED. M_SKIP2 = clean sweep. Exact edit: `if (cpu == 2) continue;` at the top of the loop (or `cpu == 2` skipped any way). Predicted 27/27 PASS \u2014 measurement owed. The row that should have caught it: a session F (-n 3): MASK2 + SOFT_INT2 \u2192 cpu 2 enters VBR+8 (E's shape with n=3). That's a REAL gap: the enumeration says \"{slot} \u00d7 ...\" \u2014 slot 2's connected-pending cell has no row.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 actually also check: could slot 2 be witnessed in E via... MASK3+SOFT3 only. cpus 0..2 idle \u2014 slot 2's enable = 0 \u2192 nothing. Right, unwitnessed.\n\nSimilarly slot 3 on -n 4: witnessed \u2713 (E). Slot 1: \u2713 (C, C2). Slot 0: \u2713 (B2, A2?). Slot 2: \u2717. So M_SKIP2 sweeps. ALSO: a bound mutant `d->ncpus = ncpus == 3 ? 2 : ncpus` (clamp 3\u21922): swept (no -n 3 session). Same cell. And `cpu < d->ncpus` \u2192 `cpu < d->ncpus && cpu != 2`. All sweep.\n\nSo I have TWO clean-sweep families in (d): (i) per-device-line masking beyond line 5; (ii) slot-2-on-a-3-cpu-machine (no -n 3 session). Both predicted sweeps; both non-equivalent; both in the stated enumeration; neither disclosed. Also (iii) the deassert cells \u2014 but those ARE disclosed (so not \"surviving adjudication\" as breaches? they're disclosed debt).\n\nWait, but hmm: is (ii) really in the enumeration? \"the dominating predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` (its constant's source, its bound both ways, its slot set 0..3)\" \u2014 \"its slot set 0..3\" \u2014 the predicate's slot set is 0..ncpus-1 \u2286 0..3. A skip-2 mutant keeps the predicate but drops slot 2 from the SET. Yes \u2014 \"its slot set\" is enumerated. And \"{slot} x {pending...}\" joint cells \u2014 slot 2 \u00d7 pending. So yes, in-enumeration.\n\nHmm, and is M_SKIP2 non-equivalent on REACHABLE configurations? Reachable: ncpus 1..4. On -n 3: slot 2 connected, walk skips it \u2192 cpu 2's interrupts lost. -n 3 is reachable (-n is 1..256, DEVINIT allows \u22644). So NON-EQUIVALENT on a reachable config, and the probe has no -n 3 session \u2192 sweep. SOLID.\n\nSimilarly, hmm, let me also check {pending: device line} \u00d7 {slot: 1,2,3} cells: device-line delivery to slots 1..3 \u2014 B2 delivers line 5 to slot 0 only. E delivers SOFT (bit 26 via sw) to slot 3. C/C2: sw to slots 0,1. So a mutant that drops DEVICE-line delivery for slots > 0... e.g., M_SWONLY kills device delivery for ALL slots (caught via B2's slot 0). But a mutant like \"device lines deliver only to slot 0\": status = interrupt_status only for cpu==0, else sw-only: hmm \u2014 status is machine-wide; a mutant `if (cpu != 0) status = 0; if (sw) status |= bit26;` \u2014 then slot 1: status = bit26 (if sw) \u2192 C passes (sw), C2 passes, E passes (slot 3: sw \u2192 bit 26) \u2014 device lines never tested on slots 1..3 \u2192 sweep. Non-equivalent (device line to cpu 1 lost). In-enumeration ({slot} \u00d7 {pending: device line}). Another family. But honestly the slot-2 and per-line findings suffice; the general statement: the {slot} \u00d7 {pending} cross is only sparsely witnessed (slot 0 \u00d7 device \u2713 (B2), slot 0 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 1 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 3 \u00d7 sw \u2713, slot 2 \u00d7 anything \u2717, slot 1/2/3 \u00d7 device \u2717).\n\nSo Q1's honest answer: YES, clean-sweep mutants exist in (d). The cleanest, most undeniable: **M_SKIP2** (`if (cpu == 2) continue;`) \u2014 no -n 3 session exists; predicted 27/27; owed row: session F (-n 3, MASK2+SOFT_INT2, cpu 2 enters VBR+8). Also per-line masking beyond bit 30 (M_MASK27 etc.) \u2014 predicted sweeps; owed rows per raisable line, else a contract narrowing. And the deassert cells are disclosed (not breaches).\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, now let me reconsider: is the intended answer maybe \"none \u2014 CLOSE\"? The packet is framed as the CLOSING pass, \"SHORT BY DESIGN\", with the contract claiming full coverage of the enumeration... The nine dispositions include three new sessions specifically to close pass-2 escapes. The question \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" (of the nine) suggests the author EXPECTS the panel to find one wrong disposition. And Q1 \"does a clean-sweep exist\" \u2014 the author might expect \"no\" (close) with the deassert cells disclosed... But my job: audit premises as stated, find real defects. The slot-2 gap is REAL from the packet: sessions are -n 1, 2, 4 only (READ: session list), the enumeration includes the slot set 0..3 and {slot} cells (READ), -n 3 is reachable (READ: -n 1..256, DEVINIT allows \u22644), no row delivers to slot 2 (READ: E uses slot 3; mask2 readback is storage-only; C/C2 slots 0/1; B2 slot 0). Therefore M_SKIP2 is a predicted sweep \u2014 REASONED, with the measurement owed. This is exactly what the question asks for (\"give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it (a predicted sweep is a measurement owed -- say so)\").\n\nAnd Q2: which of the nine is wrong? Given my analysis, candidates:\n- The M_AND1 table word (not one of the nine).\n- Hmm, among the nine: is one of the THREE NEW SESSIONS wrong? Session E is the glm/grok answer \u2014 it covers slot 3 and the alias \u2014 but NOT slot 2 (no -n 3). Is session E \"wrong\"? It does what it claims (kills M_AND1, M_MIN2, M_HARD1 partially \u2014 M_HARD1 red includes cpu3 deliver). E is sound for its purpose. The GAP (slot 2) is a MISSING session, not a wrong one. Hmm.\n- The capture move: sound.\n- M_NULLAB equivalence: sound.\n- M_NOREASSERT debt: sound-ish.\n- lunadefaultstop: LOW filing \u2014 sound.\n- lunaabsentmaskdecl: LOW documented-only \u2014 sound.\n- es438: annotate/close \u2014 \"its owed mask2/mask3 pair is discharged by this detector's rows\". Hmm!! WAIT. Let me check this ONE carefully. es438 owed a **mask2/mask3 pair**. The detector's rows: \"a declined store to an absent slot (mask1/mask2/mask3 readbacks)\". Hmm \u2014 but WHERE are the mask2/mask3 readback rows? On how many cpus? The mask rows check storage-with-readback for ABSENT slots \u2014 mask2/mask3 are absent on 1- and 2-cpu machines. Session B (1 cpu) would host mask1/2/3 readbacks \u2014 all three absent. So the detector's mask2/mask3 rows exist (per the contract) \u2192 es438's pair discharged. Hmm, seems fine...\n\nUNLESS \u2014 the point is that es438's owed pair was about something else... I don't have es438's origin inlined (pass-1 row). Can't settle. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at lunadefaultstop once more: \"the switch's `default:` graceful stop plus the unimplemented RESET_CPU0..3 / SOFT_INT_FLAG* arms\". Hmm \u2014 RESET_CPU0..3 and SOFT_INT_FLAG* \u2014 these are register ARMS in the switch (device register read/write dispatch). Unimplemented arms \u2192 what do they do? Fall to default? or break? \"the switch's default: graceful stop\" \u2014 the default arm stops gracefully. Unimplemented arms (RESET_CPU0..3, SOFT_INT_FLAG*) \u2014 if they hit the default \u2192 graceful stop \u2192 a guest store to RESET_CPU0 stops the machine?! On REAL hardware, RESET_CPUn resets that cpu. An unimplemented arm that STOPS the emulator on a legitimate guest access is... a behavior bug (the emulator dies/quits on guest IPI-reset). Filed LOW. Is LOW right? Eh \u2014 board-model severity judgment; the panel filed LOW. Can't adjudicate from packet.\n\nHmm, OK \u2014 let me now think about what the AUTHOR planted. This is pass 3 of a repair chain; the packet is elaborate and mostly self-consistent. The question structure: \"If you find one [clean-sweep], give the exact edit and the row that should have caught it...; if none, CLOSE with your own denominator. Second question...: of the nine pass-3 dispositions..., WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the second question PRESUPPOSES one is wrong (\"WHICH IS WRONG?\" not \"IS ANY WRONG?\"). So the author believes exactly one disposition is wrong. Let me hunt again with that prior.\n\nNine: (1) capture move; (2) B2; (3) C2; (4) E; (5) M_NULLAB-equivalent disclosure; (6) M_NOREASSERT debt disclosure; (7) lunadefaultstop; (8) lunaabsentmaskdecl; (9) es438.\n\nRe-examine (5): M_NULLAB \"EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/; the choice is warrant-based\". Hmm \u2014 wait: \"measured 22/22 in killtable/\" \u2014 but table 2 (killtable2) shows M_NULLAB 27/27 PASS. The contract v3 cites the OLD killtable (22/22) rather than killtable2 (27/27)?? The diff: `+(EQUIVALENT on every reachable configuration -- measured 22/22 in killtable/; the choice is warrant-based),` \u2014 hmm, that's in the v3 docstring, and killtable2's M_NULLAB is 27/27. The docstring cites killtable/ (pass-2's table) \u2014 stale-ish but not wrong (both support equivalence). Meh.\n\nRe-examine (6): M_NOREASSERT \"disclosed debt: the clock's own reassert carries the delivery\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. Something's off. If the SOFT arm's reassert is deleted, delivery of a software interrupt relies on the CLOCK calling the walk. But WHEN does the clock call it? luna88k_interrupt_assert(clock's line) \u2014 the clock asserts its line periodically (timer tick). Each assert \u2192 walk. So within 15 s, delivery happens. Measured 27/27 PASS. But hold on \u2014 is the clock's assert actually periodic, or ONE-SHOT at init? \"the clock/timer route (host-timer driven...)\" \u2014 periodic. And M_NOREASSERT's 27/27 confirms delivery still occurs (else soft rows would STOP). Fine \u2014 the disclosure is empirically supported by the table. \u2713 sound.\n\nHmm \u2014 re-examine (4) session E's row \"four cpus\": `four = (\"cpu3\" in s.buf[mark:]) or (\"cpu 3\" in s.buf[mark:]); row(\"four cpus\", \"OK\" if four else \"BADREGS\", ...)`. Reads the `machine` listing for cpu3. Fine. But \u2014 hmm \u2014 \"four cpus\" OK on M_NOASSIGN (bound 0): the machine listing still names cpu3 (the machine HAS 4 cpus regardless of d->ncpus) \u2192 OK \u2713 consistent (M_NOASSIGN's reds don't include four cpus \u2713). And on M_MIN2: four cpus OK \u2713 (listing has cpu3). \u2713.\n\nRe-examine (2) B2's row semantics \u2014 \"sio5 deliver\": on M_CALLGUARD (bound the CALLS), red rows: \"sio5, siofirst HALT\" \u2014 sio5 is a SESSION B row (v2) and sio5 deliver is B2's row. Table M_CALLGUARD: \"21/23 | sio5, siofirst HALT\". Hmm \u2014 TWO red rows named \"sio5\" and \"siofirst\" \u2014 where's \"sio5 deliver\"? M_CALLGUARD bounds the reassert CALLS (in store arms?) \u2014 the SIO's luna88k_interrupt_assert caller still calls the walk unbounded \u2192 walk reaches absent slot 1 (if MASK1 armed?) \u2192 HALT. In session B, siofirst: SIO line raised FIRST (before soft1?) on a machine with MASK1 armed? Hmm \u2014 the names: soft1, siofirst, sio5 (session B rows). M_CALLGUARD red: sio5 + siofirst. So session B has a sio5 row (SIO line 5 on... 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30? \u2014 that's B2's shape \u2014 wait, but B2 is NEW in v3 (deepseek/grok found v2 had NO such row: \"v2 had no such row (deepseek, grok, pass 2)\"). So session B's \"sio5\" row is DIFFERENT from B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row: session B's sio5 = SIO line 5 to an ABSENT slot? (MASK1 bit 30 on 1 cpu \u2192 walk hits slot 1 \u2192 HALT on HEAD \u2713). And \"siofirst\" = SIO raised before the soft store (ordering) \u2192 HALT on HEAD \u2713. OK \u2014 session B (1 cpu) rows: soft1 (MASK1+SOFT1 \u2192 crash), siofirst (SIO before soft \u2192 crash), sio5 (MASK1 bit 30 + SIO line 5 \u2192 crash), slot0 deliver?, mask1/2/3 readbacks, survive... These are the PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM rows: HEAD scores 20/22 with soft1+siofirst HALT. Hmm \u2014 but if session B ALSO has a sio5 row that crashes HEAD, why isn't sio5 red on HEAD? HEAD: 20/22, red = soft1, siofirst only. So on HEAD, sio5 did NOT crash?? Hmm \u2014 maybe sio5 is a row that ARMS line 5 without enabling its bit in any absent slot's mask (e.g., MASK0 bit 30 on 1 cpu \u2192 slot 0 connected \u2192 delivers OK on HEAD; walk continues 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 no crash \u2713). Then why is sio5 red on M_CALLGUARD?? M_CALLGUARD: \"the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS\" \u2014 the fix alternative: instead of bounding the walk, bound the reassert CALLS in the store arms (only call for cpu < n?) \u2014 hmm, but the walk itself still walks MAX_CPUS when called from the DEVICE callers \u2192 crash when a device line has an enabled bit in an absent slot's mask. sio5 red on M_CALLGUARD: sio5's session-B shape must arm an absent slot's mask with bit 30 AND raise line 5 \u2192 walk (from the SIO caller) \u2192 slot 1 assert \u2192 NULL \u2192 HALT. But then sio5 would ALSO crash on HEAD! Contradiction? HEAD: sio5 OK (not red). Hmm!\n\nUnless sio5 (session B) arms MASK0 bit 30 (slot 0, connected) \u2014 on HEAD: walk 0..3, slot 0 delivers \u2713, slots 1..3: enable 0 \u2192 fine \u2192 OK. On M_CALLGUARD: the CALLS are bounded \u2014 which calls? \"bound the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms instead of the walk (sio5)\" (from the contract's catch list \u2014 session B2 catches this!). Wait \u2014 the contract v3: \"the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms instead of the walk (sio5)\" \u2014 caught by session B2's sio5 deliver row! Hmm \u2014 so M_CALLGUARD is killed by B2's sio5 deliver (device caller \u2192 walk unbounded... no wait). Hmm, let me re-think M_CALLGUARD: alternative fix = bound the reassert CALLS (in the four callers? or the store arms?) instead of bounding the walk. If the calls are guarded (`if (cpu < ncpus) reassert()` \u2014 no, reassert isn't per-cpu...). Hmm \u2014 \"the reassert CALL bounded in the store arms\" \u2014 the store arms call reassert_interrupts(); the alternative: make the store arms NOT call it (or call a bounded variant) \u2014 but the WALK itself still walks MAX_CPUS when the DEVICE callers (luna88k_interrupt_assert/deassert) invoke it \u2192 any device line with an armed absent-slot mask \u2192 crash. B2: 1 cpu, MASK0 bit 30, SIO line 5 raised \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 0: delivers \u2713 (so sio5 deliver row: does it crash or pass on M_CALLGUARD?). Table M_CALLGUARD red rows: \"sio5, siofirst HALT\" \u2014 hmm, \"sio5\" not \"sio5 deliver\". And the contract says the call-bounded alternative is caught by (sio5) \u2014 B2's row name is \"sio5 deliver\"! The table's M_CALLGUARD row says red: \"sio5, siofirst\" \u2014 if \"sio5\" here means B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row, the row name is abbreviated. But wait \u2014 B2's sio5 deliver row: on M_CALLGUARD, would it HALT or STOP? M_CALLGUARD (bound the calls): the walk is UNBOUNDED (MAX_CPUS) whenever called; the store arms don't call it (or call it bounded \u2014 the walk itself is still unbounded!). B2: MASK0 bit 30 store (store arm \u2014 call bounded/absent \u2192 no crash), SIO WR1 TX_IE \u2192 sio_tx_pending \u2192 luna88k_interrupt_assert(line 5) \u2192 walk (UNBOUNDED) \u2192 slot 0: enable bit 30 & status bit 30 \u2192 assert \u2713 (cpu 0 enters VBR+8 \u2192 breakpoint hit \u2192 row OK?) \u2014 then walk CONTINUES: slot 1: status bit 30 & enable[1]=0 \u2192 0 \u2192 else \u2192 latch[1]=0 \u2192 no call \u2192 no crash. Slots 2,3 same. So B2 on M_CALLGUARD: sio5 deliver OK?! But the table says sio5 HALT on M_CALLGUARD. CONTRADICTION?\n\nHmm wait \u2014 maybe M_CALLGUARD's shape is different: \"bound the CALLS\" = the four callers' calls to reassert_interrupts are guarded by `cpu < ncpus`?? But reassert isn't per-cpu... Or maybe: the alternative fix = guard INTERRUPT_ASSERT/DEASSERT calls inside the walk with `if (cpu < d->ncpus)` \u2014 i.e., move the bound to the CALLS INSIDE the walk body (walk still iterates MAX_CPUS but only calls assert/deassert for connected slots): that's... M_GUARDASSERT (\"bound on the ASSERT call only\") \u2014 21/22, soft1 HALT. And \"M_CALLGUARD (the pass-1 F4 alternative: bound the CALLS)\" \u2014 bound the calls TO reassert_interrupts at the CALL SITES (the four callers): the store arms and the device callers get `if (something) reassert()`. Hmm \u2014 if ALL FOUR callers' calls are bounded... how? The calls aren't per-slot. Maybe: `luna88k_interrupt_assert` checks `if (line's slot...)`. I think M_CALLGUARD = at each of the four call sites, wrap with a guard that only calls when ncpus \u2265 2 (or per the store's cpunr < ncpus?). For the store arms: `if (cpunr < ncpus) reassert()` \u2014 hmm, but the walk walks ALL slots when called from slot 0's store... The pass-2 grok note: \"the SIO reaches the walk through the third AND fourth callers\" \u2014 the SIO's assert/deassert call the walk. So even if the STORE arms' calls are guarded, the SIO callers still call the walk \u2192 walk (unbounded, MAX_CPUS) \u2192 if any absent slot has enable&status \u2260 0 \u2192 crash. Which row arms an absent slot's mask AND has a device line pending? THAT must be session B's \"sio5\" row: MASK1 (absent slot 1) bit 30 + SIO line 5 raised \u2192 SIO caller \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 1: bit30 & enable \u2192 assert NULL \u2192 HALT. And on HEAD, sio5 \u2192 HALT TOO! But HEAD's reds are only soft1, siofirst (20/22). So sio5 would be red on HEAD \u2014 CONTRADICTION with the table's HEAD line!\n\nHmm hold on \u2014 wait: 20/22: TWO red (soft1, siofirst) \u2192 20 OK out of 22 run. If sio5 also HALTed, it'd be 19/22 or the reds listed would include sio5. The HEAD line: \"**20/22 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM** (`probe_v3_head.out`: soft1 and siofirst HALT, signal 11...)\". So sio5 PASSED on HEAD. So session B's sio5 does NOT crash HEAD \u2192 it doesn't arm an absent slot with a raised line. Then why does sio5 HALT on M_CALLGUARD?!\n\nUnless \"sio5\" in M_CALLGUARD's red column refers to B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row!! And on M_CALLGUARD, B2's sio5 deliver HALTs \u2014 how? M_CALLGUARD: the CALLS bounded \u2014 if the guard is `if (cpu < d->ncpus)` at... no. Hmm, ALTERNATIVELY: M_CALLGUARD = the four callers' calls are REMOVED for... \"bound the CALLS\" \u2014 the pass-1 F4 alternative: maybe F4 was \"don't call reassert from the store arms at all; instead call it only from the interrupt_assert/deassert callers\"? Or \"bound the calls\" = the walk is called with a bound argument...? I can't pin M_CALLGUARD's exact shape from the packet. Its red rows: sio5, siofirst HALT, 21/23 (2 red, 23 ran \u2014 4 rows lost: the host died mid-session... twice? two HALTs \u2192 two sessions' tails lost: 27 - 23 = 4 lost). siofirst is a session-B row; sio5 \u2014 if it's B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row, then B2's session died at its only row \u2192 B2 contributes 1 HALT + 0 lost (B2 has ONE row!). Then the 4 lost rows are from session B's tail after siofirst. 22 ran: hmm, 27 - 4 = 23 \u2713 (session B tail: 4 rows after siofirst). OK \u2014 so \"sio5\" in M_CALLGUARD's red column = B2's \"sio5 deliver\" row (name abbreviated), HALT. HOW does sio5 deliver HALT on M_CALLGUARD but not on HEAD? On HEAD the walk is unbounded but B2's slot 0 is connected and slots 1..3 have enable 0 \u2192 no crash. On M_CALLGUARD \u2014 the calls bounded... if the bound on the calls is `cpu < d->ncpus` applied PER-CALL... the walk body's INTERRUPT_ASSERT calls guarded by `cpu < d->ncpus` \u2014 that's M_GUARDASSERT-ish (bound on the ASSERT call only \u2192 soft1 HALT \u2014 wait, M_GUARDASSERT: soft1 HALT on a 1-cpu machine: MASK1+SOFT1: walk: slot 1: status&enable \u2260 0, ASSERT call guarded (cpu < 1 \u2192 skip) \u2192 no crash from ASSERT... but then the LATCH: `d->irqActive[1] = 1` still set; NEXT walk (clock): status&enable \u2260 0 \u2192 skip ASSERT again... no crash?! But table says M_GUARDASSERT soft1 HALT. Hmm \u2014 unless the guard is only on the ASSERT call and NOT the DEASSERT: slot 1: first walk: assert skipped, latch = 1. Then SOFT_INT1 CLEAR? soft1 row: store SOFT_INT1 then...? The soft1 row on M_GUARDASSERT HALTs \u2014 maybe the row CLEARS the soft int later \u2192 walk: slot 1: status&enable = 0 now \u2192 else \u2192 `if (irqActive[1]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(cpu_irq[1])` \u2014 UNGUARDED deassert \u2192 NULL \u2192 CRASH. THAT's why ASSERT-only guard crashes at soft1! And \"M_NULLA (ASSERT-only NULL guard) | 21/22 | soft1 HALT\" \u2014 hmm, M_NULLA = ASSERT-side NULL guard: `if (d->cpu_irq[cpu].interrupt_assert) ASSERT` \u2014 a NULL CHECK on the assert pointer only: slot 1: assert skipped (NULL), latch set... then deassert later (clear) \u2192 unguarded \u2192 NULL deassert \u2192 crash at soft1 \u2713 consistent (\"sio5clr did not kill M_NULLA (the host had already died at soft1)\" \u2014 pass-2 note \u2713).\n\nOK so M_GUARDASSERT = bound on the ASSERT call only \u2192 soft1 HALT via the deassert path \u2713 consistent.\n\nM_CALLGUARD = bound at the CALL SITES (the four callers): the store arms' calls bounded/removed \u2192 the SIO callers (3rd and 4th) still call the walk \u2192 hmm, but then B2 on M_CALLGUARD: SIO line 5 \u2192 walk \u2192 slot 0 delivers \u2192 OK, not HALT. STILL contradicts. UNLESS the bound at the call sites is per-slot somehow... OR M_CALLGUARD's shape: `reassert_interrupts` calls REPLACED by a bounded variant at the STORE arms, and the DEVICE callers' calls REMOVED entirely?? Then B2: SIO line 5 \u2192 no walk call \u2192 line 5 never delivered \u2192 sio5 deliver STOP (not HALT). Table says HALT. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 M_CALLGUARD: the calls bounded = `if (ncpus > 1) reassert()` \u2014 on 1-cpu machines NO calls at all?? Then B2: no delivery \u2192 STOP. Not HALT.\n\nI can't reconstruct M_CALLGUARD's exact shape from the packet \u2014 its red rows (sio5, siofirst HALT) are just READ to me. Is there an internal inconsistency? On HEAD, siofirst HALTs; on M_CALLGUARD, siofirst HALTs too \u2713 plausible (both leave the walk unbounded via the SIO caller with an armed absent mask \u2014 so siofirst DOES arm an absent slot + raise a line! But then HEAD's siofirst HALT \u2713 \u2014 and HEAD's sio5 (session B) \u2014 hmm, wait \u2014 if siofirst arms an absent slot's mask and raises the SIO, then session B's sio5 row might be a DIFFERENT shape (MASK0 bit 30 \u2192 connected slot \u2014 passes HEAD \u2713). OK so session B has BOTH: siofirst (absent-slot armed + SIO \u2192 crash on HEAD) and sio5 (slot 0 armed + SIO \u2192 OK on HEAD, the \"sio5\" row maybe = \"the SIO reaches the walk through the 3rd/4th callers\" witness: delivery to slot 0 via device line \u2713 \u2014 wait, but that's what B2's sio5 deliver does! And pass-2 said \"v2 had no such row (deepseek, grok, pass 2)\" \u2014 v2 had no row proving a DEVICE line delivers to a CONNECTED slot. So session B's sio5 row is NOT a device-line-delivery row.", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 360990065066}
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2398:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\pass3_panel_20260906_102412\kimi.txt:50:  - Source of constant: `d->ncpus = devinit->machine->ncpus` captured below the `ncpus > 4` exit. Mutants: wrong source (e.g., `devinit->machine->ncpus` vs something else — there's not much else available). Capture above the exit? On ncpus > 4 the machine exits at init, so capture-above is equivalent on reachable configs... wait — no. If captured above the exit, `d->ncpus` could be 5..256, but DEVINIT exits before the machine runs, so the walk never executes with ncpus > 4. Actually the connect predicate: slots 1..3 connected under ncpus >= 2/3/4, and ncpus > 4 exits. So on any running machine, ncpus ∈ {1,2,3,4}. The capture site (above/below the exit) only matters for... hmm, actually the minimax point in pass 2 was that with capture ABOVE the exit, the exit becomes load-bearing. But behaviorally, both are equivalent because the exit fires before any walk. So capture-site mutants are equivalent — but that's the pass-2 minimax finding that was already handled by moving the capture below. A mutant moving it back above would pass 27/27 but is it a "floor breach"? It's the disclosed design choice — the comment says "Captured BELOW the ncpus > 4 exit so the bound is <= MAX_CPUS by construction, not by that exit." Moving it above reintroduces the reliance on the exit. Hmm — but does any row catch it? No. Is it in the (d) enumeration? The enumeration says "its constant's source". The source is `devinit->machine->ncpus`. A mutant could be `d->ncpus = 4` (hardcoded to 4)? Let's check: on a 1-cpu machine, walk slots 0..3; slots 1..3 unconnected → NULL deref on pending. Session B: MASK1 + SOFT_INT1 on 1 cpu → slot 1 pending → walk reaches slot 1 (since d->ncpus=4) → status & enable... wait, in session B, what happens? Session B (1 cpu): MASK1 <- 0x04000000 and SOFT_INT1 <- 1 stores — on the ORIGINAL code this crashes (that's the bug). With d->ncpus=4 hardcoded, walk covers slots 0..3, slot 1 has software_interrupt_status[1]=1 and enable bit 26 → status&enable true → INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[1]) → NULL deref → HALT. So session B's "survive" row catches hardcode-to-4. Similarly hardcode to 2 or 3: on 1-cpu machine, slot 1 is within bound → SOFT_INT1 store → NULL assert → HALT → caught by session B. Hardcode to 1 = M_HARD1, caught by session C. So constants 1,2,3,4 all caught. M_NOASSIGN (=0) caught. M_MIN2 caught by E. What about `ncpus > 4 ? 4 : ncpus` clamp to 4? That's... equivalent on reachable configs since ncpus ≤ 4 always when running. Equivalent — fine, that's not a floor breach, it's a safe clamp.
