regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2415:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\pass2_panel_20260906_094854\kimi.txt:33:  Q2: floor question — does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the composite (predicate + fields read by body)? The body reads `interrupt_status`, `interrupt_enable[cpu]`, `software_interrupt_status[cpu]`, `irqActive[cpu]`, `cpu_irq[cpu]`. A mutant that e.g. reads `interrupt_enable[0]` instead of `[cpu]`? On 1-cpu rows: soft1 would walk slot 0 only with the fix... wait, mutant floor question is about mutants of the SHIPPED composite — i.e., plausible wrong edits to the fix/walk that survive all 22 rows. The kill table already covers: revert, off-by-one, noassign, hard1, skip1, nulla, nullab (equivalent), latch, guardassert, callguard, noreassert. The question asks about joint cells {slot} x {pending} x {irqActive}: e.g., a mutant that uses `interrupt_enable[0]` for all slots: on 1-cpu machine identical (only slot 0 walked). On 2-cpu rows: cpu1 deliver requires enable[1] = 0x04000000; if the walk used enable[0] for slot 1, enable[0] was never armed in session C... Actually in session C, cpu 0 steps MASK1 <- 0x04000000 (slot 1's mask) and SOFT_INT1 <- 1. MASK0 is not armed in session C. So a mutant reading `interrupt_enable[0]` at slot 1 would see enable 0, not assert cpu 1, and "cpu1 deliver" would STOP → caught. Also `irqActive[cpu]` → a mutant using `irqActive[0]` for all slots? Session C: slot 1 assert sets irqActive[0]=1 instead... then "cpu0 parked" row — cpu 0 parked at `br .`, and nothing pending on slot 0, so no delivery either way; but would cpu1 deliver still fire? Assert is called for slot 1 (status&enable nonzero, irqActive[0]==0 → assert cpu_irq[1], set irqActive[0]=1). Delivery occurs → cpu1 deliver OK. Then subsequent reasserts: slot 1 status&enable still nonzero (soft int pending until read), irqActive[0]==1 now, so no re-assert; slot 0: nothing. Hmm, would anything catch it? "cpu0 parked" checks cpu 0 never enters vector — still true. Actually wait — the DEASSERT: the guest handler at VBR+8 is just a breakpoint, no read of SOFT_INT1 to clear, so pending stays. irqActive[1] stays 0, so every reassert would call ASSERT again (harmless). Would any row catch `irqActive[0]`-for-all? Consider mutant `if (!d->irqActive[0])` in the loop. On 1-cpu rows, identical to correct. On session C: works as analyzed → still delivers. nodeliver ctl: nothing pending → no assert. So that mutant might sweep clean! Hmm, but is it in the (d) enumeration? The composite's fields include irqActive[cpu] — a substitution `irqActive[cpu]`→`irqActive[0]` is a plausible floor mutant. Let me think more carefully later. Actually wait — is there a row where slot 0 is asserted AND slot 1 state matters? Session B delivers slot 0 only (1-cpu). Session C delivers slot 1 while cpu 0 parked. There's no session where both slots have pending bits with different irqActive states... The `slot0 shadow` and `cpu1 shadow` rows — what are they? "slot0 deliver (pc == VBR+8), slot0 shadow" — shadow rows probably assert something about the OTHER slot not firing. If shadow rows check that irqActive of the other slot is untouched... they're observable-based (no vector entry), not register-based.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2416:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\pass2_panel_20260906_094854\kimi.txt:39:  3. `irqActive[cpu]` → `[0]`: as analyzed, likely survives?? Hmm wait — session C: "cpu0 parked" row. After cpu1 delivers and the breakpoint hits, the probe checks cpu 0 is still at `br .`. With the mutant, cpu_irq[1] was asserted — cpu 1 enters vector. Slot 0's walk: nothing pending (MASK0 not armed, SOFT_INT0 not set) → deassert arm: `if (d->irqActive[0])` — after the slot-1 iteration set irqActive[0]=1... wait, order: loop cpu=0 first: slot 0 nothing pending → irqActive[0] is 0 (initial) → stays 0. cpu=1: status&enable nonzero → `if (!d->irqActive[0])` true (0) → assert cpu_irq[1], set irqActive[0]=1. Next reassert: cpu=0: nothing pending, `if (d->irqActive[0])` → TRUE → INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq[0]); irqActive[0]=0. That DEASSERTs cpu 0's irq — but cpu 0's irq was never asserted, so deassert is a no-op on the cpu (clears an already-clear line). No observable. cpu=1: still pending, `!irqActive[0]` true again → assert again, set irqActive[0]=1. So cpu 1's line toggles re-assert each reassert; cpu 0 gets spurious deasserts (no observable since never asserted). Would cpu1 deliver still see the vector entry? Yes — the first assert happens at the SOFT_INT1 store's reassert. So mutant survives all 22 rows? Hmm, wait — is there a row with BOTH slots pending? No. Is there a row where slot 0 is pending and its delivery matters AND slot 1 previously set irqActive? Session B is 1-cpu. So an `irqActive[0]`-for-all-slots mutant seems to sweep clean on the OBSERVABLES... unless the `survive` row or shadow rows read back something. Actually — hold on. Is the loop order per reassert over all cpus? Yes. When does reassert get called in session C after the initial store? The clock tick (line 6) fires periodically while free-running → reasserts. Each one re-asserts slot 1 (fine, already in vector or pending) and deasserts slot 0 (no-op). So yes, M_IRQA0 survives. But wait — is this mutant in scope for Q2? The (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration as quoted: "the predicate `cpu < d->ncpus` and the fields the walk's body reads (interrupt_status, interrupt_enable[cpu], software_interrupt_status[cpu], irqActive[cpu], cpu_irq[cpu])". So yes, irqActive[cpu] is enumerated. The exact edit: `if (!d->irqActive[cpu])` → `if (!d->irqActive[0])` (and the two assignments similarly). Would the row "cpu1 deliver" catch it? No, as analyzed. Hmm, but hold on — does any row check "cpu0 parked" in a scenario where a deassert of cpu0 could... no, deassert of a never-asserted line is silent.
