regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2458:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1129:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:62:ba30e64	2026-08-23	panel_20260823_140419 panel_20260823_213058	#461 both default: arms of the GD-ROM device ended the host process	9of9	SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, every change applied before this receipt. THREE PANELS on one round -- pass 1 (design), pass 2 (diff), pass 3 (final state) -- plus two flagship reviews. All nine seats answered at least one; NO SEAT DOWN at pass 3, and grok was recorded as MID-ANSWER rather than as a failure, which is a distinction this project has mis-made before. *** THE ROUND WAS HELD UNCOMMITTED BY OWNER DECISION ACROSS TWO FABLE USAGE-LIMIT FAILURES, AND THE HOLD PAID FOR ITSELF FOUR TIMES. *** codex returned DO NOT SHIP on pass 2 for a write alias THE FIX WOULD HAVE CREATED: the DMA tail stores through truncating division, so a declined byte write at +0x05 landed in dma_reg[1], the DMA destination, with the same alias reaching length (+0x09) and count (+0x0d). `case 0x18` reads count and enable to arm, then destination and length -- so a guest could arm and AIM a transfer entirely through declined offsets, bypassing every check the implemented cases perform. Pre-fix each of those exited. The round's own comment claimed the opposite. Closed by returning before BOTH tails on any unaligned access, whose soundness is an INJECTIVITY PROOF -- on multiples of 4 the map off -> off/4 is injective, so an aligned offset reaching the arm cannot share an index with any implemented case, because if it did it would BE that case -- and a seat re-derived that proof independently before it was believed. opus5 then built FOUR mutants that scored a full 27/27 against the detector: `& 3` -> `& 1` (a 16-bit store at +0x06 is 2-byte aligned, architecturally legal, and re-opens the alias); the guard restricted to MEM_WRITE (the read half the comment claimed and no row pinned); and each arm keyed into the OTHER window's latch array, one of which additionally writes past the shorter row onto dma_reg[0] -- invisible to ASan, being member-to-member inside one malloc. *** `dma alias`, THE ROW WRITTEN TO PIN THAT VERY REPAIR, WAS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THE FIRST *** because it uses an ODD address that both guard forms drop: a row written from the fix's intent tests the intent, not the boundary. That measurement is what the owner adopted as vacuity-floor clause (d) NULL-REPAIR RED. fable5 found the round's UNDISCHARGED L3 OBLIGATION, which no other seat named across two full panels: `accept8` exists for the control window because three narrowing mutants scored full marks, and the DMA window got the same fatal->survive fix with NO accept row -- delete `case 0x18:` and the DMA trigger dies silently forever, where before this round that deletion was a loud exit on first touch. It also found SEVEN wrong records, every one confirmed by grep or by reading before being acted on; the sharpest was a gate comment boasting its numbers were 'MEASURED at the final row count, not carried over' which was ITSELF carried over and false three ways -- the third generation of the frozen-number defect, inside the sentence memorialising the first two. PASS 3 WAS OWED AND NEARLY MISSED: pass 2 reviewed a 27-ROW detector, and the diff had since gained five rows, the GDROM_UNIMPL_FIRST macro, the injectivity proof, a diagnostic correction and the unaligned guard. Reviewed whole for the first time it produced three real findings: glm found the macro's true constraint (`sizeof(arr) * 8` is element-type-AGNOSTIC so it computes a correct BIT COUNT for any array, while gdrom_unimpl_first indexes `bits[k >> 5]` through a uint32_t* -- a uint8_t[] gives the right count and the wrong indexing, the worst combination because the obvious sanity check passes; codex flagged the same brittleness independently); glm and minimax both asked that the CONTROL window's structural safety be written down; and codex asked for an explicit write-direction guard around the DMA arming, which was CHECKED IN TWO MINUTES rather than judged and found already satisfied 140 lines above (`idata` is declared = 0 and assigned only under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`), so no code change -- but the probe comment now names the real property, because a guard 140 lines from what it protects is correct and hard to see. glm's request for a `& 3` guard on the control arm was DECLINED WITH A REASON: that arm has no store tail, so the guard would be DEAD CODE, and this round already rejected one dead bounds check for exactly that. deepseek was the only seat asked for a new 32/32 mutant that said plainly it could not find one AND enumerated what it had eliminated -- an honest empty result with its search space named, from a packet-fed seat. kimi named four measurements rather than asserting a verdict it had not earned; M4 was checked immediately and confirmed (:749 and :889 are character-identical index expressions, so `return 1` -> `break` has no silent read-path variant). MEASURED: 9/32 pre-fix and 32/32 post-fix, BOTH at 32 rows (pre461f.out) -- the heading had said 8/32 'both MEASURED' seventeen minutes BEFORE that run started, and the carried 8 was wrong as well as unmeasured, which is why the carrier now requires a score claim to name its log. Every row added because a mutant escaped is discharged by a measured kill: m_deldma18 31/32 (dma accept), m_wrcross 31/32 (both windows), m_delalign 29/32 (three rows; clause (d)'s own shape), m_dmarevert 22/31 (floor clause (b) at BOTH fixed sites). Gate 10 PASS at 190 checks run alone, 179 before. Four trees cmp-identical; build rc=0, zero warnings. The 32/32 re-verification after the final comment-only edits held under SEVEN concurrent emulators, which makes it a stronger result than a quiet-machine run.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2459:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1134:GXEMUL-SEC/regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:140:01a0339	2026-09-07	PASS 2 on the 01a0339 diff, run AFTER that SHA existed and BEFORE the next commit, because precommit section F refused the next commit for want of it -- the THIRD time a round has shipped without one, and the gate was right every time.  Panel: preserved at regress/artifacts/armbfxwidth32/pass2_01a0339/ (brief BRIEF.md, with BOTH files' complete diffs inlined -- 6 hunks, COUNTED AND REFUSED-ON at generation time rather than reported, since a `git show <sha> -- <f> | head -N' inlines the commit MESSAGE and has produced zero-hunk briefs here three times in one session).  SEATS: codex, grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (6); the kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED, reserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  *** THE FLOOR QUESTION IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR, WHICH IS WHAT THE STOP CONDITION REQUIRES. *** grok, having read the tree: "no in-rule clause-(d) mutant of `width == 32' or `width < 32' sweeps the 14-row detector except the two definedness escapes already disclosed."  A bare zero does not close a repair chain; a zero naming which cells and which mutant shapes were swept does.  *** AND THE PANEL WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE HEADLINE ROW WHILE I WAS WRONG, WHICH IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS RECEIPT.  All four landed seats that addressed it called `s w32 pc' an ATTRIBUTION CONTROL, NOT A KILL.  I refuted them in the ledger on the ground that `run_word()' zeroes r0 in a fresh emulator, so a non-executing word leaves exactly the pre-fix failure value -- every fact true, conclusion wrong.  grok settled it: a word that never ran leaves r0 = 0, which REDS `s w32' (it wants 0x89abcdef) rather than greening it, so the row cannot produce a FALSE PASS; it distinguishes executed-and-wrong from never-ran, which is attribution, not coverage.  I conflated diagnostic attribution with coverage, and the probe's own row note already said CONTROL.  The ledger refutation is struck in place with the correction beside it. ***  NOTE minimax's STATED PREMISE WAS FALSE ("r0 retains its prior-row value" -- there is no prior-row value) AND ITS CONCLUSION WAS RIGHT.  A wrong reason may accompany a right conclusion; neither credit nor dismissal may rest on the reason alone.  I found the false premise, felt the refutation, and stopped one paragraph short of checking the conclusion.  *** A DEFECT I INTRODUCED IN 01a0339 AND SHIPPED, FOUND BY codex: THE PRODUCT COMMENT WAS LEFT UNGRAMMATICAL IN BOTH HANDLERS. ***  My positional line-range edit replaced line i-1 whole, deleting "`1U' -> `1' regression;", so both X(ubfx) and X(sbfx) read "No row can kill a / UBSAN or a source pin can."  **My own comment-only proof could not catch it**: stripping comments and diffing establishes that no CODE changed and says nothing about whether the PROSE still parses.  I verified the thing I had checked for and not the thing I had broken.  Repaired at both sites, and the repaired sentences were READ IN FULL this time rather than diffed.  *The string-anchored form refuses when ambiguous; the positional form silently eats adjacent text.  Neither is safe alone -- the missing step is reading the RESULT.*  THREE FURTHER codex FINDINGS, ALL VERIFIED BY READING AND ALL FIXED IN-ROUND AS L4: (1) `enc ok' claimed "every planted word" while omitting `sbfx(1,4)' and `sbfx(0,31)' -- the two words the pass-2 rows plant; the CHECK was widened rather than the CLAIM narrowed, since both satisfy the mask so no score moves and the sentence becomes true.  (2) the `pc adv' ROW NOTE still carried the "a bad encoding sets running=0" claim that this very round corrected in the header -- fixed in one place and left in the other, which is the grep-for-siblings rule unlearned.  (3) the round's new prose says "the SBFX rows are exactly the ones whose expected FAILURE value is also EXACTLY ZERO" -- TOO WIDE, caught independently by glm AND grok: UBFX's `w32' also returned exactly 0x0 pre-fix, and only `s w32' has that property among the SBFX rows.  That is the FOURTH false prose claim this round has shipped about its own rows, and the asymmetry argument that motivated adding a pc control was wrong in its stated form even though the row is sound as a control.  TWO SEAT FINDINGS ADJUDICATED AGAINST THE SEAT, both recorded with reasons: deepseek's claimed clause-(d) floor breach `width < 32' -> `width <= 32' is an EQUIVALENT MUTANT -- at width 32 the mask is 0xffffffff so `~mask' is 0 and `x |= ~mask' is a no-op, while `1U << 31' is defined, so nothing observable changes and no UB is re-opened; a mutant that cannot fail is a RESULT, not a breach (the #449 precedent).  PREDICTED, not measured, and owed a build.  And glm's second finding -- that deleting `x |= ~mask' is green against every row -- is refuted by an EXISTING measurement my brief failed to inline: that mutant was built last pass with a pre-registered prediction and measured 10/11, KILLED BY `s mid' ALONE (M_nosext.raw), because `s mid's field 0xde has bit 7 set.  **glm could not have known; the brief withheld it.**  That is the THIRD packet defect of mine this session, all the same shape -- a premise the seats needed and I did not inline.  WHAT IS CARRIED, NOT CLOSED: kimi's warning that "filed is not run" for `ubsanshiftlane' stands -- the two definedness escapes are DISCLOSED on the claim that a UBSAN differential would pin them, and no such receipt exists; codex adds that "unkillable by a guest-value row on principle" is too strong, since non-trapping execution does not determine how a compiler treats UB, so the claim should be scoped to the observed lowering.  codex also proposes a git note on c0c7f55 pointing at the correction, since a reader of the raw commit message cannot discover it -- FILED as a records question, not done here (it changes what the repo publishes).  And codex found one more records error: 01a0339's message says "11 -> 14 rows" when its parent holds 12.  CHAIN DISPOSITION, stated so a later pass can disagree with it explicitly: the floor question closed on grok's zero-with-denominator.  The repairs made in response to THIS pass are four L4 records corrections whose only row change (widening `enc ok') makes an existing claim true without altering the detector's coverage of the fix, so the chain closes here rather than spawning a further pass.  A future pass that disagrees files as a late dissent under the closed-round rule.
