r the len-1 accesses; nothing in the packet states the memcpy's byte order (which byte of pcctwo_reg maps to data[0]) \u2014 but the 0x34 row (packet, measured by panel) shows store-layout and memcpy-layout agree, so no contradiction. Settling fact for the byte-order premise if wanted: the memcpy line itself (its source/dest and len), or a cased-word-store\u2192uncased-byte-read at each of the four positions. Eh \u2014 the 0x34 row partially settles it. Keep it as: not fully settleable which byte data[0] maps to at len 4 relative to register order (endianness of the memcpy), settling fact: the memcpy line's code; but no contradiction arises in the packet.\n\nAlright \u2014 also the instruction: \"A short answer that names one real defect beats a long one.\" I'll be disciplined: verdict line; Q1 (~8 lines); Q2 (2 lines); premises not settleable (4 bullets, one line each). Total ~ 30 lines. \n\nFormat thoughts: headers not needed beyond maybe bold mini-labels; the panel wants verdict line first. Use compact bullets. Label READ/REASONED inline per claim.\n\nFinal check on the verdict line wording \u2014 must contain: the edit, predicted 36/36, the row that stays green and why, and the Q2 answer (data[0] at len 1 yes / len 4 first-of-four, no contradiction). And the runner-up edit mention can go in the body, though a half-clause in the verdict helps survival under truncation. Let me write it:\n\n\"VERDICT: Survivor found \u2014 print data[len-1] instead of data[0] in the READ announce; predicted 36/36 because the only nonzero served value (0x34) is checked at len 1 where data[len-1]==data[0], and the only multi-byte read row loads all zeros (the row I'd expect to catch it \u2014 'a word load there ... (len 4)' \u2014 stays green because 0x00 is byte-position-insensitive). Q2: nothing in the FACTS contradicts the repair \u2014 data[0] is exactly 'the byte delivered' only at len 1; at len 4 it is the first of four bytes served.\"\n\nGood. Body:\n\n**Q1.**\n- The edit (REASONED, from READ row descriptions): in the served announce, `data[0]` \u2192 `data[len-1]`. One token, well-defined at both lens the rows drive (1\u2192data[0], 4\u2192data[3]).\n- READ: pass-2 mutant 1 (\"announce value printed as 0\") passed all 23 old rows \u2192 no old row pins a nonzero served value; READ: mutant 4 (`if (len != 1) exit(1);`) passed all 23 old rows \u2192 every old-row access at an uncased offset is len 1 \u2192 data[len-1]==data[0] on all 23 old rows.\n- READ: of the 13 new rows, the only nonzero served value is the 0x34 row (a len-1 byte read); the only multi-byte read is \"a word load there is served zeros and announced with (len 4)\", and its window is all zeros (writes at uncased offsets are declined and not stored; nothing else seeds it per the row list).\n- REASONED: output is byte-identical on all 36 \u2192 predicted 36/36, with the two-writes read-back and declined-count rows untouched (announce counts and the word \"declined\" don't depend on the printed byte).\n- Row expected to stay green and why: the word-load row \u2014 it is the row built to pin multi-byte serving, and it stays green because every byte it loads is 0x00, so which byte the announce prints is unobservable; a killer row would seed pcctwo_reg[U+3] through a cased arm, then word-load at uncased aligned U and expect \"served 0x00 (len 4)\".\n- Runner-up, same prediction (REASONED): `if (len == 2) exit(1);` \u2014 READ: mutant 4's old 23/23 proves no old row drives len\u22601 at an uncased offset; READ: new rows drive len 1 and len 4 only, and mutant 4's red set on the repaired instrument is EXACTly its built-for (len 4) rows, so no other row (incl. the 43-offset row) drives a non-byte width. REASONED: len 2 never fires \u2192 36/36. This is the direct mirror of pass-2 mutant 4 with the width the repair never probed.\n\n**Q2 (one line).** READ: at len 1, data[0] is exactly the byte the pre-switch memcpy served, and the 0x34 row confirms store-layout \u2192 memcpy \u2192 announce end-to-end; REASONED: at len 4 four bytes are delivered and the announce prints only data[0] (the byte at the accessed offset), so \"(the byte delivered)\" is literally true only at len 1 \u2014 an under-reporting log line, not a serving/storing/return defect; nothing in the FACTS contradicts the repair.\n\n**Premises I cannot settle (each with the single settling fact).**\n1. READ/REASONED packet-internal ambiguity: \"a word load there\" \u2014 if \"there\" is the word-store's own offset, the load is latch-silent and that row would be red on BASE, contradicting the packet's 36/36; the packet is only consistent if \"there\" is a second offset. (This is a wording defect in the packet, not the code.)\n2. Fold injectivity: whether the 43 offsets fold to 43 distinct bits in 0..63. A collision would not make the emulator wrong (the latch gates only the announce; reads are still served by the memcpy, writes still not stored) but would change the 43-row's announce counts. Settling fact: the list of the 43 offsets (or the fold expression and the max offset).\n3. Whether a cased write can seed the last byte of an aligned uncased word window (decides whether the data[len-1] edit is a real defect or an equivalent mutant). Settling fact: the cased arms' offsets vs the 43.\n4. Whether the guest can present len==2 to this handler (same question for the runner-up edit). Settling fact: whether the CPU/bus layer ever calls this handler with len 2 (e.g., a guest halfword access to an uncased offset).\n5. Whether any of the 23 old rows asserts the declined announce's VALUE \u2014 if none does, \"declined value printed as 0\" (write-side twin of pass-2 mutant 1) is a third survivor. Settling fact: the old rows' expected-output strings.\n\nHmm, 5 premises \u2014 is that padding? Each is one line and each is genuinely unsettled and material. Keep. Maybe merge 3 and 4 style. Fine.\n\nAlso I should double check the claim \"mutant 4's red set on the repaired instrument is EXACTly its built-for (len 4) rows\" \u2014 packet: \"all twelve mutants killed with every predicted score and red set EXACT; each of the six pass-2 mutants scored 23/23 on the OLD instrument and red on the repaired one on the row built for it.\" So red set EXACT = predictions matched; the packet states the six are red on their built-for rows. It doesn't explicitly say mutant 4's red set is ONLY the len-4 rows \u2014 but the predictions were the repairer's and EXACT; the described built-for row for mutant 4 is the (len 4) pair. For my len==2 argument I need: no row drives len 2 at uncased offsets. From the packet: old rows proven len-1-only (mutant 4 old 23/23). New rows: described as byte and word accesses; none described as halfword. If a new row drove len 2, mutant 4 (len\u22601 exit) would be red on it too \u2014 the packet doesn't forbid that (its red set could include that row, predicted EXACT). Hmm! So strictly: a new len-2 row could exist and be red under mutant 4 and green under BASE \u2014 my len==2 edit would then be caught by it. The packet's 13-row list doesn't mention one, and I'm told to reason \"from the inlined text alone\" \u2014 the list is the packet's authoritative description of the 13 rows. I'll ground on: READ: the 13 rows as listed contain no halfword access; REASONED: therefore len==2 never fires. And add the honest caveat: if the packet's list is not exhaustive of the 13 rows' traffic, this prediction weakens (but the listed 13 are exhaustive per \"The REPAIR added 13 rows (36):\" \u2014 the colon introduces the list of 13). Count the listed rows: (1) two reads at one offset announce once; (2) a read then a write announce once; (3) A,B,A announces twice; (4) after two writes read-back 0x0; (5) cased-word byte served 0x34; (6) word store at uncased aligned offset reads back 0x0, announced once (len 4); (7) word load there served zeros, announced (len 4); (8) all 43 offsets written+read once; (9) \"declined\" counted. That's 9 items \u2014 but 13 rows