n unlisted row drove len 2, mutant 4 would be red there too \u2014 consistent with \"red set EXACT\" only if predicted. The packet doesn't enumerate red sets per mutant beyond \"the row built for it\". So: cannot settle. I must name it: the premise \"the 9-item list exhausts the 13 rows' traffic (len 1 and len 4 only)\" \u2014 settling fact: the 13 rows' actual drive scripts. For data[len-1], same caveat: an unlisted row could do a multi-byte read with mixed bytes (e.g., seeding then word-load). Hmm \u2014 but would that be likely? The repairer built rows for the six mutants; the extra ~4 rows could be the \"declined counted\" variants etc. I'll carry the caveat for both predictions: both predictions assume the 9-item description exhausts the 13 rows' traffic; 13\u22129=4 rows of undescribed traffic is the packet's own gap \u2014 the settling fact is the drive script of those rows.\n\nGood \u2014 that's an honest, packet-grounded audit and exactly the kind of catch this seat is for: the arithmetic 23+13=36 checks, but the itemized list has 9 items, so 4 added rows are undescribed; predictions are conditional on those 4 rows not probing (a) halfwords, (b) mixed-byte multi-reads, (c) declined-value text. Actually \u2014 hmm, wait: maybe some items ARE multiple rows. E.g., item 6 \"a word store ... reads back 0x0 and is announced once with (len 4)\" could be 2 rows (one asserting read-back, one asserting the announce), item 7 one row, item 5 maybe 2 rows (store through cased arm + read announce)... 13 = 9 + 4 could be fully absorbed by multi-row items. Either way: unsettled. Name it once, generically: \"the mapping of the 9 listed behaviors onto 13 rows leaves up to 4 rows whose traffic/assertions the packet doesn't state; both survivor predictions are conditional on those rows containing no halfword access and no mixed-byte multi-byte read at an uncased offset and no declined-value assertion. Settling fact: the 13 rows' drive scripts/expected outputs.\"\n\nNow, given this gap, which survivor do I LEAD with? The data[len-1] one is less exposed (a mixed-byte multi-read row would need cased-seeding adjacent to an uncased aligned offset \u2014 an elaborate row; and any len-1 assertion is immune). The len==2 one is more exposed (any stray halfword row kills it). Also \"smallest\": data[0]\u2192data[len-1] is the smaller diff. Lead with data[len-1]. \u2713.\n\nAlso reconsider: is there any survivor with NO exposure to the 4-row gap? A no-op/equivalent edit (e.g., `& 63` \u2192 `% 64`) passes trivially but is not a \"real defect\" \u2014 the panel wants a defect-shaped edit presumably; also an equivalent mutant isn't a finding about the detector's power... actually an equivalent mutant DOES pass all 36 rows by definition, and the question literally asks for \"the smallest one-line edit ... that you predict passes ALL 36 rows\" \u2014 an equivalent edit technically answers it, but it's a cheap answer; the panel's pass-2 history (mutant 6: exit at an undriven offset) shows they accept \"unreached code\" mutants as legitimate findings about detector coverage. data[len-1] is behavior-changing in reachable states (probably), len==2 likewise. Lead with data[len-1]. \u2713.\n\nOne more possible even-smaller edit: `data[0]` \u2192 `data[0] & 0xff`? No-op. `0x%02x` \u2192 `0x%02x` with data[0]... `served` announce prints data[0]; what about printing `pcctwo_reg[ra]` instead of data[0]? For len-1 reads, identical (memcpy copied pcctwo_reg[ra]... assuming memcpy copies pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] \u2192 data). Identical output \u2192 survives, but it's near-equivalent (differs only if the memcpy did something else, e.g., byte-swapped \u2014 unknown). Skip; data[len-1] is the crisp one.\n\nAlso check: could the 0x34 row involve a len>1 read? \"a byte the guest wrote ... is served and announced as served 0x34\" \u2014 a byte read, len 1 \u2713 (\"a byte ... is served\").\n\nOK, also make sure about the claim \"writes at uncased offsets are declined and not stored\" \u2014 READ from FACTS \u2713. And the word-load window being all zeros: the row says \"served zeros\" \u2014 READ \u2713 (the packet asserts the expectation). My reasoning that data[len-1] prints 0x00 there: data[3] is one of the four zeros \u2713 REASONED.\n\nNow write the final answer. Verdict line first, tight. Use \"READ:\"/\"REASONED:\" labels. Keep total moderate. Also the seat instruction says name any premise you cannot settle \u2014 I have: (i) the 9-vs-13 row accounting gap (biggest, conditions both predictions), (ii) fold injectivity, (iii) \"there\" ambiguity (packet-internal, resolvable), (iv) len-2 reachability (for runner-up), (v) declined-value old-row assertion, (vi) whether cased writes can seed data[len-1] (defect vs equivalent for the primary). I'll compress: present (iii) under premise audit as an internal-consistency note; (i)+(v) merged-ish but distinct; keep each to one line.\n\nAlso \u2014 should double-check the \"two of them MEASURED\" in pass 2 \u2014 just flavor (two of the six pass-2 mutants were actually run). No bearing.\n\nAnd \"Gate: 36/36 and 251/251, 0 FAIL\" \u2014 251 other tests pass on BASE; my survivor prediction is about the detector rows only. One clause noting the survivor question concerns only the 36 rows, as asked.\n\nDraft final:\n\n---\n\n**VERDICT:** A survivor exists \u2014 in the READ announce, change `data[0]` to `data[len-1]`; I predict 36/36, because the only row that pins a nonzero served value (0x34) is a len-1 read where `data[len-1]` *is* `data[0]`, and the only multi-byte read row loads all zeros, so the printed byte is position-insensitive \u2014 that word-load row is the one I expect to stay green. Nothing in the FACTS contradicts the repair; `data[0]` is exactly \"the byte delivered\" only at len 1 \u2014 at len 4 it is the first of four bytes served.\n\n**Q1 \u2014 the edit and the green row.**\n- READ: the row list pins the served announce's value in exactly one place \u2014 \"served 0x34\", a len-1 byte read of a byte a cased word arm wrote. Every other read assertion is either all-zeros (\"word load there is served zeros ... (len 4)\"), an announce count, or a read-back value.\n- READ: pass-2 mutant 1 (\"announce value printed as 0\") passed all 23 old rows, and mutant 4 (`if (len != 1) exit(1);`) also passed all 23 \u2014 so the old rows drive only len-1 accesses at uncased offsets and never pin a nonzero served value.\n- REASONED: therefore `fatal(\"... served 0x%02x ...\", data[len-1])` is well-defined at both lens the rows drive (1 \u2192 data[0], 4 \u2192 data[3]) and prints byte-identical output on every described row \u2192 predicted 36/36. (The runner-up, same prediction: `if (len == 2) exit(1);` \u2014 the exact mirror of pass-2 mutant 4 at the width no row drives; real only if the guest can issue halfwords here, which the packet doesn't say.)\n- The row I expect to stay green and why: \"a word load there is served zeros and announced with (len 4).\" It's the row built to pin multi-byte serving, but all four bytes are 0x00 (uncased writes are declined/not stored, and per the row nothing else seeds that window), so whichever byte the announce prints, the line reads \"served 0x00 (len 4)\". The 0x34 row also stays green \u2014 at len 1 there is no byte-position choice. The missing killer row: seed a byte at U+3 through a cased arm, word-load at uncased aligned U, expect \"served 0x00 (len 4)\".\n\n**Q2 \u2014 one line.** No contradiction: at len 1 `data[0]` is exactly the byte the pre-switch memcpy served, and the 0x34 row confirms store\u2192memcpy\u2192announce end-to-end; at len 4 four bytes are delivered and the announce prints only `data[0]` (the byte at the accessed offset), so the FACTS' parenthetical \"(the byte delivered)\" is literally true only for len 1 \u2014 an under-reporting log line, not a serving/storing/return defect.\n\n**Premises I cannot settle (and the single fact that would settle each).**\n1. The packet's arithmetic: 13 rows were added but the list itemizes 9 behaviors, so up to ~4 rows of traffic/assertions are undescribed. Both predictions above are conditional on those rows containing no halfword at an uncased offset and no mixed-byte multi-byte read. Settling fact: the 13 rows' drive scripts and expected strings.\n2. Whether any of the 23 old rows asserts the declined announce's *value*; if none does, \"declined value printed as 0\" (write-side twin of pass-2 mutant 1) is a third survivor. Settling fact: the old rows' expected strings.\n3. Whether the 43 offsets fold to 43 distinct latch bits. A collision would not make the emulator wrong (the latch gates only the log; the memcpy still serves, writes still aren't stored) but would shift the 43-row's announce counts. Settling fact: the list of the 43 offsets (or the fold expression plus the max offset).\n4. Whether a cased write can land at U+3 of an aligned uncased offset \u2014 this decides whether the `data[len-1]` edit is an observable defect or an equivalent mutant. Settling fact: the cased arms' offsets relative to the 43.\n\n**Packet-internal note (READ + REASONED):** \"a word load *there*\" is ambiguous: if \"there\" is the word-store's own offset, the load is latch-silent and that row would be red on BASE, contradicting the packet's own 36/36; the packet is consistent only if \"there\" is a second, fresh offset.\n\n---\n\nHmm, this is fairly long but each element is load-bearing. The seat instructions: \"Do not pad. A short answer that names one real defect beats a long one.\" I think I should trim. Which parts can go?\n\n- The runner-up len==2: keep one clause (it's a second real find, cheap to state).\n- Premise 4 could merge into Q1's caveat... it's the \"is it a real defect\" question for my primary edit \u2014 keep, one line.\n- The packet-internal note on \"there\": this is a genuine audit catch \u2014 keep, one line.\n- Premise 3 (fold injectivity): keep one line.\n- The \"missing killer row\" sentence: keep, it's the \"why\".\n- Maybe cut the explicit mutant-1/old-rows grounding to one line.\n\nAlso the label discipline: mark READ/REASONED on the key claims. I've done that. No MEASURED labels \u2014 careful: when citing Table 3 I say READ (\"the packet states Table 3...\"). Avoid the word MEASURED entirely except maybe quoting the packet's own use \u2014 safer to avoid entirely. Hmm, the packet itself says \"(measured)\" for Table 3; I can say \"READ (Table 3, as given)\". Fine.\n\nTighten Q1 bullets. Also double-check the verdict line contains: edit \u2713, prediction \u2713, row expected to stay green + why \u2713, Q2 answer \u2713. \n\nOne more re-read of the question: \"Name the smallest one-line edit to the arm described above that you predict passes ALL 36 rows, and the row you expect to stay green and why -- or state that you cannot find one and which cell you checked. Then: does anything in the FACTS contradict the repair being right (is data[0] always the byte delivered)? One line each.\"\n\n\"One line each\" \u2014 the two sub-answers should each be ~one line. My Q1 is necessarily longer because I must show grounding; but I can put the one-line answer at the top of Q1 and the support after. The verdict line already carries both one-liners. Then support sections. I think that's the right structure: verdict line (the two one-line answers), then support/audit, then unsettled premises.\n\nAlso \u2014 the seat instruction wants me to audit premises AS STATED and catch derivations that only work if you already believe the author. The \"there\" ambiguity is exactly that. The 9-vs-13 accounting is another. The fold-injectivity is another (\"every one of the 43 offsets ... announce once\" only follows if the fold is injective \u2014 the FACTS don't state the offsets). Good coverage.\n\nAlso one more derivation check: \"each of the six pass-2 mutants scored 23/23 on the OLD instrument and red on the repaired one on the row built for it\" \u2014 for mutant 5 (`|=`\u2192`=`), is it red ONLY on A,B,A? Under `=`, the 43-row (if driven write-then-read per offset, one offset at a time) passes as I showed. Under `=`, \"read then a write announce once\": read announces (latch=A), write announces (latch=A... wait, write at the SAME offset? \"a read then a write\" \u2014 same offset? \"a read then a write announce once\" \u2014 probably same offset: read announces, latch=A; write: latch has A \u2192 silent \u2192 total 1 \u2713 passes under mutant 5 too \u2713. \"two reads at one offset\": read1 announces latch=A, read2 silent \u2192 1 \u2713 passes. \"two writes read-back\": write1 announces latch=A, write2 silent, read silent \u2713 passes. 0x34: single read \u2713. Word rows: single store + latched read-back \u2713. So mutant 5 red only on A,B,A \u2713 consistent with \"the row built for it\" being the only red \u2014 internally consistent \u2713. Similarly mutant 3 (latch on writes only): \"two reads\" \u2192 2 announces \u2192 red \u2713; \"read then write\" \u2192 read announces, write announces \u2192 2 \u2192 red as well! So mutant 3 is red on TWO rows. The packet says each pass-2 mutant is red \"on the row built for it\" \u2014 not \"only on\" \u2014 consistent \u2713. But \"red set EXACT\" \u2014 the repairer's prediction would have included both rows; fine, consistent. OK.\n\nAnd mutant 2 (store on latched path): red rows: \"after two writes read-back 0x0\" \u2713 (write2 stores \u2192 read-back \u2260 0). Also 43-row: write1 announces (no store... wait mutant 2 stores only on LATCHED path: write1 unlatched \u2192 announce, no store; read1: latched \u2192 silent, no store (reads don't store); so 43-row passes \u2713. Word-store row: store1 announces, read-back latched-silent, not stored (only one store) \u2192 reads 0x0 \u2713 passes. So mutant 2 red only on the two-writes row \u2713 consistent.\n\nMutant 1 (served value printed as 0): red on 0x34 row only (other served announces are 0x00 anyway) \u2713 consistent.\n\nMutant 6 (exit at 0x06): red on 43-row only (assuming 0x06 among the 43 and no other row drives 0x06 \u2014 pass 2 said \"an offset no row drove\"; the repair's rows: do any of the other 12 rows drive 0x06? Possibly (A,B,A at arbitrary offsets) \u2014 if A or B were 0x06, mutant 6 red there too \u2014 the packet says red \"on the row built for it\", again non-exclusive \u2713.\n\nMutant 4: red on the word rows \u2713.\n\nAll internally consistent. Great \u2014 the packet's Table 3 is coherent with the row list under the natural assignments. I can state briefly that the row list and Table 3 are mutually consistent (one line), since auditing premises includes confirming derivations follow. Maybe skip to save space \u2014 or one clause: \"The pass-2 kill assignments are internally consistent (I re", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 451693368307}
