premise says: \"`data` was filled by the memcpy above the switch (or zero-filled on the #119 end-span path, where `data[0]` is then truthfully 0x00).\"\n\nQuestion 1 asks: is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received (memcpy path and end-span zero-fill path)?\n\nKey question: is `data[0]` the byte served for a WRITE? For a write, no byte is \"served\" to the guest \u2014 the guest sends a byte. But the read announce only prints on reads. The write announce prints `idata`.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 the memcpy above the switch. The typical GXemul device access pattern: the device's access function receives `data` pointer. For writes, data contains what the guest wrote; for reads, the emulator reads from the device's array into data and then serves it to the guest. Hmm, actually in GXemul, `dev_pcc2` style access functions typically have a signature like:\n\n```c\nvoid dev_pcc2_access(struct cpu *cpu, struct memory *mem, uint64_t relative_addr, int writeflag, ...)\n{\n    uint8_t data[4] = ...;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but actually in GXemul (the real gxemul), device access functions often do:\n\n```c\nsize_t rs = ...;\nif (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) memcpy(..., data, rs);\nelse { memcpy(data, ..., rs); }\n```\n\nThe packet says: \"READ keeps the byte the memcpy served\" \u2014 so the memcpy serves reads from `d->pcctwo_reg` into `data` before the switch. So on a read, `data[0]` is the byte from the array \u2014 which is the byte the guest receives. On the end-span zero-fill path (#119), data is zero-filled and data[0] is 0x00, which is the byte the guest receives (truthfully).\n\nNow, is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received on a read?\n\nSub-issues:\n\n1. **Endianness / wide reads.** If the read is `len > 1` (e.g., len 4), the memcpy would copy `len` bytes from the array into `data`. Then `data[0]` is only the first byte. For a little-endian guest, a 4-byte read at offset X returns bytes X..X+3, and `data[0]` is byte X \u2014 the low byte. The comment in the diff says \"a wide read is labelled\" via `(len %d)`. So the announce prints only data[0] but labels the length. Is data[0] \"the byte the guest received\"? For a wide read, the guest received 4 bytes; data[0] is one of them (which one depends on endianness and the memcpy implementation \u2014 if memcpy copies from `&d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` into `data`, then data[0] = array[relative_addr] regardless of endianness, and the guest's received value depends on endianness interpretation). Actually if the memcpy is `memcpy(data, &d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr], len)`, then data[0] = array byte at relative_addr. On a little-endian guest, the guest's loaded word's low byte is data[0]. So data[0] is the byte at the accessed offset, which is arguably \"the byte at the offset\" rather than \"the byte the guest received\" for a wide read. But the announce says \"served 0x%02x\" with data[0] and now labels len, so it's the byte at the offset \u2014 disclosed by the len label. The premise says \"a wide read is labelled\", which handles this.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 for a WIDE read, is the memcpy path even taken? The premise mentions \"#119 end-span path\" where data is zero-filled. That suggests there's a check: if the access spans beyond the register array (relative_addr + len > PCC2_SIZE?), data is zero-filled. #119 presumably is a line number or an issue number. The packet says \"zero-filled on the #119 end-span path, where `data[0]` is then truthfully 0x00\".\n\n2. **The tick race.** Premise 2 (repair to the product): the read announce prints `data[0]` \u2014 the byte actually SERVED \u2014 instead of reloading `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]`, \"because the SIGALRM tick rewrites the counter interiors between the memcpy and the announce\". This is codex's accepted disposition #7: the read announce RELOADS the array after the response was copied \u2014 a PRODUCT defect for the twelve interiors the tick rewrites. Fixed: data[0].\n\nNow \u2014 is there a hidden problem: on the LATCHED path, the announce fires only once per offset (the latch). The latch bit is set BEFORE the announce (look at the diff: `d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << relative_addr;` then the announce). So the first access announces, subsequent accesses don't. The `sameoff val` row tests the VALUE channel on the latched path: same offset, first access (announce), then a read-back that reads the array... wait, let me re-read.\n\n\"`sameoff val` (wants 0x0, reads 0x5a)\" \u2014 for mutant M_BACKED2 (member 6 on the LATCHED path, i.e., the write actually stores idata to the array on the latched path?). Hmm, M_O2 is \"member 6 on the LATCHED path\" \u2014 i.e., the mutant makes the write a backed store (stores to the array) instead of declining, on the path after the latch. Then `sameoff val` does: write at offset (announce fires, latch set), then read back at the same offset \u2014 the read sees the stored value 0x5a instead of 0x0 (the array default/current). So the row detects a backed store.\n\n3. **Is data[0] correct on the memcpy path?** The question is whether the memcpy happens BEFORE the switch for reads. Premise 2 says \"`data` was filled by the memcpy above the switch\". READ: \"data was filled by the memcpy above the switch (or zero-filled on the #119 end-span path...)\". So on a normal read, data[0] = the byte from the array at the time of the memcpy \u2014 the byte served. On the end-span path, data[0] = 0x00, and that's what the guest receives. So yes, data[0] is the byte served, per the packet's own premises.\n\nBut wait \u2014 can I verify from the diff that the memcpy is above the switch? No \u2014 the diff only shows the arm. The premise asserts it. I should flag: \"the memcpy above the switch fills `data` before the arm\" is READ (asserted in premises 1/2) but not verifiable from the packet's diff; the single fact that would settle it is the lines above the switch in dev_pcc2.c (the memcpy and the end-span zero-fill). I cannot check it. The packet explicitly says premises are \"each a QUESTION if you can check it\" \u2014 I can't, so I name it.\n\nAlso: is `data` a `uint8_t` array? If `data` is, e.g., `uint64_t data` or an int, `data[0]` would be a compile error for a non-array. The original read announce printed `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` with `%02x`. The new prints `data[0]`. If data were a scalar, the diff wouldn't compile \u2014 but the packet says each mutant was compile-checked before the table (12/12), and BASE was rebuilt; the gate 14 ran with 36 green. The repair itself, though \u2014 was BASE (the repaired product) compile-checked? \"each mutant compile-checked before the table (12/12)\" \u2014 mutants, not necessarily BASE. But gate 14 ran green 36, which implies the repaired build compiled and ran. Gate 14 log: \"pcc2 default rows emitted 36, green 36; GATE_RC=0\" \u2014 READ. That's on the repaired build presumably. So the repaired product compiles and its announce format works. Good.\n\nHmm, but one subtle thing: `data[0]` \u2014 in GXemul, the access function signature is typically:\n\n```c\nvoid dev_pcc2_access(struct cpu *cpu, struct memory *mem, uint64_t relative_addr, int writeflag, unsigned char *data, size_t len, void *extra)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in GXemul the convention is `unsigned char *data` passed in, and devices read/write through it. Let me recall... In gxemul's `dev_pcc2.c`, the access function is:\n\n```c\nDEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)\n{\n    struct pcc2_data *d = extra;\n    uint64_t d0 = 0, d1 = 0, d2 = 0, d3 = 0;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. The packet's premise says \"data was filled by the memcpy above the switch\" \u2014 implying data is a local array filled by memcpy from the register array for reads, or from the incoming data for writes. Actually wait \u2014 re-read premise 2: \"`data` was filled by the memcpy above the switch (or zero-filled on the #119 end-span path, where `data[0]` is then truthfully 0x00).\"\n\nHmm \u2014 for a WRITE, the memcpy would fill data from the guest's written bytes (so `data[0]` = the byte written). For a READ, the memcpy fills data from the array (serving). On the end-span path (offset + len beyond array?), zero-filled.\n\nActually, hold on. If the memcpy fills `data` for BOTH reads and writes \u2014 for a read it copies FROM the array TO data (serving), for a write it copies FROM the guest's incoming data TO data (or directly to the array?). But the fix says writes to the default arm are DECLINED, not stored. So the memcpy for writes must NOT store to the array \u2014 the store to the array presumably happens in the cased arms (each cased arm does the store) or after the switch (but the default arm breaks out without storing). Hmm.\n\nActually the original GXemul pattern for devices is:\n\n```c\nDEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)\n{\n    struct pcc2_data *d = extra;\n    uint64_t d0 = 0, d1 = 0, ...;\n    ...\n    /*  Read from/write to the device:  */\n    if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {\n        /*  store into local d0..d3 from data  */\n    } else {\n        /*  load into data from d0..d3  */\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut this fork clearly has a memcpy above the switch and a switch on relative_addr with a default arm. The packet describes it. I can't verify. I'll name the premise.\n\nOK here's the crux for Question 1: **\"which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\"**\n\nThe brief explicitly hints there's a wrong accepted disposition among #1\u2013#10. Let me scrutinize each:\n\n1. M_O1 \u2014 read announce value unpinned; row `srvd val text`; product prints data[0]. Seems fine.\n\n2. M_O2 \u2014 disposition on latched path unpinned; row `sameoff val`. Fine.\n\n3. grok A \u2014 latch SET conditioned on writes; rows `latch rd rd`, `latch rd wr`. Fine.\n\n4. grok B \u2014 `len != 1` re-kill; rows `wide wr`, `wide rd`. Note the packet says \"The pass-1 PRETABLE's own 'byte-and-wide' sentence had no row \u2014 a CLAIM->ROW miss\". Fine.\n\n5. codex \u2014 `|=` -> `=`; row `latch aba`. Fine.\n\n6. fable5 \u2014 re-kill at unsampled offset; inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows). Hmm \u2014 \"inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways\". Wait, premise 1 says 43 offsets reach the arm: 18 named-unimplemented, 12 counter interiors, 13 fork-header-unnamed. The rows: \"inword wr at 12/13\" (from PRETABLE: \"`43 offsets` -> every one now written and read (inword/unnamed wr at 12/13, + inword rd, unnamed rd)\"). So inword = the 12 counter interiors, unnamed = the 13 fork-header ones. 12+13=25, plus 18 named = 43. OK.\n\n7. codex \u2014 the read announce RELOADS the array after the response was copied \u2014 a PRODUCT defect; fixed data[0]. Also \"makes 'the array's default, not a computed value' false for those twelve \u2014 corrected in comment and docstring.\"\n\nWait \u2014 \"corrected in comment and docstring\". The diff shows the comment corrected. A docstring? The diff shows no docstring change. Hmm \u2014 premise 2 says \"Three comment sentences corrected\". The disposition #7 says \"corrected in comment and docstring\". Is there a docstring? The diff only shows comment changes. If there's a docstring elsewhere (e.g., in the killtable artifact or a header comment), it's not in the diff. Hmm \u2014 \"the repair to the product is confined to the arm\" \u2014 premise 2 says both announces gain (len %d), read announce prints data[0], three comment sentences corrected. The diff shows exactly: two comment sentences in the first hunk (\"default, not a computed value\" and \"no guest reaches\" and \"four arms up\" \u2014 that's three) and the announces. Wait: first hunk has three sentence changes: (a) \"the array's default, said so -- not a computed value\" -> \"the array's current byte, said so -- for a counter interior that is the tick's value, not a default\"; (b) \"no guest reaches these offsets\" -> \"no inspected OpenBSD driver path reaches them\"; (c) \"four arms up\" -> \"above\". Yes, three. So premise 2 matches the diff. Good.\n\nBut #7 says \"corrected in comment and docstring\" \u2014 docstring not in the diff. Is the \"docstring\" the product comment? Possibly they conflate. Minor.\n\n8. The announce under-describes a wide access -> `(len %d)` in both announces; rows `wide wr msg`, `wide rd srvd` pin the label. Fine.\n\n9. L4 corrections. Fine.\n\n10. opus5's safety sentence: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress\" \u2014 adopted as the safety sentence of the record.\n\nIs #10 wrong? Consider: the old code called exit(1) on ANY access reaching the default arm (both reads and writes) \u2014 wait, no. Let me re-read. The fix: \"the `default:` arm no longer calls `exit(1)`\". So previously, any guest access to any of the 43 offsets killed the host. Now, reads keep the byte and writes are declined, with a latched announce. The safety sentence: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"no boot that works today can regress\". Today's code (pass-2 product, i.e., pre-repair? or pre-fix?) \u2014 wait, the repair diff is the pass-2-product -> pass-3 product. The FIX (pass 1) removed exit(1). The REPAIR (this diff) is on top. The safety sentence presumably was about the FIX, not the repair. \"where the old code called exit(1)\" \u2014 the old code being the original. If the original called exit(1) at these offsets, then no boot that works today (with the original code) can reach this arm at all (it would have died). So adding output/behavior at the arm can't regress a boot that works today. Hmm, but wait \u2014 the repair diff also CHANGES the read announce from a reload of the array to data[0] \u2014 that changes output, but only announce text, not behavior. And the write announce adds (len %d). Announces are fatal() \u2014 does fatal() kill the host? \"One latched fatal() per offset per instance, the house `(once)' shape\". Hmm \u2014 in GXemul, `fatal()` prints AND exits (fatal is \"print and exit\"). Wait, actually in GXemul, `fatal()` prints a message and calls exit(1). There's also `debug()` and `debugmsg()`. Hmm, but the packet says \"a READ keeps the byte... one latched fatal() per offset per instance\" \u2014 implying fatal() here does NOT kill, or does it?\n\nHold on. Let me re-read premise 1: \"the `default:` arm no longer calls `exit(1)`; a READ keeps the byte the memcpy served; a WRITE is DECLINED...; one latched `fatal()` per offset per instance\". So the arm now calls fatal() (which announces) instead of exit(1). In GXemul, fatal() IS exit \u2014 `fatal()` in gxemul/emul.c: prints and exit(1). Hmm, but \"the house `(once)` shape\" suggests the announce prints once and execution CONTINUES. If fatal() exits, then \"latched fatal() per offset per instance\" would be a kill per offset \u2014 that can't be, because the rows show sessions continuing after the announce (latch rd rd, latch rd wr \u2014 two announces counted, \"want 1, print 2\", etc.).\n\nWait \u2014 \"grok A latch SET only on writes -> `latch rd rd`, `latch rd wr` (want 1, print 2)\". So the expected count of announces is 1 (latched), and mutant A prints 2. If fatal() killed the host, printing 2 would be impossible. So in this fork, fatal() must be a non-fatal announce (or the fork redefined fatal, or the announce is debug/fatal-without-exit). Hmm, this is GXEMUL-SEC, a security fork \u2014 maybe they have a \"fatal()\" that's a print. Actually the phrase \"the house `(once)` shape\" and \"PSCALEADJ's UNLATCHED print\" (pcc2pscaleflood) suggests the house pattern is fatal(\"[ ... (once) ]\\n\") and it does NOT exit \u2014 it's an announce. OK, in stock gxemul, dev_pcc2.c's unimplemented offsets do:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n    if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {\n        fatal(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented write to offset 0x%x: 0x%x ]\\n\", ...);\n    } else {\n        fatal(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented read from offset 0x%x ]\\n\", ...);\n    }\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in gxemul's dev_pcc2.c there IS a default case that prints something like \"pcc2: unimplemented access to...\" \u2014 and in gxemul fatal() exits. Hmm, no wait \u2014 I think in gxemul, `fatal()` is defined in include/misc.h as a printf-like that then exits. And dev_pcc2.c in stock gxemul... Let me think. Stock gxemul dev_pcc2.c has cases for many offsets and a default that... I believe stock gxemul's pcc2 default case prints a debug message, e.g. `fatal(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented ...\")`. And in gxemul, fatal() calls exit. Hmm, but then stock gxemul would kill on unimplemented pcc2 access \u2014 that's plausible, actually. The premise says the fork's pre-fix code called exit(1) in the default arm (rung-3 witness: \"A guest ld.bu or st.b at any of them was a host kill\"). And the fix replaced exit(1) with a latched fatal() that does NOT kill. So this fork's fatal() in this context is non-killing, OR they use a different print. The packet consistently treats fatal() as an announce that continues (\"One latched fatal() per offset per instance, the house `(once)` shape \u2014 unlike PSCALEADJ's UNLATCHED print\"). The detector counts announces per session and expects the session to continue. So within the packet's world, fatal() here announces and continues. That's internally consistent... unless it isn't, but the packet says MEASURED results exist (36/36 etc.), and those are READ facts about the detector's behavior. Fine.\n\nNow #10: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress\". Scrutiny: the arm now (post-fix, pre-repair) prints an announce and continues. The repair changes the announce TEXT and removes an array RELOAD. Could the repair regress a working boot? The announce is output only; the array reload in the OLD read announce \u2014 `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` \u2014 is a pure read of the array, side-effect free (presumably). So no. But the claim as stated is about the FIX: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1)\". Is that true of the FIX? The fix: reads keep the byte (previously exit(1)); writes declined (previously exit(1)). The arm now calls fatal() \u2014 if fatal() in this fork is print-and-continue, then the arm adds output where exit(1) was. \"no boot that works today can regress\" \u2014 a boot that works today (on the pre-fix code) never reaches this arm (it would have been killed), so post-fix behavior at the arm can't regress any currently-working boot. That logic holds IF no boot that works today reaches the arm \u2014 which follows from the arm being exit(1) in the old code: any access reaching it kills, so a working boot never reaches it. Yes, sound.\n\nBUT \u2014 there's a subtlety: \"the arm can only add output\" \u2014 is that true? The FIX also changed behavior: a WRITE is DECLINED (previously exit(1)) \u2014 declining is not \"output\"; a READ keeps the byte \u2014 serving is not \"output\". So the arm adds output AND behavior (serving/declining). But since no working boot reaches the arm, behavior changes there can't regress a working boot either. The sentence is loosely worded but its conclusion holds. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 is #10 the wrong one? Let me reconsider. \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1)\" \u2014 the repair diff ALSO touches the arm... but the repair changes the read announce from reloading the array to data[0] \u2014 again only output. And comment changes \u2014 output none. So the safety sentence extends to the repair. OK.\n\nHmm, let me now scrutinize #6: \"Fable filed it as coverage debt; taking it in-round costs two sessions and closes the axis.\" And the rows: \"inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows)\". Premise 3 lists Fable's escape: \"`if (relative_addr == 0x06) exit(1);` -> `inword wr`/`inword rd` (all 12 interiors now)\". So Fable's mutant re-kills at ONE unsampled offset (0x06); the fix is to sample ALL 12 interiors (inword = interior?) \u2014 wait, \"inword\" \u2014 in-word? The 12 counter interiors are the second/third bytes of multi-byte counters, i.e., the INTERIOR bytes of a multi-byte (in-word) counter. So \"inword wr/rd\" drives all 12 interior bytes. And \"unnamed wr/rd\" drives the 13 fork-header-unnamed offsets. And \"named wr/rd\" drives the 18 named ones. 18+12+13=43. So M_OFFKILL (`if (relative_addr == 0x06) exit(1)`) is killed by inword wr and inword rd (per table: M_OFFKILL 34/36, red rows \"inword wr,inword rd\"). Good.\n\nNow \u2014 M_EXIT's red list in table 3: it includes \"inword wr,inword rd,unnamed wr,unnamed rd\" \u2014 fine.\n\nNow Question 2: compare table 3 measurements against PRETABLE predictions, line by line.\n\nPredictions vs measured:\n\n- BASE 36/36, 23/23 on old. Measured: BASE 36/36, BASE.old23 23/23. Match.\n\n- M_WRLATCH: predicted 34/36, red: latch rd rd 2, latch rd wr 2. Measured 34/36, red: latch rd rd, latch rd wr. Match (2 red rows each with count 2? The table lists \"latch rd rd,latch rd wr\" without counts; 34/36 = 2 red rows. Match.)\n\n- M_WIDEKILL: predicted 32/36, red: wide wr, wide wr msg n/a, wide rd, wide rd srvd n/a. Hmm \u2014 \"n/a\" in the prediction for wide wr msg and wide rd srvd. Why n/a? Because M_WIDEKILL is the `len != 1` re-kill mutant \u2014 it exits on wide accesses, so the rows that would print the (len 4) label never get to print \u2014 they're killed differently? Hmm wait. If M_WIDEKILL exits on any len != 1 access, then a wide wr at the arm kills the host BEFORE the announce \u2014 so `wide wr` (fate row: host exit expected \u2014 the row EXPECTS the exit?) Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what the rows are. From PRETABLE's CHANNEL block:\n- latch rd rd / rd wr / aba: announce COUNT under DKA/DKE\n- sameoff val: read-back VALUE under DK2 (and DKB)\n- srvd val text: announce TEXT under DKZ\n- wide wr / wide rd: FATE under DKW, VALUE under DKB\n- wide wr msg / wide rd srvd: TEXT `(len 4)`\n- inword rd / unnamed rd: FATE under DKF and DKO\n- wr declined: TEXT under DKS\n\nSo `wide wr` checks the FATE (does the host survive? is the write declined \u2014 array unchanged?) and VALUE (array byte unchanged). `wide wr msg` checks the announce TEXT contains \"(len 4)\".\n\nFor M_WIDEKILL (`if (len != 1) exit(1);` re-kill): a wide write hits exit(1) -> host dies. So:\n- `wide wr` (fate row: expects host to survive / write declined): host DIES -> row RED. Match.\n- `wide wr msg` (text row: expects announce with (len 4)): with the mutant, the host dies before announcing \u2014 so the row... the prediction says \"n/a\". Hmm, \"n/a\" meaning the row's expected text never appears \u2014 is the row red or green? The row is a mutant-detection row: it's GREEN on BASE (announce printed with (len 4)). On the mutant, the host dies, so the announce never prints, so the row is RED too \u2014 unless the row's harness treats host-exit differently. The measured M_WIDEKILL red list: \"wide wr,wide wr msg,wide rd,wide rd srvd\" \u2014 4 rows red, 32/36. The prediction listed: \"wide wr, wide wr msg n/a, wide rd, wide rd srvd n/a\" \u2014 also 4 rows, 32/36. The \"n/a\" is annotation (maybe meaning the expected text is not applicable because the host died first), but the count matches: 32/36 measured, 32/36 predicted, same four rows. Match.\n\n- M_OFFKILL: predicted 34/36, red: inword wr, inword rd. Measured 34/36: inword wr, inword rd. Match. Wait \u2014 M_OFFKILL is `if (relative_addr == 0x06) exit(1);` \u2014 re-kill at offset 0x06 only. The predicted reds: inword wr, inword rd. But if only offset 0x06 re-kills, then only the SESSIONS that touch 0x06 die. The inword rows drive all 12 interiors \u2014 do the inword sessions each touch 0x06? \"inword rd\" is described in PRETABLE as FATE under DKF \u2014 \"inword rd / unnamed rd: FATE under DKF and DKO\". If inword rd touches only SOME interiors, and 0x06 isn't among them, the mutant wouldn't be caught. The measured result: M_OFFKILL 34/36 with inword wr, inword rd red \u2014 so both inword rows caught it, meaning both touch 0x06. Consistent with \"all 12 interiors\" driven by each row. But wait \u2014 measured says \"inword wr, inword rd\" but NOT \"unnamed wr/unnamed rd\" \u2014 correct, since 0x06 is an interior (counter interior), the unnamed rows don't touch it. And 34/36 = 2 red. Match.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but is 0x06 an interior? Premise: 12 counter interiors. The counters are at... e.g., PSCALECNT (16-bit) at some offset \u2014 interior = the high byte. 0x06 \u2014 hmm, the ICRs are at 0x06? In real PCC2, offset 0x06-0x07... Actually premise 1 says 18 named-unimplemented, 12 counter interiors, 13 fork-header-unnamed, and 0x39 is SPEED's second byte (an interior), 0x3b PRTDATA's low byte (not an interior \u2014 low byte, but unnamed?). Hmm, 0x3b PRTDATA's low byte is one of the 13 unnamed? The MANUAL names some of the 13 fork-header-unnamed: 0x39 SPEED second byte, 0x3b PRTDATA low byte. So interiors include 0x39 (SPEED is 16-bit at 0x38-0x39). And 0x06: \"M_OFFKILL (`if (relative_addr == 0x06) exit(1)`)... inword rd (host exit1 at 0x06)\" \u2014 so 0x06 is an interior. Fine \u2014 the premise asserts the 12 interiors include 0x06. Can't check from packet which offsets are interiors; it's consistent though.\n\n- M_SRVD0: predicted 35/36, red: srvd val text 0 (wide rd srvd stays green: it IS 0x00 there). Measured: 35/36, red: srvd val text. Match. And the parenthetical: wide rd srvd stays green because on the wide read at that offset the served byte is 0x00 \u2014 consistent (M_SRVD0 is \"read announce value -> 0\", i.e., prints 0 always; srvd val text expects 0x34; wide rd srvd expects 0x00 \u2014 mutant prints 0 which matches... hmm, mutant prints 0 for everything, wide rd srvd wants 0x00, so mutant coincidentally matches \u2014 row stays green. Consistent.)\n\n- M_BACKED2: predicted 35/36, red: sameoff val 0x5a. Measured 35/36: sameoff val. Match.\n\n- M_ABA: predicted 35/36, red: latch aba 3. Measured 35/36: latch aba. Match.\n\n- M_EXIT: predicted 7/36, \"the 16 of pass 2 + [list] (29 red)\". Let me count the predicted red list: \"the 16 of pass 2\" \u2014 hmm, wait. In table 3, M_EXIT is 7/36, so 29 red rows. The prediction lists: \"the 16 of pass 2 + latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, sameoff val, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined (29 red)\". Hmm \u2014 16 + 13 = 29. Let me count the appended list: latch rd rd (1), latch rd wr (2), latch aba (3), sameoff val (4), srvd val (5), srvd val text (6), wide wr (7), wide wr msg (8), wide rd (9), wide rd srvd (10), inword rd (11), unnamed rd (12), wr declined (13). 16+13=29. And measured red list for M_EXIT: let me count the measured list:\n\n\"named wr,named wr msg,named rd,named rd msg,named rd srvd,inword wr,inword rd,unnamed wr,unnamed rd,ieicr rdawr,ieerr rdawr,latch sameoff,sameoff val,latch twooff,latch rw one,latch rd rd,latch rd wr,latch aba,alias 0x6a,alias msg,dir wr text,wr declined,dir rd text,srvd val,srvd val text,wide wr,wide wr msg,wide rd,wide rd srvd\"\n\nCount: named wr (1), named wr msg (2), named rd (3), named rd msg (4), named rd srvd (5), inword wr (6), inword rd (7), unnamed wr (8), unnamed rd (9), ieicr rdawr (10), ieerr rdawr (11), latch sameoff (12), sameoff val (13), latch twooff (14), latch rw one (15), latch rd rd (16), latch rd wr (17), latch aba (18), alias 0x6a (19), alias msg (20), dir wr text (21), wr declined (22), dir rd text (23), srvd val (24), srvd val text (25), wide wr (26), wide wr msg (27), wide rd (28), wide rd srvd (29). Yes, 29 red \u2014 36-29 = 7 green. Match: 7/36.\n\nBut do the SETS match? The prediction says \"the 16 of pass 2 + [13 named]\". The measured list \u2014 which 16 are \"the pass-2 16\"? The pass-2 instrument had 23 rows; M_EXIT's red set on it was 16 (23-7). Hmm wait, \"M_EXIT diagnosis -- the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong (opus5, codex, kimi)\" (disposition 9). So pass-2 M_EXIT was 16 red on 23 (7 green). The 16 pass-2 rows presumably: named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, unnamed wr, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, latch twooff, latch rw one, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, dir rd text \u2014 that's 16. Then the 13 new: sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined. Union = 29. The measured list contains exactly: the 16 (named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, unnamed wr, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, latch twooff, latch rw one, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, dir rd text) plus the 13 (sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined). Let me verify each of the 13 is in the measured list: sameoff val \u2713, latch rd rd \u2713, latch rd wr \u2713, latch aba \u2713, srvd val \u2713, srvd val text \u2713, wide wr \u2713, wide wr msg \u2713, wide rd \u2713, wide rd srvd \u2713, inword rd \u2713, unnamed rd \u2713, wr declined \u2713. And the 16: named wr \u2713, named wr msg \u2713, named rd \u2713, named rd msg \u2713, named rd srvd \u2713, inword wr \u2713, unnamed wr \u2713, ieicr rdawr \u2713, ieerr rdawr \u2713, latch sameoff \u2713, latch twooff \u2713, latch rw one \u2713, alias 0x6a \u2713, alias msg \u2713, dir wr text \u2713, dir rd text \u2713. Total 29. Match.\n\n- M_UNLATCH: predicted 30/36, red: latch sameoff 2, latch rw one 2, latch rd rd 2, latch rd wr 2, latch aba 3, wide wr msg 2. That's 6 rows red -> 30/36. Measured: 30/36, red: latch sameoff,latch rw one,latch rd rd,latch rd wr,latch aba,wide wr msg. 6 rows. Match.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 M_UNLATCH removes the latch (announces every time). Then rows expecting exactly 1 announce go red: latch sameoff (2 announces instead of 1), latch rw one (2), latch rd rd (2), latch rd wr (2), latch aba (3? aba = three offsets' announces accumulate to 3; unlatched \u2014 hmm, latch aba \"wants 2, prints 3\" for M_ABA. For M_UNLATCH, latch aba wants... the aba row does three accesses at different offsets and counts total announces? If latched, 3 offsets -> 3 announces (one each). Wait \u2014 \"latch aba (wants 2, prints 3)\" \u2014 hmm. Let me re-read: \"codex `|=` -> `=` at the latch update -> `latch aba` (wants 2, prints 3)\". So the aba row does accesses at offsets A, B, A (aba = A-B-A pattern)? With `|=` (correct), bits accumulate: A sets bit A, B sets bit B, second A already latched -> announces: A, B (2 total). With `=` (mutant), the second A... wait, `=` would OVERWRITE: after B, the latch word = bit B only; second A access: bit A not set -> announces again -> 3 total. Yes! \"aba\" = the A-B-A pattern. So latch aba wants 2, mutant prints 3. \n\nFor M_UNLATCH (latch removed entirely): aba pattern -> 3 announces, wants 2 -> red. \u2713. wide wr msg 2: hmm, wide wr msg expects the (len 4) announce text \u2014 with latch removed... the wide wr msg row: does it do TWO wide writes at the same offset, expecting the second to be silent (latched)? Then unlatched -> 2 announces -> red (\"wide wr msg 2\"). \u2713 plausible.\n\n- M_ONELATCH: predicted 31/36, red: named wr msg 1, named rd msg 1, named rd srvd 1, latch twooff 1, latch aba 1. That's 5 rows -> 31/36. Measured: 31/36, red: named wr msg, named rd msg, named rd srvd, latch twooff, latch aba. 5 rows. Match.\n\nHmm \u2014 M_ONELATCH: latch per-INSTANCE instead of per-offset? I.e., only ONE announce ever per instance (a single boolean). Then: named wr msg (expects announce on write at named offset \u2014 with one-latch, if some earlier access consumed the latch...). The red rows: named wr msg 1 \u2014 hmm, \"1\"? Maybe the row expects 1 announce and gets 0 (an earlier access consumed the single latch)? Or expects 1 and gets... The counts in the prediction (\"1\", \"2\", \"3\") are the MEASURED counts presumably. Whatever \u2014 measured matches predicted in score and row set. Match.\n\n- M_BACKED: predicted 32/36, red: ieicr rdawr 0x5a, ieerr rdawr 0x5a, sameoff val 0x5a, wide wr 0x5a5a5a5a. 4 rows -> 32/36. Measured: 32/36, red: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, sameoff val, wide wr. 4 rows. Match.\n\n- M_RDKILL: predicted 17/36, red: \"the seven of pass 2 (named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, dir rd text, latch rw one, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr) + latch sameoff (its session now ends in a read), sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd -- nineteen\". So predicted: 7 + 12 = 19 red -> 17 green. Let me count the appended: latch sameoff (1), sameoff val (2), latch rd rd (3), latch rd wr (4), srvd val (5), srvd val text (6), wide wr (7), wide wr msg (8), wide rd (9), wide rd srvd (10), inword rd (11), unnamed rd (12). 7+12 = 19. 36-19 = 17. \u2713 predicted 17/36.\n\nMeasured M_RDKILL: 17/36, red list: \"named rd,named rd msg,named rd srvd,inword rd,unnamed rd,ieicr rdawr,ieerr rdawr,latch sameoff,sameoff val,latch rw one,latch rd rd,latch rd wr,dir rd text,srvd val,srvd val text,wide wr,wide wr msg,wide rd,wide rd srvd\". Count: named rd (1), named rd msg (2), named rd srvd (3), inword rd (4), unnamed rd (5), ieicr rdawr (6), ieerr rdawr (7), latch sameoff (8), sameoff val (9), latch rw one (10), latch rd rd (11), latch rd wr (12), dir rd text (13), srvd val (14), srvd val text (15), wide wr (16), wide wr msg (17), wide rd (18), wide rd srvd (19). 19 red. 36-19=17. \u2713 Match, and the row set matches the prediction exactly (the prediction's \"seven of pass 2\": named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, dir rd text, latch rw one, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr \u2014 all in the measured list \u2713; the appended 12 all in \u2713).\n\nWait \u2014 but hold on. M_RDKILL is presumably \"read path re-kill\": `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) exit(1)` or the mutant restores exit(1) on reads only. Then ALL read rows die. The green rows would be the write-only rows: named wr, named wr msg, inword wr, unnamed wr, latch sameoff? \u2014 no wait, latch sameoff's session ends in a read per the prediction note, so it's red. Green: named wr, named wr msg, inword wr, unnamed wr, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, wr declined, latch twooff \u2014 let me count green: 36 - 19 = 17 green: named wr, named wr msg, inword wr, unnamed wr, latch twooff, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, wr declined, + BASE-ish rows? No wait, the 36 rows are the detector rows (no BASE row \u2014 \"BASE 36/36\" is the detector's own 36 rows all green). So the 36 rows include: named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, inword rd, unnamed wr, unnamed rd, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, sameoff val, latch twooff, latch rw one, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, wr declined, dir rd text, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword... \n\nHmm, let me enumerate the 36 rows from the union of all red lists across mutants. Collecting from M_EXIT (29) + the 7 green for M_EXIT. M_EXIT green (36-29=7): from the 36-row set minus the 29. The 36 rows \u2014 from all the red lists combined: named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, inword rd, unnamed wr, unnamed rd, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, sameoff val, latch twooff, latch rw one, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, wr declined, dir rd text, srvd val, srvd val text, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd = 29. Need 7 more: presumably latch off... hmm: rows mentioned in PRETABLE: \"latch rd rd + latch rd wr\", \"latch aba\", \"sameoff val\", \"srvd val + srvd val text\", \"wide wr + wide rd\", \"inword/unnamed wr ... + inword rd, unnamed rd\", \"wide wr msg, wide rd srvd\", \"wr declined\", \"wr declined: TEXT under DKS\", \"dir wr text, dir rd text\", \"named rd srvd\", \"latch twooff\", \"latch rw one\", \"latch sameoff\", \"alias 0x6a, alias msg\", \"ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr\", \"named wr msg\", \"named rd msg\", \"named wr\", \"named rd\".\n\nAlso from M_EXIT green (7 rows not in its red list): candidates: latch twooff is IN the red list. The 7 green for M_EXIT: rows not listed: from the 36: (29 red) \u2014 missing 7: maybe \"srvd val text\" is red... Let me just take the 36-row union from M_RDKILL green (17): 36 - 19 red = 17 green: named wr, named wr msg, inword wr, unnamed wr, latch twooff, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, wr declined + 8 more. Hmm, that's only 9. So there are more rows not appearing in any red list I've enumerated. Possibly: \"latch off...\", \"srvd...\", hmm.\n\nActually \u2014 the 23-row pass-2 instrument had 23 rows, and the repair added 13 rows (23+13=36). The 13 new rows are (from premise 3 and PRETABLE): srvd val text, sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined, + one more. From PRETABLE CLAIM->ROW: \"srvd val + srvd val text\" (2), \"latch rd rd + latch rd wr\" (2), \"latch aba\" (1), \"sameoff val\" (1), \"wide wr + wide rd\" (2), \"wide wr msg + wide rd srvd\" (2 \u2014 pin the label), \"inword rd, unnamed rd\" (2), \"wr declined\" (1). Total: 2+2+1+1+2+2+2+1 = 13. \u2713 So the pass-2 23 rows include: named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, unnamed wr, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, latch twooff, latch rw one, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, dir rd text, srvd val, wide... \n\nHmm wait \u2014 srvd val is in the 23 (pass-2) and srvd val text is new. Let me recount the pass-2 23: from M_RDKILL's \"seven of pass 2\": named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, dir rd text, latch rw one, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr (7). Plus named wr, named wr msg, inword wr, unnamed wr, latch sameoff, latch twooff, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, srvd val, + 2 more to reach 23. Hmm: 7+10=17, need 6 more. Ugh, I can't fully enumerate; not necessary. The key checks are per-mutant score and red-set match, which I did.\n\nSo per-mutant comparison: ALL match. Let me double check M_DIRSWAP: predicted 30/36, red: dir wr text 0, dir rd text 0, named rd srvd 0, srvd val text 0, wide rd srvd 0 \u2014 6 rows -> 30/36. Measured: 30/36, red: \"named rd srvd,dir wr text,wr declined,dir rd text,srvd val text,wide rd srvd\". That's 6 rows: named rd srvd, dir wr text, wr declined, dir rd text, srvd val text, wide rd srvd. Predicted set: dir wr text, dir rd text, named rd srvd, srvd val text, wide rd srvd \u2014 5 rows! Predicted 5 rows but 30/36 implies 6 red. Hmm \u2014 prediction says \"M_DIRSWAP 30/36 dir wr text 0, dir rd text 0, named rd srvd 0, srvd val text 0, wide rd srvd 0\" \u2014 that's 5 named rows, but 36-30 = 6 red. The measured has 6 including \"wr declined\". So the PREDICTION MISSED \"wr declined\" \u2014 a predicted red that... wait, no: the prediction predicted 30/36 (6 red) but listed only 5 rows. The measured red set includes wr declined, which was NOT in the prediction's list. So the prediction's SCORE matched (30/36) but the ROW LIST was incomplete \u2014 the prediction named 5 of the 6 red rows.\n\nHmm \u2014 is that \"a miss in the SAFE direction\"? The round's rule: \"A miss in the SAFE direction is recorded; one in the UNSAFE direction (a predicted red that stays green) stops the round.\" The prediction's red-set for M_DIRSWAP listed 5 rows; the measured has 6 (wr declined additionally red). A row that went red but wasn't predicted red \u2014 that's the SAFE direction? Hmm. Wait \u2014 the rule from premise in PRETABLE: \"A miss in the SAFE direction is recorded; one in the UNSAFE direction (a predicted red that stays green) stops the round.\" So UNSAFE = predicted red that stayed green. SAFE = predicted green (or unlisted) that went red? Hmm, actually that seems backwards from usual mutation-testing terminology, but the round defines it: unsafe = predicted red stays green (mutant survives when predicted killed \u2014 the detector is weaker than claimed). Safe = extra reds (detector stronger than claimed). \n\nBut wait \u2014 the prediction's SCORE was 30/36, which implies 6 red \u2014 but only listed 5. So the score prediction itself implicitly says 6 red while listing 5 named rows. That's an internal inconsistency in the PRETABLE: 30/36 with a 5-row list. And the measured shows the 6th row is wr declined. Interesting: \"wr declined: TEXT under DKS\" \u2014 wr declined checks the announce text \"declined\". M_DIRSWAP swaps direction test (writeflag == MEM_WRITE <-> MEM_READ inverted?) \u2014 i.e., the mutant swaps the read/write branches. Then a WRITE gets the read announce (\"served...\") and a READ gets the write announce (\"declined... idata\"). So \"wr declined\" (expects the write announce to say \"declined\") goes red \u2713 \u2014 the prediction MISSED that row even though it predicted the score 30/36. So the score was right but the enumeration was short by one. Hmm \u2014 actually maybe the prediction listed the MINIMUM red set (\"MINIMUM red set per mutant\") \u2014 \"PREDICTIONS (36-row instrument...; MINIMUM red set per mutant)\". So the prediction gives a minimum red set \u2014 rows guaranteed red \u2014 and the score. The measured score 30/36 = 6 red \u2265 5 minimum. Then no contradiction: the minimum set (5) is a subset of measured (6). Let me re-check: measured M_DIRSWAP reds: named rd srvd, dir wr text, wr declined, dir rd text, srvd val text, wide rd srvd. Predicted minimum: dir wr text, dir rd text, named rd srvd, srvd val text, wide rd srvd \u2014 all 5 \u2282 measured 6. \u2713 Score matches 30/36. OK so no contradiction \u2014 but it IS notable that the measured has one MORE red than the minimum, which is fine per the minimum-red-set framing.\n\nBut then for other mutants, does the \"minimum red set\" framing hold? E.g., M_EXIT predicted \"29 red\" with a full list of 29 and measured 29 \u2014 exact. M_RDKILL predicted \"nineteen\" and measured 19 \u2014 exact. M_DIRSWAP: minimum 5, measured 6. Hmm \u2014 so the PRETABLE for M_DIRSWAP either (a) gave a minimum set of 5 with score 30 (implying it knew there were 6 but couldn't name one?) or (b) intended 5 red = 31/36 and wrote 30/36, and got lucky. Actually if it says 30/36 AND lists 5, and the measured is 30/36 with 6 red \u2014 the score matched. If the intended red set was exactly those 5, the score should have been 31/36. So either the score prediction independently accounted for 6 red (and the list is a subset/minimum), or the score is right by luck. The PRETABLE header says \"MINIMUM red set per mutant\" \u2014 so the list is explicitly a minimum; the score 30/36 then implies at least one more red beyond the minimum. Fine \u2014 internally consistent under the \"minimum\" framing. Hmm, but then \"M_DIRSWAP 30/36\" with minimum 5 \u2014 the measured exactly 30/36. OK.\n\nWait, I should double-check the other predictions under the \"minimum\" framing: M_WIDEKILL \"wide wr, wide wr msg n/a, wide rd, wide rd srvd n/a\" \u2014 4 rows, 32/36 = 4 red. Exact. M_WRLATCH 2 rows, 34/36 = 2 red. Exact. M_OFFKILL 2 rows, 34/36. Exact. M_SRVD0 1 row, 35/36. Exact. M_BACKED2 1 row, 35/36. Exact. M_ABA 1 row, 35/36. Exact. M_UNLATCH 6 rows, 30/36. Exact. M_ONELATCH 5 rows, 31/36. Exact. M_BACKED 4 rows, 32/36. Exact. M_EXIT 29, 7/36. Exact. M_RDKILL 19, 17/36. Exact. M_DIRSWAP 5 listed, 30/36 = 6 red. MISMATCH in enumeration: one unlisted red (wr declined), score matches. So under the exact-list framing, M_DIRSWAP's list is short one; under the minimum framing, it's fine and the score is the prediction. Either way the SCORE matches. Is this a contradiction \"from table 3 and its predictions\"? The question: \"does any mutant's result contradict its prediction\". The SCORE never contradicts. The ROW LIST for M_DIRSWAP is one short of its own score if read as an exact list; the PRETABLE self-describes lists as minimums. So: REASONED \u2014 no contradiction in score; one row (wr declined) red beyond M_DIRSWAP's named minimum, which the score already accounted for. Worth naming as the only prediction/measurement seam.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider: maybe the PRETABLE's \"30/36\" was a typo for 31/36, and the measured 30/36 is one MORE red than predicted \u2014 a \"miss in the SAFE direction\" (an unpredicted red), which is \"recorded\" but doesn't stop the round. Either way: no unsafe miss. Good.\n\nNow the six pass-2 mutants on the old instrument: table shows M_WRLATCH.old23 23/23, M_WIDEKILL.old23 23/23, M_OFFKILL.old23 23/23, M_SRVD0.old23 23/23, M_BACKED2.old23 23/23, M_ABA.old23 23/23. All six at 23/23 (full kill = the breach... wait). Hold on. The pass-2 instrument has 23 rows. A mutant that is KILLED by the instrument shows RED on the rows that detect it \u2014 \"23/23\" means... hmm. \"Pass 2 found the 23-row detector below the floor six ways (two MEASURED clean sweeps, four predicted)\". \"Clean sweep\" = the mutant scores 23/23 = ALL 23 rows red? No wait \u2014 that would mean every row catches it, which is not \"below the floor\".\n\nHmm, let me re-think. The floor question: \"does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps the 36-row detector clean?\" \u2014 \"sweeps the detector clean\" = the detector finds NOTHING \u2014 all rows GREEN \u2014 the mutant is EQUIVALENT to BASE as far as the detector can see. So a clean sweep = X/X GREEN, i.e., the detector fails to distinguish mutant from base. Hmm, but then \"23/23\" \u2014 is that 23 green or 23 red?\n\n\"M_EXIT 7/36\" with a 29-row red list \u2014 7/36 means 7 GREEN (29 red). So the score is GREEN count! M_EXIT leaves only 7 rows green \u2014 heavily killed. M_RDKILL 17/36 = 17 green, 19 red. M_SRVD0 35/36 = 35 green, 1 red \u2014 barely killed (one row catches it). BASE 36/36 = all green (nothing red on base \u2014 correct, the detector is consistent with the product).\n\nSo \"23/23\" on the pass-2 instrument = ALL 23 ROWS GREEN = the mutant is INVISIBLE to the pass-2 detector = the BREACH. Yes: \"Pass 2 found the 23-row detector below the floor six ways\" \u2014 six mutants that the 23-row detector cannot see at all (clean sweeps, all green). \"two MEASURED clean sweeps, four predicted\" \u2014 Opus measured two at 23/23 (M_O1, M_O2); the other four predicted. Now table 3 confirms all six .old23 rows at 23/23 \u2014 so the breach claim holds for all six: none is NOT 23/23. \u2713 (Question 2: \"is any of the six pass-2 mutants NOT 23/23 on the pass-2 instrument (which would make the breach claim wrong)\" \u2014 answer: no, all six measured 23/23 in table 3. READ.)\n\nAnd on the repaired 36-row instrument, each of the six is caught (M_WRLATCH 34/36 \u2014 2 red, etc.). \u2713 class closed.\n\nNow \u2014 the floor question for the SHIPPED COMPOSITE: does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps the 36-row detector clean? The repair adds 13 rows; the six known escape classes each have \u22651 red row. Is any cell of the closure still open \u2014 name the edit?\n\nLet me think about what could still escape the 36-row detector. The detector's rows and the guard/body fields:\n\nGuard fields: writeflag, idata, len, data[0] (body); warned_unimpl bit relative_addr (guard). Joint cells: setter-direction x tester-direction (W,W / W,R / R,R / R,W) \u2014 all four rowed (sameoff, rw one, rd rd, rd wr); accumulation (aba); disposition at latch-set (sameoff val); read-announce value (srvd val text, sameoff val, srvd val); wide label (wide wr msg, wide rd srvd); wide fate/value (wide wr, wide rd); 43-offset coverage both ways (named/inword/unnamed wr/rd); declined text (wr declined); direction text (dir wr text, dir rd text); alias (alias 0x6a, alias msg); rdawr (ieicr/ieerr rdawr); twooff (latch twooff); srvd val...\n\nPremise 4: \"`len` is NOT tested by the arm, so width cells are CLAIM rows (the pass-1 PRETABLE claimed 'byte-and-wide'), not floor: one per direction at len 4; len 2 DISCLOSED.\"\n\nSo: the arm does not test `len` except in the announce text `(len %d)`. The width rows use len 4 only; len 2 is DISCLOSED (not rowed). So a mutant that behaves differently at len 2 (e.g., `if (len == 2) exit(1);`) would NOT be caught \u2014 no len-2 row exists. Is that an open cell? The premise says width cells are CLAIM rows, not floor \u2014 the claim \"byte-and-wide\" is rowed at len 4; len 2 is disclosed as untested. So the closure EXCLUDES len 2 (and len 3, and any other len). That's an admitted open cell \u2014 disclosed, not open-by-accident. The question says \"is any cell of the closure still open \u2014 name the edit?\" \u2014 the answer might be: yes, len 2 (disclosed): a mutant `if (len == 2) exit(1)` would sweep clean... wait, would it? The rows are all at len 1 or len 4. If a mutant re-kills at len 2 only, all 36 rows (len 1 and len 4) stay green \u2014 clean sweep. So the 36-row detector is below floor for a len-2 mutant. But the premise DISCLOSES this: \"len 2 DISCLOSED\". So the closure is: byte + len-4, per direction; len 2 (and len 3?) excluded. Hmm \u2014 len 3: \"one per direction at len 4; len 2 DISCLOSED\" \u2014 what about len 3? Unaligned/misaligned accesses \u2014 can a guest issue len 3? On m88k, a 32-bit access is len 4; a 16-bit is len 2; an 8-bit is len 1. len 3 doesn't arise from aligned accesses; misaligned might fault. So len 3 is presumably unreachable. But the packet doesn't say. Hmm.\n\nAlso the end-span path (#119): zero-fill on end-span. If relative_addr + len > PCC2_SIZE... but the %= fold: \"the `%= PCC2_SIZE` fold ... as the handler's first statement so the shift is 0..63\" \u2014 wait, premise 1 says `warned_unimpl` bit `relative_addr` \"is folded `%= PCC2_SIZE` (0x40) as the handler's first statement\". Hmm \u2014 \"the %= fold above IS the real part's\" (from the comment in the diff: \"The %= fold above IS the real part's:\"). So relative_addr is folded mod 0x40 at the top of the handler. Then all accesses land in 0..0x3f, and the end-span path (#119) handles relative_addr + len > 0x40? E.g., a 4-byte read at 0x3e spans past the end -> zero-fill? And then the switch on relative_addr \u2014 the default arm at 0x3e (an unnamed offset?) with len 4 -> the memcpy... hmm, \"zero-filled on the #119 end-span path\". So on the end-span path, data is zero-filled and data[0] = 0x00 truthfully. OK.\n\nNow \u2014 is there any mutant that still sweeps the 36-row detector clean? Let me think about the guard field: the latch bit index: `1ULL << relative_addr` with relative_addr folded to 0..63 on a 64-bit type \u2014 the fold is what makes the shift in range. A mutant that changes the fold (e.g., removes %=) would shift by up to... the guest's relative_addr could exceed 0x40? The access is within the device's address range \u2014 the device is mapped at some size; relative_addr could be larger than 0x40 if the mapping is bigger? PCC2 is 0x40 bytes typically. Hmm \u2014 the %= fold exists because \"the real part's\" \u2014 the real part folds too. If relative_addr is always < 0x40 anyway, the fold is defensive. A mutant removing the fold: shift by relative_addr (0..0x3f anyway) \u2014 no change if accesses never exceed 0x3f. But can they? The end-span path suggests accesses AT 0x3e/0x3f with len 4 \u2014 relative_addr \u2264 0x3f. So the fold is a no-op for in-range accesses; removing it changes nothing observable unless relative_addr \u2265 0x40 occurs. Can the guest issue relative_addr \u2265 0x40? Depends on the mapping size \u2014 not in the packet. This is a potential unrowed cell but only if the mapping allows larger offsets. Hmm \u2014 the packet says 43 of the 0x40 offsets reach the arm; 0x40 = 64 offsets, 43 reach the default arm (the other 21 are cased). If the mapping were larger than 0x40, the %= fold would alias offsets \u2014 and \"alias 0x6a\" row! There's an \"alias 0x6a\" row and \"alias msg\" row. 0x6a = 106 = 0x40 + 0x2a. So the guest CAN issue relative_addr 0x6a (the mapping is bigger than 0x40), and the %= fold aliases it to 0x2a! And \"alias 0x6a\" is a pass-2 row \u2014 it checks that the alias behaves per the fold (announce at 0x2a, or the latch bit shared?). And \"alias msg\" checks the announce TEXT (which offset is printed \u2014 the folded one?). Good \u2014 so the alias rows cover the fold. The M_EXIT red list includes alias 0x6a and alias msg \u2713.\n\nSo what's still open? Let me list the guard/body fields and check each has rows in BOTH... hmm, the question asks \"is any cell of the closure still open \u2014 name the edit?\"\n\nCandidate open cells:\n\n(a) **len 2** \u2014 disclosed as not rowed. A len-2-targeted mutant (e.g., `if (len == 2) exit(1)`) sweeps the 36 rows clean. DISCLOSED, so not a discovery, but it IS the answer to \"name the edit\" if the question wants the residual gap. But premise 4 frames width cells as CLAIM rows \u2014 the arm doesn't test len, so a len-2 mutant is... hmm, wait. Clause (d) mutant \u2014 what is clause (d)? Not defined in the packet beyond \"clause-(d) mutant\". The floor question: \"does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps the 36-row detector clean?\" The repair claims closure of the six named classes. The residual: len 2 is disclosed. So the composite's floor is: all mutants EXCEPT len-2 (and len-3?) variants are caught? That seems to be the carried denominator for Q3.\n\n(b) **idata on the READ announce?** The write announce prints idata. The read announce prints data[0]. A mutant that prints idata on reads (or data[0] on writes)? The write announce's idata is pinned by \"named wr msg\"? Hmm \u2014 does any row pin the WRITE announce's VALUE (idata)? Rows: \"dir wr text\" (direction phrase), \"wr declined\" (\"declined\" text), \"named wr msg\" (once? or the full text?), \"wide wr msg\" (\"(len 4)\"). Is there a row pinning the write announce's idata VALUE \u2014 e.g., a mutant that prints the wrong value in the write announce (`idata -> 0`)? The read side has \"srvd val\" and \"srvd val text\" pinning the served value. The write side: is idata pinned anywhere? Looking at the rows: named wr (fate: write declined \u2014 array unchanged), named wr msg (announce text \u2014 but WHICH part of the text? If it checks the full format including the idata value, then idata is pinned). Not specified. Hmm. M_EXIT's red list has \"named wr, named wr msg\" \u2014 those catch exit. Is there a mutant \"write announce idata -> 0\" in the table? Not among the 12 mutants. So the idata value in the write announce is pinned only if a msg row checks it. Can't settle from the packet. Name it: \"does any row assert the write announce's idata value (e.g., `0x5a` written shows as 0x5a)?\" \u2014 the single fact: the row definitions (what text `named wr msg` matches). Hmm, but is that a real cell? The write announce value \u2014 a mutant printing the wrong idata is a \"announce TEXT\" defect, not a behavior defect. The class closed was about the six escapes; the composite floor question is about any mutant sweeping clean. A `write-announce idata -> 0` mutant (M_WIDATA?) \u2014 if no row checks the idata value, it sweeps clean. Hmm, but wait \u2014 the same class on the READ side (M_SRVD0, \"read announce value -> 0\") needed a NEW row (`srvd val text`) because the pass-2 rows didn't pin the value. So by symmetry, the write-announce value (idata) needs a row. Does the 36-row instrument have one? The 13 new rows: srvd val text, sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, wide wr, wide wr msg, wide rd, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined, +1 more. Wait let me recount the 13 new: srvd val text (1), sameoff val (2), latch rd rd (3), latch rd wr (4), latch aba (5), wide wr (6), wide rd (7), wide wr msg (8), wide rd srvd (9), inword rd (10), unnamed rd (11), wr declined (12), and... the PRETABLE CLAIM->ROW lists \"srvd val + srvd val text\" \u2014 is srvd val pass-2 or new? Opus's M_O1 escape was \"read announce value -> 0\" killed by NEW row `srvd val text` \u2014 and M_SRVD0's measured red on the 36-row is ONLY \"srvd val text\" (35/36, one red). So `srvd val` (if it exists as a separate row) did NOT catch M_SRVD0 \u2014 meaning srvd val checks the VALUE CHANNEL (read-back of the array byte?) not the announce text. Hmm: PRETABLE CHANNEL: \"sameoff val: read-back VALUE under DK2 (and DKB); srvd val text: announce TEXT under DKZ\". So srvd val \u2014 hmm, it's not given a channel in that list... The list: \"latch rd rd / rd wr / aba: announce COUNT under DKA/DKE; sameoff val: read-back VALUE under DK2 (and DKB); srvd val text: announce TEXT under DKZ (value 0x34 the guest wrote); wide wr / wide rd: FATE under DKW, VALUE under DKB; wide wr msg / wide rd srvd: TEXT `(len 4)`; inword rd / unnamed rd: FATE under DKF and DKO; wr declined: TEXT under DKS.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"srvd val text: announce TEXT under DKZ (value 0x34 the guest wrote)\" \u2014 wait, \"value 0x34 the guest wrote\"? srvd val text checks the READ announce text showing 0x34? But the guest wrote 0x34 then read it? No \u2014 the write is DECLINED, so a read-back would show the array byte, not 0x34... unless the row writes 0x34 to a DIFFERENT (cased, backed) offset? Hmm: \"srvd val text (wants `served 0x34`)\" (premise 3, Opus M_O1 row). And M_SRVD0 = \"read announce value -> 0\". So the row expects the read announce to print `served 0x34` \u2014 the byte served is 0x34. Where does 0x34 come from? \"(value 0x34 the guest wrote)\" \u2014 the guest wrote 0x34 somewhere that DOES back it (a cased register? or the same offset pre-latch?). Hmm, wait \u2014 if the guest wrote 0x34 to the same unnamed offset, the write is declined (array unchanged), so the read would serve the array's current byte (0x00?). For the announce to print 0x34, the array byte must BE 0x34 \u2014 how? Maybe the guest wrote 0x34 to a cased-writable register then read it back \u2014 no, that wouldn't hit the default arm. OR: the row writes 0x34 at offset X via a WIDE write to a cased... hmm.\n\nOR: maybe the guest wrote 0x34 to the offset BEFORE the latch was set \u2014 no, the write is declined regardless of latch.\n\nHmm, alternatively the row's 0x34 comes from the initial state of some counter interior after ticks? \"the counter interiors\" are rewritten by the tick \u2014 an interior's byte could be 0x34 after ticks. But \"(value 0x34 the guest wrote)\" says the guest wrote it. Hmm \u2014 maybe the row drives a CASed register whose sibling is the default arm? No...\n\nWait \u2014 maybe I'm overcomplicating: perhaps `srvd val text` writes 0x34 to the SAME offset at len 1 \u2014 but writes are DECLINED, so the array keeps its byte; then reads \u2014 announce prints the array byte, not 0x34. UNLESS the write is to an offset whose array byte is guest-writable through a CASed path at a different width... e.g., a wide write at offset X (len 4) that spans a cased byte + default-arm bytes? If the write is WIDE and spans multiple offsets, the memcpy/store above the switch... hmm, but the arm declines the default offsets only for len 1? The arm handles the whole access or per-byte? This is getting deep into unverifiable territory.\n\nActually, hold on \u2014 re-read premise 3: \"Opus M_O1 read announce value -> 0 (MEASURED 23/23) -> `srvd val text` (wants `served 0x34`)\". And PRETABLE: \"srvd val text: announce TEXT under DKZ (value 0x34 the guest wrote)\". Hmm, so the guest wrote 0x34 SOMEWHERE and the read announce prints 0x34. The only way a default-arm offset serves 0x34 is if the array byte at that offset is 0x34. How can the guest make an array byte 0x34 at a default-arm offset? Answer: WIDE write to a CASed offset that spans into the default-arm byte? E.g., a 4-byte write at a cased register whose neighbors include a default-arm offset \u2014 the cased arm stores the full word including the neighbor byte? If the cased arm stores 4 bytes (the wide write backs all 4 bytes), the neighboring default-arm byte gets 0x34. Then a len-1 read at that default-arm offset serves 0x34. Hmm \u2014 plausible but speculative.\n\nOR: the counter interiors \u2014 the TICK rewrites them; if the guest can't write them, the tick's value... not \"the guest wrote\".\n\nOR maybe: the guest wrote 0x34 to the SAME offset in a PREVIOUS session... no, sessions are separate emulator runs.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. Maybe the write is not declined for len-1 at... no. OR: `srvd val` and `srvd val text` \u2014 maybe srvd val is the READ-BACK of the served byte: the guest reads offset X (announce, latch set, byte served into the guest's register \u2014 the detector checks the guest's loaded register value == the array byte = \"served VALUE\" on the CPU channel), and srvd val text checks the ANNOUNCE's printed value. For srvd val text to want `served 0x34`, the array byte must be 0x34. \"(value 0x34 the guest wrote)\" \u2014 hmm, so the guest wrote 0x34 to that offset... and the write is DECLINED (array unchanged)... then the read serves the OLD byte, not 0x34. CONTRADICTION?\n\nUnless: the row writes 0x34 at an offset where the write is declined, then reads \u2014 the announce prints the CURRENT array byte. If the array byte at that offset happens to be 0x00, the row wants `served 0x00`. But the row wants `served 0x34`. So the array byte must be 0x34. For it to be 0x34 via \"the guest wrote\", the write must have been BACKED \u2014 i.e., at a CASed... no, it's a default-arm row.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 maybe the wide-write path: \"wide wr\" rows are at len 4. A wide write at a default-arm offset is DECLINED (the whole 4-byte write? or just...?). If a wide write at offset X is declined entirely, the array is unchanged. Hmm.\n\nOR \u2014 the alias: 0x6a folds to 0x2a. If the guest writes 0x34 at 0x2a... no, declined.\n\nLet me try another reading: maybe `srvd val text` writes 0x34 to the offset, then reads, and the ANNOUNCE prints the SERVED byte \u2014 which, since the write was declined, is the array's current byte. If the row wants `served 0x34`, the current byte must be 0x34 \u2014 which requires the array byte to have been set to 0x34 somehow. The only guest-writable path into a default-arm offset's byte: a WIDE write at a lower offset that spans it, where the spanning write is handled by a CASed arm that backs all 4 bytes. E.g., a 4-byte write at 0x04 (a cased writable register, 32-bit) writes bytes 0x04-0x07; if 0x06 is a default-arm... no wait, 0x06 would be part of the cased register's own bytes \u2014 the cased arm stores the whole word regardless of the interior bytes' unimplemented status. Hmm \u2014 but premise says 0x06 is a \"counter interior\" that reaches the DEFAULT arm \u2014 meaning a len-1 access at 0x06 hits the default arm. But a len-4 access at 0x04 hits the CASED arm for the register at 0x04, and the cased arm's store writes bytes 0x04-0x07 including 0x06! So the guest CAN put 0x34 into byte 0x06 via a wide write at 0x04 (if the cased arm backs wide writes). Then a len-1 READ at 0x06 serves 0x34, and the announce (repaired: data[0]) prints `served 0x34`. That's coherent! And \"(value 0x34 the guest wrote)\" = the guest wrote it via the wide cased access. OK \u2014 plausible, but I cannot verify from the packet. It's at least not self-contradictory.\n\nHmm, wait, but is the wide write at 0x04-0x07 handled by ONE cased arm (relative_addr 0x04)? The switch is on relative_addr (folded). A len-4 write at 0x04 -> case 0x04 -> the arm handles the full word. A len-1 write at 0x06 -> default arm. Yes \u2014 the switch is on the STARTING offset. Consistent.\n\nOK so Q1: \"is data[0] always the byte the guest received (memcpy path and end-span zero-fill path)?\" \u2014 per the packet's premises: memcpy path \u2014 data filled from the array (for reads) before the switch, so data[0] = the byte served \u2713 (READ premise, REASONED consistency). End-span zero-fill path: data[0] = 0x00, and the guest receives 0x00 (the zero-fill IS the serve) \u2713 per premise. BUT \u2014 caveats:\n\n1. On the end-span path, does the guest receive 0x00 for the IN-RANGE bytes too? If a 4-byte read at 0x3e spans 0x3e-0x41 (end at 0x40), the in-range bytes 0x3e-0x3f would presumably come from the array and the out-of-range bytes 0x40-0x41 zero-filled \u2014 then data[0] (byte at 0x3e) would be the array byte, not 0x00. The premise says \"zero-filled on the #119 end-span path, where `data[0]` is then truthfully 0x00\" \u2014 implying on the end-span path data[0] is 0x00 \u2014 i.e., the whole access is zero-filled? OR the end-span path is when relative_addr itself is out of... but relative_addr is folded % 0x40 to 0..0x3f, so it's always in range. Hmm \u2014 so end-span = relative_addr + len > 0x40. If data is ENTIRELY zero-filled in that case (not just the out-of-range tail), then the guest receives 0x00 for bytes 0x3e/0x3f too \u2014 which would NOT match the array \u2014 but that's the #119 path's PRE-EXISTING behavior (the serve happens by the zero-fill, not by the memcpy), and the announce printing data[0]=0x00 truthfully reports the served byte. \"truthfully 0x00\" \u2014 the premise says the announce is truthful. So data[0] = the byte served even on the end-span path \u2713 \u2014 as long as the zero-fill is what's served. Consistent per premise. But \u2014 can a WIDE READ at 0x3e-0x41 even reach the default arm with relative_addr 0x3e? Is 0x3e a default-arm offset? The unnamed 13 include 0x3b; 0x3e/0x3f might be cased or not. Can't settle; doesn't matter much \u2014 the announce prints data[0] which is by construction what was served (either memcpy'd or zero-filled). \u2713 per premises.\n\n2. The WRITE path: for a write, \"data[0]\" would be the byte the guest WROTE, but the write announce prints idata, not data[0] \u2014 fine.\n\n3. The tick race: the announce now prints data[0] \u2014 captured at memcpy time \u2014 the byte served \u2713. The OLD code reloaded the array, which the tick may have changed \u2014 the fix is right for the twelve interiors. \u2713 (codex #7).\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT. Now scrutinize the latch interaction: the latch is per-offset; the announce fires ONCE per offset. The tick rewrites interiors BETWEEN accesses too \u2014 the SERVED byte at first access is data[0]; fine.\n\nNow \u2014 Q1's second half: \"which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\" The brief WANTS us to find one (\"be harder on the ACCEPTED dispositions\"; \"a seat whose escape was adopted scrutinises its own repair row MORE\"). Let me re-scan dispositions 1-10 for a wrong one.\n\n#1 M_O1: \"the announced value unpinned. Row `srvd val text`; the product now prints data[0].\" \u2014 Is the row sufficient? M_SRVD0 measured 35/36 with red = srvd val text only. \u2713 killed. But \u2014 does `srvd val text` kill M_SRVD0 for the REASON claimed (the announce value) or by accident? The row wants `served 0x34` text; M_SRVD0 prints `served 0x00` -> text mismatch -> red. Reason \u2713.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with M_SRVD0 on the WIDE read: the prediction says \"wide rd srvd stays: it IS 0x00 there\" \u2014 M_SRVD0 prints 0 always; wide rd srvd wants `served 0x00` (at that offset the byte is 0x00) -> coincidentally green. Fine \u2014 disclosed in the prediction.\n\n#2 M_O2: \"the disposition on the latched path unpinned. Row `sameoff val` (the existing session gains a read-back; zero new sessions).\" M_BACKED2 = member 6 on the latched path \u2014 i.e., the mutant makes the default arm, on the LATCHED path (after the latch bit is set?), store idata into the array (\"member 6\" = the array store \u2014 some structure member index? member 3 vs member 6: premise 1 says \"a WRITE is DECLINED (member 3, not member 6 -- the cased siblings contradict a blanket store)\". Hmm \u2014 \"member 3, not member 6\" \u2014 maybe member 3 = the idata/byte written vs member 6 = pcctwo_reg array? So \"declining\" = not storing to member 6 (the array). M_BACKED2 = store to the array on the latched path. `sameoff val` = same-offset write then read-back: wants 0x0 (the write declined, array byte unchanged at 0x00) \u2014 mutant reads back 0x5a (stored). \u2713 killed for the right reason.\n\n#3 grok A: rows latch rd rd, latch rd wr. M_WRLATCH = latch SET only on writes (i.e., the `|=` update moved inside the MEM_WRITE branch? or conditioned on writeflag). Then reads never latch -> repeated reads announce every time. `latch rd rd` = read, read at the same offset: wants 1 announce, mutant prints 2 \u2713. `latch rd wr` = read, write: wants 1 (the write's announce suppressed by the latch set by the read), mutant prints 2 \u2713. Measured M_WRLATCH 34/36 red: latch rd rd, latch rd wr \u2713.\n\n#4 grok B: rows wide wr, wide rd (fate) + wide wr msg, wide rd srvd (label). M_WIDEKILL = `if (len != 1) exit(1);`. wide wr/wide rd (fate rows: host must survive) go red on host exit \u2713; wide wr msg/wide rd srvd also red (announce never printed) \u2713. Measured \u2713.\n\n#5 codex: latch aba. M_ABA: `=` instead of `|=`. latch aba wants 2, prints 3 \u2713. Measured \u2713.\n\n#6 fable5: \"inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows).\" M_OFFKILL = re-kill at 0x06: killed by inword wr + inword rd \u2713 measured. Hmm \u2014 but \"all 43 offsets both ways\" \u2014 named (18) by named wr/rd, interiors (12) by inword wr/rd, unnamed (13) by unnamed wr/rd. \u2713.\n\n#7 codex: the read announce RELOADS the array after the response was copied \u2014 a PRODUCT defect; fixed: data[0]. \u2014 Is this RIGHT? The reload vs data[0]: on the memcpy path, the array byte at announce time may differ from serve time (tick). Printing data[0] = the served byte. The announce text says \"served 0x%02x\" \u2014 printing the served byte is the truthful reading \u2713. Fine.\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT: is it a \"PRODUCT defect\"? The announce is diagnostic output; the SERVE was correct (memcpy); only the ANNOUNCE could misreport. Calling it a product defect (L1 repaired in-round) \u2014 it's a defect in the announce's truthfulness. The disposition ACCEPTED it as repaired. Fine-ish.\n\n#8: `(len %d)` in both announces; rows wide wr msg, wide rd srvd pin the label. \u2713.\n\n#9: L4 corrections \u2014 including \"the M_EXIT diagnosis -- the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong (opus5, codex, kimi)\". Hmm \u2014 wait: M_EXIT on pass 2: 16 red on 23 = 7 green. The numeral wrong \u2014 someone wrote the wrong count. Corrected. \u2713.\n\nHmm, and \"'would have shipped green' -- the gate FAILED 25/844 and caught it (codex)\". So an earlier claim \"would have shipped green\" was wrong \u2014 the gate caught it at 25/844. Corrected. \u2713.\n\n#10: opus5's safety sentence. \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress\". Hmm \u2014 scrutinize: is it true that the arm \"can only ADD OUTPUT\"? The repaired arm still SERVES reads (returns the byte) and DECLINES writes \u2014 these are behavior, but they replaced exit(1), and no working boot reaches the arm (it would have died). So on the set of boots that work today, the arm is dead code \u2014 unreachable \u2014 hence no regression. Sound. BUT \u2014 one hole: the arm is reached via the %= fold ALIAS (0x6a -> 0x2a)! \"alias 0x6a\" \u2014 a guest access at 0x6a hits the arm (folded). Under the OLD code (exit(1) in the arm), that access killed the host too. So still: any boot reaching the arm dies under old code. Sound.\n\nHmm \u2014 so which disposition is WRONG? Let me look again... Maybe #7's parenthetical: \"(Also makes 'the array's default, not a computed value' false for those twelve -- corrected in comment and docstring.)\" \u2014 \"docstring\" \u2014 the diff shows NO docstring change. Premise 2 says \"Three comment sentences corrected\". The disposition says \"corrected in comment and docstring\" \u2014 if the docstring exists and was NOT corrected (not in the diff), the disposition's parenthetical overclaims. But maybe the \"docstring\" is elsewhere (e.g., the pass-2 artifact header?) and the packet just doesn't show it. Premise 2 (the author's own summary) says THREE comment sentences \u2014 matching the diff exactly. The disposition (#7) says \"comment and docstring\" \u2014 DISCREPANCY with the diff, which contains only comments. Hmm \u2014 is there a docstring in dev_pcc2.c? C doesn't have docstrings; the \"docstring\" might be the kill-table artifact or the regression doc. Can't settle \u2014 but the DIFF (the thing under review) does not contain a docstring correction. If the docstring correction is claimed but absent, that's a defect in the repair as shipped... but premise 2 doesn't mention a docstring. Hmm.\n\nWait \u2014 maybe I should look for a more substantive wrong disposition. Let me re-read #6: \"Fable filed it as coverage debt; taking it in-round costs two sessions and closes the axis.\" \u2014 \"two sessions\" = the 2 new read rows (inword rd, unnamed rd). But wait \u2014 \"inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows)\" \u2014 the WRITE rows (inword wr, unnamed wr, named wr) were pass-2 rows; the READ rows (inword rd, unnamed rd) are new \u2014 2 new sessions \u2713. Hmm, and named rd was pass-2 (it's in the pass-2 seven of M_RDKILL). So reads previously covered only the named 18 (named rd) + ... hmm, pass-2 read rows: named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, dir rd text, srvd val... \u2014 did pass-2 have ANY read row at an interior or unnamed offset? Apparently not (hence Fable's escape at 0x06 \u2014 an interior). The repair adds inword rd + unnamed rd \u2713 closes the read axis for all 43. But \u2014 named rd covers the named 18; inword rd covers the interiors 12; unnamed rd covers the 13. Do the *msg* variants exist for inword/unnamed? No \u2014 but not needed; the fate/value rows suffice.\n\nHmm, what about #4's parenthetical: \"The pass-1 PRETABLE's own 'byte-and-wide' sentence had no row -- a CLAIM->ROW miss of the pre-table block itself.\" Fine.\n\nLet me reconsider #10 more adversarially: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress\". What is \"the old code\"? If \"old\" = pass-2 product (which already has the fix, no exit(1)), then the arm DOESN'T call exit(1) in the old code, and boots that work today (on pass-2 product) DO reach the arm (they get announces and served/declined accesses). Then the repair could regress them? The repair changes: announce text (len label, data[0] instead of reload), comments. Could the announce-text change regress a boot? fatal() output \u2014 if a guest boot is sensitive to... no, the guest doesn't see host stderr. Behavior identical (serve/decline/latch unchanged). So no regression either way. But the SENTENCE's validity depends on \"old code\" = pre-fix (exit(1)) \u2014 in context (\"the safety sentence of the record\" for the FIX), old = pre-fix. Sound as stated.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at the M_EXIT diagnosis correction again: \"the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong\" \u2014 pass-2 M_EXIT had 16 red rows on 23. In table 3, M_EXIT.old23 isn't shown! Table 3 shows .old23 only for the six pass-2 mutants (M_WRLATCH, M_WIDEKILL, M_OFFKILL, M_SRVD0, M_BACKED2, M_ABA) and BASE. M_EXIT.old23 is NOT in the table \u2014 so the \"16-row set\" isn't re-measured here. Not required.\n\nOK here's another angle for Q1's \"which accepted disposition is wrong\": maybe it's #7 \u2014 and the reason is the END-SPAN path! Consider: codex's defect claim is \"the read announce RELOADS the array after the response was copied\". The fix prints data[0]. But on the END-SPAN zero-fill path, the OLD code printed `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` \u2014 the ARRAY byte \u2014 while the guest actually received 0x00 (zero-fill). So the OLD announce was ALREADY wrong on the end-span path (printed the array byte, not the served 0x00). The fix (data[0]) corrects that too \u2713 \u2014 consistent with \"data[0] is then truthfully 0x00\". So #7 is right, and the fix is right on both paths. \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, actually, now let me re-examine the claim in premise 2 / disposition #7 once more: \"the SIGALRM tick rewrites the counter interiors between the memcpy and the announce\". For the announce to fire, it must be the FIRST access at that offset (latch). The memcpy serves the byte; the tick may fire between memcpy and fatal() \u2014 the announce would print the NEW byte, not the served one. \u2713 defect real. Fix right. \u2713.\n\nLet me now re-scan #2: \"Row `sameoff val` (the existing session gains a read-back; zero new sessions).\" \u2014 M_BACKED2's escape was \"member 6 on the LATCHED path\". Wait \u2014 what is \"the latched path\"? The arm: latch check -> if not latched, set bit + announce; then serve/decline. \"Member 6 on the LATCHED path\" = the mutant stores to the array AFTER the latch (i.e., on the SUBSEQUENT accesses \u2014 the path where the latch is already set)? Hmm \u2014 \"M_O2 -- the disposition on the latched path unpinned. Row `sameoff val` (the existing session gains a read-back; zero new sessions).\" So the sameoff session (write, read at same offset) gains a read-back \u2014 the session: write at offset X (announce #1, latch set), then READ at X (read-back of the array byte). BASE: the write declined -> array byte unchanged (0x00) -> read serves 0x00 \u2014 and the read announce is suppressed (latched). M_BACKED2: the WRITE... stores 0x5a into the array (on the latched path \u2014 meaning when the latch bit was already set? But the write is the FIRST access \u2014 latch not yet set). Hmm \u2014 unless \"latched path\" means the path AFTER the latch-update statement (the code path that runs once the latch has been updated \u2014 i.e., the disposition code: serve/decline). The mutant changes the disposition (member 3 = decline-idata vs member 6 = store-to-array): on the write, store idata to the array instead of declining. Then the read-back sees 0x5a. The pass-2 sameoff session counted announces (COUNT channel) \u2014 with the store, the announce behavior is IDENTICAL (announce once, latch, etc.) \u2014 so the pass-2 sameoff row (count channel) stayed green \u2014 the escape. The repair adds a READ-BACK to the same session (VALUE channel): read serves 0x5a \u2260 0x00 -> red \u2713. \"zero new sessions\" \u2713 (the session already existed; it gains a step). \u2713 disposition right.\n\nHmm OK. So maybe the wrong disposition is #12-14? No \u2014 those are REJECTED/not credited. The question says \"which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\" \u2014 so among 1-10 (accepted). Let me look at #9's items again:\n\n- \"kill-table header md5 (grok, codex, opus5)\" \u2014 correction of an md5 in a header. Can't check.\n- \"'three predictions exact' (fable5, codex, opus5)\" \u2014 hmm, corrected in place. \n- \"the M_EXIT diagnosis -- the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong (opus5, codex, kimi)\".\n- \"'would have shipped green' -- the gate FAILED 25/844 and caught it (codex)\".\n- \"`alias 0x6a` row vs docstring (grok)\".\n- \"`unnamed wr` overrun note (grok)\".\n- \"'four arms up' (grok)\".\n- \"'no guest reaches' (codex)\".\n- \"'unnamed' = the fork header (codex)\".\n\nThese are L4 (cosmetic?) corrections. \"L4s, corrected in place\" \u2014 fine.\n\nHmm \u2014 what about the M_EXIT numeral: \"the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong\". Wait \u2014 earlier in the header: \"Pass 2's five PASS/GO seats had not tried the six edits three seats then named; two of those were measured at 23/23 on the instrument those seats had passed.\" Fine.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: is disposition #7's product-defect claim actually WRONG because the announce prints data[0] but for the LATCHED read... no wait. Let me think about whether data[0] can ever NOT be the served byte. \n\nScenario: WRITE access. The memcpy above the switch \u2014 for a write, does the memcpy fill `data` FROM the guest's write data? If the memcpy is `memcpy(data, ..., len)` unconditionally... hmm, in GXemul devices the pattern is: the incoming `data` pointer already holds the guest's written bytes (for writes); for reads, the device fills `data`. If this fork's handler copies into a LOCAL `data` array: for writes, from the incoming buffer; for reads, from the array \u2014 \"the memcpy above the switch\" \u2014 \"READ keeps the byte the memcpy served\" \u2014 so the memcpy SERVES reads. For writes, what does the memcpy do? Probably copies the guest's bytes into a local `data` too (so `idata` = the guest's byte). Then data[0] on a write = the guest's written byte \u2014 the write announce prints idata (= data[0]? or a separate variable). Whatever \u2014 the read announce only fires on reads.\n\nScenario: the LATCHED read (second read at same offset): announce suppressed \u2014 no printing \u2014 fine.\n\nScenario: end-span zero-fill: data[0] = 0x00 = served \u2713.\n\nSo data[0] is the served byte on all paths per premises. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 the REAL potential defect I should hunt: premise 1 says \"one latched fatal() per offset per instance (`uint64_t warned_unimpl`, bit `relative_addr`, which is folded `%= PCC2_SIZE` (0x40) as the handler's first statement so the shift is 0..63 on a 64-bit type)\". Hmm \u2014 WAIT. The fold is \"the handler's first statement\" \u2014 relative_addr %= 0x40. But the announce prints `(int) relative_addr` \u2014 the FOLDED value! So an access at 0x6a announces offset 0x2a. The \"alias 0x6a\" row checks... \"alias msg\" \u2014 the announce text for the aliased access. If the announce printed the ORIGINAL offset (0x6a), the fold... the diff shows the announce prints `(int) relative_addr` \u2014 the folded one (since the fold is the handler's first statement, relative_addr is already folded at the announce). Hmm \u2014 but is that the RIGHT behavior? \"The %= fold above IS the real part's\" \u2014 the real part folds, so the announce naming the folded offset matches the real part's semantics \u2713. And the alias rows pin it. \u2713.\n\nNow Q2's \"is any cell of the closure still open \u2014 name the edit?\" \u2014 candidates:\n\n(i) **len 2**: disclosed, unrowed. A mutant keyed on len 2 (e.g., `if (len == 2) exit(1)` or serving/declining differently at len 2) sweeps all 36 rows (which use len 1 and len 4 only). OPEN but DISCLOSED. Also len 3 (if reachable). This is the main one.\n\n(ii) **Write-announce idata value**: is there a row pinning the WRITE announce's printed idata? The read side got srvd val text (M_O1). The write side: \"wide wr 0x5a5a5a5a\" (M_BACKED red row: wide wr \u2014 the VALUE channel: after a wide write (declined), the array shows 0x5a5a5a5a on mutant M_BACKED). Hmm \u2014 that's array state, not the announce text. The write announce's idata: \"named wr msg\" \u2014 what does it check? If it checks the full announce string including `0x%x: 0x%x` values, then idata IS pinned. Unknown from packet. If NOT pinned, a mutant `write announce idata -> 0` (print 0 instead of idata) sweeps clean \u2014 an unrowed cell SYMMETRIC to M_O1/M_SRVD0 on the write side! Hmm \u2014 this is a REAL candidate. The class \"announce value unpinned\" was closed on the READ side (srvd val text) but was it closed on the WRITE side? Premise 3's escape table lists M_O1 = \"read announce value -> 0\". The symmetric write-side mutant (\"write announce value -> 0\") \u2014 does any row catch it? Rows that check the write announce text: named wr msg, wide wr msg, wr declined, dir wr text, alias msg(?). If those check substrings like \"declined\" and \"(len 4)\" and \"unimplemented write\" but not the idata value... then the write-side value mutant escapes. The packet doesn't say what the msg rows match. Can't settle \u2014 NAME IT: \"does any row assert the write announce's idata value?\" \u2014 settling fact: the row definitions (what text `named wr msg` matches).\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but is the write-announce value part of the \"closure\" the repair claims? The repair's claim-to-row map: \"'served' means THE byte -> srvd val + srvd val text\" \u2014 the WRITE side's idata... premise 4's joint cells: \"Body: writeflag, idata, len, data[0]\" \u2014 idata is a body field! And \"Joint cells (body-tested writeflag x latch state...)\" \u2014 the rows listed: sameoff, rw one, rd rd, rd wr, aba, sameoff val. Is there a row testing the write announce's USE of idata? Hmm \u2014 \"dir wr text\" (direction phrase in the write announce), \"wr declined\" (\"declined\" text). If the write announce prints idata and no row pins it, then idata is an unpinned body field \u2014 an OPEN CELL: a mutant printing the wrong idata (or worse: STORING idata somewhere) \u2014 well, storing is sameoff val's job. The announce-value mutant on the write side: cosmetic (announce text wrong), same severity class as M_SRVD0 (which was accepted as an escape needing a row). So by the round's own standard (Opus's M_O1 was a BREACH), the write-side value mutant is the same class. If unrowed, the closure is open there. Can't settle from packet \u2014 name it.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually, let me reconsider: does `sameoff val` pin idata? sameoff val: write 0x5a at offset X, read back \u2014 VALUE channel (array byte / served byte). The write is DECLINED in BASE, so the read-back is 0x00 regardless of what the announce printed. It doesn't check the announce text. `named wr msg` \u2014 if it greps the full expected announce line `\"[ pcc2: unimplemented write to offset 0x2a (len 1): 0x5a -- declined.  (once) ]\"` then idata IS pinned by named wr msg. The pass-2 23 rows included named wr msg, and the pass-2 anchors survive `(len N)` \u2014 \"its anchors `offset 0x2a', `served 0x', the direction phrases and MSG all survive the `(len N)' announce\" \u2014 hmm! \"served 0x'\" is an anchor (read side value pinned by an anchor \"served 0x\" \u2014 hmm, \"served 0x\" is a prefix \u2014 that anchor survives but pins only the prefix \"served 0x\", not the full value? M_O1 printed \"served 0x00\" vs expected \"served 0x34\" \u2014 the anchor \"served 0x\" matches BOTH! So the pass-2 anchor did NOT pin the value \u2014 that's why M_O1 swept 23/23. The repair adds srvd val text wanting `served 0x34` \u2014 full value. On the write side, the anchor \"MSG\"... \"the direction phrases and MSG\" \u2014 MSG = \"declined\"? or the whole message? If the write-side anchor is \"declined\" (substring), the idata value is NOT pinned by it. So the write-side value mutant (announce prints 0 instead of idata) would keep \"declined\" matching -> green. OPEN CELL unless named wr msg / wide wr msg pin the full text including idata.\n\nThe packet is silent. So: **open cell candidate A: the write announce's idata value** \u2014 cannot settle from packet; settling fact: the exact match pattern of `named wr msg`/`wide wr msg` rows (do they assert the printed idata?).\n\nHmm \u2014 but actually, wait. Is a \"write announce prints wrong idata\" mutant a clause-(d) mutant? What's clause (d)? The packet never defines clauses (a)-(d). \"clause-(d) mutant\" \u2014 unknown definition. Probably from an earlier round's charter: classes of mutants (e.g., (a) exit restore, (b) latch removal, (c) direction swap, (d) value/announce mutants?). Can't settle. I'll note the term is undefined in the packet.\n\n(iii) **The latch-bit width / offset \u2265 0x40**: covered by alias rows \u2713.\n\n(iv) **Per-INSTANCE vs per-OFFSET latch**: M_ONELATCH covers per-instance-single; M_UNLATCH covers no-latch. What about per-instance-per-offset (correct) vs per-DIRECTION latch (latch separately for reads and writes)? grok A's mutant is \"latch SET only on writes\" \u2014 the reverse (\"set only on reads\") would be caught by... latch rd rd wants 1: mutant \"set only on reads\" \u2014 read sets latch; second read suppressed -> 1 \u2713 green... wait: latch rd rd (read, read): first read sets latch + announces; second read latched -> suppressed. Count 1 = wants 1 -> GREEN on \"set only on reads\"! And latch rd wr (read, write): read sets latch, write suppressed -> 1 \u2713 green! And latch sameoff (write, write at same offset): writes don't set the latch under \"set only on reads\" -> BOTH writes announce -> 2 \u2260 1 -> RED \u2713. And latch rw one (write, read): write doesn't set; write announces; read sets latch + announces -> 2? wants... latch rw one wants 1 (write announces, read suppressed) -> mutant prints 2 -> RED \u2713. So \"set only on reads\" IS caught (by sameoff, rw one). And \"set only on writes\" (grok A) is caught by rd rd, rd wr. Per-direction latch (separate bits for read and write): sameoff (w,w): first w announces+sets w-latch; second w suppressed -> 1 \u2713 green. rw one (w,r): w announces+sets w-bit; r announces (r-bit not set)+sets r-bit -> 2 vs wants 1 -> RED \u2713 caught. OK.\n\n(v) **The announce ordering / latch set BEFORE announce**: the diff shows `d->warned_unimpl |= ...; if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) fatal(...) else fatal(...)`. A mutant that announces BEFORE setting the latch: then the announce itself... count unaffected (the bit is set either way before the NEXT access). Announce-then-set: first access announces then sets; second access sees bit set -> suppressed. Same counts. Green sweep? Behavior identical! Because the set happens unconditionally on the same path. Unless the announce is fatal()-exits... it doesn't. So announce-before-set is EQUIVALENT \u2014 fine, not a defect (it's semantically identical).\n\n(vi) **The `(once)` tag**: a mutant dropping \"(once)\" from the text \u2014 pinned by msg rows? \"the house `(once)` shape\" \u2014 if the msg rows check \"(once)\", pinned. Unknown. Same category as (ii).\n\n(vii) **The zero-fill end-span path**: a mutant that exits or misbehaves on the end-span path \u2014 is there a row driving an end-span access? The 36 rows: wide accesses at len 4 \u2014 at which offsets? If a wide access at 0x3d-0x40 or similar spans the end... the rows \"wide wr/wide rd\" \u2014 offset unspecified. If no row drives the end-span path, a mutant `if (end-span) exit(1)` sweeps clean. Unknown from packet. Hmm \u2014 but is the end-span path IN the arm? The zero-fill is #119 \u2014 above the switch \u2014 a mutant there isn't an \"arm\" mutant (clause-(d)?). The floor question is about the arm? \"does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps the 36-row detector clean?\" \u2014 clause (d) presumably = default-arm mutants. The end-span zero-fill is outside the arm. Out of scope maybe.\n\n(viii) **`fatal()` return / continued execution**: fine.\n\n(ix) **The 18 named offsets' read path**: named rd rows exist \u2713.\n\n(x) **M_RDKILL green set**: 17 green \u2014 all write-side rows. Fine.\n\n(xi) Hmm \u2014 **the `sameoff val` row and the TICK**: sameoff val reads back the array byte and wants 0x0. If the same-offset is an INTERIOR (tick-rewritten), the read-back could be non-zero naturally! The row presumably uses a non-counter offset (so the byte is stable 0x00). If it used an interior, the tick race would make the row flaky. Unknown which offset the row uses. The packet's channel note: \"sameoff val: read-back VALUE under DK2 (and DKB)\" \u2014 DK2, DKB = session/test ids. Can't settle; the measured 36/36 BASE (all green, stable) suggests no flakiness in the run \u2014 one run though. Not a closure hole per se.\n\n(xii) **Wide write at len 4 spanning interiors**: wide wr wants the array unchanged (declined). \u2713.\n\n(xiii) What about a mutant that declines reads too (serves 0 instead of the byte)? A read mutant: \"read serves 0x00 regardless\" \u2014 then srvd val (the served-value channel \u2014 the guest's loaded register) goes red (wants 0x34, gets 0x00) \u2014 wait, srvd val is a pass-2 row: it checks the SERVED value on the CPU channel? Hmm \u2014 \"srvd val\" \u2014 from M_RDKILL's seven and M_EXIT's list; channel? PRETABLE's channel list doesn't give srvd val's channel explicitly (srvd val text is TEXT under DKZ; srvd val is presumably the CPU-loaded value under some DK). If srvd val checks the guest-visible served byte, then \"read serves 0\" is caught \u2713. Actually wait \u2014 but how does the guest \"receive\" the byte? The memcpy above the switch fills data from the array \u2014 the SERVE happens at the memcpy, BEFORE the arm! So the arm CANNOT change what the guest receives (the memcpy already served it). \"a READ keeps the byte the memcpy served\" \u2014 the arm does NOTHING on reads (just announces). So a mutant \"read serves 0\" would have to change the memcpy \u2014 outside the arm. So within the ARM, reads are announce-only; the serve is upstream. Hence \"read keeps the byte\" is structural. OK.\n\n(xiv) **Write side: what does the arm do to the array?** Nothing (declined). The store happens... where? For cased arms, each arm stores. The default arm must NOT store \u2014 the memcpy above the switch must be read-only-serve... hmm, wait \u2014 how does the emulator know a write was declined? In GXemul, the device's access function is called with the data; the WRITE effect happens inside the function (each arm stores). The default arm simply doesn't store = declined. \u2713 \"member 3, not member 6\" \u2014 some encoding: member 3 = ? eh.\n\n(xv) \u2014 **the latch update `|=` vs the guard**: M_ABA \u2713 rowed.\n\nSo the main OPEN cells I can name: (A) len 2 (and len 3 if reachable) \u2014 DISCLOSED in the packet (premise 4). (B) write-announce idata value \u2014 unknown whether rowed; symmetric to the accepted M_O1 escape. (C) end-span path \u2014 outside the arm presumably. (D) \"(once)\" tag / full-text pinning \u2014 unknown.\n\nGiven the question \"is any cell of the closure still open -- name the edit?\", the packet's own disclosure answers: len 2 is the disclosed open cell \u2014 \"a mutant keyed at len 2 (e.g., `if (len == 2) exit(1);`) has no row\" \u2014 that's the edit to name. And premise 4 says len is NOT tested by the arm... wait, \"len is NOT tested by the arm\" \u2014 hmm, the arm DOES now use len \u2014 in the ANNOUNCE TEXT `(len %d)`. \"len is NOT tested by the arm\" \u2014 the arm doesn't BRANCH on len (no behavior change with len) \u2014 only labels it. So a len-keyed mutant changes only the label or re-kills. The label at len 2: a mutant printing the wrong len value in the announce (e.g., `(len %d)` prints len+1) \u2014 caught at len 1 and len 4 rows? wide wr msg wants \"(len 4)\" \u2014 mutant prints \"(len 5)\" -> red \u2713; but a mutant that prints correct len for 1 and 4 and wrong for 2 \u2014 no row -> clean sweep. Same disclosed cell.\n\nSo Q2's answer: the only open cell I can DEFEND from the packet is the len-2 width cell \u2014 which the packet itself discloses (\"len 2 DISCLOSED\"); the closure claim is \"byte-and-wide\" where wide = len 4 only. Plus the write-side-announce-value question I cannot settle.\n\nNow \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider Q1's \"which accepted disposition is wrong, by number\" once more. The brief strongly implies at least one is wrong (\"be harder on the ACCEPTED dispositions\"). Candidates I've found weak: #7's \"docstring\" (not in the diff); #10's loose wording (but conclusion holds); #6's \"closes the axis\" (inword/unnamed rows \u2014 do the READ rows drive all 43? named rd (pass-2) drives the named 18 \u2014 but named rd drives ONE named offset or all 18? \"inword/unnamed rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways\" \u2014 hmm, this sentence says inword/unnamed rows drive all 43 \u2014 but inword covers 12 interiors, unnamed 13; the named 18 are driven by named wr/rd. Unless each row LOOPS over its class. Do the named rows loop over all 18? M_OFFKILL (re-kill at 0x06) is caught by inword wr/rd \u2014 so inword wr/rd reach 0x06 \u2713 (loops over all 12). A re-kill at a NAMED offset (e.g., `if (relative_addr == 0x2a) exit(1)`) \u2014 caught by named wr/named rd IF those loop over all 18. Fable's original escape was \"a re-kill at an unsampled offset\" \u2014 at 0x06 (an interior) \u2014 because