 for named, both ways existed already (named wr, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd). So plausibly named rows loop all 18. Can't settle. Hmm \u2014 the settling fact: whether the named rows iterate all 18 named offsets.\n\nOK let me now also double-check the arithmetic claims in the packet for internal consistency (self-contradiction hunting):\n\n- Premise 1: \"43 of the 0x40 offsets reach the arm: 18 named-unimplemented, 12 counter interiors, 13 the fork header leaves unnamed\". 18+12+13 = 43 \u2713. 0x40 = 64 offsets; 64-43 = 21 cased. Hmm \u2014 the twelve cased byte arms? \"SIBLING PARITY -- the twelve cased byte arms\" \u2014 12 cased ARMS, but 21 cased OFFSETS (multi-byte registers: e.g., a 32-bit register = 1 case covering 4 offsets). 12 arms covering 21 offsets \u2014 plausible (e.g., 12 cases, some 2-4 bytes wide: 12 arms with total width 21 bytes \u2014 e.g., 9 byte-arms + some wide \u2014 plausible). Not contradictory.\n\n- Premise 3 rows vs PRETABLE rows: premise 3 lists for grok A: rows latch rd rd, latch rd wr \u2014 \"want 1, print 2\" \u2713. For codex: latch aba \"wants 2, prints 3\" \u2713. Fable: inword wr/inword rd \"(all 12 interiors now)\" \u2713 matches PRETABLE \"inword/unnamed wr at 12/13\".\n\n- M_O2's row: \"sameoff val (wants 0x0, reads 0x5a)\" \u2713 matches M_BACKED2 prediction \"sameoff val 0x5a\".\n\n- Table 3's M_EXIT list has 29 entries; 36-29=7 \u2713 matches 7/36.\n\n- Gate 14: \"pcc2 default rows emitted 36, green 36\" \u2713 consistent with BASE 36/36. \"pcc2 width rows emitted 251, green 251\" \u2014 width rows 251? That's the width battery \u2014 fine, separate.\n\n- Premise 5: witness 7/13 committed, 13/13 on fix \u2713 consistent (not re-run this pass \u2014 \"unchanged\" \u2014 fine).\n\n- \"the frozen repaired instrument md5 10737375, live 10737375\" \u2713 same \u2014 the instrument used = frozen \u2713. Pass-2 instrument \"old23/ a96b8bcf\" \u2713 matches premise header \"frozen_probe.py (md5 a96b8bcf)\" \u2713.\n\n- PRETABLE mtime: \"written 2026-09-05 06:25:42\" and the diff target file mtime \"2026-09-05 06:25:42.901877900\" \u2014 the repaired product file mtime EQUALS the pretable write time (to the second). And the diff's source file (pass2.c) mtime 06:27:41 \u2014 LATER than the product file. Fine (artifact copied later). The claim \"written BEFORE the table (mtime against killtable3_20260905/driver.log)\" \u2014 the pretable at 06:25:42 vs driver.log mtime \u2014 driver.log mtime not given. Can't verify the ordering of pretable vs driver.log \u2014 but the product file's mtime (06:25:42) equals the pretable's timestamp \u2014 coincidence? The pretable says \"Repair from the nine-seat pass 2 (_scratchpad/panel_20260905_055656)\" \u2014 panel at 05:56, pretable 06:25, product edited 06:25:42, pass2.c artifact 06:27:41. So the product was edited at the same second the pretable was written \u2014 plausible workflow (write predictions, save). No contradiction. But NOTE: the diff header shows the PRODUCT file (dev_pcc2.c) mtime 06:25:42 \u2014 BEFORE the pass-2 artifact copy (06:27:41) \u2014 and the killtable3 driver.log presumably later still. Can't check driver.log's mtime \u2014 the \"written BEFORE\" claim rests on it. Name it? The packet says \"(mtime against killtable3_20260905/driver.log)\" \u2014 asserts it; I can't verify. It's a minor premise. I could mention it in one line.\n\n- \"BASE rebuilt in the clone, each mutant compile-checked before the table (12/12)\" \u2014 12 mutants: M_WRLATCH, M_WIDEKILL, M_OFFKILL, M_SRVD0, M_BACKED2, M_ABA, M_EXIT, M_UNLATCH, M_ONELATCH, M_BACKED, M_RDKILL, M_DIRSWAP = 12 \u2713.\n\n- Table 3 shows 12 mutant rows + 6 .old23 + BASE + BASE.old23 = 20 rows \u2713.\n\n- The six escapes vs the six mutants: M_O1 = M_SRVD0, M_O2 = M_BACKED2, grok A = M_WRLATCH, grok B = M_WIDEKILL, codex = M_ABA, Fable = M_OFFKILL \u2713 six. All six .old23 at 23/23 \u2713 \u2014 the breach claim CONFIRMED (none is NOT 23/23).\n\n- \"two MEASURED clean sweeps, four predicted\" \u2014 Opus measured M_O1, M_O2; the table now measures all six \u2713 consistent.\n\nNow, Question 1 again: \"is `data[0]` always the byte the guest received (memcpy path and end-span zero-fill path)?\" \n\nREAD premises: memcpy fills data (reads: from array = served; end-span: zero-filled = served). REASONED: yes on both paths \u2014 PROVIDED the memcpy precedes the switch and the end-span zero-fill is the serve. Not checkable from the packet's diff (the diff starts at the arm). NAME: the premise \"the memcpy above the switch fills data for reads\" \u2014 settling fact: the lines above the switch in dev_pcc2.c (the memcpy and the #119 zero-fill).\n\nHmm \u2014 one more wrinkle for data[0]: **len > 1 reads**: data[0] is the byte at relative_addr \u2014 but is THAT \"the byte the guest received\"? For a WIDE read, the guest received len bytes; the announce prints only data[0] but LABELS len \u2014 so the announce says \"served 0xNN (len 4)\" \u2014 a disclosure. Is data[0] the byte at the LOW address = the guest's low-order byte (little-endian) or...? If the guest is BIG-endian (m88k is big-endian!), a 4-byte read: the emulator's data buffer \u2014 memcpy(data, &reg[addr], 4) puts reg[addr] in data[0]; the guest's loaded word (big-endian) has reg[addr] as the MOST-SIGNIFICANT byte. \"served 0x%02x\" printing data[0] = reg[addr] = the byte AT THE OFFSET \u2014 the announce says \"unimplemented read from offset 0x%x (len 4) -- served 0x%02x\" \u2014 serving the byte at that offset \u2014 truthful regardless of endianness \u2713. And \"the byte the guest received\" \u2014 the guest received 4 bytes; the announce reports the first \u2014 with the len label, adequate. The question's phrasing \"always the byte the guest received\" \u2014 for len 1, exactly; for len 4, the byte at the offset (one of the four, disclosed). REASONED: fine, with the len label as the disclosure. Actually \u2014 mvme88k = Motorola 88k = BIG-endian. PCC2 on mvme88k. Does the memcpy fill data[0] with reg[relative_addr]? If the emulator's `data` for a big-endian wide read is ordered MSB-first, then data[0] = MSB = reg[relative_addr] \u2713 (the byte at the offset) \u2014 as long as the memcpy is host-order linear from the offset. \"the memcpy above the switch\" serving `data` linearly from `&reg[relative_addr]` \u2014 then data[0] = reg[relative_addr] \u2713. The packet's premise \"data was filled by the memcpy\" + \"data[0] is the byte actually SERVED\" \u2014 consistent. OK.\n\nHmm \u2014 WAIT. One more scenario for data[0]: **the WRITE at len 1 vs the read announce.** The read announce is in the else branch \u2014 reads only. \u2713.\n\nAnd: **`idata`** \u2014 the write announce prints idata \u2014 where does idata come from? Probably `idata = data[0]` above the switch (the guest's written byte). Fine.\n\nNow, which disposition is WRONG \u2014 let me take one more hard look at #7, because the brief says \"a seat whose escape was adopted scrutinises its own repair row MORE (does the row kill the mutant for the reason claimed, or by accident?)\". The seats whose escapes were adopted: opus5 (#1, #2), grok (#3, #4), codex (#5), fable5 (#6), codex (#7). Scrutinise the repair rows:\n\n- #1's row srvd val text: kills M_SRVD0 (35/36, red = srvd val text) \u2014 for the reason claimed (announce value) \u2713. But \u2014 ALSO scrutinise: does srvd val text stay GREEN on BASE? BASE 36/36 \u2713. And on M_BACKED (32/36 \u2014 reds: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, sameoff val, wide wr \u2014 srvd val text GREEN on M_BACKED \u2713 good, no accidental kill... wait, actually accidental RED on other mutants is FINE for detection (extra kills are safe-direction). It's accidental GREEN that's the problem.\n\n- #2's row sameoff val: kills M_BACKED2 \u2713; also red on M_EXIT, M_BACKED, M_RDKILL \u2713 consistent.\n\n- #3's rows: kill M_WRLATCH \u2713.\n\n- #4's rows: kill M_WIDEKILL \u2713.\n\n- #5's row: kills M_ABA \u2713; also red on M_EXIT, M_UNLATCH, M_ONELATCH, M_RDKILL \u2713 (aba count 3 on unlatch etc.).\n\n- #6's rows: kill M_OFFKILL \u2713.\n\n- #7 (codex's product defect): the fix data[0]. Scrutinise: is the DEFECT real? The old read announce reloaded `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` \u2014 after the memcpy serve. For the twelve counter interiors, the SIGALRM tick can rewrite between serve and announce -> announce misreports. Real \u2713 (given the tick premise \u2014 the tick exists: premise 2 \"the SIGALRM tick rewrites the counter interiors\" \u2014 READ). The fix prints data[0] = served \u2713. RIGHT.\n\nHmm \u2014 so maybe the \"wrong disposition\" is #10 after all. Let me stress-test #10 once more: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress.\"\n\nCounter-consideration: the OLD code called exit(1) \u2014 where? In the default arm, on ANY access (read or write) to the 43 offsets \u2014 the pre-fix code. But ALSO \u2014 hmm, does the OLD code's exit(1) live ONLY at those 43 offsets? The alias 0x6a -> 0x2a fold means accesses at 0x6a also hit the arm \u2014 same arm, same exit. OK.\n\nBut here's a hole: **the wide (len 4) accesses**. Pre-fix: did the arm exit on WIDE accesses at these offsets? If the pre-fix arm exited for ANY len, then no working boot issues wide accesses there either. Post-fix: wide accesses are served/declined. Fine.\n\nAnother hole: **the latch is per-INSTANCE** \u2014 \"one latched fatal() per offset per instance\". fatal() prints to stderr. A boot that works today... the safety sentence is about behavior, and announce output isn't guest-visible. Fine.\n\nHmm, what about: \"no boot that works today can regress\" \u2014 \"today\" = on the CURRENT committed tree (7076134), which has the ORIGINAL code (exit(1))? Or the current tree = pass-2 product (uncommitted)? \"THE TREE UNDER REVIEW IS GXEMUL-SEC/src/ ... UNCOMMITTED on top of 7076134\" \u2014 the fix is IN the uncommitted tree. \"Works today\" \u2014 a boot that works on... the sentence was adopted at pass 2 (about the fix). At pass 2, \"the old code\" = the committed original (exit(1) at the arm). A boot working on the original never reaches the arm. So the fix can't regress it. \u2713 Sound.\n\nUnless \u2014 a boot that works today ON THE FORK'S PRE-PASS-1 STATE... same thing. OK, #10 sound.\n\nHmm, what about #12? \"PASS on the 23-row instrument -- overtaken by the two MEASURED sweeps. Not a seat failure: a reading that did not build the edit. Recorded by name.\" \u2014 REJECTED disposition. Sound.\n\n#11: minimax's M_RDKILL prediction miss labelled \"unsafe\" \u2014 REJECTED as mislabeled: \"a predicted-GREEN row that went RED is the SAFE direction by the round's own rule\" \u2713 consistent with the PRETABLE's rule (\"one in the UNSAFE direction (a predicted red that stays green)\"). Wait \u2014 the PRETABLE rule: UNSAFE = predicted red stays green. Minimax's miss: predicted GREEN went RED = SAFE \u2713. The rejection of minimax's \"unsafe\" label is CORRECT per the rule. \u2713. And in THIS table, M_DIRSWAP's wr declined (unlisted/predicted-green by omission, went red) = SAFE direction, recorded \u2713. Consistent.\n\nHmm OK \u2014 so maybe the wrong disposition is subtler. Let me re-read #9: \"L4s, corrected in place: ... 'three predictions exact' (fable5, codex, opus5)\". \"Three predictions exact\" \u2014 a claim corrected. Hmm, we don't have the original context. \n\nAnd: \"`alias 0x6a` row vs docstring (grok)\" \u2014 corrected. \n\nHmm, and #9's \"the M_EXIT diagnosis -- the 16-row SET was right, the numeral wrong (opus5, codex, kimi)\" \u2014 the M_EXIT diagnosis correction \u2014 in THIS packet's table, M_EXIT = 7/36 (29 red). Pass-2 M_EXIT: 16 red on 23 (7 green). The pass-2 diagnosis apparently said something like \"M_EXIT kills 16\" but the numeral written was wrong (like \"17\"?) \u2014 corrected. Fine.\n\nHmm \u2014 WAIT. Let me recount the pass-2 M_EXIT green rows: 23 - 16 = 7 green on the pass-2 instrument. And in table 3, M_EXIT = 7/36 = 7 green. The SAME 7 green rows? The 23-row instrument's rows \u2282 the 36-row instrument's rows (23 old + 13 new = 36). M_EXIT kills all 13 new rows + 16 of the 23 old = 29 red; green = the 7 old rows that stayed green. So the 7 green are the same rows \u2713 internally consistent (M_EXIT exits on EVERY default-arm access, so every row whose session touches the arm dies; 7 rows never touch the arm \u2014 e.g., rows driving CASed offsets only? like ieicr/ieerr rdawr? no wait, those are red... the 7 green: rows that never reach the default arm \u2014 hmm, which rows would never touch the arm? Rows about CASed registers: alias 0x6a? no \u2014 red. Hmm: \"named wr, named wr msg\" \u2014 red. What's GREEN on M_EXIT? 36-29=7 green \u2014 not enumerated in the packet! The red list has 29; the 7 green are the complement. Which rows never touch the default arm? Possibly rows that drive CASed offsets exclusively: e.g., \"latch twooff\"? \u2014 red (in list). \"latch rw one\"? \u2014 red. Hmm... ieicr rdawr \u2014 red. Interesting: EVERY row I can enumerate is red except... the packet's 36 rows \u2014 I could only enumerate 29 from the red list! The 7 green rows are NOT NAMED anywhere in the packet. So there are 7 rows whose names I don't know (they're in the 23 pass-2 rows presumably \u2014 the pass-2 instrument had 23 rows; the packet names maybe 16 of them via the M_EXIT red list and the seven-of-pass-2 in M_RDKILL...). \n\nLet me try: the pass-2 23 rows, from the packet: named wr, named wr msg, named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, inword wr, unnamed wr, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch sameoff, latch twooff, latch rw one, alias 0x6a, alias msg, dir wr text, dir rd text, srvd val (17) + 6 more unnamed-in-packet = 23. The 13 new: srvd val text, sameoff val, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, wide wr, wide rd, wide wr msg, wide rd srvd, inword rd, unnamed rd, wr declined (12) + 1 more = 13. Hmm \u2014 so TWO rows are unnamed in the packet (6+1... wait: 36 total = 23 + 13; named-in-packet: pass-2: 17 named + 6 unknown = 23; new: 12 named + 1 unknown = 13). So 7 rows total are never named in the packet. And M_EXIT's 7 green = those 7? Plausible: the 7 green rows on M_EXIT are rows that don't touch the default arm \u2014 maybe rows like \"srvd wide\"? I can't know. FINE \u2014 the point: the packet does not name all 36 rows; my per-mutant checks used only the named ones. The scores are internally consistent (each red list length = 36 - score). Let me VERIFY each: \n\n- M_WRLATCH 34/36: red list 2 items (latch rd rd, latch rd wr) \u2713 36-2=34 \u2713.\n- M_WIDEKILL 32/36: 4 items \u2713.\n- M_OFFKILL 34/36: 2 items \u2713.\n- M_SRVD0 35/36: 1 \u2713.\n- M_BACKED2 35/36: 1 \u2713.\n- M_ABA 35/36: 1 \u2713.\n- M_EXIT 7/36: 29 items \u2713 (counted 29 \u2713).\n- M_UNLATCH 30/36: 6 items (latch sameoff, latch rw one, latch rd rd, latch rd wr, latch aba, wide wr msg) \u2713.\n- M_ONELATCH 31/36: 5 items \u2713.\n- M_BACKED 32/36: 4 items \u2713.\n- M_RDKILL 17/36: 19 items \u2713 (counted 19 \u2713).\n- M_DIRSWAP 30/36: 6 items \u2713.\n\nAll arithmetic \u2713. Internally consistent.\n\nPredictions vs measured \u2014 all scores match; the only seam: M_DIRSWAP's prediction lists 5 rows but scores 30/36 (6 red); measured 6 red including wr declined. Under \"MINIMUM red set\" framing: no contradiction; the minimum held (all 5 \u2286 measured 6). REASONED: the prediction's own score already implied a 6th red it didn't name \u2014 sloppy but not unsafe. Actually hmm, wait: if the MINIMUM is 5 and the prediction says 30/36, the prediction is asserting 6 red \u2014 i.e., it KNEW there was a 6th and didn't name it. If instead it meant \"exactly these 5,\" the score should be 31/36. Either way the measured 30/36 matches the SCORE. The row wr declined going red unlisted = SAFE direction per the round's rule \u2713 recorded, doesn't stop the round.\n\nNow \u2014 Q2 also asks: \"is any of the six pass-2 mutants NOT 23/23 on the pass-2 instrument (which would make the breach claim wrong)?\" \u2014 READ: all six .old23 rows show 23/23 \u2713. Breach claim CONFIRMED (the pass-2 detector was below floor for all six).\n\nHmm wait \u2014 one more check on the breach framing: \"Pass 2 found the 23-row detector below the floor six ways (two MEASURED clean sweeps, four predicted)\". A clean sweep = 23/23 GREEN (all rows pass on the mutant = mutant undetected). In table 3, the .old23 rows: \"M_WRLATCH.old23 23/23\" \u2014 the same notation \"X/36\" for the 36-row = green count. So 23/23 = all green = undetected \u2713 consistent. And BASE.old23 23/23 \u2713 (base passes all). \u2713.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 hmm, if the .old23 instrument is run on the SAME binary as the 36-row \u2014 \"Table 3 ... frozen repaired instrument md5 10737375, live 10737375; the pass-2 instrument old23/ a96b8bcf; scratch clone of build/, BASE rebuilt in the clone\" \u2014 the .old23 runs: on the same MUTANT binaries (M_WRLATCH etc.) \u2014 \"each of those six is scored by BOTH instruments: the pass-2 one (23/23 = the breach, measured) and the repaired one (red)\" \u2713 premise 3. Table \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 the \".old23\" runs were done with the PASS-2 instrument against the REPAIRED... no \u2014 the pass-2 instrument against the MUTANT binaries built from... the mutants are edits of the REPAIRED product (the 36-row table is on the repaired product's mutants). The .old23 runs score the same mutant binaries with the old 23-row instrument. M_WRLATCH (an edit of the repaired product) scored by old23: 23/23 \u2014 i.e., the old instrument can't see M_WRLATCH even in the repaired product \u2713 (the mutant class is orthogonal to the announce-text changes). \u2713 consistent with the breach story.\n\nHmm \u2014 but WAIT: there's a subtle issue. The six pass-2 escapes were measured (Opus) against the PASS-2 PRODUCT (pre-repair). Table 3's .old23 rows are against the REPAIRED product's mutants. Are these the same mutants? M_SRVD0 = \"read announce value -> 0\" \u2014 as an edit of the repaired product, the mutant prints 0 for the served value (data[0] path). As an edit of the pass-2 product, it printed 0 instead of the array reload. Slightly different edits, same class. The .old23 23/23 on the repaired-product mutants re-confirms the old instrument's blindness to the CLASS \u2713. Fine.\n\nNow Q3: \"Should #501 close and commit? Carry the denominator: which mutant shapes, which cells, what is excluded.\"\n\nThe composite floor: the 36-row detector catches all 12 measured mutants + the 6 escape classes; the closure covers: direction (read/write announces, dir texts), fate (serve/decline), value (served byte on CPU channel + announce text), latch (presence, per-offset, per-instance, accumulation, setter-direction x tester-direction), width (len 4 per direction, LABEL), offset coverage (43 both ways via named/inword/unnamed), alias (0x6a fold), once-text. EXCLUDED (disclosed): len 2 (and other lens), the write-announce idata value (if unrowed \u2014 can't settle), end-span path (outside the arm), and anything outside the default arm.\n\nAlso: gate 14 green (36/36 default rows; 251/251 width rows) READ \u2014 the composite's gate passes. The witness 13/13 on the fix (pass-2, unchanged \u2014 premise 5). \n\nCommit recommendation: the repair is right (per premises), the predictions hold (no unsafe miss), the six breach classes are closed and re-measured, the residual is disclosed (len 2) not hidden. So: CLOSE AND COMMIT, with the denominator: byte + len-4 accesses at the 43 offsets, both directions, latch axes, alias; excluding len-2/len-3 behavior, non-arm code, and (unsettleable from packet) the write-announce idata value if no row pins it \u2014 recommend either a row or an explicit exclusion before commit... hmm, but I shouldn't overreach: I'll say the packet DISCLOSES len 2; the write-side idata value pinning is not settleable from the packet and should be named in the close record either way.\n\nNow, back to Q1's \"which accepted disposition is wrong, by number?\" \u2014 I need to commit to an answer. Let me think REALLY hard about each again, looking for a substantive error:\n\n#1: M_O1 row srvd val text \u2014 fine.\n#2: M_O2 row sameoff val \u2014 fine.\n#3: grok A rows \u2014 fine.\n#4: grok B rows \u2014 fine.\n#5: codex aba \u2014 fine.\n#6: fable5 \u2014 \"taking it in-round costs two sessions and closes the axis\" \u2014 the axis = offset coverage. But does it? The named rows: do they drive all 18? If named rd/wr sample ONE named offset, the axis is NOT closed \u2014 17 named offsets unsampled... but wait, premise 3 (Fable): \"inword wr/inword rd (all 12 interiors now)\" and PRETABLE: \"43 offsets -> every one now written and read (inword/unnamed wr at 12/13, + inword rd, unnamed rd)\". This says inword/unnamed rows at 12/13 \u2014 i.e., inword covers the 12 interiors, unnamed the 13 \u2014 and the NAMED 18 are covered by... the PRETABLE sentence lists inword/unnamed wr at 12/13 \u2014 12+13 = 25 \u2014 plus named = 43. So \"every one now written and read\" requires the named rows to cover 18. Do they? The pass-2 rows named wr/named rd \u2014 hmm, if pass-2's named rows sampled only 0x2a (the anchor \"offset 0x2a\" \u2014 the pass-2 instrument's anchor is offset 0x2a!), then the named rows sample ONE offset (0x2a) \u2014 and 17 other named offsets are unsampled \u2014 the axis NOT closed! Hmm \u2014 \"its anchors `offset 0x2a'...\" \u2014 the old23 anchors include \"offset 0x2a\" \u2014 suggesting the named rows announce at 0x2a. If the named rows drive only 0x2a, then Fable's escape class (re-kill at an unsampled offset) survives at the 17 unsampled NAMED offsets \u2014 e.g., `if (relative_addr == 0x2c) exit(1);` sweeps the 36-row detector clean (if no row touches 0x2c)! Unless the inword/unnamed rows ALSO touch named offsets... no \u2014 inword = interiors, unnamed = fork-header. Hmm, but wait \u2014 maybe the repaired instrument's rows were REWRITTEN to loop: premise 3 Fable row: \"inword wr/inword rd (all 12 interiors now)\" \u2014 \"now\" = the repaired rows drive all 12. Did the repair ALSO extend the named rows to all 18? The PRETABLE: \"'43 offsets' -> every one now written and read (inword/unnamed wr at 12/13, + inword rd, unnamed rd)\" \u2014 it lists only inword/unnamed as driving 12/13 \u2014 the named 18 are NOT said to be driven by anything! If named wr/rd drive only one offset each, \"every one now written and read\" is FALSE for 17 of the 18 named. Hmm \u2014 but maybe the named rows were ALREADY looping all 18 at pass 2. The pass-2 anchor \"offset 0x2a\" \u2014 anchors are strings the rows match; a looping row over 18 offsets would have 18 announces with different offsets \u2014 the anchor \"offset 0x2a\" would match only the 0x2a announce \u2014 as an anchor (a substring that must appear) it works for a looping row too. Can't settle. \n\nHmm \u2014 so is THIS the wrong disposition \u2014 #6 (\"closes the axis\")? The axis closes only if the named rows drive all 18. M_OFFKILL's catch by inword rows shows the INTERIOR axis closed (all 12 driven). The named axis: unknown. The unnamed axis: unnamed wr/rd \u2014 do they drive all 13? \"inword/unnamed wr at 12/13\" \u2014 the PRETABLE asserts unnamed wr drives 13. OK so the packet ASSERTS 12/13 coverage for inword/unnamed but is SILENT on the named 18. Fable's escape was at 0x06 (an interior) \u2014 sampled now. But the CLASS \"re-kill at an unsampled offset\" \u2014 if named rows sample one offset, the class remains open at named offsets \u2260 the sampled one. Disposition #6 says the inword/unnamed rows \"now drive all 43 offsets both ways\" \u2014 12+13 = 25 \u2260 43 \u2014 unless \"inword/unnamed\" is shorthand for \"named/inword/unnamed\" (all three classes). The sentence: \"`inword`/`unnamed` rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows)\" \u2014 inword (12) + unnamed (13) = 25, not 43. For the sentence to be true, the named rows must drive the other 18 \u2014 but then it's the NAMED rows doing that, and \"2 new read rows\" refers to inword rd + unnamed rd... The sentence attributes all-43 coverage to the inword/unnamed rows \u2014 arithmetically FALSE as written unless the named rows already covered the 18 (in which case the sentence is misattributed but the coverage claim stands). So: the claim \"closes the axis\" (disposition 6) is only TRUE if the pass-2 named rows already looped all 18 \u2014 NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet. If they didn't, the axis is still open at up to 17 named offsets, and the answer to Q2's \"is any cell still open \u2014 name the edit\" is: `if (relative_addr == <any unsampled named offset>) exit(1);` \u2014 Fable's own shape, at a NAMED offset. Hmm! And the settling fact: whether the named wr/named rd rows iterate all 18 named-unimplemented offsets (or the instrument's row definitions).\n\nHmm, this is a genuinely good catch candidate. But is it THE intended wrong disposition? Let me reconsider... Actually wait \u2014 let me re-read premise 3's Fable line: \"Fable `if (relative_addr == 0x06) exit(1);` -> `inword wr`/`inword rd` (all 12 interiors now)\". And disposition 6: \"`inword`/`unnamed` rows now drive all 43 offsets both ways (2 new read rows). Fable filed it as coverage debt; taking it in-round costs two sessions and closes the axis.\"\n\nThe most natural reading: the rows TOGETHER (named + inword + unnamed, wr and rd) drive all 43 both ways; the disposition sentence sloppily names only the new ones. The QUESTION is whether named rows loop. Given the pass-2 instrument had \"named wr\" as a row and M_OFFKILL-like escapes were Fable's concern... Fable's escape was at 0x06 \u2014 an interior \u2014 NOT at a named offset \u2014 suggesting named offsets were already covered (else Fable/opus would have escaped at a named offset too \u2014 they were looking for escapes!). Opus MEASURED two escapes (M_O1, M_O2) \u2014 both announce-value/disposition mutants, not offset-coverage ones. Fable PREDICTED the offset-coverage escape at 0x06 \u2014 an interior \u2014 implying named offsets were NOT exploitable (already sampled). That's evidence (REASONED, weak) that the pass-2 named rows DID drive all 18. So the axis likely IS closed, and disposition #6 is right, with sloppy wording.\n\nHmm. OK so let me look for the wrong disposition elsewhere. Let me re-read #1 and #2 once more with the \"by accident\" lens:\n\n#1: \"M_O1 -- the announced value unpinned. Row `srvd val text`; the product now prints data[0].\" \u2014 Hmm, M_O1's escape was measured at 23/23 on the PASS-2 product, where the read announce RELOADED the array. M_SRVD0 (the table-3 mutant, on the repaired product) prints 0. The repair BOTH prints data[0] AND adds the row. Fine.\n\n#2: \"M_O2 -- the disposition on the latched path unpinned.\" \u2014 hmm, \"the LATCHED path\" \u2014 M_BACKED2 = \"member 6 on the LATCHED path\" \u2014 the store happens on the latched path \u2014 i.e., AFTER the latch check (the disposition code path). The row sameoff val: write then read same offset. Under M_BACKED2, does the FIRST write store? The write goes: latch not set -> set bit, announce, then disposition (store). The read-back sees the store \u2713 red. But wait \u2014 \"on the LATCHED path\" might mean the store happens only when the latch IS set (second access): then the first write doesn't store; the read (second access, latch set) \u2014 the READ stores?? \"member 6 on the latched path\" \u2014 if the mutant stores on the latched path, the READ at the latched offset stores... idata? The read's idata is garbage/0? Then the read-back (a THIRD access) sees the stored value... The row sameoff val: session = write X (announce), read X (read-back VALUE wants 0x0). Under M_BACKED2 (store on latched path): the read X is the latched path (latch set by the write) \u2014 the mutant STORES member 6 during the read?? Stores WHAT? If it stores idata (the guest's written byte from the write \u2014 still in a register?) hmm. The measured: sameoff val reads 0x5a \u2014 0x5a is what the detector WROTE. So the mutant stored 0x5a during... the write is the only place 0x5a exists (the guest wrote 0x5a). So the store happened at the WRITE \u2014 the FIRST access \u2014 the UNLATCHED path! \"member 6 on the LATCHED path\" \u2014 hmm, maybe \"latched path\" = the code path where the latch has just been updated (the announce path \u2014 as opposed to the silent path). I.e., the disposition (decline vs store) on the path that latches/announces. So M_BACKED2 = the announce-path write STORES instead of declining. Then the read-back sees 0x5a \u2713. And the pass-2 sameoff row (COUNT channel) missed it because the announce still happened identically \u2713. OK disposition #2 right.\n\nHmm, so let me now consider: maybe the intended wrong disposition IS #7's \"docstring\" claim, or... let me re-read #7 exactly: \"(codex) the read announce RELOADS the array after the response was copied -- a PRODUCT defect for the twelve interiors the tick rewrites. Fixed: data[0]. (Also makes 'the array's default, not a computed value' false for those twelve -- corrected in comment and docstring.)\"\n\nWait \u2014 \"makes 'the array's default...' false\" \u2014 the comment sentence \"the array's default, not a computed value\" was in the PRODUCT comment (pre-repair), describing what a READ serves: the array's DEFAULT value (i.e., the initialized 0x00), NOT a computed value. Codex's point: for the twelve counter interiors, the array byte is NOT the default \u2014 it's the TICK's computed value (the tick rewrote it). So the sentence was false for interiors. The repair corrects it to \"the array's current byte... for a counter interior that is the tick's value, not a default\" \u2713 in the diff. \"corrected in comment and docstring\" \u2014 the diff shows ONLY the comment. If a \"docstring\" (a doc comment elsewhere, e.g., the file header or the regress doc) contained the same sentence and was NOT corrected, the repair is incomplete. The packet premise 2 says \"Three comment sentences corrected\" \u2014 matching the diff. So disposition #7's \"and docstring\" is EITHER referring to something outside the diff (unsettleable) or WRONG (no docstring corrected). Since premise 2 (the author's own accounting of the repair) says three comment sentences and the diff shows exactly those, the \"docstring\" half of #7 is NOT evidenced. If there IS a docstring with the sentence, the repair diff fails to correct it \u2014 a defect in the repair. Either way: #7's parenthetical cannot be verified from the packet and conflicts with the diff's actual content. NAME IT as unsettleable; the settling fact: whether a docstring/doc-comment outside the shown hunks contains the \"array's default\" sentence and was corrected.\n\nHmm \u2014 but is that the intended \"wrong disposition\"? \"Which accepted disposition above is wrong, by number?\" \u2014 maybe the answer they want is #10. Let me stress #10 ONE more time, because it's flagged as \"adopted as the safety sentence of the record\" \u2014 a strong adoption, prime target.\n\n\"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress\"\n\nAttack: \"can only ADD OUTPUT\" \u2014 FALSE for the REPAIR? The repair diff changes the arm's OUTPUT only (announce text) \u2014 but the FIX (pass 1) changed behavior: serve + decline. The sentence is about the fix (\"where the old code called exit(1)\"). For the FIX: at the 43 offsets, old = exit(1); new = serve/decline + announce. For a boot working TODAY (on old code), no access reaches the arm (would exit). So no regression. \u2713.\n\nBUT \u2014 subtle: the arm is reached by accesses at ALIASED offsets (0x6a -> 0x2a) AND by WIDE accesses spanning... hmm, a wide access at 0x29 (len 4) spans 0x29-0x2c: relative_addr 0x29 \u2014 cased or default? If 0x29 is cased (say part of a register), the wide access takes the CASED arm \u2014 the default arm isn't invoked for the spanning bytes. So no.\n\nAttack 2: \"no boot that works today can regress\" \u2014 what about boots that DON'T work today (killed by the exit(1))? They might NOW work (improve) \u2014 not a regression. \u2713.\n\nAttack 3: THE LATCH IS PER INSTANCE \u2014 `warned_unimpl` is a member of `d` (per instance). Adding a member changes the struct size \u2014 ABI/layout \u2014 internal only. Fine.\n\nAttack 4: The announce is fatal() \u2014 if fatal() in THIS fork is print-AND-CONTINUE (as established), then... the sentence \"can only add output\" \u2014 TRUE for the fix's announce; but the fix ALSO adds the serve/decline behavior \u2014 the sentence says \"can only add output\" \u2014 the fix adds behavior too, but only where exit(1) was \u2014 so no working boot sees it. The SENTENCE's reasoning: \"the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), SO no boot that works today can regress\" \u2014 the inference holds regardless (anything added where exit(1) was can't affect boots that never reach there). \u2713 SOUND.\n\nOK \u2014 I'm now fairly convinced #10 is sound. Hmm.\n\nLet me re-read #8: \"(deepseek, grok, opus5, codex) the announce under-describes a wide access -> `(len %d)` in both announces; rows `wide wr msg`, `wide rd srvd` pin the label.\" \u2014 fine.\n\nAnd #9 items \u2014 all corrections, \"corrected in place\" \u2014 fine.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 let me re-read #12: \"PASS on the 23-row instrument -- overtaken by the two MEASURED sweeps. Not a seat failure: a reading that did not build the edit. Recorded by name.\" \u2014 and the header: \"Pass 2's five PASS/GO seats had not tried the six edits three seats then named\". Five PASS/GO seats; #12 lists kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax (+fable5 GO-WITH-CHANGES) = 4 PASS + 1 GO-WC = 5 \u2713. And the roster is nine seats: opus5, grok, codex, fable5, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax, agy = 9 \u2713. \"agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve)\" \u2713. Consistent.\n\nHmm \u2014 the header says \"two of those were measured at 23/23 on the instrument those seats had passed\" \u2713 = Opus's two.\n\nOK here's another thought \u2014 maybe the wrong disposition is #2's parenthetical: \"the existing session gains a read-back; zero new sessions\" \u2014 is sameoff val REALLY the existing sameoff session + read-back? The existing pass-2 row \"latch sameoff\" = write,write same offset (COUNT). If sameoff val = write, READ same offset \u2014 that's the rw-one SHAPE (write then read) but same-offset. Pass-2 had \"latch rw one\" (rw = read-write? \"latch rw one\" \u2014 hmm, \"W,R `latch rw one`\" from premise 4 \u2014 setter W, tester R. So rw one = write at offset A, read at offset B? \"one\" = ... hmm, \"latch rw one\" vs \"latch sameoff\" vs \"latch twooff\" \u2014 sameoff = same offset, twooff = two different offsets, rw one = ...? Premise 4: \"W,R `latch rw one`\" \u2014 the W,R cell. And \"latch twooff\" \u2014 setter and tester at TWO DIFFERENT offsets (bit A set by write at A; read at B announces \u2014 2 announces? twooff wants 2 \u2713). So rw one = write then read \u2014 at the SAME offset? Then what does sameoff val add \u2014 same SHAPE (write, read same offset) but checking VALUE instead of COUNT? Then \"the existing session gains a read-back\" \u2014 the EXISTING session = latch rw one (W,R)! rw one: write A (announce), read A (suppressed \u2014 latch set) \u2014 count 1 \u2713. sameoff val: write A (announce), read A \u2014 read-back VALUE 0x0. SAME session shape, different channel. \"the existing session gains a read-back; zero new sessions\" \u2713 \u2014 the rw-one", "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 360657102363}
