regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2665:_scratchpad\panel_20260905_081718\kimi.txt:67:  a) Announce text mutants that evade anchors: e.g., change "declined" to "Declined"? That would break `wr declined` row (counts "declined"). Change "(once)" text — not pinned by any row! The `(once)` token: no row counts "(once)". A mutant changing "(once)" to something else passes 36/36. Is that clause-(d)? Clause (d) seems to be "mutants of the new arm". Does the property require the literal "(once)"? The contract doesn't mention pinning "(once)". Hmm — is the "(once)" text part of the property? The property is: reads keep served byte, writes declined, one latched announce per offset. The "(once)" house idiom text is cosmetic. But it's a "smallest edit that breaks the property and passes 36 rows"? Changing "(once)" doesn't break the property. Question 4 asks for the smallest edit that BREAKS THE PROPERTY and passes 36 rows. So text tweaks that don't break the property don't answer Q4. But they do demonstrate the detector is "below the floor" if the floor is "every clause-(d) mutant dies". Actually the FLOOR QUESTION per the header: "does a clause-(d) mutant exist that sweeps the 36-row detector clean?" — a sweep = 36/36. Clause (d) = presumably "mutant of the repair" (need to know the taxonomy: (b) reinstate exit(1) verbatim; (d) mutate the new arm's logic). A text-only change like `%02x` → `%x`? Let's see: "served 0x%02x" → "served 0x%x": for srvd val text, served byte 0x34 prints "served 0x34" either way (0x34 has two digits). named rd srvd counts "served 0x" — survives. wide rd srvd counts "(len 4) -- served 0x00" — with %x, 0 prints as "0", so "served 0x0" — the row would FAIL (count 0). So that one dies. What about write announce `0x%x` idata → not pinned by value anywhere? dir wr text counts "unimplemented write to" only. wr declined counts "declined". The idata value printed is never checked! A mutant printing `idata` as 0 in the write announce: which row catches it? `wide wr msg` counts "offset 0x28 (len 4)" — substring before the value. No row counts the idata value in the write announce. So M: replace `(int) idata` with `0` in the write announce — sweeps 36/36. Does it "break the property"? The property per the fix: writes declined + announce. The announce says what was written ("0x%x" idata). A wrong value in the decline announce is a record defect — the write announce would lie about the value. By analogy with M_O1 (read announce printing wrong value was a MEASURED escape and was repaired), the write announce printing a wrong value is the SAME defect class — and no row pins it! Wait — does any row? Let me scan the 36 rows: "dir wr text" counts phrase; "wr declined" counts "declined"; "wide wr msg" counts "offset 0x28 (len 4)". `srvd val`/`srvd val text` are read-side. The idata VALUE in the write announce is unpinned. That's a genuine M_O1-shaped hole on the write side: `srvd val text` repaired the read side; the write side got no corresponding `wd val text` row (e.g., counting "0x34" in the write announce or ": 0x5a " text). Hmm, but does the brief's contract claim to cover this? The contract line: "the read announce printing a wrong or constant byte ... -> + `srvd val text'". It only mentions the read announce. The write announce's value is not covered by any contract line. So there IS an open cell: the write announce value channel. A mutant `idata -> 0` (or printing relative_addr twice, etc.) passes 36/36. That's a clause-(d)-shaped edit (one token) that sweeps clean — the very shape pass 2 was punished for missing (Opus M_O1 on the read side). This is a strong NO-GO/GO-WITH-CHANGES finding.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2666:_scratchpad\panel_20260905_081718\kimi.txt:112:  What about a mutant that stores on writes ONLY at offsets ≥ 0x20 (unnamed)? `unnamed wr`/`unnamed rd` rows only check survival and counts, not values! A backed store at unnamed offsets: is there a read-back-value row at an unnamed offset? `unnamed rd` is fate only (survived). No rdawr at an unnamed offset. So M: `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && relative_addr >= 0x20 && relative_addr < 0x28) pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] = idata;` — sweeps clean? Check rows: unnamed wr survives (yes), unnamed rd survives, MSG counts: each announce still prints (latch set before/after — order: if (!(bit)) { set; announce } — announce happens regardless of store). dir texts fine. Hmm — but does any row read back a value at an unnamed offset? No. The rdawr rows are at IEICR/IEERR (0x2a/0x28, named). So a backed store limited to the 13 unnamed offsets passes 36/36. Is that clause-(d)? It's a member-6 variant scoped to offsets the value rows don't sample. Fable's M_OFFKILL repair drove all offsets for FATE but not for VALUE. The "43 offsets each written and read once" closure claim covers survival, not disposition-per-offset. The disposition rows (rdawr) sample only 2 named offsets + wide at IEERR + T1CMP interior. So a backed-store mutant at an unsampled offset (e.g., only 0x39) sweeps clean. Hmm — but is that "the property"? The property is "writes declined" — a per-offset-scoped backed store breaks it at that offset, and no row sees the value there. HOWEVER — the pass-1 compression argument: "valid because the fix has no per-offset branch". A mutant CAN introduce a per-offset branch (that's what M_OFFKILL did — but M_OFFKILL was caught by FATE because it KILLED at 0x06; a STORE at 0x06... wait, is there a value row at 0x06? inword rd is fate-only. srvd val is at 0x05 (T1CMP interior — cased word arm at 0x04-0x07? T1CMP=0x04, so bytes 0x04..0x07 are the word; 0x05 read in srvd val session). M_BACKED (store everywhere) is caught at ieicr/ieerr/sameoff/wide. But M_BACKED_AT_ONE_UNNAMED_OFFSET: no value row. So the "every offset both directions" closure closes the FATE axis, not the DISPOSITION axis per offset. The table's own logic: M_BACKED is caught by 4 rows none of which is at an unnamed offset. So yes — second open cell: per-offset-scoped member 6 at an unnamed (or inword-other) offset sweeps clean. This is exactly the Fable-shaped escape (re-kill at unsampled offset) lifted to the VALUE channel — the same shape pass 2 was burned by, one channel over.
regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2669:_scratchpad\panel_20260911_173912\grok.txt:1:I'll treat this as a closing-pass review: first reconstruct the 32-row detector and the measured mutants from the tree, then hunt for a clause-(d) escape rather than ratify the green table.HEAD matches the checkpoint. Next I’ll open the product sites, the 32-row probe, and the kill-table artifacts.The p_bit=1 load cell looks unrowed — I’ll confirm dispatch, the remaining `arm_push` slow path, and whether a POP-side `M_PBITCOND` would actually sweep.The dump command already 16-byte-aligns, so the `read_mem32` repair is the right shape. Next I’ll measure the unrowed `p_bit=1` load cell in a scratch clone.**VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH — do not close the chain.** `M_PBITCOND_POP` is a clause-(d) mutant the 32-row detector cannot see. The class closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` actually used and left the load-side `p_bit=1` cell open (`#499` again). Mask-width (`~1` / `~7` / `~15`) is closed; the predicate × site product is not.
