regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth\panel_20260912_095238_ppcpass2\codex.txt:2780:_scratchpad\panel_20260906_193000_m88k_p10\ollama_glm.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-07T00:37:02.390341017Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me carefully parse this brief. It's a \"pass 10\" review of a repair to a regression detector for an MC88100 exception-delivery path in GXemul. I'm asked to audit the premises as stated, identify contradictions or non-following derivations, and answer the one question: does this repair reproduce the defect it repairs (a bit closed at one cell claimed as an axis)?\n\nKey facts from the packet:\n\n- PASS 9 found: the repair it reviewed closed an axis at ONE cell out of seven while the contract claimed the whole axis. Specifically: \"that closure covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven.\"\n- Pass 9 corrected the denominator: SIX settable status bits, not five \u2014 eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses \u2014 so 7 arms \u00d7 6 = 42 cells, minus 2 unreachable = 40, where pass 8 claimed 35.\n\nWait \u2014 let me check that arithmetic. 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits = 42. Minus 2 unreachable = 40. OK. Pass 8 claimed 35. Hmm, pass 8's claim of 35... where would 35 come from? 7 \u00d7 5 = 35. Yes \u2014 7 arms \u00d7 5 bits = 35. So pass 8 used 5 bits; pass 9 corrected to 6 bits, giving 42 - 2 unreachable = 40. That's internally consistent.\n\nBut wait \u2014 what are the \"2 unreachable\"? Not specified in the packet. That's a premise I can't settle: which two cells are unreachable and why. Actually the packet says \"minus 2 unreachable = 40\". Hmm, is that checkable? Not from the packet \u2014 we don't know which 2 cells are unreachable. But the arithmetic 7\u00d76\u22122=40 is consistent.\n\nHmm, but wait: \"eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses\" = 6 settable bits. OK. So 6 settable bits: carry, interrupt-disable, serial-mode, and... three more. The packet mentions \"carry and interrupt-disable bits\" as closed by the five dispatch-arm rows plus serial-mode. So bits mentioned: carry, interrupt-disable, serial-mode. Three others unnamed. Not checkable which three others; fine.\n\n- THE REPAIR (detector v16 \u2192 v17, 57 fault rows + 2 controls): \"No product code. Three rows added and five re-valued.\" Wait \u2014 later the second question says \"THREE new rows; FIVE rows re-valued\". But the repair description lists FOUR bullet points:\n  * the five dispatch-arm rows share a status value that now carries serial-mode (that's the \"five re-valued\" \u2014 the five dispatch-arm rows re-valued)\n  * `ovftbser` \u2014 the overflow arm, where no other row sets the bit \u2014 that's a new row\n  * `resid50ser` \u2014 the fallback \u2014 another new row\n  * `errser` \u2014 the non-trap branch, which closes the misaligned arm at the same time \u2014 another new row\n\nSo three new rows: `ovftbser`, `resid50ser`, `errser`. Five re-valued: the five dispatch-arm rows. That's 3 + 5 = 8 rows touched. The header says \"detector 54 -> 57 rows\" and \"Three rows added and five re-valued.\" 54 + 3 = 57. Consistent. \u2713\n\n- Pass 9 said \"the same shape at the re-route's TRAP side AND at the dispatch arms -- eight rows, called a 'class closure'\". So pass 8's repair added eight rows. And pass 9 found that closure covered serial-mode at ONE arm of seven.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think about the axis structure. \"7 arms \u00d7 6 bits = 42 cells\". So the axis is arms \u00d7 bits. The serial-mode bit needed closing at 7 arms. Pass 8's repair closed it at one arm. This repair (pass 9's, reviewed in pass 10) closes it at... let's see:\n\nThe three new rows:\n* `ovftbser` \u2014 the overflow arm (arm #1)\n* `resid50ser` \u2014 the fallback... is the fallback an \"arm\"? The axis is 7 arms. Hmm. Let me re-read: \"7 arms x 6 bits = 42 cells\". The sites mentioned across the chain: fallback (2 rows at the fallback, pass 6), re-route non-trap side (2 rows, pass 7), re-route trap side AND dispatch arms (8 rows, pass 8).\n\nSo the seven arms might be: fallback, re-route non-trap, re-route trap, and four dispatch arms? Or: the five dispatch arms plus overflow plus misaligned = 7 arms? Let's count dispatch arms: \"the five dispatch-arm rows share now carries the serial-mode bit, so those five close that bit at their arms\". So there are five dispatch arms. Plus overflow arm = 6. Plus misaligned arm = 7? \"errser -- the non-trap branch, which closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there.\" So the seven arms could be: 5 dispatch arms + overflow arm + misaligned arm = 7. And the fallback and re-route are... separate sites not counted as arms? Hmm, but pass 6 and 7 repaired the fallback and re-route.\n\nActually the question asks: \"is any of the three new rows closing its site at one *arm* the way the row it replaces did?\" Hmm wait, the three new rows close serial-mode at: the overflow arm (`ovftbser`), the fallback (`resid50ser`), the non-trap branch / misaligned arm (`errser`).\n\nHmm, so the fallback is being treated as a \"site\" \u2014 but is the fallback one of the 7 arms? The packet says \"7 arms x 6 = 42 cells\". If the fallback is an arm, then the 7 arms might be: fallback, re-route non-trap, re-route trap, and 4 dispatch arms? But there are 5 dispatch arms...\n\nLet me try: sites named: fallback (pass 6), re-route non-trap (pass 7), re-route trap (pass 8), dispatch arms (pass 8). Then the serial-mode closure at 7 sites: 5 dispatch arms + overflow + misaligned? That gives 7 \"arms\" for the serial-mode axis. And the fallback, re-route trap, re-route non-trap are... different axes?\n\nActually, from pass 9's finding: \"that closure covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven.\" So serial-mode needed to be closed at 7 arms. Pass 8's repair closed it at one arm of the seven. Which arms are the seven? Candidates from the packet: five dispatch arms + overflow arm + misaligned arm = 7. That works numerically.\n\nBut then `resid50ser` \u2014 the fallback \u2014 \"whose own blurb already claimed every settable bit on both sides\". The fallback's blurb claimed every settable bit on both sides. So the fallback needed serial-mode on both sides (trap and non-trap?). Hmm.\n\nAnd `errser` \u2014 the non-trap branch \u2014 \"closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there.\" So errser covers the non-trap branch site AND the misaligned arm.\n\nSo serial-mode at 7 arms: the repair covers:\n- 5 dispatch arms (via the five re-valued rows)\n- overflow arm (`ovftbser`)\n- misaligned arm (`errser`'s second fault)\n\nThat's 7 arms. \u2713 Plus the fallback (`resid50ser`) and the non-trap branch (`errser`'s first fault) \u2014 but wait, are those part of the 7 arms or additional sites?\n\nHmm, here's a potential defect: the question says \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\". Previously-closed 23? Where does 23 come from? Hmm. \"the previously-closed 23\". Hmm \u2014 40 cells total; pass 9 found serial-mode closed at 1 of 7 arms. Let me think.\n\nActually maybe: 40 cells = 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits \u2212 2 unreachable. Before this repair, serial-mode was closed at 1 arm (by pass 8's repair). The other 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35 cells were closed by previous passes (pass 8 claimed 35 \u2014 the \"class closure\" of eight rows closed... hmm, pass 8 claimed 35 cells closed). So previously-closed = 35 cells (5 bits \u00d7 7 arms) minus... wait, but pass 9's finding was that serial-mode was closed at 1 arm. So of the 42 (or 40) cells, the serial-mode row of 7 cells had only 1 closed; the other 5 bits \u00d7 7 = 35 closed. So previously-closed = 35 + 1 = 36? But the question says \"the previously-closed 23\". Hmm, that doesn't match 36.\n\nWait \u2014 \"the polarity twin ... (CONTROL) | 23/41\" \u2014 in the table, the polarity twin scores 23/41 on frozen v16. Hmm, 41? The detector has 57 fault rows. 23/41 on v16 and 30/57 on v17. Hmm, 41 vs 57 \u2014 different denominators for the same control mutant on v16 vs v17? That's odd. Oh wait \u2014 maybe on frozen v16 the detector had 54 rows, and the control caught 23 of... 41? Hmm, 54 rows, control caught 23, so 23/54? But it says 23/41. And on v17, 30/57. The denominators differ (41 vs 57) for the same control mutant across two detector versions. v16 has 54 fault rows, v17 has 57 fault rows. The sweep mutants score \"54/54\" on v16 (all 54 rows fire \u2014 sweep) and 56/57 on v17 (one row doesn't fire: the named row catches it... wait).\n\nHold on. Let me parse the table semantics. \"Each mutant scored on BOTH the instrument pass 9 reviewed and the repaired one\" \u2014 so each mutant (fault injected into the product code) is scored by running the detector: how many detector rows fire? For the sweep mutants on frozen v16: 54/54 \u2014 all 54 rows fire. That's a \"SWEEP\" \u2014 the detector can't distinguish; every row catches it. On v17: 56/57 \u2014 56 of 57 rows fire, and \"the row that caught it\" is named (e.g., `div0tbc`). Hmm, so on v17, 56 rows fire and the \"row that caught it\" is the one that DIDN'T fire? That doesn't make sense either.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the score is \"rows that fire\" and the sweep on v16 means all 54 fire (bad \u2014 no localization, but detection is total). On v17, 56/57 fire, meaning 57 rows total and 56 fire \u2014 still nearly a sweep? That would be bad, not good. Unless the score is inverted: \"56/57\" means 56 rows PASS (don't fire) and the one that fires is the catcher. That makes more sense! On v16, the sweep: 54/54 \u2014 all 54 rows... hmm, \"54/54 SWEEP\" \u2014 if the score is rows that fire, a sweep is all firing. If it's rows that fire = 54/54, then every row catches it.\n\nHmm wait. Let me think about what a \"sweep\" means in this chain's history. Pass 6: \"a status-bit conjunct sweeps the FALLBACK predicate\" \u2014 a mutant (fault) in the product code where a status-bit condition sweeps (covers all of) the fallback predicate, so all rows... Hmm.\n\nAlternative reading: the mutants are faults injected into the DETECTOR (the instrument), and the score is how many of the... no wait, \"Each mutant scored on BOTH the instrument pass 9 reviewed and the repaired one\". The instrument = the detector. So mutants are faults in the PRODUCT (the CPU model's exception-delivery path), and the detector (instrument) scores them: does each detector row catch the mutant?\n\n\"54/54 SWEEP\" on frozen v16: all 54 detector rows fire (catch) the mutant. That's a sweep \u2014 every row catches it, meaning no localization: the detector can't tell you where. Hmm, but for detection purposes, being caught by all rows is still \"caught\". But the chain's concern is that each row should be a precise assertion (\"this cell closed at this arm\"), so a mutant caught by ALL rows means the rows are not discriminating... Hmm, actually no.\n\nWait, actually, I think the semantics is: each detector row is a fault-injection test (a \"fault row\" \u2014 57 fault rows + 2 controls). Each row injects a fault into the product and checks the detector catches it? No...\n\nLet me re-read: \"detector 54 -> 57 rows\" \u2014 the detector is a table of fault rows. \"57 fault rows + 2 controls\". Table 14: \"Each mutant scored on BOTH the instrument pass 9 reviewed and the repaired one, so a prediction becomes a measurement in one direction and a closure becomes a measurement in the other.\"\n\nMutants listed: \"case ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: implemented = !(PSR & SER)\" \u2014 that's a fault injected into the product code (changing the behavior of an instruction to depend on the serial-mode bit, SER). \"fallback gains && !(PSR & SER)\" \u2014 product fault. \"re-route gains && !(PSR & SER)\" \u2014 product fault. \"polarity twin implemented = (PSR & SER) != 0 (CONTROL)\" \u2014 control.\n\nSo mutants are faults in the PRODUCT. Scored on the instrument: run the detector against the mutant product, count how many detector rows fire (detect/differ).\n\nOn frozen v16: the ILLEGAL_DIVIDE mutant causes 54/54 rows to fire \u2014 every fault row's expected outcome changes? That seems weird for a mutant that only changes divide behavior. Unless... the detector rows are fault-injection rows: each row = inject fault N, run, compare against reference. If the product mutant changes behavior globally, then... hmm, no.\n\nHmm, alternatively: each detector row injects a fault into the product and asserts the model's behavior matches the reference. When the product has an extra mutant (like `implemented = !(PSR & SER)` on divide), the model's behavior differs from the reference in many scenarios, so many rows fire. A \"sweep\" = all rows fire = the instrument can't localize but does detect.\n\nOn v17: 56/57 \u2014 56 rows fire out of 57? And \"the row that caught it\" = `div0tbc`. Hmm, if 56 fire and one doesn't, the \"row that caught it\" would be among the 56 that fired... but then which one is special?\n\nAlternatively the score might be \"rows that PASS\": on v17, 56/57 pass and the one that fails is the catcher (`div0tbc`). That is: under the repaired detector, the mutant is caught by exactly ONE row (the precise one), and 56 rows correctly don't fire (pass). That's the ideal: precise localization. And on frozen v16, 54/54 \u2014 hmm, all 54 pass? Then the sweep would mean... no, \"SWEEP\" is bolded as bad on v16.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me reconsider: maybe on v16 the sweep means all 54 rows fire (fail), because the serial-mode bit was closed at only one arm \u2014 the mutant `!(PSR & SER)` on divide... hmm, why would closing a bit at only one arm cause all rows to fire?\n\nLet me think about this differently. This is a mutant-testing (mutation testing) setup: the detector is a mutation-testing instrument for the CPU model. Each \"fault row\" = a mutant of the product that the detector claims to catch (kill). The detector runs the product with fault N injected and checks that some test detects the divergence. So \"54/54 SWEEP\" for a mutant... no wait, the mutant IS the thing scored.\n\nOK here's another reading: Table 14's mutants are mutants of the PRODUCT, and the score is how many detector rows' assertions the mutant violates. The detector rows each assert a specific cell closure: \"if I inject fault F at arm A with bit B set, the model diverges from the architectural reference at that point.\" Hmm, that doesn't quite work either because the detector rows inject faults.\n\nActually \u2014 maybe simplest: the detector is a fault-injection regression suite. Each row = (inject fault into product, expected detection). Table 14's four \"mutants\" are faults injected into the PRODUCT MODEL ITSELF (i.e., they simulate the detector's fault injections being real). The score = number of detector rows that \"catch\" (fire/detect) the mutant.\n\n- On frozen v16, the ILLEGAL_DIVIDE-with-SER mutant is caught by ALL 54 rows (54/54 sweep). A sweep is bad in this framework because... hmm, actually in the history table, \"a status-bit conjunct sweeps the FALLBACK predicate\" (pass 6) was a defect FOUND \u2014 the defect was in the PRODUCT: a predicate that should gate on one thing was gated on a status bit, so it \"sweeps\" (overrides) the whole fallback. So the mutants in Table 14 replicate the historical defect class in the product: adding `&& !(PSR & SER)` conjuncts that sweep predicates.\n\nSo the mutant `case ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: implemented = !(PSR & SER)` makes integer divide unimplemented when SER is set \u2014 reproducing the pass-6 defect shape (a status-bit conjunct gating behavior). The detector should catch this.\n\nOn frozen v16: 54/54 sweep \u2014 every detector row fires. Why would every row fire? Because... hmm, maybe because the detector's rows each check \"model matches reference under fault N\", and the product mutant makes the model diverge everywhere, so all rows fire. A sweep = the instrument detects it but can't localize \u2014 or maybe a sweep means something specific: the conjunct sweeps the predicate so the behavior change is total (unconditional-ish), so all rows fire. The point: on v16 the mutant is caught by everything (no single row is responsible \u2014 or rather, the mutant is \"swept\", i.e., all rows catch it, which paradoxically means the specific closure the detector claims is NOT what catches it).\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I have the direction backwards. Let me reconsider: maybe the score is \"how many rows PASS (do not fire)\". On v16, the ILLEGAL_DIVIDE mutant: 54/54 rows pass \u2014 NO row catches it! That's the defect pass 9 found: the serial-mode bit was closed at only one arm, so a mutant that changes divide behavior when SER is set... hmm, but then \"SWEEP\" would be mislabeled.\n\nHmm, hmm. \"54/54 SWEEP\" \u2014 in the pass-9 review (pass 9 of this chain), \"All three sweeps were PREDICTED BY READING on pass 9 and explicitly not built by the seat that named them; this table built them.\" So pass 9 predicted that these three mutants would sweep the frozen v16 detector. Pass 9's finding: the serial-mode bit was closed at ONE arm of seven \u2014 so on v16, mutants involving SER would... sweep? Why would under-closure cause a sweep?\n\nLet me think: if the detector has a row that closes SER at only one arm (say, the divide arm via `div0tbc`... wait, `div0tbc` \u2014 divide-by-zero trap bit carry? \"div0tb\" = divide-by-zero trap, \"c\" = carry? Hmm: row names seem to be <site><bit>: `ovftbser` = overflow trap + SER? `resid50ser` = reserved-50 + SER. `errser` = error + SER. `div0tbc` \u2014 hmm, that doesn't fit <site><bit> unless `c` = carry? But it \"caught\" the ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE mutant.\n\nOK here's the thing: I think the score direction is: rows that FIRE (catch). On v16, the ILLEGAL_DIVIDE mutant sweeps: all 54 rows fire. Why? Because the detector's rows are fault-injection rows: each row injects a fault and expects specific behavior. If the product itself is mutant (divide unimplemented when SER set), then... hmm, when the detector injects fault F and runs, the model has BOTH fault F and the mutant M. The detector compares against expected outcome. If M changes behavior at the divide site only when SER is set, then rows that run with SER set and touch divide would differ...\n\nHonestly, the exact mechanism isn't fully determinable, and maybe that's fine. But the key numbers to audit:\n\n1. Sweep mutants: 54/54 on v16, 56/57 on v17, with a single named catching row each. Wait \u2014 56/57 with a single catching row? If one row catches it, the score should be 1/57, not 56/57. Unless the score counts rows that DON'T fire (pass). So on v17, 56 rows pass, 1 fires (`div0tbc` catches it). And on v16, 54/54... all 54 pass?? Then nothing catches it \u2014 but that contradicts \"SWEEP\".\n\nHmm wait, unless on v16 the score 54/54 means all 54 rows FIRE (sweep = everything catches it), and on v17 the score 56/57 means 56 rows fire and one doesn't... and the \"row that caught it\" is... no.\n\nLet me try yet another reading: the score is \"rows that fire\". Sweep on v16: 54/54 fire \u2014 every row catches the mutant. Why is that bad? Because in this framework, a detector row is supposed to be a PRECISE instrument: row R is supposed to catch fault F and nothing else (each row = one cell closure). If a mutant is caught by ALL rows, then the detector can't localize, but more importantly... hmm, actually wait \u2014 for the repair's claim, what matters is that the REPAIRED detector catches the mutant at the specific new row. On v17, the score is 56/57 and the catching row is named. If 56 rows fire, the catching row is one of 56 \u2014 not precise. If the score means 56 pass and 1 fires, then the named row is the one that fired \u2014 precise. The latter makes sense for v17 (\"a closure becomes a measurement\": the closure row now catches the mutant precisely).\n\nBut then v16's 54/54 SWEEP would mean 54 pass and 0 fire \u2014 the mutant is NOT caught by v16 at all. And \"SWEEP\"... hmm, \"sweep\" as in the mutant sweeps past the detector undetected? No \u2014 the history says \"a status-bit conjunct sweeps the FALLBACK predicate\" \u2014 the conjunct sweeps (covers all of) the predicate. So a sweeping mutant = one whose behavioral change is total (unconditional w.r.t. other conditions). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively: on v16, 54/54 = all rows fire = the mutant's effect is so sweeping that every detector row's scenario diverges. On v17, the detector has 57 rows; the mutant is caught and the score 56/57 means... 56 fire? That would mean v17 is WORSE (56 vs 54 firing rows)? No \u2014 the point of the table is that v17 improves things.\n\nHmm, let me look at the CONTROL row: \"polarity twin implemented = (PSR & SER) != 0 (CONTROL) | 23/41 | 30/57\". The polarity twin: divide implemented ONLY when SER set (opposite polarity). On v16: 23/41. Wait \u2014 41? v16 has 54 fault rows (+2 controls? \"57 fault rows + 2 controls\" is v17; v16 would be 54 fault rows + 2 controls = 56 total? or 54 total?). The control scores 23/41 on v16 \u2014 denominator 41 \u2260 54. And 30/57 on v17 \u2014 denominator 57 = v17's fault rows.\n\nSo the denominators differ BETWEEN rows of the same table: sweeps are /54 on v16 (fault rows = 54) but the control is /41 on v16?? That's inconsistent. Unless... on v16, the control mutant only exercises 41 rows (13 rows don't apply?), and on v17 it exercises all 57? Hmm, that's possible: the score might be \"rows that fire / rows applicable\". For the control on v16: 23 fire out of 41 applicable. On v17: 30 fire out of 57 applicable. And for the sweep mutants on v16: 54 fire out of 54 applicable; on v17: 56 fire out of 57?? But then \"the row that caught it\" \u2014 hmm.\n\nHold on, wait. Let me re-read: \"the row that caught it | `div0tbc`\". For the sweep mutants on v17, the catching row is a SINGLE named row: `div0tbc`, `resid50ser`, `errser` \u2014 these are exactly the three NEW rows! `resid50ser` and `errser` are named as new rows in the repair. `div0tbc`... hmm, is `div0tbc` new? The repair says three rows added: `ovftbser`, `resid50ser`, `errser`. `div0tbc` is NOT among them \u2014 it must be a pre-existing row (one of the five re-valued? The five dispatch-arm rows? `div0tbc` = divide-by-zero trap bit... c = carry? Hmm, \"div0tb\" could be \"divide-by-zero trap\" site, \"c\" = carry bit. So `div0tbc` is one of the five dispatch-arm rows (divide dispatch arm, carry bit), re-valued in this repair to also carry SER).\n\nSo on v17, the ILLEGAL_DIVIDE mutant is caught by `div0tbc` \u2014 one of the five re-valued dispatch-arm rows. The fallback mutant is caught by `resid50ser` (new row). The re-route mutant is caught by `errser` (new row). Good \u2014 each of the three predicted sweeps is now caught by a specific row, three different rows, each at the right site: divide\u2192dispatch arm (re-valued row), fallback\u2192`resid50ser`, re-route\u2192`errser`.\n\nNow the scores: \"56/57\" \u2014 if the catching row is ONE row, the score 56/57 must mean 56 rows PASS (don't fire) and the 1 that fired is the catcher. So the score format is \"<rows passing>/<total rows>\"? But then the sweeps on v16: \"54/54 SWEEP\" would mean all 54 pass \u2014 NOTHING catches the sweep mutants on v16! And \"SWEEP\" describes the MUTANT's behavior (the conjunct sweeps the predicate \u2014 the defect class), not the detector's response. Pass 9 predicted: \"if you build these three sweep mutants, the frozen v16 detector will not catch them\" \u2014 because v16 only closes SER at one arm, so mutants that gate OTHER sites' behavior on SER escape v16's rows... wait, but v16 closes SER at one arm \u2014 the mutant at that arm's site would be caught. Hmm, the three sweeps: divide (dispatch arm \u2014 v16's SER closure was at ONE arm; if that one arm was NOT the divide arm, the divide mutant escapes). Hmm, but 54/54 = all pass = zero catch. If v16 closed SER at one arm, say arm X, then a sweep mutant at site X would be caught by that one row (1 fires, 53 pass = 53/54). But the sweeps score 54/54 \u2014 all pass \u2014 so none of the three sweep mutants is at v16's one closed arm. The three sweep sites: divide (dispatch), fallback, re-route. v16's one closed arm (from pass 8's repair) \u2014 which arm? Pass 8's repair closed \"the re-route's TRAP side AND at the dispatch arms -- eight rows\". So pass 8 added 8 rows: re-route trap side + dispatch arms. Pass 9 found pass 8's closure covered SER at ONE arm of seven. Hmm.\n\nWait, I need to re-examine. Actually, hmm, wait: if v16 (the detector as of pass 9's review, i.e., after pass 8's repair... no wait). Let me get the versioning right: \"THE REPAIR UNDER REVIEW (detector v16 -> v17, 57 fault rows + 2 controls)\". So v16 = 54 fault rows (pass 9 reviewed v16 and found the one-arm defect), v17 = 57 fault rows (this repair). The sweeps were \"PREDICTED BY READING on pass 9\" \u2014 pass 9 predicted these three mutants would sweep... hmm, \"sweep\" the v16 detector.\n\nOK so if on v16 all three sweeps score 54/54 = undetected (all rows pass), that means v16's SER closure at its one arm doesn't catch any of these three mutants. The three mutants are at: divide (a dispatch arm?), fallback, re-route. v16's one SER-closed arm \u2014 from pass 8's \"class closure\" of 8 rows at \"the re-route's TRAP side AND at the dispatch arms\". Pass 9 found that among those 8 rows, only ONE carried SER (one arm of seven). So v16 has one row with SER \u2014 at some arm. If that arm were, say, the overflow arm (via `ovftb`-something?), then the three sweep mutants (divide dispatch, fallback, re-route) would all escape: 54/54 pass each. Then the repair adds SER to the five dispatch arms (catching the divide sweep at `div0tbc`), plus `resid50ser` (catching the fallback sweep), plus `errser` (catching the re-route sweep), plus `ovftbser` (closing the overflow arm for SER... but v16 already had one SER row at one arm \u2014 maybe the overflow arm? Then `ovftbser` would be... hmm).\n\nWait, the repair says `ovftbser` is added because it's \"the overflow arm, where no other row sets the bit\". So v16's one SER-closed arm was NOT the overflow arm. Then which arm was it? Hmm. If v16's one SER row was at, say, the misaligned arm, then `errser`'s second fault at the misaligned arm would duplicate it? The repair says `errser` \"closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there\" \u2014 implying the misaligned arm was NOT closed for SER before (so `errser`'s second fault closes it).\n\nSo where was v16's one SER row? Unknown from the packet. Hmm, but actually \u2014 wait. If v16 had ONE row closing SER at one arm, then a sweep mutant AT THAT ARM would be caught by v16 (score 53/54). The three sweeps all scored 54/54 (not caught). So the one v16 SER arm is not divide-dispatch, not fallback, not re-route-non-trap... The repair's new rows: `ovftbser` (overflow \u2014 \"where no other row sets the bit\"), `resid50ser` (fallback), `errser` (non-trap branch + misaligned arm). And five re-valued dispatch arms. Total SER closures in v17: 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned + fallback + non-trap = 9 sites?! But the axis is 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits. Hmm, so 7 arms + fallback + re-route sides?\n\nUgh, wait. Let me recount. \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\". Previously-closed 23?? Hmm. 40 cells total. Eight rows in this repair (5 re-valued + 3 new). Previously-closed 23. Hmm, where does 23 come from?\n\nHold on \u2014 maybe \"the previously-closed 23\" refers to the CONTROL's score on v16: 23/41! \"the polarity twin ... 23/41 [on v16]\". Hmm, 23 of 41. Hmm.\n\nOr maybe: 40 cells = 42 \u2212 2 unreachable. The eight rows close... let me count cells closed by this repair: five re-valued dispatch rows close SER at 5 arms = 5 cells. `ovftbser` closes SER at overflow arm = 1 cell. `errser` closes SER at misaligned arm (its second fault) = 1 cell, and its first fault closes SER at the non-trap branch (a site \u2014 is the non-trap branch one of the 7 arms?). `resid50ser` closes SER at the fallback (\"both sides\"? \"whose own blurb already claimed every settable bit on both sides\" \u2014 so the fallback has two sides?).\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually wait \u2014 maybe the 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits axis is: 7 arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned? And the fallback and re-route are covered under a DIFFERENT axis (they're not \"arms\" but predicates with \"sides\" \u2014 trap side / non-trap side). The pass 6/7/8 defects were at the fallback, re-route non-trap, re-route trap, and dispatch arms \u2014 the \"predicate\" sites. Hmm, and pass 9's finding: pass 8's class closure covered SER at ONE arm of seven. So the seven arms include the dispatch arms... 5 dispatch + 2 others = 7.\n\nAlternatively: 7 arms = fallback + re-route-trap + re-route-non-trap + 4 dispatch? No, 5 dispatch rows.\n\nLet me just enumerate sites named in the packet: fallback (pass 6, 2 rows), re-route non-trap (pass 7, 2 rows), re-route trap + dispatch arms (pass 8, 8 rows: maybe 3 + 5?). 2+2+8 = 12 rows from passes 6-8? Plus earlier rows... v16 has 54 fault rows total, so there's a lot else.\n\n\"7 arms x 6 = 42 cells, minus 2 unreachable = 40\". If the 7 arms are: fallback, re-route non-trap, re-route trap, and 4 dispatch arms \u2014 but there are FIVE dispatch-arm rows. Hmm, 5 dispatch arms + fallback + re-route = 8 sites, not 7.\n\nUnless the axis is specifically about the DISPATCH arms: 7 dispatch arms? But \"the five dispatch-arm rows\" suggests 5 dispatch arms. Hmm, but maybe there are 7 dispatch arms and 5 rows cover... no.\n\nHmm, what about: arms = exception-vector arms: the MC88100 exception dispatch has arms like: divide-by-zero, overflow, misaligned, privileged, illegal, trace, etc. The packet names: divide (`div0tb`), overflow (`ovftb`), misaligned, reserved-50 (`resid50` \u2014 0x50 vector?), error (`err`). And \"the five dispatch-arm rows share now carries the serial-mode bit\" \u2014 five dispatch arms. Plus overflow arm = 6. Plus misaligned = 7! So the 7 arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned. And the fallback + re-route are NOT arms \u2014 they're the earlier passes' predicate sites, which sit BEFORE the arms (fallback = when no vector matches? re-route = re-delivery?).\n\nHmm OK wait, but then `resid50ser` \u2014 \"the fallback, whose own blurb already claimed every settable bit on both sides\". So `resid50` = the fallback site (vector 0x50 = reserved \u2192 fallback?). And `errser` \u2014 \"the non-trap branch, which closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there\". So `err` = the non-trap branch (re-route non-trap?) and its second fault lands on the misaligned arm.\n\nSo this repair's three new rows close SER at: overflow arm (`ovftbser`), fallback (`resid50ser`), non-trap branch (`errser`, first fault) + misaligned arm (`errser`, second fault). Plus five re-valued dispatch arms. Total SER sites closed by this repair: overflow, fallback, non-trap, misaligned, + 5 dispatch = 9 sites.\n\nBut the axis has 7 arms. If the 7 arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned, then this repair closes SER at all 7 arms: 5 (re-valued) + overflow (`ovftbser`) + misaligned (`errser` fault 2). \u2713 And additionally closes SER at the fallback and the non-trap branch \u2014 which are NOT arms but were the sites of passes 6-7's defects (the fallback and re-route predicates themselves gate on status bits).\n\nHmm wait, but actually \u2014 do the fallback and non-trap branch belong to the 42-cell axis? \"7 arms x 6 = 42 cells\". If arms are 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned, then fallback/non-trap are outside this axis (they're covered by \"every settable bit on both sides\" claims \u2014 the fallback's blurb claimed every settable bit on both sides, so `resid50ser` makes good on that claim for SER).\n\nHmm OK, so with that reading, the serial-mode row of the axis: 7 cells (one per arm). Before the repair: closed at ONE arm (pass 9's finding \u2014 v16 had SER at one arm; which one? From pass 8's 8 rows: \"the re-route's TRAP side AND at the dispatch arms\". Hmm, so v16's 8 rows from pass 8 were at re-route-trap + dispatch arms, and pass 9 found only ONE of those 8 rows carried SER). Wait, but if v16's one SER row is at a dispatch arm, then the divide sweep mutant (a dispatch-arm mutant!) would be caught by v16 at that arm... but the divide sweep scored 54/54 (uncathed) on v16. Hmm, but wait \u2014 the divide sweep mutant is `case ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: implemented = !(PSR & SER)` \u2014 that's not a dispatch-arm mutant; it's an INSTRUCTION-implemented mutant (integer divide instruction becomes unimplemented when SER set \u2192 that generates an exception \u2192 goes through the exception path \u2192 the detector's rows compare...). Hmm.\n\nOK you know what, I've been going around in circles on mechanism. The packet's key question is answerable at the counting level. Let me now carefully audit the counts, because the brief says the previous two rounds' human-readable summaries were wrong while measurements were right, and this round DISCLOSED two apparatus defects. My job (pass 10): test whether THIS repair reproduces the one-cell-claimed-as-axis defect, and answer \"WHICH [disposition] IS WRONG?\" among the listed dispositions.\n\nThe second question: \"of this repair's dispositions (THREE new rows; FIVE rows re-valued; ONE corrected denominator; ONE disclosed driver defect; ONE disclosed configuration defect; ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled), WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 that's 3+5+1+1+1+1 = 12 dispositions. One of them is wrong. Which?\n\nLet me audit each:\n\n(a) THREE new rows: `ovftbser`, `resid50ser`, `errser`. 54 + 3 = 57 \u2713. The repair description lists exactly three bullets for new rows (ovftbser, resid50ser, errser) plus one