e dispatch-arm rows share now carries the serial-mode bit, so those five close that bit at their arms as well as the carry and interrupt-disable bits\". So the five dispatch rows previously carried carry + interrupt-disable (2 bits) at 5 arms = 10 cells; now +SER = 15 cells.\n\nHmm hold on, wait. Actually \u2014 wait a moment. Let me reconsider. \"the status value the five dispatch-arm rows share\" \u2014 the five rows share ONE status value (a PSR value used in the test). Re-valuing = changing that shared value to include SER. So the five dispatch rows previously tested with a PSR that had carry + interrupt-disable set (2 bits), now also SER (3 bits). So each of the five dispatch rows closes 3 bits at its arm instead of 2.\n\nNow the axis: 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits = 42, minus 2 unreachable = 40. The 6 settable bits: carry, interrupt-disable, SER, + 3 unnamed. The 7 arms: 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned (from the repair's coverage: 5 re-valued dispatch + ovftbser at overflow + errser's second fault at misaligned = 7 arms getting SER). \u2713 That composition works: the repair closes SER at all 7 arms.\n\nPreviously-closed: before this repair, how many of the 40 cells were closed? The question says \"the previously-closed 23\". Hmm \u2014 23. Let me see: 40 \u2212 23 = 17 cells closed by this repair's eight rows? Eight rows... five re-valued rows close SER at 5 arms (5 cells) \u2014 but wait, re-valued rows previously closed carry+ID at their arms (10 cells, already counted in the 23?). The three new rows: `ovftbser` (SER at overflow: 1 cell), `resid50ser` (SER at fallback: fallback is not an arm...), `errser` (SER at non-trap + SER at misaligned: 2 cells?).\n\nHmm, so this repair closes SER at: 5 dispatch arms (5 cells) + overflow (1) + misaligned (1) = 7 arm-cells, plus fallback and non-trap (not arms). 40 cells: 42 \u2212 2 unreachable. The SER row: 7 cells (7 arms). Other 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35 cells. Pass 8 claimed 35. Pass 9: SER closed at 1 arm \u2192 total closed before this repair = 35 + 1 = 36? But the question says \"previously-closed 23\". 23 \u2260 36. Hmm!\n\nWait, unless the 35/40 claims were about all six bits and the \"previously-closed 23\" is a different count. Hmm. Let me re-read the question: \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\". Hmm \u2014 8 rows... but 8 rows don't map to cells 1:1 (errser covers 2 sites; the 5 re-valued rows each re-cover their arm's SER cell...). And \"previously-closed 23\": hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the coverage accounting is different: maybe before this repair, only 23 of the 40 cells were closed, i.e., pass 8's \"35\" claim was wrong not just in denominator but in actual coverage? Pass 9 \"corrected the denominator: SIX settable status bits, not five -- ... 7 arms x 6 = 42 cells, minus 2 unreachable = 40, where pass 8 claimed 35.\" So pass 8 claimed 35 cells closed; pass 9 said the true denominator is 40. And pass 9 found SER closed at ONE arm \u2014 so pass 8's claimed 35 closed cells... if the axis is 40 and SER has 7 cells with only 1 closed, then at most 33 + 1 = 34 other cells closed... so pass 8's claim of 35 closed was ALSO wrong (overstated by at least 1) even under the old denominator? Hmm wait: pass 8 claimed 35 = 7\u00d75. Under the old (wrong) denominator of 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35, pass 8 claimed ALL 35 closed. Pass 9 corrected: 6 bits \u2192 42 \u2212 2 = 40 cells; SER at only 1 arm \u2192 so closed = (5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35, minus unreachable?) hmm.\n\nWait, the 2 unreachable: \"minus 2 unreachable\". Which 2? Unknown from packet. If 2 of the 42 are unreachable (can't be closed), then reachable = 40. Pass 8's claim of 35 closed \u2014 under 5-bit thinking, 35 = all of 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms. Pass 9: of the 40 reachable cells, SER row has 7 cells (arms) \u2014 wait, are any of the 2 unreachable cells SER cells? Unknown.\n\nIf closed-before = 23... hmm, where would 23 come from? 23 = ? Let me think: maybe the previously-closed count comes from the control mutant's score? \"polarity twin ... 23/41 [on frozen v16]\". Hmm \u2014 the polarity twin on v16: 23/41. And \"the row that caught it: 27 rows\" on v17 (30/57). Hmm, \"27 rows\" \u2014 the polarity twin is caught by 27 rows on v17? So the control's catch is NOT localized to one row (27 rows fire). Interesting.\n\nHmm, wait. Now I need to nail the score direction. If score = rows that fire (catch), then:\n- Sweep mutants on v16: 54/54 \u2014 ALL rows catch. \"SWEEP\": the mutant sweeps the detector \u2014 every row fires. Hmm, why would every row fire for a divide-implemented-only-when-!SER mutant? Because... hmm. If the detector's 54 rows are fault-injection rows each with a specific PSR status value, and the mutant makes divide behavior depend on SER... only rows that exercise divide would fire. Unless the detector rows all run a common prologue that includes a divide? Hmm.\n\n- Sweep mutants on v17: 56/57 \u2014 56 rows fire?? And the catching row named as ONE row. If 56 fire, naming one \"catching\" row is odd. Unless the score is inverted for... no.\n\nAlternatively score = rows that PASS (don't fire):\n- v16: 54/54 pass \u2014 mutant undetected by v16. \"SWEEP\" = the mutant sweeps through undetected?? Hmm, but then the control on v16: 23/41 \u2014 23 of 41 pass, 18 fire \u2192 v16 catches the polarity twin at 18 rows?? And on v17: 30/57 pass, 27 fire \u2192 \"27 rows\" catch it. That matches \"the row that caught it: 27 rows\"! YES \u2014 the control row's catcher column says \"27 rows\", and 57 \u2212 30 = 27. \u2713\u2713\n\nSo score = X/Y where Y = total rows (41 on v16?? but v16 has 54 fault rows...). Hmm wait: control on v16: 23/41. If score = passing/total, then 41 = total rows for the control run on v16, and 23 pass \u2192 41 \u2212 23 = 18 rows fire on v16. On v17: 30/57 \u2192 27 fire. And the sweeps on v16: 54/54 \u2192 0 fire (undetected \u2014 the SWEEP escapes v16 entirely). On v17: 56/57 \u2192 1 fires (the named row). \u2713 This is consistent!\n\nBut the denominator inconsistency: sweeps on v16 are scored /54, control on v16 is scored /41. v16 has 54 fault rows (+2 controls = 56 total?). Why would the control's denominator be 41 on v16 but the sweeps' be 54? Possible: some rows are inapplicable to the control mutant (e.g., 13 rows don't apply... 54 \u2212 41 = 13). Hmm, that's a real inconsistency in the table as stated: same detector (frozen v16), same kind of run, two different denominators (54 and 41). Unless the denominator = \"rows applicable to this mutant\" and the sweeps are applicable to all 54 while the control is applicable to only 41. Hmm, that could be legitimate (a polarity-twin divide mutant only exercises rows whose fault scenarios touch divide-related paths \u2014 41 of them?). But wait \u2014 if only 41 rows were applicable to a DIVIDE mutant, then the ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE sweep (also a divide mutant!) should also be applicable to ~41 rows, not 54. Both the first sweep mutant and the control are mutations of the same ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE case! First: `implemented = !(PSR & SER)`; control: `implemented = (PSR & SER) != 0`. Same site, different polarity. Yet one is scored over 54 rows on v16 and the other over 41. THAT is an internal inconsistency in Table 14 as stated. Hmm!\n\nWait, unless \"54/54 SWEEP\" means something like \"swept all 54\" (all fire) \u2014 i.e., the sweep's denominator is 54 because ALL rows fire (all applicable). And the control fires on only 18... no wait, with score=fire direction: sweep on v16: 54 fire / 54 total \u2014 every row fires. Control on v16: 23 fire / 41 applicable?? Hmm, and on v17: sweep 56/57... 56 fire? Then \"the row that caught it: `div0tbc`\" \u2014 of 56 firing rows, `div0tbc` is... the designated one? That makes no sense.\n\nWith score=pass direction: sweep v16: 54/54 pass \u2192 0 fire. Sweep v17: 56/57 pass \u2192 1 fire = the named row \u2713. Control v16: 23/41 pass \u2192 18 fire. Control v17: 30/57 pass \u2192 27 fire \u2713 (\"27 rows\" catch it). The \"pass\" direction is consistent for v17. For v16, sweeps /54 and control /41 \u2014 inconsistent denominator UNLESS \"41\" is a typo for \"54\" or the applicability differs.\n\nHmm wait, actually, hmm. Let me reconsider: maybe on v16 the control's denominator 41 is real: maybe on v16, the control run had only 41 rows applicable... no wait. OR: maybe \"23/41\" is a typo for \"23/54\"? Or the control on v16 legitimately only ran 41 rows? The packet doesn't explain the 41. This is a genuine internal inconsistency to flag: same frozen v16 detector, same mutant site (ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE), but the sweep is scored over 54 rows and the polarity twin over 41. If denominators are \"applicable rows,\" the two divide mutants should have the same applicability; if denominators are \"total rows,\" both should read 54. Either way, 54 \u2260 41 for the same detector is unexplained in the packet.\n\nHmm, wait wait. Hold on. Actually, let me reconsider once more. Maybe the denominators differ because of the \"+2 controls\": v17 = 57 fault + 2 controls = 59 total; v16 = 54 fault + 2 controls = 56 total. Control on v17: 30/57 \u2014 denominator 57 = fault rows (excluding controls). Sweep on v17: 56/57 \u2014 denominator 57 = fault rows \u2713 consistent. Control on v16: 23/41 \u2014 denominator should be 54 (fault rows). 41 \u2260 54. So on v16, the control's denominator (41) doesn't match v16's fault-row count (54). That's an arithmetic inconsistency in the table as stated \u2014 unless the 41 is explained somewhere (it isn't in the packet).\n\nHmm, hmm. But wait \u2014 the disclosed defect #1: \"The driver's red-row echo attributes rows to the wrong mutant. Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one; the extra row belongs to the next block. The result files are correct -- diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score.\" So the driver's human-readable echo misattributes rows, but result files are correct and \"matching every score\". Hmm \u2014 so the scores are claimed correct. But the 23/41 vs 54 denominator issue is in the SCORES themselves, not the echo.\n\nHmm, wait, unless... \"23/41\" \u2014 maybe on v16, the control run is scored only over the 41 rows that existed before some subset? Hmm. OR maybe the 41 is the count of rows applicable to the control ON V16 because 13 rows crash/skip under the control? Honestly, from the packet, it's unexplained. Should I flag it? The brief says: \"Audit the premises AS STATED. If a premise is self-contradictory, or the derivation does not follow from the facts given, say so.\" The 41 vs 54 denominator for the same frozen detector is a candidate inconsistency. But hold on \u2014 let me reconsider whether \"23\" and \"41\" might relate to \"the previously-closed 23\" in the question!\n\n\"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\" \u2014 hmm! The question's \"previously-closed 23\" \u2014 where does 23 come from in the packet? The only 23 is the control's v16 score numerator: 23/41. Coincidence? Hmm. If the question's \"23\" refers to previously-closed CELLS (of the 40), then 23 should be derivable from the coverage accounting. Let me try to derive previously-closed cells:\n\nBefore this repair (v16), closed cells of the 40:\n- Pass 8's repair: 8 rows at re-route trap + dispatch arms. Pass 9 found SER closed at 1 arm.\n- Hmm, but what about passes 6, 7's rows (fallback, re-route non-trap)? Those close bits at the fallback/re-route sites \u2014 are those part of the 40-cell axis? If the 7 arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned, then fallback/re-route cells aren't in the 40.\n\nLet me try to construct the coverage table. 40 cells = 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits \u2212 2 unreachable.\n\nBits: carry (C), interrupt-disable (ID), SER, + 3 others (unnamed \u2014 maybe mode, trap-enable, etc. \u2014 8 architectural \u2212 2 non-storable = 6).\n\nArms: 5 dispatch (including divide `div0tb`, and presumably `resid50`?? hmm wait \u2014 `resid50ser` is \"the fallback\"... so resid50 = fallback site, not a dispatch arm). Arms named: divide (`div0tb`), overflow (`ovftb`), misaligned, + 3 more unnamed dispatch arms.\n\nHmm OK here's my problem: the packet doesn't fully enumerate the 7 arms or the 6 bits, so I cannot independently verify the \"23\". Let me see if 23 is derivable: 40 total. This repair closes: SER at 7 arms (7 cells) via 5 re-valued + ovftbser + errser's second fault. Hmm, but ALSO \u2014 wait, do the re-valued rows close anything NEW besides SER? They previously closed C and ID at their arms. Now they close C, ID, SER. So new cells from re-valued rows: 5 (SER at 5 dispatch arms). New rows: ovftbser \u2192 SER at overflow (1). resid50ser \u2192 SER at fallback (fallback = arm? if fallback is an arm, then the 7 arms include fallback...). errser \u2192 SER at non-trap branch + SER at misaligned arm.\n\nHmm, if the 7 arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned: repair closes 7 SER cells \u2192 40 \u2212 7 = 33 previously open, so previously-closed = 33?? But question says 23. If fallback and non-trap are also arms: 9 arm-like sites, but the axis says 7. Contradiction-ish.\n\nAlternatively: \"the previously-closed 23\" might be an error in the question, OR might refer to something else. Hmm, wait \u2014 actually, maybe I should reconsider: maybe the 40-cell axis counts ALL sites (fallback, re-route sides, arms) \u00d7 6 bits... 7 arms where \"arms\" includes fallback and re-route? Like: fallback, re-route-trap, re-route-non-trap, + 4 dispatch?? But there are 5 dispatch rows...\n\nHmm, hold on, wait. Let me recount with a different arm composition. Sites where SER needed closing per this repair: 5 dispatch arms, overflow arm, misaligned arm, fallback, non-trap branch = 9 sites. The axis is 7 arms. So either 2 of those 9 aren't \"arms\" (fallback + non-trap, my earlier reading), or the axis composition differs.\n\nWith arms = {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned}: SER row = 7 cells. Previously (v16): SER closed at 1 arm (pass 9's finding). So previously-closed SER cells = 1. Other bits: 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35 cells, minus unreachable. The 2 unreachable cells: which? If the 2 unreachable are among the non-SER bits, then reachable non-SER = 33, and previously-closed total = 33 + 1 = 34 IF all non-SER reachable cells were closed. Pass 8 claimed 35 closed (7\u00d75) \u2014 under 5-bit thinking, pass 8 claimed all 35 non-SER... wait no. Hmm, pass 8 claimed 35 = 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms \u2014 but which 5 bits? Before pass 9's correction, the belief was 5 settable bits. The 5 bits would have been {C, ID, + 3 others} and SER was... hmm, pass 9 ADDED SER as the 6th settable bit (\"SIX settable status bits, not five\"). So before pass 9, SER wasn't counted as settable?? But SER is serial-mode \u2014 the store path refuses 2 of the 8 bits; SER is storable. Pass 9's correction: 8 architectural \u2212 2 refused = 6 settable. Previously believed: 5 settable. So the previously-believed 5 bits EXCLUDED one genuinely settable bit \u2014 namely SER (the bit whose closure was deficient!). That's... convenient. Pass 9 found both the denominator error AND the SER closure gap in the same pass.\n\nSo under the old 5-bit view: 35 cells (5 bits \u00d7 7 arms). Pass 8 claimed its 8 rows closed... hmm, pass 8's claim was \"class closure\" \u2014 of what? The history: pass 6 found \"a status-bit conjunct sweeps the FALLBACK predicate\" \u2192 repaired with 2 rows at the fallback. Pass 7: same shape at re-route non-trap \u2192 2 rows. Pass 8: same shape at re-route trap + dispatch arms \u2192 8 rows, \"class closure\". So passes 6-8 were closing the \"status-bit conjunct\" defect class across sites: fallback, re-route non-trap, re-route trap, dispatch arms. The rows added assert that behavior doesn't depend on status bits at those sites... or that specific bits ARE handled at specific sites. Hmm.\n\nHonestly, the exact semantics of \"closing a cell\" is: a detector row (fault row) = an injected fault + a status value; \"cell (arm, bit) closed\" = there exists a row that injects a fault at that arm with that bit set (proving the detector would catch a status-bit conjunct sweeping that arm for that bit). So the 40 cells = for each of 7 arms, each of 6 settable bits: at least one row must inject a fault at that arm with that bit set (or prove it unreachable). The 2 unreachable: 2 (arm, bit) combos that can't be constructed.\n\nGiven that: this repair adds SER rows at: 5 dispatch arms (re-valued shared status value \u2014 hmm wait, if the five rows SHARE one status value, then re-valuing the shared value adds SER to all five \u2014 efficient!), overflow (ovftbser), misaligned (errser fault 2), fallback (resid50ser), non-trap (errser fault 1). So SER closed at 9 sites total. If the axis's 7 arms \u2287 {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned}, then SER is closed at all 7 arms \u2713, and additionally at fallback + non-trap (extra sites, outside the 40-cell axis \u2014 or the axis includes them and the count is different).\n\nPreviously-closed (v16): the question says 23. Hmm, can I derive 23? Let me try: if before this repair, closed cells = ?\n\nFrom pass 8's \"35 claimed\": pass 8 claimed 35 cells closed (the whole 5-bit axis). Pass 9 found: (i) denominator is 40 not 35 (SER is a 6th settable bit), (ii) SER closed at only 1 of 7 arms. So pass 9's finding implies pass 8's 35-claim was about the 5 old bits; were all 35 of those actually closed? The 2 unreachable cells \u2014 if they're among the old 5 bits' cells, then reachable old-bit cells = 35 \u2212 2 = 33... and if all were closed, previously-closed = 33 + 1 (SER at one arm) = 34. Question says 23. 34 \u2260 23.\n\nAlternatively, maybe not all old-bit cells were closed. Hmm, 23 + this-repair's-new-cells should = 40 (if this repair completes the axis). This repair's new cells: 7 SER arm-cells (5 re-valued + ovftbser + errser-misaligned)... 23 + 7 = 30 \u2260 40. Plus resid50ser (fallback SER) + errser fault 1 (non-trap SER) = 9 new cells \u2192 23 + 9 = 32 \u2260 40. Hmm. Plus... the re-valued rows: did re-valuing change which cells the 5 dispatch rows close? They now close C+ID+SER (3 bits) at 5 arms; before they closed C+ID at 5 arms = 10 cells (already closed). So no new non-SER cells. Hmm.\n\nSo \"eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\" \u2192 8 rows... if each of the 8 rows closed ONE cell, 8 cells; 23 + 8 = 31 \u2260 40. If errser closes 2 cells, 9 cells; 23 + 9 = 32 \u2260 40. Hmm, none of these hit 40. So either \"23\" is wrong, or the 8 rows close 17 cells (40 \u2212 23), which would need the 8 rows to close 17 cells \u2014 impossible unless rows close multiple cells each and previously-closed 23 excludes... hmm wait, unless the re-valued rows' PREVIOUS closures are being recounted? No...\n\nHmm, hold on. Actually \u2014 wait. Maybe I'm misreading \"the previously-closed 23\". Let me re-read the question: \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover -- and specifically, is any of the three new rows closing its site at one arm the way the row it replaces did?\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\". Maybe \"23\" refers to 23 previously-closed CELLS on the serial... no. Or \"the previously-closed 23\" = the 23 rows/... Hmm. Could \"23\" be the count of PREVIOUSLY-CLOSED CELLS as measured by the control? The control on v16: 23/41. Hmm, what if the control's semantics is: the polarity twin, when injected, is caught by exactly the rows that would catch a status-conjunct defect... no.\n\nOR: maybe \"23\" is a deliberately planted error, and the second question (\"WHICH IS WRONG?\") is answered by \"the count 23 in the question itself\" \u2014 no wait, the second question lists 12 dispositions and asks which is wrong. Hmm, but maybe the answer is that NONE of the 12 is wrong and the trap is different... or the answer is one of them.\n\nLet me now list the 12 dispositions and audit each for internal consistency:\n\n1. THREE new rows \u2014 `ovftbser`, `resid50ser`, `errser`. Internally consistent: 54 + 3 = 57 \u2713; table names `resid50ser` and `errser` as catchers \u2713; `ovftbser` is the third \u2713. But: does `ovftbser` appear in Table 14? No \u2014 none of the four mutants is caught by `ovftbser`. The overflow arm's SER closure is UNTESTED by Table 14. Hmm! The repair closes SER at 7 arms; Table 14's sweeps test: divide dispatch arm (caught by `div0tbc` \u2014 a re-valued row), fallback (`resid50ser`), re-route/non-trap (`errser`). NOT tested: the other 4 dispatch arms' SER closure (well, they share the re-valued status value with `div0tbc`, so if the shared value carries SER, all 5 get it \u2014 one measurement covers the shared value \u2713 reasonable), the overflow arm (`ovftbser` \u2014 NO mutant tests it!), and the misaligned arm (errser's second fault \u2014 no mutant named for misaligned). Hmm wait, the sweeps were predicted on pass 9 (the three sweep sites). Table 14 built exactly those three. So `ovftbser`'s cell closure is claimed but unmeasured. Is that a defect? The repair claims closure of the SER axis at 7 arms; measurements cover: 5 dispatch (via shared value + div0tbc) + fallback + non-trap. The overflow arm's SER cell (`ovftbser`) and misaligned arm's SER cell (errser fault 2) are closed by READING, not measurement. Hmm \u2014 but wait, is that a defect per the chain's standard? The chain's standard seems to be: closures should be measured (that's what Table 14 is for: \"a prediction becomes a measurement in one direction and a closure becomes a measurement in the other\"). \"a closure becomes a measurement in the other\" \u2014 i.e., the repair's closures become measurements via the table. But only 3 of the closures are measured (div0tbc-arm via sweep 1, fallback via sweep 2, non-trap via sweep 3). The overflow (`ovftbser`) closure is NOT measured by Table 14. Hmm!! That's exactly the shape of the recurring defect: claiming an axis while measuring one cell!! Wait \u2014 no, wait. The repair's rows close the axis; the table MEASURES that specific mutants are caught. The question \"does this repair reproduce the defect it repairs\" = does the repair close SER at one arm while claiming the axis? By READING (row names + description), the repair closes SER at: 5 dispatch arms (shared re-valued value), overflow (ovftbser), misaligned (errser fault 2), fallback (resid50ser), non-trap (errser fault 1). That's 9 sites, covering the 7 arms (if arms = 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned) plus fallback + non-trap. So BY READING, the axis is closed at all 7 arms. BUT the MEASUREMENT covers only 3 of those sites (divide arm, fallback, non-trap) \u2014 the overflow arm and misaligned arm closures are unmeasured, and 4 of the 5 dispatch arms are measured only via the shared value assumption (if the five rows truly share one status value, then re-valuing it closes all 5 \u2014 and div0tbc's catch confirms the shared value carries SER... IF div0tbc is one of the five sharing rows. The packet says \"the five dispatch-arm rows share\" the status value; `div0tbc` caught the divide sweep \u2014 consistent with div0tb being a dispatch arm whose re-valued row carries SER \u2713).\n\nHmm, so is the answer to question 1: \"By reading, the axis is closed; but the overflow-arm cell (`ovftbser`) is closed by reading only \u2014 Table 14 measures three closures and `ovftbser` is not among them \u2014 so the repair MEASURES its SER closure at 3 of 7 arm-sites while CLAIMING seven\"?? Hmm, wait \u2014 but is that the defect class? The defect class across passes 6-9: \"a repair reproduced, inside itself, the class of defect it was repairing\" \u2014 pass 9's specific finding: pass 8's repair closed SER at ONE arm of seven while claiming the axis. The class: claiming an axis, closing one cell. This repair: claims the SER axis (7 arms); closes (by rows) all 7; measures 3 (or 4 counting misaligned? no \u2014 errser's SECOND fault at misaligned isn't measured by any listed mutant; the re-route sweep mutant is caught by errser's FIRST fault presumably... or which? \"re-route gains && !(PSR & SER)\" caught by `errser` \u2014 errser is the non-trap branch row; the re-route non-trap side = errser's site \u2713).\n\nHmm, so measured sites: divide-dispatch (div0tbc), fallback (resid50ser), re-route-non-trap (errser fault 1). Unmeasured SER closures: overflow arm (ovftbser), misaligned arm (errser fault 2), and 4 dispatch arms (covered only by the shared-value reading). So the REPAIR closes the axis by rows but the TABLE measures 3 cells of the 7-arm SER row. Is that \"reproducing the defect\"? The defect class is about CLOSURE claims vs actual closure. The rows themselves close the cells (the rows exist, each injecting SER at its site \u2014 that IS closure, as closure is defined by row existence). The table is a separate verification. Hmm. Pass 9's finding was that pass 8's repair had only ONE ROW carrying SER while claiming the axis \u2014 a row-existence deficiency. This repair has SER-carrying rows at all 7 arm-sites (by the description). So by the packet's own definition of closure (rows), the axis IS closed this time. The residual gap: `ovftbser` and `errser`-fault-2 closures are unmeasured \u2014 but \"closure\" in this chain has been about ROW EXISTENCE (pass 9 counted rows: \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\").\n\nHmm hold on, \"the previously-closed 23\" \u2014 23 ROWS? \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\" \u2014 maybe 23 = previously-closed ROWS (the rows from passes 6-8: 2 + 2 + 8 = 12... no, that's 12, not 23). Hmm. v16 has 54 rows; how many close axis cells? Unknown. 23... Let me try: if each axis cell needs \u22651 row, 40 cells; v16 closed some set with its 54 rows; pass 9 said SER at 1 arm; pass 8 claimed 35... I keep failing to derive 23.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 maybe 23 comes from: 40 cells \u2212 8 new-row-cells \u2212 9? Ugh. Let me try assuming the question's \"23\" is simply WRONG (a planted arithmetic defect), and see if the second question's answer is \"the count of previously-closed cells\" \u2014 but the second question lists dispositions: \"THREE new rows; FIVE rows re-valued; ONE corrected denominator; ONE disclosed driver defect; ONE disclosed configuration defect; ONE record correction\". The \"23\" appears only in QUESTION 1, not in the disposition list. Hmm. So the second question asks: of those 12 (well, 6 kinds) dispositions, which is wrong?\n\nLet me audit each disposition against the packet:\n\n(1) THREE new rows: names given (ovftbser, resid50ser, errser); 54\u219257 \u2713; two of them appear as catchers in Table 14 \u2713. Consistent. Is there any issue? `errser` \"closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there\" \u2014 a row with TWO faults. Hmm \u2014 a single detector row carrying two injected faults: that means the row proves BOTH sites see SER... but if the row has two faults, a catch proves at least one diverges \u2014 it can't LOCALIZE which. Hmm, but for closure purposes (would a SER-sweep at the misaligned arm be caught?), a row with faults at both non-trap and misaligned catches a sweep at EITHER \u2014 but if only one of the two were broken, the row still catches \u2014 fine for detection. But for the axis-closure semantics (each cell needs a row whose fault is AT that cell with the bit set), errser's second fault at misaligned: if errser's FIRST fault alone would catch the re-route sweep, then the misaligned closure rides along unmeasurably... Specifically: is the misaligned cell CLOSED? A row with a fault at misaligned + SER set \u2014 yes, the row exists, the fault is there, the bit is set \u2014 by row-existence semantics, closed \u2713. OK.\n\n(2) FIVE rows re-valued: \"the status value the five dispatch-arm rows share\" \u2014 consistent with five dispatch arms; and the divide arm (div0tb) is one of them (div0tbc = divide arm, carry bit \u2014 wait!! `div0tbc` \u2014 if row names are <site><bit>, then `div0tbc` = div0tb + c = divide arm + CARRY bit?! But the catcher of the ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE SER-sweep should be a row with SER set... The re-valued rows now carry SER \u2014 but their NAMES would still be named after their original bit? Hmm: \"the status value the five dispatch-arm rows share now carries the serial-mode bit, so those five close that bit at their arms as well as the carry and interrupt-disable bits\" \u2014 so the five rows are named for carry?? or for their arms? If the five rows each close carry+ID+SER at their arms, the row `div0tbc` (divide arm, carry) now also closes SER at the divide arm. So the catcher of the SER-divide-sweep is `div0tbc` \u2014 a CARRY-named row catching a SER mutant! That's... consistent with the shared-status-value design: the row's status value now has C, ID, and SER all set, so the row is sensitive to sweeps of any of the three bits at the divide arm. OK, consistent. Though it does mean the closure's localization is coarse (the row can't distinguish WHICH bit was swept \u2014 but it can distinguish the ARM). Fine.\n\nHmm, but wait: if the five dispatch rows share ONE status value with C+ID+SER set, then each row closes 3 bits \u00d7 its arm = 3 cells. Five rows \u2192 15 cells. Previously (C+ID): 10 cells.\n\n(3) ONE corrected denominator: 6 settable bits (8 architectural \u2212 2 refused by store path); 7 arms \u00d7 6 = 42 \u2212 2 unreachable = 40 (pass 8 claimed 35). Arithmetic: 7\u00d76=42 \u2713; 42\u22122=40 \u2713; 35 = 7\u00d75 \u2713 (consistent with the old 5-bit belief). Internally consistent. BUT: the \"2 unreachable\" is unexplained \u2014 which 2 cells and why unreachable? Not checkable from packet. Also: \"eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses\" \u2014 is the MC88100 PSR 8 bits? Hmm \u2014 actually, hmm, the real MC88100 PSR has more bits... let me recall: MC88100 PSR: mode bit (M), interrupt enable (IE), serial mode (SER... actually it's \"SM\"? hmm), carry (C), SE?? Let me think. Real 88100 PSR bits: M (mode), IE (interrupt enable), S (serial?? no...). Hmm: PSR bits per MC88100 manual: M, IE, SE (sign extend? no that's in... hmm), C (carry), TM/TE?... Actually: PSR contains: mode (bit 31?), IE, SER (serial mode), C (carry), TP (trace pending), TE (trace enable)... Hmm. I genuinely don't remember exactly; the 88100 PSR is 32-bit with fields: M, IE, SER?, C, ... And SU (supervisor/user)? Hmm. The packet says \"eight architectural bits, two refused by the store path\". Real fact (from memory of 88100): the PSR has bits like M (mode), SU, IE, C, TE, TM/TP, SER (serial), and... \"the two the store path refuses\" \u2014 hmm, on 88100, some PSR bits are read-only? I recall that writing PSR can't change certain bits (e.g., trace-enable? or the serial bit?). Honestly I can't verify the 8\u22122=6 claim from the packet \u2014 it's an architectural premise NOT checkable from the packet. Per instructions: name it and say what single fact would settle it: the MC88100 PSR bit list + which bits a PSR store can alter (from the architecture manual / the model's PSR store path). The packet says \"SIX settable status bits, not five -- eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses\". This is a premise from outside the packet (architecture). Not checkable \u2192 say so.\n\n(4) ONE disclosed driver defect: the red-row echo misattributes rows (two of four blocks list two rows while the score says one; the extra belongs to the next block; result files correct \u2014 \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\"). Audit: \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 hmm, \"their own score says one\": which scores say one? The three sweep mutants on v17 score 56/57 \u2192 1 row fires \u2192 echo should list 1 row. The control scores 30/57 \u2192 27 rows fire \u2192 echo lists 27. Four blocks (one per mutant). Two of four blocks list TWO rows while their score says one \u2014 so two of the three sweep-mutant blocks list 2 rows (one extra, belonging to the next block). \"The result files are correct -- diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score.\" WAIT \u2014 \"exactly one row per mutant\"?? But the CONTROL fires 27 rows! \"exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\" \u2014 if every result file diffs to exactly one row per mutant, that CONTRADICTS the control's 27-row catch and its 30/57 score!! Hmm!! Unless \"each\" refers only to the three sweep mutants' files... but it says \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\" \u2014 \"matching EVERY score\". The control's score (30/57, 27 rows) is NOT matched by \"exactly one row\". SELF-CONTRADICTION in the disclosure as stated!!\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read very carefully: \"The result files are correct -- diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score.\" Hmm. If \"each\" = each of the four mutants' result files, and each diffs to exactly one row, then the control's catch = 1 row, not 27 \u2014 contradicting \"30/57 | 27 rows\". If \"each\" = each of the three sweep mutants, then \"matching every score\" is false for the control (its file would diff to 27 rows). Either way, the disclosure's verification claim is internally inconsistent with Table 14's control row.\n\nHmm, unless \"one row per mutant\" means \"one DIFF row per mutant\" in some red-row-echo sense... The driver's echo lists red rows per mutant block; the result files diff to one red row per mutant for the sweeps... The control block lists 27 rows. Hmm. OK here's the thing: the disclosure says the result files are correct and \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score.\" For this to be consistent with the control's 27, the sentence must be about only the three sweep mutants, and \"every score\" = every sweep score. But as WRITTEN, \"one row per mutant\" across four mutants contradicts 27. This is a real textual inconsistency worth flagging \u2014 the disclosure that's supposed to establish the table's arithmetic itself contains an arithmetic that cannot hold (\"caught by arithmetic that could not hold\" \u2014 ironically!).\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually, maybe \"exactly one row per mutant\" is about the MISATTRIBUTED rows: the echo lists 2 rows in a block; diffing shows only 1 row actually changed for that mutant; so the extra listed row belongs to the next block... For the control block (27 rows listed), hmm. \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 two blocks with score-says-one are sweep blocks listing 2 (one extra each). \"the extra row belongs to the next block\" \u2014 a one-off-by-one block boundary bug. The result files: \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant\" \u2014 hmm, if the CONTROL's result file diffs to 27 rows, then \"exactly one row per mutant\" is false. UNLESS the control's 27 rows... hmm.\n\nActually hold on \u2014 maybe I have the score direction backwards. Let me re-examine with score = ROWS THAT FIRE:\n\n- Sweeps on v16: 54/54 \u2014 all 54 rows fire. \"SWEEP\" \u2713 \u2014 the mutant sweeps the detector: every row catches it. Why is that the predicted pass-9 finding? Pass 9 found SER closed at ONE arm of seven on v16. Hmm \u2014 if v16 has ONE row with SER set (at one arm), then a SER-sweep mutant... every row fires?? Why?? If the detector rows inject faults with various status values, and the product mutant makes behavior depend on SER... only rows with SER set in their status value would behave differently. v16 has ONE such row. So a SER-sweep mutant should fire \u22641 row on v16, not 54!! So score=fire gives a nonsensical story for v16 sweeps. With score=PASS: sweeps on v16: all 54 rows pass = 0 fire. But v16 HAS one SER row (pass 8's one-arm closure) \u2014 if a sweep mutant hits THAT arm, the row fires \u2192 53/54. The three sweeps (divide, fallback, re-route) scored 54/54 \u2014 so the one v16 SER row is at NONE of those three sites. Fine \u2014 the one v16 SER row is at some OTHER arm (overflow? misaligned? another dispatch arm?). Hmm wait, but the repair says `ovftbser` is added at \"the overflow arm, where NO OTHER ROW sets the bit\" \u2014 so v16's one SER row is NOT at overflow. And `errser`'s second fault closes misaligned \"at the same time\" \u2014 implying misaligned wasn't SER-closed in v16 either?? Hmm, \"closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there\" \u2014 this says the errser row's second fault lands at the misaligned arm, so adding errser closes BOTH the non-trap branch AND the misaligned arm. If v16 already had a SER row at misaligned, this phrasing would be redundant... though not necessarily contradictory (\"closes\" could mean \"adds a second closure at\").\n\nHmm hmm. But wait \u2014 if v16 had exactly ONE SER row at some arm X, and X is one of the 7 arms, then which arm? Not overflow (per ovftbser's rationale), not any of the 5 dispatch arms (per \"the five dispatch-arm rows share now carries the serial-mode bit\" \u2014 implying they didn't before), not misaligned (implied), not fallback (resid50ser added there), not non-trap (errser added there). So ALL of the repair's SER sites were previously SER-less?! Then where was v16's ONE SER-closed arm (pass 9's finding: \"covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven\")???\n\nCONTRADICTION CANDIDATE: Pass 9 found v16 closed SER at ONE arm of seven. The repair adds/re-values SER at: 5 dispatch arms, overflow, misaligned (via errser), fallback, non-trap. If the 7 arms = {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned}, then the repair touches ALL 7 arms \u2014 so v16's one SER-closed arm must be among them \u2014 but the repair's descriptions imply NONE of the 7 arms previously had SER (\"no other row sets the bit\" at overflow; the dispatch rows \"now carries\" SER; errser \"closes the misaligned arm at the same time\"; pass 9 found ONE arm was covered). If the ONE previously-covered arm was, say, one of the 5 dispatch arms \u2014 then re-valuing the SHARED status value... wait, the five dispatch rows SHARE a status value \u2014 if they shared it in v16 too, then either ALL five had SER or NONE did (shared value!). Pass 9: ONE arm of seven covered. If the five dispatch rows share a value, they can't cover exactly ONE dispatch arm... unless the previously-covered arm was overflow or misaligned \u2014 but ovftbser's rationale (\"no other row sets the bit\") excludes overflow, and errser's phrasing weakly excludes misaligned.\n\nHmm, so: v16's one SER-covered arm \u2014 which? If arms = {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned}: not overflow (ovftbser rationale), not misaligned (errser phrasing), and the 5 dispatch share a value so it's all-or-none \u2014 and \"now carries\" implies none did. CONTRADICTION: pass 9's \"one arm of seven covered\" cannot be reconciled with the repair's site descriptions IF the seven arms are {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned}.\n\nUNLESS the seven arms include the fallback and/or re-route sides! E.g., 7 arms = {fallback, re-route-trap, re-route-non-trap, + 4 dispatch}?? But there are 5 dispatch rows... or 7 arms = {fallback, non-trap, 5 dispatch}: then overflow and misaligned aren't arms, and the repair's SER sites: fallback (resid50ser \u2713 arm), non-trap (errser \u2713 arm), 5 dispatch (re-valued \u2713 arms) \u2014 that's 7 arms, ALL touched by the repair, and v16's one covered arm would be among these \u2014 but again the repair's phrasing implies none were covered (\"now carries\"; resid50ser/errser added because the fallback/non-trap lacked SER rows...). Hmm \u2014 wait, actually, for the FALLBACK: \"resid50ser -- the fallback, whose own blurb already claimed every settable bit on both sides\" \u2014 the fallback's BLURB claimed every settable bit on both sides, but pass 9's sweep prediction says a fallback SER-sweep escapes v16 (54/54) \u2014 so the blurb's claim was FALSE for SER (that's presumably WHY pass 9 flagged the fallback sweep!). So the fallback lacked SER despite its blurb. So the fallback wasn't v16's one covered arm either... or WAS it? If the fallback blurb claimed everything but the coverage was one arm... ugh.\n\nHmm, hold on, actually \u2014 wait. Let me re-read pass 9's finding once more: \"Pass 9 found that the repair it reviewed had closed an axis at ONE cell out of seven while the contract claimed the whole axis.\" and the history table: pass 9 = \"that closure covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven\" \u2192 repaired by \"this repair\". So pass 8's repair (the 8-row \"class closure\") covered SER at ONE arm of seven. The eight rows were at \"the re-route's TRAP side AND at the dispatch arms\". So the ONE SER-covered arm is among the re-route-trap or dispatch sites. If the five dispatch rows share a status value, they're all-or-none for SER \u2014 so the ONE covered arm must be... the re-route TRAP side?! Hmm \u2014 the re-route trap side is one of the seven arms?? Then 7 arms = {re-route-trap, 5 dispatch, + 1 more (overflow or misaligned or non-trap or fallback)}.\n\nHmm, wait, but the repair under review doesn't touch the re-route TRAP side for SER! The repair's SER sites: 5 dispatch (re-valued), overflow (ovftbser), fallback (resid50ser), non-trap + misaligned (errser). NOT re-route-trap. If v16's one SER-covered arm = re-route-trap, then the repair's closures: 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned = 7 sites... and re-route-trap stays covered by v16's row \u2192 total 8 SER-covered arm-sites?? But the axis has 7 arms. So one of {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned, re-route-trap} isn't an \"arm\", or something overlaps.\n\nI'm going in circles because the packet genuinely underdetermines the arm composition. KEY INSIGHT for my review: the packet's own accounting of the seven arms is never stated, and the repair's site list (5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned + fallback + non-trap + [v16's one arm, unlocated]) cannot be reconciled with \"7 arms \u00d7 6 = 42\" without knowing which sites are arms. This is a premise that CANNOT be settled from the packet \u2014 and it's EXACTLY the premise the one question turns on (\"is there a cell of the 40 uncovered?\"). I should name it: the composition of the seven arms (and which two cells are unreachable) is not in the packet; the single fact that would settle it: the axis enumeration (the 7 arms \u00d7 6 bits table with the 2 unreachable marked), which pass 9's corrected denominator implies exists.\n\nHmm, wait. Hmm, but actually \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-read the repair bullet 4: \"errser -- the non-trap branch, which closes the misaligned arm at the same time, because its second fault lands there.\" And bullet 2: \"ovftbser -- the overflow arm, where no other row sets the bit\". And bullet 1: five dispatch arms. So new/re-valued SER coverage: 5 + 1 + 1(misaligned) + 1(fallback) + 1(non-trap) = 9 sites. The axis is 7 arms. The packet's phrase in the question: \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites.\" \u2014 SEVEN sites! \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites. Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\". So the repair closes SER at seven sites \u2014 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned = 7? or 5 dispatch + overflow + fallback = 7? or including non-trap? If the repair closes SER at 7 sites = the 7 arms, then the 7 arms = {5 dispatch, overflow, + 1 of (misaligned/fallback/non-trap)}. And the repair ALSO closes fallback/non-trap (extra, non-arm sites?). Hmm \u2014 \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites\" \u2014 the seven sites ARE the seven arms (the question equates them: \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites. Is there a cell of the 40...\").\n\nOK so the question's own accounting: repair closes SER at 7 sites = 7 arms = the whole SER row of the axis. Then: 8 rows close 7 arm-cells (+ possibly fallback/non-trap cells outside the axis). Previously-closed 23. 23 + 7 = 30 \u2260 40. So under the question's own numbers, 10 cells of the 40 would be uncovered!! Hmm!! Wait: 40 \u2212 23 = 17 cells for the repair to close; the repair closes 7 SER arm-cells; 23 + 7 = 30; 40 \u2212 30 = 10 uncovered. UNLESS the previously-closed 23 is wrong, or the repair's rows close more cells (the re-valued rows: 5 rows \u00d7 3 bits = 15 cells \u2014 but C+ID cells (10) were presumably already closed/previously-counted...).\n\nHmm hmm hmm. Wait. Let me try yet another accounting. What if \"previously-closed 23\" is right and derivable: cells closed before = 23. This repair adds 8 rows: 3 new + 5 re-valued. New cells: resid50ser \u2192 SER at fallback (1 cell, if fallback is an arm); ovftbser \u2192 SER at overflow (1); errser \u2192 SER at non-trap (1) + SER at misaligned (1) = 2 cells. Re-valued: +SER at 5 dispatch arms (5 cells). Total new = 1+1+2+5 = 9 cells. 23 + 9 = 32 \u2260 40. Still 8 short. Hmm. 40 \u2212 23 = 17 = new cells needed. The repair's 8 rows would need to close 17 cells \u2014 impossible at \u22642 cells/row unless re-valuing the shared status value closes more than SER... WAIT. What if the shared status value previously carried NO extra bits beyond... hmm, \"so those five close that bit at their arms as well as the carry and interrupt-disable bits\" \u2014 the five rows close C, ID, SER. What if the three unnamed settable bits ALSO need closure at the dispatch arms and the shared value now carries ALL SIX bits?? \"as well as the carry and interrupt-disable bits\" \u2014 the sentence names only C and ID as the OTHER bits the five rows close. So the five rows close 3 of 6 bits. The other 3 bits at dispatch arms: closed by OTHER rows (previously). OK.\n\nSo: per-arm per-bit closure requires SOME row. Previously-closed 23 cells + repair's new cells (7 SER arm-cells + fallback/non-trap SER if arms...) = 40? 23 + 17 = 40. The repair closes 17 cells?? Only if the re-valued status value... hmm, OR if \"previously-closed 23\" is per the CONTROL measurement... I cannot derive 23. CONCLUSION: the \"23\" in the question is not derivable from anything in the packet, and the packet's own numbers (pass 8 claimed 35; SER at 1 arm; 40-cell axis) suggest previously-closed should be ~34 (35 claimed \u2212 1 SER... hmm, wait: pass 8 claimed 35 closed. Pass 9: denominator 40; SER covered at 1 arm. If pass 8's 35 claim was otherwise accurate (all 5 old bits \u00d7 7 arms... but 2 of those 35 are unreachable!) \u2014 AH WAIT. If 2 of the 42 cells are unreachable, are the 2 unreachable among the 35 old-bit cells? Pass 8 claimed 35 = 7\u00d75 closed. If 2 cells are unreachable, they can't be closed \u2014 so pass 8's claim of 35 closed was impossible (max = 33 old-bit cells if both unreachable are old-bit cells) \u2014 pass 9 corrected the denominator to 40 (42\u22122), so pass 9's finding implies pass 8's 35 was wrong both in denominator AND in reachability... The packet says pass 9 \"corrected the denominator\" \u2014 40 where pass 8 claimed 35. If previously-closed were 23, then pass 8's claim of 35 was overstated by 12, not just mis-denominated. The packet's narrative (\"corrected the denominator\") implies pass 8's 35 was a DENOMINATOR error (5 bits vs 6 bits, minus unreachables), not a coverage error \u2014 i.e., pass 8 really closed what it closed, it just used the wrong total. Under that narrative, previously-closed = 35 \u2212 (unreachable old-bit cells?) hmm...\n\nHonestly: 23 is NOT derivable, and it CONFLICTS with the packet's implied accounting (pass 8 closed ~35\u22122=33-ish + 1 SER = ~34). Actually hmm, wait \u2014 one more attempt: 23. Control on v16: 23/41. Coincidence? 23 = control's v16 passing rows?? The question says \"the previously-closed 23\" \u2014 if the author confused the control's numerator with a cell count, that's an error. OR \"23\" = cells closed by the 23 rows that... v16's 54 rows \u2212 31? Ugh.\n\nHmm, hold on. Wait. Let me re-read the question once more: \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\". Hmm \u2014 maybe \"the previously-closed 23\" is a typo/plant for what should be ~33 or 34. If previously-closed = 34 and repair closes 7 SER arm-cells \u2192 34 + 7 = 41 > 40 \u2014 hmm, 40 with 1 to spare? If previously-closed = 33 (35 claimed \u2212 2 unreachable... no wait). Let me be careful: pass 8 claimed 35 closed. If both unreachable cells are old-bit cells, actually-closed old-bit \u2264 33. Plus SER at 1 arm: 34 max. If previously-closed = 33 (say both unreachable were old-bit cells AND pass 8 overclaimed by reachability, closing 33 of 33 reachable old-bit cells) + 1 SER = 34. Repair closes 7\u22121=6 new SER arm-cells (one arm was already covered!) \u2192 34 + 6 = 40 \u2713\u2713\u2713 THAT WORKS: previously-closed 34, repair adds 6 SER arm-cells \u2192 40. So \"previously-closed 23\" should be 34?? Hmm, or 33+... whatever \u2014 the point: under the packet's own narrative, previously-closed \u2248 34, NOT 23. And with 23, the arithmetic 23 + 7 = 30 \u2260 40 leaves 10 uncovered \u2014 the question's own numbers would answer \"YES, 10 cells uncovered\", which contradicts \"this repair closes that bit at seven sites [ = the axis]\".\n\nHmm wait, no \u2014 hold on. The question is: \"Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover?\" If the answer via arithmetic is \"23 + 8-rows'-cells < 40 \u2192 yes, cells uncovered\" \u2014 then the repair FAILED? But the packet's narrative says the repair closes the axis. The tension suggests \"23\" is the planted error. And the SECOND question asks \"WHICH [disposition] IS WRONG?\" \u2014 maybe the intended answer: the corrected denominator disposition? or...\n\nHmm, wait. Let me recount the dispositions: \"THREE new rows; FIVE rows re-valued; ONE corrected denominator; ONE disclosed driver defect; ONE disclosed configuration defect; ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled\". Which is wrong?\n\nLet me audit the \"ONE corrected denominator\" disposition: \"SIX settable status bits, not five -- eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses -- so 7 arms x 6 = 42 cells, minus 2 unreachable = 40, where pass 8 claimed 35.\" Audit: 8 \u2212 2 = 6 \u2713. 7 \u00d7 6 = 42 \u2713. 42 \u2212 2 = 40 \u2713. Internally fine. BUT \u2014 hmm, is the denominator really 6? \"eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses\" = 6 SETTABLE. But then \"minus 2 unreachable\" \u2014 unreachable CELLS (arm\u00d7bit combos). Fine. Pass 8 claimed 35 \u2014 under 5-bit thinking: 7 \u00d7 5 = 35 \u2014 but pass 8's 35 should ALSO have subtracted unreachables (35 \u2212 2 = 33?) \u2014 the packet says pass 8 claimed 35 flat. Consistent with pass 8 being wrong. OK.\n\nHmm, now the \"ONE disclosed configuration defect\": \"The detector has never run on the processor it names. It launches the test machine with no CPU option and that machine defaults to the 88110, not the MC88100 every record names. Nothing in the model reads the CPU type to change behaviour outside the name and an identity register, so the scores stand; the claim did not.\"\n\nAUDIT THIS. This is juicy. The detector tests the MC88100 exception-delivery path. It has been running on the 88110 all along (v1\u2013v16!). The packet claims: \"Nothing in the model reads the CPU type to change behaviour outside the name and an identity register, so the scores stand\".\n\nIs that credible? In GXemul, the 88110 is the successor of the 88100 \u2014 with DIFFERENCES: the 88110 has a different exception model (notably: the 88110 handles misaligned accesses IN HARDWARE \u2014 misaligned loads/stores do NOT trap on the 88110, whereas on the 88100 they cause exceptions!). Also the 88110 has on-chip floating-point, different PSR usage... Hmm \u2014 actually, the MC88100 traps on misaligned access (misaligned exception vector 0x...?), while the MC88110 handles misaligned accesses in hardware (mostly). GXemul's 88110 vs 88100 implementations certainly differ in more than \"the name and an identity register\" \u2014 e.g., the very MISALIGNED ARM this repair closes (`errser`'s second fault at the misaligned arm!) \u2014 whether a misaligned access traps at all DEPENDS ON THE CPU MODEL. On an 88110, a misaligned-access detector row expecting an 88100-style exception would NOT behave the same. So the claim \"nothing in the model reads the CPU type to change behaviour outside the name and an identity register\" is a strong premise that is NOT checkable from the packet \u2014 and it's suspicious precisely because the repair touches a MISALIGNED arm, whose very existence as an exception arm is 88100-specific behavior. Hmm!! But wait \u2014 the packet says the test MACHINE defaults to 88110 \u2014 and all 57 rows presumably PASS on it (baseline 57/57). If the rows encode 88100 exception behavior and the machine is 88110, would they pass? If GXemul's 88110 model shares the exception-delivery code with the 88100 (plausible \u2014 the exception vectors/PSR handling may be shared, with 88110 differences like misalign-handling elsewhere), then rows could pass. But the claim \"scores stand\" requires that NO row's behavior depends on 88100-vs-88110 differences \u2014 including the misaligned arm! The packet itself names \"an identity register\" as a difference \u2014 meaning SOMETHING reads the CPU type (to set the identity register!) \u2014 so \"nothing reads the CPU type\" is FALSE as literally stated; it should be \"nothing EXCEPT the name and identity register\" \u2014 which is what it says (\"outside the name and an identity register\"). OK, so the claim is: behavior differences = name + identity register only. NOT CHECKABLE from packet. And the misaligned-arm fact makes it DOUBTFUL on general 88k knowledge (88110 handles misaligned data accesses without trapping \u2014 that's a real architectural difference; a \"misaligned arm\" in the exception path would be exactly where 88110 vs 88100 diverge). Hmm \u2014 but hold on: if the detector rows all pass 57/57 on the 88110 machine, then whatever the misaligned row does, it behaves as the row expects ON THE 88110. The question is whether the row tests what it claims (MC88100 behavior). The packet's disposition: \"the scores stand; the claim did not. Pinning the CPU with an identity row is owed as its own revision.\" That's a reasonable disposition IF the premise (no behavior divergence) holds. The premise is not checkable from the packet. I should flag: the single fact that settles it: run the detector pinned to the 88100 and diff against the 88110 run \u2014 any row differing refutes \"the scores stand\"; or read the model for cpu-type conditionals beyond the identity register. ALSO: the misaligned arm is the exact place to doubt \u2014 on the real 88110, misaligned accesses don't take the misaligned exception the way the 88100 does... Actually hmm, let me be careful about real-world facts here: MC88100: misaligned access \u2192 exception (vector at 0x...? \"Misaligned\" exception, vector... ). MC88110: performs misaligned accesses in hardware for some ops (unaligned support added). So a shared exception-delivery path in the model would still have the misaligned-except logic gated by CPU type IF the model implements 88110 unaligned support. Does GXemul implement that? UNKNOWN from packet. So: not checkable; the doubt is real; the disposition (\"scores stand\") rests on an unverified premise \u2014 and the packet's own repair closes a MISALIGNED arm, making this premise load-bearing for exactly one of the eight rows (errser's second fault). Flag it.\n\nHmm OK wait, but now \u2014 the SECOND question: which disposition is wrong? Let me tabulate all 12 (6 kinds) again and look for the one that's WRONG per the packet's own text:\n\n1. THREE new rows (ovftbser, resid50ser, errser) \u2014 consistent with 54\u219257, with the table's catchers, with the question's \"seven sites\". Hmm, but WAIT: the question says \"is any of the three new rows closing its site at one arm the way the row it replaces did?\" \u2014 \"the row it replaces did\"?? REPLACES? The three new rows REPLACE rows?? The repair description says \"Three rows added and five re-valued\" \u2014 ADDED, not replaced! But the question says \"the way the row it replaces did\" \u2014 implying each new row REPLACED a prior row! v16\u2192v17: 54 \u2192 57 = +3 net. If three rows were added AND some were replaced (removed), the count wouldn't be 54+3. \"the row it replaces\" \u2014 CONTRADICTION with \"three rows added\"?? Hmm, unless \"replaces\" is loose language (the new row replaces the ROLE of... no prior row existed at overflow \u2014 \"no other row sets the bit\"!). At the overflow arm, NO prior row set SER \u2014 so `ovftbser` doesn't REPLACE anything. At the fallback, the fallback HAD rows (pass 6 added two rows there) \u2014 `resid50ser` could be said to extend them. Hmm. \"the way the row it replaces did\" is at best sloppy, at worst reveals that the repair description is wrong somewhere. Hmm, but the question is the QUESTIONER's language (the pass-10 author asking me), not the repair's claim. The question's premise \"the row it replaces\" doesn't fit \"three rows added\". Flag as a question-premise defect? Meh \u2014 minor. Actually hmm, wait: maybe pass 9's finding was about ONE row (the one row that covered SER at one arm) \u2014 and the repair... no, the repair doesn't remove it.\n\nHmm, hold on, let me reconsider. What did pass 9's finding say exactly: \"that closure covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven\" \u2014 the closure = pass 8's 8 rows. Of those 8 rows, ONE covered SER (at one arm). This repair: adds SER at 5 dispatch arms (re-valued shared value \u2014 so pass 8's rows at dispatch arms were among rows sharing... hmm wait, were the five dispatch rows among pass 8's eight?? Pass 8's rows: re-route trap + dispatch arms. If the 8 = 3 re-route-trap + 5 dispatch, then the five dispatch rows (with the shared value) date from pass 8, and pass 9's ONE-SER-covered arm was one of the 3 re-route-trap rows! THAT fits: the 3 re-route-trap rows each had different status values; one of them carried SER (covering ONE arm \u2014 a re-route-trap arm?? but \"arm\"...). Hmm, and then the seven arms = {3 re-route-trap?, ...} no wait, pass 9 said \"ONE arm of seven\" \u2014 the seven arms of the axis. If the one covered arm = re-route-trap-side... and the seven arms include the re-route-trap side as an arm?? Then arms = {re-route-trap, 5 dispatch, +1} = 7 with the +1 being overflow or misaligned or... and the repair covers SER at {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned, fallback, non-trap} \u2014 which would include only 6 of the 7 arms if re-route-trap is an arm... leaving... hmm, no wait: 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned = 7 = the arms, and re-route-trap is NOT an arm (it's a predicate side), and v16's ONE SER-covered arm was... AAARGH.\n\nOK. Decision time. The packet is deliberately underdetermined on: (a) the seven arms' composition; (b) the two unreachable cells; (c) which arm v16 covered for SER; (d) the derivation of \"previously-closed 23\". And it has these candidate internal inconsistencies:\n\n(I1) \"the previously-closed 23\" is not derivable and appears to contradict the packet's own coverage narrative (pass 8 claimed 35; corrected axis 40; SER at 1 arm \u2192 previously-closed should be ~34, and 23 + 7 = 30 \u2260 40, which would mean the repair FAILED to cover 10 cells \u2014 contradicting \"this repair closes that bit at seven sites [the axis]\").\n\nHmm wait, hmm. Actually \u2014 hold on, hold on. Let me reconsider (I1) once more. What if \"previously-closed 23\" is about the OTHER FIVE bits, not all cells? Hmm: 23... 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35; minus unreachable... if 2 unreachable \u2192 33; 23 \u2260 33. What if not all old-bit cells were closed: e.g., C and ID closed at 7 arms (14 cells) + 3 unnamed bits closed at 3 arms (9 cells) = 23?! 14 + 9 = 23!! Hmm!! Interesting \u2014 23 = 2 bits \u00d7 7 arms + 3 bits \u00d7 3 arms. Possible but underdetermined. OR 23 = 5 bits... no. There's no way to verify. The question embeds \"23\" as if established; it is NOT established anywhere in the packet. Pass 8 claimed 35 closed. If only 23 were closed, pass 8's claim was off by 12 \u2014 a MUCH bigger finding than \"one arm of seven\", and pass 9 (which \"corrected the denominator\" and found the one-arm gap) would have said so. So within the packet's narrative, \"23\" is almost certainly WRONG \u2014 it contradicts the history table's implication that pass 8's 35-cell claim was nearly right (off by the SER axis and the unreachables). OR \"23\" refers to something else entirely (23 rows?) that the question misapplies to cells.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually \u2014 WAIT. Let me reconsider: maybe \"the previously-closed 23\" = the 23 rows... \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\" \u2014 could parse as \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed twenty-three [rows]\" \u2014 i.e., 31 ROWS total close the 40 cells! Eight rows from this repair + 23 pre-existing rows = 31 rows covering 40 cells?? But wait, v16 had 54 rows \u2014 of which only 23 close axis cells?? And 40 cells need \u226540 row-cells... 31 rows can close \u2264 31\u00d7(cells per row)... if some rows close 2 cells (like errser), 31 rows could close ~40 cells. Hmm, but \"previously-closed 23\" as ROWS: pass 6 (2 rows) + pass 7 (2) + pass 8 (8) = 12 rows from the class-closure chain \u2014 not 23. Plus the one-arm row... 12 \u2260 23. Hmm. What about pass 8's \"eight rows, called a class closure\" + pass 9's... no. Can't derive 23 rows either.\n\nOK so \"23\" is underivable in ANY reading I can construct. It's either (a) a planted error for me to catch (the pass-10 question testing whether I do the arithmetic: 23 + 7 \u2260 40!), or (b) a reference to a fact not in the packet. Given the brief's own emphasis (\"both were caught by arithmetic that could not hold\"), I SHOULD do the arithmetic: IF previously-closed were 23 and the eight rows close 7 SER arm-cells (+2 non-arm sites), then 23 + 7 = 30, and 40 \u2212 30 = 10 cells uncovered \u2014 the repair would NOT have closed the axis, and the answer to the one question would be \"YES \u2014 by the packet's own numbers, 10 cells uncovered.\" But the packet ALSO says the repair closes the bit at seven sites = the axis... The resolution: \"23\" must be wrong (should be ~33\u201334), OR the seven sites \u2260 the axis. EITHER WAY, the packet is internally inconsistent, and I should say: the arithmetic 23+7=30\u226040 cannot hold; one of {23, \"closes the axis\"} is wrong; the single fact that settles it: the enumerated 40-cell coverage table (which cells closed by which row) \u2014 one fact, settles it.\n\nHmm, hmm. But wait \u2014 maybe I should double-check the \"seven sites\" reading: \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites.\" \u2014 yes, the question states the repair closes SER at seven sites. And the axis needs SER at 7 arms. So the question equates the repair's 7 SER sites with the 7 arms. For the whole 40-cell axis: 6 bits \u00d7 7 arms \u2212 2. SER row: 7 cells (all closed by this repair per the question). Other 5 bits: 35 cells \u2212 2 unreachable (if unreachables are non-SER) = 33. Previously-closed must be \u2248 33 (non-SER) + possibly 1 SER arm (pass 9's finding \u2014 v16 closed SER at ONE arm!). Wait \u2014 if v16 closed SER at one arm, and this repair closes SER at SEVEN sites (all 7 arms), then one of the 7 was already closed \u2192 this repair adds 6 new SER cells. And previously-closed = 33 non-SER + 1 SER = 34. 34 + 6 = 40 \u2713\u2713. SO: previously-closed should be 34 (or 33+1). The question's \"23\" is WRONG by ~11. UNLESS the previously-closed non-SER cells weren't all closed...\n\nHmm, and NOTE: if previously-closed = 34 and this repair closes 6 new cells, total = 40 = the axis \u2713 \u2014 and the answer to \"is there an uncovered cell\" would be \"NO \u2014 provided the seven sites are the seven arms and the unreachables are non-SER\" \u2014 both unproven from the packet.\n\nSo my answer to Question 1: By reading, the repair closes SER at 7 named sites (5 re-valued dispatch arms, overflow via ovftbser, misaligned via errser's second fault) \u2014 IF those are the 7 arms, the SER row is closed. The residual risk: (a) the packet never lists the 7 arms, so \"7 sites = 7 arms\" is assumed, not shown \u2014 the fallback and non-trap sites closed by resid50ser/errser suggest the site-space is larger than 7, and which 7 count as \"arms\" is not in the packet; (b) Table 14 measures only 3 of the 7 closures (divide arm via div0tbc, fallback via resid50ser, non-trap via errser) \u2014 the overflow closure (ovftbser) and misaligned closure (errser fault 2) are UNMEASURED \u2014 measured closure is 3-of-7 while the claim is 7-of-7 \u2014 WHICH IS EXACTLY THE DEFECT SHAPE (claimed axis, measured subset)! Hmm!! Wait, is it? Pass 9's defect: ROW-existence at 1 of 7 (the rows didn't carry SER at 6 arms). This repair: rows exist at all 7 (by description); MEASUREMENT exists at 3. Is the defect class \"claim axis, close one\" about rows or measurements? The chain's table: \"a prediction becomes a measurement in one direction and a closure becomes a measurement in the other\" \u2014 the table's PURPOSE is to turn closures into measurements. It measured 3 of the repair's closures. The other 4 (or at least 2: ovftbser's overflow arm and errser's misaligned second fault, plus 4 dispatch arms via the shared value) rest on reading. Hmm, the shared-value design means one measurement (div0tbc catching the divide sweep) confirms the shared value carries SER \u2014 IF the five rows truly share one status value (stated), then 5 closures are confirmed by 1 measurement \u2713 reasonable. But ovftbser (overflow) has NO mutant in Table 14 \u2014 an overflow-arm SER sweep mutant is absent; and a misaligned-arm SER sweep mutant is absent. So two of the seven closures are unmeasured BY CHOICE (the table built only pass 9's three predicted sweeps). So: the repair claims 7, measures 3 (+shared-value inference for 5-of-them... wait, the divide arm is one of the five; so measured: divide(1 of the 5 shared) + fallback + non-trap = 3 sites; unmeasured: 4 dispatch arms (inferred via shared value), overflow, misaligned). Hmm, so strictly: measured 3, inferred 4, unmeasured-but-inferred... no: 4 dispatch arms inferred from shared value + div0tbc catch \u2713 (if the sharing claim holds); overflow and misaligned: NO measurement and NO inference except the rows' existence. Rows existing = closure (per the chain's row-existence semantics). So arguably fine. But the honest pass-10 answer: the overflow-arm closure is the weakest \u2014 it's the one new row with no mutant in the table, and it's exactly \"a row that exists to close a cell nobody predicted a sweep for\". Hmm, wait \u2014 why was there no predicted sweep at the overflow arm on pass 9? Pass 9 predicted THREE sweeps (divide, fallback, re-route) \u2014 all measured. If a sweep at the overflow arm were possible (a product fault `case INTEGER_OVERFLOW: implemented = !(PSR & SER)`-style), pass 9 would have predicted a fourth... unless the overflow arm can't be swept (unreachable?) \u2014 but then why add ovftbser?! The repair adds ovftbser BECAUSE the overflow arm is a cell needing closure \u2014 if it's closable, it's sweepable, and the table should have a fourth sweep mutant for it. THE TABLE DOESN'T. So the measurement apparatus again covers a subset of the closure claim \u2014 the same shape, one level up: the TABLE claims to measure the repair's closures (\"a closure becomes a measurement\") but measures 3 of 7. Hmm, hmm! But wait \u2014 the table's stated purpose: \"Each mutant scored on BOTH the instrument pass 9 reviewed and the repaired one, so a prediction becomes a measurement in one direction and a closure becomes a measurement in the other\" \u2014 the three sweeps were pass 9's PREDICTIONS; the table measures those. It doesn't claim to measure every closure. The repair's closures at overflow/misaligned were never predicted-sweeps, so never measured. Is that a defect? For the CHAIN's standard (pass 9 caught pass 8 claiming an axis from one cell), the repair closes 7 sites with 8 rows \u2014 the closure is by construction (rows with SER at each site), and the packet SHOWS the rows. The question asks: \"is any of the three new rows closing its site at one arm the way the row it replaces did?\" \u2014 i.e., is any new row closing FEWER sites than claimed? `errser` closes TWO sites (non-trap + misaligned) \u2713 more than one. `resid50ser` \u2014 the fallback \"on both sides\"?? The fallback blurb claims \"every settable bit on BOTH SIDES\" \u2014 does resid50ser close SER on both sides of the fallback, or one?? The repair bullet: \"resid50ser -- the fallback, whose own blurb already claimed every settable bit on both sides\" \u2014 the blurb claimed it; the ROW `resid50ser`... does ONE row close both sides? A row with faults on both sides? Not stated! If the fallback has two sides (trap/non-trap? or two predicates?) and `resid50ser` is one row, it either closes one side (REPRODUCING THE DEFECT \u2014 claimed both sides, closed one!) or carries two faults like errser (not stated). HMMMM!! That's a real catch-candidate: the fallback's own blurb claims EVERY settable bit on BOTH sides; the repair adds ONE row (`resid50ser`) for SER at the fallback; unless that row faults both sides, the fallback's SER closure covers ONE side while the blurb (again!) claims both \u2014 the repair would reproduce the exact defect (claim axis, close one cell) INSIDE the very row added to fix it. The packet doesn't say resid50ser covers both sides. NOT SETTLEABLE from the packet \u2014 flag it: the single fact: whether `resid50ser`'s fault(s) exercise both sides of the fallback predicate with SER set (or whether a second fallback-side row is needed).\n\nSimilarly: does the fallback have \"both sides\" as part of the 40-cell axis? If the fallback has 2 sides \u00d7 6 bits = 12 cells... the axis is 42 cells = 7 arms \u00d7 6 \u2014 so \"sides\" aren't in the axis. Ugh, again arm-composition unknown.\n\nOK let me also audit the \"ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled\" \u2014 mentioned ONLY in the second question's list! The body never describes it! \"ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled\" \u2014 the packet gives NO details of this disposition (what comment, what correction). So it's unauditable from the packet \u2014 must say so. Interesting: the second question lists 12 dispositions, but the body describes only ~10 (three new rows, five re-valued, corrected denominator, driver defect, config defect). The record correction appears ONLY in the question. So either it's the wrong one (unverifiable), or it's a sixth item I must flag as not-in-the-packet.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 let me recount the dispositions in the question: \"(THREE new rows; FIVE rows re-valued; ONE corrected denominator; ONE disclosed driver defect; ONE disclosed configuration defect; ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled)\" \u2014 6 items, counts 3+5+1+1+1+1 = 12 dispositions. The body supports: 3 new rows \u2713 (named), 5 re-valued \u2713, corrected denominator \u2713 (the 40-vs-35 paragraph \u2014 wait, that was PASS 9's correction, not this repair's! \"Pass 9 also corrected the denominator\" \u2014 the history table says pass 9 corrected it. But the second question lists \"ONE corrected denominator\" as one of THIS REPAIR's dispositions! Hmm \u2014 \"of this repair's dispositions (... ONE corrected denominator ...)\". The body attributes the denominator correction to PASS 9, not to this repair!! Wait: \"Pass 9 also corrected the denominator: SIX settable status bits, not five...\" \u2014 the history table: pass 9 = \"that closure covered the SERIAL-MODE bit at ONE arm of seven\" | repaired by \"this repair\". And \"Pass 9 also corrected the denominator\" \u2014 so the denominator correction was pass 9's finding (recorded in pass 9), and THIS REPAIR (v16\u2192v17) implemented... the repair under review = the detector change. The denominator correction is a RECORD change, presumably folded into this repair's records. The second question calls it one of \"this repair's dispositions\" \u2014 consistent enough (the repair's records carry the corrected denominator). OK fine.\n\nDriver defect \u2713 (described), config defect \u2713 (described), record correction \u2014 NOT described. So of the 6 kinds, 5 are described in the body; the record correction is not. The question asks WHICH IS WRONG. Candidates for \"wrong\":\n\n(i) The \"record correction to a comment\" \u2014 unverifiable from the packet (no comment text given). Can't call it wrong; can call it uncheckable.\n(ii) The corrected denominator \u2014 internally consistent arithmetic (42\u22122=40), but the \"2 unreachable\" and the 8\u22122 bits are external facts (architecture) \u2014 uncheckable from packet, not self-contradictory.\n(iii) The three new rows \u2014 consistent.\n(iv) The five re-valued \u2014 consistent (five dispatch arms).\n(v) The driver defect \u2014 hmm: \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 WAIT. Let me check THIS arithmetic!! Four blocks = four mutants. Scores that \"say one\": the three sweeps on v17 each fire ONE row (56/57 pass \u2192 1 fire) \u2713 three blocks with score-says-one. The control fires 27 rows \u2014 its block legitimately lists 27. \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 so TWO blocks list 2 rows each. But THREE blocks have score-says-one! If the driver's echo misattributes by shifting rows to the next block (\"the extra row belongs to the next block\"), then: block1 (divide sweep): lists div0tbc + [extra = first row of block2's true set?]. Hmm \u2014 \"the extra row belongs to the next block\": if each sweep block's true catch is 1 row, and the echo lists 2 in two blocks... e.g., block1 lists {div0tbc, resid50ser}, block2 lists {resid50ser, errser}?? then \"the extra row belongs to the next block\" \u2014 hmm, that would mean block1's extra (resid50ser) belongs to block2 \u2713, and block2's extra (errser) belongs to block3 \u2713, and block3 lists just {errser}?? Then only TWO blocks list 2 rows (blocks 1,2) and the third lists 1 \u2014 \"Two of four blocks list two rows\" \u2713\u2713 consistent! And the control block lists 27 \u2713. And the result files: \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\" \u2014 hmm, \"exactly one row per mutant\" \u2014 for the three sweeps \u2713 one row each; for the control: 27 rows!! \"exactly one row per mutant\" is FALSE for the control. Unless \"each\" = each of the three... The sentence says \"matching every score\" \u2014 the control's score (30/57 \u2192 27 rows) is a score; if the result files match every score, the control's file must show 27 rows, NOT \"exactly one row\". SO: the disclosure's verification sentence (\"exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\") is internally inconsistent \u2014 \"exactly one row per mutant\" cannot match a 27-row catch. THIS is an arithmetic that cannot hold \u2014 in the very disclosure about an arithmetic that could not hold!! Hmm \u2014 OR \"exactly one row per mutant\" is sloppy for \"exactly the rows the score says\"... but as WRITTEN, it contradicts the control's 27. Hmm, wait \u2014 actually, maybe \"one row per mutant\" refers to the RED-ROW ECHO blocks... no, it says \"The result files are correct -- diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant\". Hmm, hold on \u2014 what if the diff is of the RESULT FILE against the BASELINE RESULT FILE (not the run), and each mutant's result file differs from baseline in exactly one row?? For the sweeps: 1 row differs \u2713. For the control: 27 rows differ \u2014 the control's result file vs baseline would differ in 27 rows, not 1. So \"exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\" \u2014 false for the control, as written. FLAG IT.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 is the control's 30/57 a \"score\" that the files match? \"matching every score\" \u2014 if files match every score, and the control's score implies 27 differing rows, then \"exactly one row per mutant\" is wrong for the control. So the driver-defect disclosure contains a false verification claim. Is THAT the wrong disposition?? Hmm, the disposition = \"ONE disclosed driver defect\" \u2014 the disclosure's substance (echo misattribution) is plausible; its verification sentence overreaches (\"exactly one row per mutant\" can't hold for a 27-row control catch). So the driver-defect disposition as stated contains an internal contradiction.\n\n(vi) The config defect \u2014 the \"scores stand\" premise is unverified (and doubtful at the misaligned arm); the disposition (pin CPU in a future revision) is fine, but \"the scores stand\" is not establishable from the packet.\n\nSo now: the second question \u2014 \"WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 the packet plants which one? Let me look for the one that's flat-out WRONG (not just uncheckable):\n\nCandidate A: the \"23\" in question 1 \u2014 but that's not in the disposition list.\nCandidate B: \"exactly one row per mutant\" (driver-defect disclosure) \u2014 contradicts the control's 27.\nCandidate C: hmm \u2014 wait, let me re-examine \"TWO rows in two of four blocks\" once more. \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one; the extra row belongs to the next block.\" Blocks in order: divide-sweep, fallback-sweep, re-route-sweep, control. If the extra row in a block belongs to the NEXT block: block1 lists {div0tbc, X} where X = block2's true catcher (resid50ser); block2 lists {resid50ser, Y} where Y = block3's catcher (errser); block3 lists {errser} \u2014 wait, then block3 lists ONE row (no extra, because block4/control catches 27 and only ONE of them would shift?)... Then \"two of four blocks list two rows\" \u2713 (blocks 1 and 2). Consistent. \u2713 (This works.)\n\nHmm wait, but actually \u2014 \"the extra row belongs to the next block\" \u2014 singular \"the extra row\" for two blocks = two extra rows. Fine.\n\nCandidate D: the record correction \u2014 not described at all. If the question asks \"which is wrong\", maybe the answer: the record correction is NOT one of this repair's dispositions per the body \u2014 the body never mentions it \u2014 so either the count (12 dispositions) is wrong or the body omitted it. Actually \u2014 hmm, the second question says \"of this repair's dispositions (... ONE record correction to a comment that a previous repair mangled), WHICH IS WRONG?\" \u2014 maybe the planted answer: the record correction is wrong because... we can't know. OR maybe the planted answer is the denominator: hmm, let me re-audit the denominator disposition SUPER carefully, since pass 9 \"corrected\" it and pass 10 asks which disposition is wrong:\n\n\"SIX settable status bits, not five -- eight architectural bits less the two the store path refuses -- so 7 arms x 6 = 42 cells, minus 2 unreachable = 40, where pass 8 claimed 35.\"\n\nArithmetic: 8\u22122=6 \u2713. 7\u00d76=42 \u2713. 42\u22122=40 \u2713. Pass 8's 35 = 7\u00d75 \u2713 (old denominator). Hmm \u2014 WAIT: pass 8 claimed 35 cells CLOSED. Under the corrected accounting, the axis is 40. Pass 9's finding: SER at 1 of 7 arms \u2192 pass 8's 35-closed claim included... if pass 8 thought 5 bits \u00d7 7 arms = 35 and claimed all closed, but actually 2 of those 35 were unreachable (if the unreachables are old-bit cells), pass 8's claim was 35 closed of which 2 impossible \u2192 pass 8's claim was wrong by MORE than the SER axis. Hmm, the packet doesn't say where the 2 unreachable live. If the 2 unreachable are SER cells (2 of the 7 arms can't have SER?), then: axis = 40 = 35 old + 5 SER-reachable; pass 9 found SER closed at ONE arm of SEVEN \u2014 but if only 5 SER cells are reachable, \"one arm of seven\" should be \"one of five\"! Hmm!! The question says \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites\" \u2014 if 2 SER cells were unreachable, the repair would close 5, not 7! And the repair adds SER at 5 dispatch + overflow + misaligned = 7 arm-sites \u2014 if 2 of those 7 were unreachable, adding rows there is impossible/wrong! So the unreachables are probably NOT SER cells. But their location is unstated \u2014 and it MATTERS: the question's arithmetic (23 + 7 sites) vs 40 depends on it. UNSETTLEABLE from packet. Name it.\n\nCandidate E: hmm \u2014 \"TWO controls\": \"detector v16 -> v17, 57 fault rows + 2 controls\" and Table 14's control is ONE of them (\"polarity twin ... (CONTROL)\"). Two controls exist; one appears. Fine.\n\nCandidate F: \"BASE 57/57 before and after; clone clean, no sentinel.\" \u2014 57/57 base: all rows pass on unmutated product \u2713 fine.\n\nCandidate G: Table header: \"TABLE 14, MEASURED (pre-table block written 19:00:50, first build 19:02:33)\" \u2014 timestamps: pre-table block written BEFORE first build \u2713 (order fine \u2014 the predictions were written before the measurements were built, which is the point: predictions sealed before measurement). \u2713 consistent \u2014 actually this is a good practice note (pre-registration). Fine.\n\nCandidate H: the control's denominators: 41 (v16) vs 57 (v17) \u2014 as discussed, 41 vs v16's 54 fault rows is UNEXPLAINED. If the denominator = rows applicable/exercised by the control: on v16, 41; on v17, 57?? The control mutant is the SAME product fault on both; v17 adds 3 rows and re-values 5 \u2014 applicability growing 41\u219257 by adding 3 rows?? 41 + 3 = 44 \u2260 57. So even \"applicability\" doesn't reconcile: v17's control denominator is 57 = ALL fault rows, but v16's was 41 \u2260 54 = all fault rows. INCONSISTENT as stated. Unless on v16 the control only ran 41 rows due to... nothing stated. FLAG: the control's v16 denominator (41) doesn't match v16's fault-row count (54), and no applicability rule is given that reconciles 41 (v16) with 57 (v17). This might be THE planted arithmetic defect in Table 14! Hmm \u2014 wait, and notice: \"polarity twin ... 23/41\" \u2014 hmm, and \"the row that caught it: 27 rows\". 57 \u2212 30 = 27 \u2713 internally consistent for v17. For v16: 41 \u2212 23 = 18 rows fire. Hmm.\n\nHold on, actually \u2014 WAIT. What if the v16 control denominator 41 is right because on v16, the control mutant is caught by... no. What if \"23/41\" is a TYPO for \"23/54\"? Then 54\u221223 = 31 fire on v16, vs 27 fire on v17 \u2014 the repair reduced the control's catch from 31 to 27?? Hmm. OR the direction: score = PASSING rows: v16: 23/54 pass \u2192 31 fire; v17: 30/57 pass \u2192 27 fire. The repair IMPROVED control localization (31\u219227 firing)? Eh. Can't settle. The packet's own disclosed driver-defect says \"The result files are correct... matching every score\" \u2014 if the scores include 23/41, and 41 is unexplainable, the \"correct result files\" claim rests on an unexplained denominator. FLAG as uncheckable + inconsistent.\n\nHmm, hmm, wait \u2014 actually, hold on: 41. Hmm, is 41 maybe = 54 \u2212 13? Or = the count of rows that \"apply\" to a DIVIDE mutant... The first sweep (divide) on v16 scored 54/54 \u2014 ALL rows \u2014 meaning ALL 54 rows \"apply\" (pass) under the sweep. If all 54 apply for a divide-sweep, all 54 apply for a divide-control (same site!). So 41 CANNOT be \"applicability\" \u2014 same site, same applicability. \u2192 41 is inconsistent with 54 IN THE SAME COLUMN (frozen v16), same product site, no stated rule. This is a REAL internal inconsistency in Table 14 as stated. GOOD CATCH. This is exactly the kind of thing \"caught by arithmetic that could not hold\".\n\nHmm, unless the denominators mean different things per row... The table column header: \"on frozen v16\" with values \"54/54\", \"54/54\", \"54/54\", \"23/41\". Three rows /54, one /41. For the same detector. Either the control's v16 run used a different row set (unexplained) or the \"41\" is an error. NOT SETTLEABLE from packet, but INCONSISTENT as stated. Similarly on v17: 56/57, 56/57, 56/57, 30/57 \u2014 all /57 \u2713 consistent. So v16's control entry is the odd one out.\n\nHmm \u2014 and one more: the sweep scores on v17: 56/57 \u2014 56 PASS, 1 fire. The catcher is named per sweep \u2713. But wait \u2014 for the FIRST sweep (divide), the catcher is `div0tbc` \u2014 a RE-VALUED row, not a new one. Fine. For the second (fallback): `resid50ser` \u2014 new row \u2713. Third (re-route): `errser` \u2014 new row \u2713. And `ovftbser` \u2014 NEVER catches anything in the table. The repair's three new rows: two catch, one (ovftbser) is unexercised by the table. Not contradictory (the table only built pass 9's three predicted sweeps), but it means ovftbser's closure is UNMEASURED \u2014 and if the pass-10 standard is \"a closure becomes a measurement\", then one of the repair's closures did NOT become a measurement. Hmm, and specifically the OVERFLOW arm \u2014 where \"no other row sets the bit\" \u2014 is the least-verified cell of the axis. AND: was there a pass-9 prediction for an overflow sweep? The packet says \"All three sweeps were PREDICTED BY READING on pass 9\" \u2014 three. If the overflow arm's SER cell is reachable (it got a row!), a sweep exists there; pass 9 didn't predict it; the table didn't build it. So the SER-axis closure at the overflow arm rests on row-existence only. THIS is my strongest answer to Question 1: the repair does not reproduce the one-cell-claimed-as-axis defect AT THE ROW LEVEL (rows exist at all seven sites), but the MEASUREMENT level covers 3 of 7 sites, and the overflow cell (ovftbser) is closed by reading alone \u2014 no mutant in Table 14 exercises it. Whether that \"reproduces the defect class\" depends on the chain's standard: pass 9's standard was row-existence (it counted rows). By row-existence, the repair is clean IF the seven sites are the seven arms \u2014 which the packet asserts (\"closes that bit at seven sites\") but never enumerates.\n\nHmm, OK wait, I want to also double-check the \"seven sites\" claim against the repair bullets: sites closed for SER: (1-5) five dispatch arms; (6) overflow arm (ovftbser); (7) fallback (resid50ser); (8) non-trap branch (errser fault 1); (9) misaligned arm (errser fault 2). That's NINE sites, not seven! The question says \"This repair closes that bit at seven sites.\" \u2014 but the bullets describe NINE SER-closures (counting errser's two faults and the fallback's... hmm, and the fallback \"on both sides\" could be even more!). So \"seven sites\" is itself inconsistent with the bullets UNLESS two of the nine aren't \"sites that count\" (e.g., fallback and non-trap aren't arms \u2014 but then the repair closes MORE than seven and the question undercounts \u2014 harmless) OR the seven = the seven ARMS {5 dispatch, overflow, misaligned} with fallback+non-trap extra. The question says \"closes that bit at seven sites. Is there a cell of the 40 that the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23 do not cover\" \u2014 if the repair closes 9 SER-sites and the axis needs 7, fine (2 extra outside the axis or overlapping). But then \"previously-closed 23\" + repair's cells: if the repair closes 7 IN-AXIS cells (SER row) and previously-closed = 33 \u2192 40 \u2713. So \"23\" remains the broken number. OK.\n\nSo, synthesis \u2014 my findings, ranked:\n\n1. (READ+REASONED) The question's own arithmetic cannot hold: \"the eight rows plus the previously-closed 23\" covering