corrected denominator.\n\n3. (READ) The driver-defect disclosure's verification sentence \u2014 \"diffing each against the baseline gives exactly one row per mutant, matching every score\" \u2014 cannot hold for the control, whose score (30/57) means 27 rows fired. \"Exactly one row per mutant\" matches only the three sweeps. The disclosure about wrong arithmetic is itself wrong arithmetic (in its verification claim). The substantive disclosure (echo misattribution: two of four blocks list two rows, extra belongs to the next block) IS internally consistent with the scores.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 hold on, \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 the scores that say one: three sweeps (1 row each). If two blocks list two rows and one lists one... block4 (control) lists 27. Two of four = blocks 1,2 (each listing 2 = own 1 + next block's 1)... but then block 3 lists 1 (errser) \u2713 and block 4 lists 27 \u2713. Wait, but the extra row \"belongs to the next block\" \u2014 block2's extra = errser = block3's catcher \u2713; block1's extra = resid50ser = block2's catcher \u2713. But then why doesn't block3 list two (errser + one of the control's 27)?? If the off-by-one misattribution shifted EVERY block's first row into the previous block, block3 would list errser + first-control-row = 2 rows, and block4 would list 26. Then THREE of four blocks list two rows. The disclosure says TWO of four. Hmm \u2014 so the echo's error shape is only partially specified; \"two of four\" is consistent with a boundary bug affecting blocks 1-2 only. Eh \u2014 weak, can't push. Skip or mention lightly. Actually \u2014 hmm, \"Two of four blocks list two rows while their own score says one\" \u2014 for this to be coherent, exactly two blocks have score-says-one AND list two. The third sweep block lists... one? Then the extra-row-shift story (\"the extra row belongs to the next block\") explains blocks 1,2 but leaves block 3's boundary clean by accident? Eh \u2014 u