    {
        "id":  "ppcrlwimiwidth",
        "state":  "in work",
        "sev":  "high",
        "opened":  "2026-09-10",
        "title":  "cpu_ppc_instr.c X(rlwimi): the 32-bit rotate is performed at 64-bit width in the 64-bit build, so HIGH-HALF source data leaks into the low 32 bits -- its two siblings truncate to uint32_t first and this one never does",
        "note":  "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex\u0027s design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py (28 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -\u003e 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. ***\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldiclzero]] witness\u0027s sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler takes its operands as MODE_uint_t and then rotates with a shift pair using 32-sh.  cpu_ppc.c defines DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32, so the file is compiled TWICE and MODE_uint_t is **uint64_t** in the 64-bit build.  The architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the LOW half of the source -- **the high half plays no part whatsoever** -- but the right-shift pulls bits out of the high half straight into the low 32 bits, which is exactly where the mask lives.\n\nMEASURED, source 0xAAAAAAAA0000000F, destination seeded 0x5A5A..., shift 4, full mask:\n    got 0x5a5a5a5aaaaaaaf0    want 0x5a5a5a5a000000f0\nThe 0xAAAAAA is high-half data the architecture says cannot reach there.\n\nATTRIBUTION IS PINNED BY TWO COMPANION ROWS: rlwinm on the SAME source and shift is CORRECT, because it casts to uint32_t before rotating, and rlwimi at SH=0 is correct because the ternary short-circuits the rotate entirely.  **So the defect is the missing truncation in this one handler, not the shape of the rotate expression.**\n\n*A row testing this is VACUOUS unless the source\u0027s two halves DIFFER: the seat\u0027s own first draft used an all-ones source, under which a 64-bit rotate and a true 32-bit one agree bit for bit, and it corrected the row rather than shipping it green.*\n\nADJACENT, NOT A DEFECT BUT WORTH RECORDING so a round does not rediscover it: rldic, rldcl and rldcr are NOT IMPLEMENTED AT ALL -- the decoder\u0027s default swallows them.  rldic in particular is documented and compiler-emitted.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: THE WIDTH PREMISE HOLDS AND **TRUNCATION ALONE IS NOT THE FIX**. ***\n\nCasting the source to `uint32_t\u0027 before the rotate is necessary and not sufficient.  The prescribed repair splits the combined declaration -- `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)\u0027 with the destination kept at the mode-dependent width -- **and additionally handles the WRAPPING mask**, because the manual\u0027s mask wraps when the beginning exceeds the end: `if (mb \u003e me) ra = (ra \u0026 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp \u003c\u003c 32);\u0027 AFTER the rotation and BEFORE the existing mask loop.\n\n*** AND A NEW FINDING THAT CONTRADICTS THE EARLIER SIBLING AUDIT: `rlwinm\u0027 AND `rlwnm\u0027 ARE NOT UNIVERSALLY CORRECT. ***  The witness measured them green on the cases it drove and the audit recorded them as correct; the design review says that is true of those cases and not of the handlers.  **A measured-green sample is not a proof of correctness**, which is this project\u0027s own reading-versus- measurement distinction cutting the other way for once -- the measurement was right about what it measured and the GENERALISATION from it was mine.  Re-check both before either is called clean, and do not let this row\u0027s fix be copied to them on the strength of the audit.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: \u0027no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.\u0027  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4\u0027, an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT\u0027S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder\u0027s whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND IT FOUND A SECOND DEFECT AT THIS SITE THAT I HAD NOT SEEN.  MY \u0027ONE-TOKEN CAST\u0027 PREMISE IS REFUTED. ***\n\nThe width contamination is confirmed exactly as filed: for `1 \u003c= SH \u003c= 31\u0027 the erroneous expression\u0027s low word is `correct_rot32(L, SH) | ((H \u003c\u003c SH) \u0026 0xffffffff)\u0027, and the existing low-word mask can select those bits.  `SH == 0\u0027 bypasses the shift expression and is clean.\n\n*** THE SECOND DEFECT: WHEN `MB \u003e ME\u0027 THE ARCHITECTURAL MASK WRAPS ACROSS THE 64-BIT BOUNDARY AND COVERS THE ENTIRE HIGH HALF, so RA\u0027s high 32 bits must be REPLACED with the rotated word -- and this implementation preserves them unconditionally. ***  `ROTL32(x, y)\u0027 is defined as rotating `x || x\u0027, the low word DUPLICATED (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:17143\u0027), and the rlwimi RTL at `:29989\u0027 combines that with `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)\u0027.  The wrapping mask visits `MB+32..63, 0..ME+32\u0027 -- which includes every architectural position `0..31\u0027.\n\n    MB \u003c= ME   high 32 mask bits all ZERO  -\u003e preserve old RA      (what the code does)\n    MB \u003e  ME   high 32 mask bits all ONE   -\u003e replace with rot word (what the code never does)\n\n**So the repair is narrowing AND a high-word insertion**, and it must sit after the rotation and before the loop mutates `mb\u0027:\n\n        uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic-\u003earg[0]);\n        MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic-\u003earg[1]);\n        ...\n        tmp = sh ? ((tmp \u003c\u003c sh) | (tmp \u003e\u003e (32-sh))) : tmp;\n        /*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */\n        if (mb \u003e me)\n                ra = (ra \u0026 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp \u003c\u003c 32);\n\nIn the 32-bit compilation the conversion back to `MODE_uint_t\u0027 discards that high half, so the existing behaviour there is unchanged.\n\nMUTANT, one character: `\u003e\u0027 -\u003e `\u003c\u0027 in the new condition.  `rlwimi r4,r3,0,31,0\u0027 = **0x506407c0**, `r3 = 0xaaaaaaaa0000000f\u0027, `r4 = 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a\u0027 gives correct `0x0000000f5a5a5a5b\u0027 and mutant `0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5b\u0027 -- the low-word insertion succeeds and the high-word replacement is skipped.\n\nTERMINATION PROVED rather than assumed: both fields are masked `\u0026 31\u0027, `mb\u0027 is reset to 0 on reaching 32, positions are `(b+k) mod 32\u0027, and ME is processed after `((ME-b+32) % 32) + 1\u0027 bodies -- between 1 and 32.  Every shift count `31-mb\u0027 is in range.\n\nROUND SCOPE: **TWO rounds, not one**, with [[ppcrldimimask]].  Same file and family, but one repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal while the other repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating the second needs word-duplication and high-half expectations that lie outside the doubleword detector\u0027s contract.\n\nValidated offline over **655,360 cases** (every SH/MB/ME at both widths x ten operand pairs).  **Not compiled** -- the seat had no native compiler and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED\u0027.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]\u0027s `want_rldimi\u0027 is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual\u0027s own `:6882\u0027 (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32\u0027 is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE HIGH-HALF INSERTION HALF IS CONFIRMED OWED, 2026-09-10.**  It was filed as depending on the [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] dispute; that dispute is now RESOLVED by PowerPC Book I v2.01, which states the wrapping MASK and makes no upper-half clearing claim.  So this round needs BOTH halves -- the source narrowing AND the `if (mb \u003e me)` high-word insertion -- and the narrowing alone is not the instruction\u0027s repair.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]\u0027s two corrected helpers were verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `\u0026 63`, the all-zero-mask assertion\u0027s normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** CHECKED FOR THE INERT-REPAIR DEFECT THAT KILLED THE SIBLING DESIGN, 2026-09-11: THIS ONE IS SAFE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN LUCKY. ***\n\n`ppcrlwhighhalf`\u0027s adopted repair turned out INERT because the DECODER mutates `mb` to equal `me` before the handler selection that tests `mb \u003e me`.  The same question had to be asked here, since the proposed repair uses the identical guard.\n\n**IT IS FINE, and the difference is WHERE `mb` COMES FROM:**\n\n        X(rlwimi):  int mb = (iword \u003e\u003e 6) \u0026 31;        \u003c-- decoded FRESH, in the handler\n                    tmp = sh ? (rotate) : tmp;\n                    if (mb \u003e me) ...                   \u003c-- the proposed insertion, HERE\n                    for (;;) { ... mb ++; ... }        \u003c-- mutation happens AFTER\n\n**`X(rlwimi)` does not consume a decoder-precomputed mask at all -- it decodes `mb` and `me` from `ic-\u003earg[2]` itself -- so it never inherits the mutated value.**  `rlwinm` and `rlwnm` DO consume a precomputed mask, and their handler selection happens in the decoder, downstream of the loop that walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*So the two rounds are not two instances of one hazard: one repair sits upstream of its own mutation and the other sat downstream of someone else\u0027s.  Recording the reason rather than only the verdict, because a future round asking \"is my guard inert?\" needs the discriminator -- **does this code decode the value it tests, or receive it?** -- and not a list of which handlers happened to be safe.*\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY agy ON **GEMINI 3.8 FLASH (HIGH)** -- the first round on the 3.8 seat, switched and liveness-verified the same hour. ***\n\n**WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `witness_committed.out` 00:46, `candidate_fix.diff` 00:47, `witness_fix.out` 00:48.\n\n    committed build   14/28   -- 93 census cells differing from the architectural result\n    repaired build    28/28   -- 0 census cells differing, no failing rows\n\n**THE DIFF IS THE DESIGNED TWO-PART REPAIR, BOTH HALVES:** `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)` splitting the declaration so `ra` keeps `MODE_uint_t`, and the `if (mb \u003e me)` high-word insertion placed AFTER the rotate and BEFORE the loop -- which is the placement that keeps it from being inert, since the loop walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*** AND IT FOLDED IN THE AXIS I HAD NOT ENUMERATED, WITHOUT BEING TOLD TO. ***  The pre-fix failing set includes **`alias nowrap`, `alias wrap`, `alias both`** -- REGISTER ALIASING, which a review named to me yesterday as an axis my four (mb, me, shift, compilation width) did not cover, and which I passed to this seat only as an open question.  **It rowed it.**  So `RA == RS` is measurably affected pre-fix and correct post-fix.\n\nThe rest of the pre-fix failing set spans both defects and both mask classes, which is what the brief demanded: three non-wrapping rows, four wrapping rows, `wrap fullword`, the two preservation rows, and the census.\n\n**STILL RUNNING when the print budget expired: the kill table** (`killtable_20260911/`).  Same orphan shape as this seat\u0027s earlier round today -- *\"root agent idle; waiting for 1 background task(s)\"* -- but the brief told it to write its report to disk as it goes, so the artifacts are accumulating rather than being held for a final flush.  **A 120-minute print budget was not enough either; the lesson is to collect from the artifacts directory, not from the seat\u0027s stdout.**\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE IS COMPLETE AND STRONG, AND ITS `NULL_MEDIUM` LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  Found by opening the mutator rather than reading the leg name. ***\n\n    BASE          28/28        M_ONLY_HIGH   19/28\n    M_REV_COND    13/28        M_POST_LOOP   19/28\n    M_ONLY_CAST   19/28        M_ORIG        14/28   (= HEAD, identical failing set)\n    HEAD          14/28        RESTORED      28/28\n    NULL_MEDIUM   28/28        \u003c-- SEE BELOW\n\nClearance: **Part 1 (cmp vs SEC) CLEAN, Part 2 (binary -nt source) FRESH, sentinel cleared.**\n\n**THREE LEGS EARN THEIR PLACE AND TWO OF THEM ANSWER QUESTIONS I ASKED EXPLICITLY:**\n  * **`M_ONLY_CAST` 19/28 and `M_ONLY_HIGH` 19/28** -- each half of the repair ALONE is killed, so **neither half suffices** and the two-part claim is measured rather than asserted.\n  * *** `M_POST_LOOP` 19/28 -- the INERT-PLACEMENT mutant. *** Moving the conditional after the loop is KILLED, which is the proof-by-measurement I asked for that the placement is load-bearing.  **That is the exact defect that made the sibling `rlwinm` design inert, and here it is a dead mutant instead of a shipped mistake.**\n\n*** BUT `NULL_MEDIUM` IS NOT A NULL MEDIUM. ***  The mutator reads `if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"): replacement = FIXED_BLOCK` -- **so the leg applies the SAME fixed block as BASE and is BASE under another name.**  It scores 28/28 because it is identical, not because the detector survives a degenerate input.\n\n**VACUITY FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS THEREFORE UNDISCHARGED**: the detector must FAIL on a stale copy or a comment-only file, proving it carries identity rows and is actually testing the binary it claims.  A leg that cannot fail proves nothing and reads in the table as though it did -- *the check-that-cannot-fail shape, inside a floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch that shape.*\n\n**OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS:** a real null medium -- run the frozen probe against a STALE binary, or against a source whose only change is a comment -- and show it RED.  Cheap, one leg, and it is the difference between claiming clause (c) and discharging it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: THE KILL TABLE\u0027S NULL-MEDIUM LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, SO VACUITY-FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS UNDISCHARGED.  L2 -- repaired in-round. ***\n\n`killtable_20260911/mutate_rlwimi.py:179` reads\n\n    if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"):\n        replacement = FIXED_BLOCK\n\nso the NULL_MEDIUM leg COMPILES BYTE-IDENTICAL CODE TO BASE.  Its 28/28 in `SCORES.txt` is not a result: all three legs are the same binary, and the leg would report 28/28 whether or not the detector could detect anything.\n\nClause (c) requires the detector to FAIL on a medium carrying none of the behaviour under test.  A leg that recompiles the fix is a third copy of the base leg wearing the clause\u0027s name.\n\n**This is the check-that-cannot-fail family arriving INSIDE the floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch vacuity** -- which is worth recording as its own shape.  The carrier\u0027s existing instances are a `say` in a shell script and a sentence in a seat table; this one is a kill-table leg, i.e. it sits in the measurement apparatus and renders as a scored row.  A reader of `SCORES.txt` sees four green identity-ish legs and reads three independent confirmations where there is one.\n\nNOTHING ELSE IN THE ROUND IS AFFECTED: the six real mutants each scored a genuine kill (M_REV_COND 13/28, M_ONLY_CAST 19/28, M_ONLY_HIGH 19/28, M_POST_LOOP 19/28, M_ORIG 14/28, HEAD 14/28), and M_POST_LOOP at 19/28 independently proves the conditional\u0027s PLACEMENT is load-bearing.  The fix is not in question; the FLOOR CLAIM is.\n\nIN FLIGHT: agy 3.8 is building the real leg -- an identity `X(rlwimi)` body, in a scratch clone, no shared build tree touched -- where every row that genuinely observes rlwimi must RED.  Any row still green under an identity body is vacuous by construction and is the most valuable thing that run can return.  Brief predicts the `census` bookkeeping rows may legitimately survive; that is to be DISCLOSED with its reason, not waived.\n\nSTILL OWED after that: the full panel pass 2 on the diff.  Held rather than fired because a measuring seat\u0027s kill table was in flight and panel seats are CPU-heavy -- a deliberate hold, neither a seat failure nor agreement.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c \u0027^row(\u0027` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) \u0026 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I\u0027s RTL is `m \u003c- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n\u003e *\u0027There is no shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.\u0027*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`\u0027s two expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule\u0027s join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy \u0027no new oracle\u0027, the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n\u003e *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *\u0027If a 64-bit processor\u0027s 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.\u0027*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *\u0027A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word\u0027* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat\u0027s more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file\u0027s idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
