 succeeded in 442ms:
18993:### `ppcrldimimask` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
18994:
18995:`cpu_ppc_instr.c`, grep anchor `X(rldimi)`: the rotate loop's post-decrement **always** leaves its shift
18996:variable at -1, so the insert mask always runs to bit 63 instead of `63 - SH`. **The instruction
18997:overwrites the `64 - SH` destination bits the architecture requires it to PRESERVE**, for every `SH != 0`.
18998:
18999:Measured, destination seeded to 0 and an all-ones source: `SH=8` gives all-ones where the architecture
19000:wants the low byte preserved; `SH=32` gives all-ones where it wants the low word preserved; `SH=0` agrees,
19001:and is the one safe case. This is the standard bitfield **insert** -- a compiler using it to splice a field
19002:destroys everything to the right of that field.
19003:
19004:*** The durable repair is NOT to patch the `63 - sh` expression. *** That loop idiom is shared by
19005:`rldicl`, `rldicr` and `rldimi`; `rldicr` survives only because it never reads the variable again. **Any
19006:future edit that reaches for it after the rotate loop in any of the three will be wrong the same way.**
19007:Save it before the loop instead. Same reachability and the same gate blind spot as `ppcrldiclzero`.
19008:
19009:### `ppcrlwimiwidth` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10
19010:
19011:`cpu_ppc_instr.c`, grep anchor `X(rlwimi)`: the 32-bit rotate is performed at **64-bit width** in the
19012:64-bit build, so high-half source data leaks into the low 32 bits. The file is compiled twice and the
19013:operand type is 64 bits in one of them; the architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the source's LOW
19014:half only, and **the high half plays no part whatsoever**.
19015:
19016:Measured with source `0xAAAAAAAA0000000F`, destination seeded, shift 4: `got 0x5a5a5a5aaaaaaaf0` against
19017:`want 0x5a5a5a5a000000f0`. The `0xAAAAAA` is high-half data that cannot architecturally reach there.
19018:
19019:**Attribution is pinned by two companion rows.** `rlwinm` on the same source and shift is correct, because
19020:it casts to 32 bits before rotating, and `rlwimi` at shift 0 is correct because the ternary short-circuits
19021:the rotate. So the defect is the missing truncation in this one handler, not the shape of the expression.
19022:
19023:*A row testing this is vacuous unless the source's two halves DIFFER: the measuring seat's own first draft
19024:used an all-ones source, under which a 64-bit rotate and a true 32-bit one agree bit for bit, and it
19025:corrected the row rather than shipping it green.*
19026:
19027:**Adjacent, not a defect but recorded so a round does not rediscover it:** `rldic`, `rldcl` and `rldcr` are
19028:not implemented at all -- the decoder's default swallows them. `rldic` in particular is documented and
19029:compiler-emitted.
19030:
19031:### `ppcrldiclzero` -- *** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3 2026-09-10, AND IT IS A CLASS. ***
19032:
19033:Confirmed on three unmodified in-tree machines including the Power Mac G5 model: **190 of 190 census
19034:cells return zero** on the committed build and 0 of 190 under the one-character repair (19/27 -> 24/27,
19035:logs `witness_committed.out` / `witness_fix.out`).
19190:### `ppcrldimimask` / `ppcrlwimiwidth` -- DESIGNED 2026-09-10, and the review found a SECOND defect plus THREE new rows
19191:
19192:`regress/artifacts/ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt` (GPT-6 Astra at max, zero content flags,
19193:13,496 bytes). It confirmed every premise about `rldimi`, **refuted my premise about `rlwimi`**, and
19194:turned up three defects nobody had asked about.
19195:
19196:**`rldimi` -- FOUR coordinated edits, and my sketch had one of them.** `sh` really is `-1` on exit for
19197:every SH including zero, the rotate body really is a correct `ROTL64`, and the mask really does always
19198:run to bit 63. But saving SH is not enough: the comparison happens *after* the increment, so the bound
19199:is the endpoint's **successor**, and the counter must be **circular** because `MASK(MB, 63-SH)` wraps.
19200:
19201:    int m, n = sh;                       /*  1: keep SH  */
19202:    while (n-- != 0) { ... }             /*  2: consume the copy  */
19203:    do {
19204:            tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));
19205:            m = (m + 1) & 63;            /*  3: circular increment  */
19206:    } while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));     /*  4: successor, modulo 64  */
19207:
19208:**My description of the defect was half of it.** For non-wrapping masks the handler overwrites the low
19209:SH bits that must survive; for **wrapping** masks it *omits* the required insertion into positions
19210:`0..63-SH`, leaving those destination bits unchanged. A repair validated only on non-wrapping encodings
19211:fixes one direction and leaves the other.
19212:
19213:A partial repair that saves SH and fixes only the bound reaches `(uint64_t)1 << -1` at `MB=40, SH=40`.
19214:**The seat declined to call that an infinite loop**, on the ground that doing so would assign defined
19215:behaviour to an undefined program - which is the right discipline and is what my brief's question got
19216:wrong.
19217:
19218:**`rlwimi` -- a cast alone is NOT the repair.** The width contamination is confirmed as filed, but there
19219:is a second defect at the same site: `ROTL32` is defined as rotating `x || x`, the low word **duplicated**,
19220:and when `MB > ME` the mask wraps to cover every architectural position `0..31` - the whole high half. So
19221:`RA`'s high 32 bits must be **replaced** with the rotated word, and this implementation preserves them
19222:unconditionally.
19223:
19224:**Scope: TWO rounds, not one.** One repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal; the other
19225:repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating it needs expectations outside the
19226:doubleword detector's contract.
19227:
19228:Both replacements were validated offline against an independent bit-membership oracle - 40,960 `rldimi`
19229:cases and 655,360 `rlwimi` cases. **Neither was compiled**: the seat had no native compiler and WSL
19230:returned `E_ACCESSDENIED`. This is a reading plus a model, not a measurement of this tree.
19231:
19232:### `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` (MED) -- filed 2026-09-10. *** The oracle and the defect share the same misreading of the same sentence. ***
19233:
19234:`regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/opus_ppc_rldicl_witness_20260910.py` is a **committed** artifact of
19235:`#522`. Two of its model oracles are wrong for wrapping masks, named by the review and then verified here
19236:by opening the file:
19237:
19238:* `want_rldimi()` computes the **intersection** of `bits mb..63` with `bits 0..63-sh`. Correct when
19239:  `mb <= 63-sh`; when `mb > 63-sh` the two ranges are **disjoint and the intersection is empty**, so it
19240:  returns the untouched destination for every wrapping encoding. Its own docstring shows how: it cites the
19241:  manual's *prose* ("1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH"), which is the non-wrapping reading, rather
19242:  than the `MASK` definition that wraps.
19243:* `mask32()` is documented "confined to the low 32 bits". Its companion `rotl32()` correctly returns the
19244:  duplicated `(r32 << 32) | r32` - so the model *has* the duplication and then masks it away.
19245:
19246:**This does not invalidate `#522`**, which is `rldicl` and uses neither oracle. The exposure is forward:
19247:both sibling rows are filed on measurements this witness produced, and **the wrapping encodings are exactly
19248:where both repairs do their new work**. An uncorrected oracle would score a correct repair wrong, or agree
19249:with a repair that fixes only the non-wrapping half - a check that cannot fail, because the oracle omits
19250:the wrap in the same way the implementation does.
19251:
19252:### `ppcrlwhighhalf` (HIGH) -- filed 2026-09-10 as a dispute and RESOLVED THE SAME DAY as a DEFECT against Book I [L4 2026-10-09, 7n-3: this heading read "(MED) -- filed 2026-09-10, and it is a DISPUTE rather than a defect"]; rung-3 witness on committed HEAD: `regress/artifacts/ppcrlwhighhalf/witness_committed_20261009/`
19253:
19254:I told the review that `rlwinm` and `rlwnm` are the correct idiom `rlwimi` fails to follow. **They are
19255:correct about the low word and, on the RTL reading, wrong about the high one.** The decoder builds a
19256:32-bit mask with a correct wrap; `X(rlwinm)` then holds `tmp` as `uint32_t` and assigns it into a 64-bit
19257:register, which **zero-extends**, so the high half is unconditionally 0 where the RTL would put the
19258:rotated word.
19259:
19260:*** Filed as a dispute because the manual contradicts itself, and the seat said so rather than letting its
19261:own reading stand unqualified: *** one line asserts blanket upper-half clearing for `rlwinm`, conflicting
19262:with that instruction's RTL and with the general duplicated-rotation / wrapping-mask definitions. **This
19263:row adopts neither reading.** Three further PowerPC manuals sit unconsulted in `_scratchpad/`, and
19264:compiler expectations are the strong practical argument either way.
19265:
19266:It must not block `ppcrlwimiwidth`'s narrowing half, which is wrong under every reading.
19267:
