*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py (28 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. ***
The handler takes its operands as MODE_uint_t and then rotates with a shift pair using 32-sh.  cpu_ppc.c defines DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32, so the file is compiled TWICE and MODE_uint_t is **uint64_t** in the 64-bit build.  The architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the LOW half of the source -- **the high half plays no part whatsoever** -- but the right-shift pulls bits out of the high half straight into the low 32 bits, which is exactly where the mask lives.
The 0xAAAAAA is high-half data the architecture says cannot reach there.
**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.
THE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.
*** THE SECOND DEFECT: WHEN `MB > ME' THE ARCHITECTURAL MASK WRAPS ACROSS THE 64-BIT BOUNDARY AND COVERS THE ENTIRE HIGH HALF, so RA's high 32 bits must be REPLACED with the rotated word -- and this implementation preserves them unconditionally. ***  `ROTL32(x, y)' is defined as rotating `x || x', the low word DUPLICATED (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:17143'), and the rlwimi RTL at `:29989' combines that with `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)'.  The wrapping mask visits `MB+32..63, 0..ME+32' -- which includes every architectural position `0..31'.
In the 32-bit compilation the conversion back to `MODE_uint_t' discards that high half, so the existing behaviour there is unchanged.
ROUND SCOPE: **TWO rounds, not one**, with [[ppcrldimimask]].  Same file and family, but one repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal while the other repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating the second needs word-duplication and high-half expectations that lie outside the doubleword detector's contract.
**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.
**THE HIGH-HALF INSERTION HALF IS CONFIRMED OWED, 2026-09-10.**  It was filed as depending on the [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] dispute; that dispute is now RESOLVED by PowerPC Book I v2.01, which states the wrapping MASK and makes no upper-half clearing claim.  So this round needs BOTH halves -- the source narrowing AND the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion -- and the narrowing alone is not the instruction's repair.
`ppcrlwhighhalf`'s adopted repair turned out INERT because the DECODER mutates `mb` to equal `me` before the handler selection that tests `mb > me`.  The same question had to be asked here, since the proposed repair uses the identical guard.
**STILL RUNNING when the print budget expired: the kill table** (`killtable_20260911/`).  Same orphan shape as this seat's earlier round today -- *"root agent idle; waiting for 1 background task(s)"* -- but the brief told it to write its report to disk as it goes, so the artifacts are accumulating rather than being held for a final flush.  **A 120-minute print budget was not enough either; the lesson is to collect from the artifacts directory, not from the seat's stdout.**
  * **`M_ONLY_CAST` 19/28 and `M_ONLY_HIGH` 19/28** -- each half of the repair ALONE is killed, so **neither half suffices** and the two-part claim is measured rather than asserted.
STILL OWED after that: the full panel pass 2 on the diff.  Held rather than fired because a measuring seat's kill table was in flight and panel seats are CPU-heavy -- a deliberate hold, neither a seat failure nor agreement.
*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE "19-ROW" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***
Measured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **"24-row detector"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.
**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring "19 rows" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*
*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*
=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===
**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*
**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.

